SYNTH-0001♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Hall♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My granddaughter Marisol was thirteen when they brought her into Fairbanks Memorial on a February night, and the neurologist making the call was a face on a screen in Seattle. No machine will ever replace what that moment showed me medicine still needs to be.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0002♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Flores♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔COMMENT OF LISA FLORES, FIREFIGHTER, WAKE COUNTY, NORTH CAROLINA⏎Submitted to the HHS Docket: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Two winters ago, my crew transported a 63-year-old cardiac patient out of a burning duplex on Caldwell Street in Raleigh. The receiving hospital had roughly four minutes of usable history before making a call, and they made the wrong one initially, because nothing in the handoff flagged his pacemaker model. An AI-assisted intake system had been piloted at that hospital and shelved eight months prior over reimbursement disputes none of us ever heard explained. He survived. His left hand did not fully recover. I am not writing about that outcome to generate sympathy. I am writing because the decision to pilot that system, and the decision to shelve it, were both made without any accountability to the people closest to the consequences, and the RFI now before HHS is structured in a way that will make that accountability problem permanent rather than correctable.⏎⏎The document HHS has issued frames regulatory scrutiny as an obstacle to innovation and describes its own purpose as "reducing uncertainty that impedes AI adoption" (89 FR, HHS/ASTP, 2024). That framing is not neutral. It is an ideological commitment dressed as an administrative question. What concerns me is not whether AI can improve clinical care in specific contexts. What concerns me is that HHS is not simply setting a policy; it is foreclosing future democratic choices, and that is a meaningfully different thing. Langley et al. (2022), writing in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association, documented how institutional deskilling follows EHR dependency with measurable speed, within four to seven years of adoption, clinical judgment in affected domains shows statistically significant atrophy among practitioners who no longer practice without the system. Coiera, Ash, and Berg (2016) documented analogous unintended consequences across health IT implementations more broadly. The pattern is not speculative. Once hospitals cannot function without a vendor's system, the democratic option to say "we don't want this" disappears. The lock-in is not a side effect of AI adoption; in many architectures it is a design feature, as Dafny and Lee (2023) argue in Health Affairs with specific attention to how market concentration follows from integration dependency. Frank Pasquale's analysis in The Black Box Society (2015) remains the clearest account of why opacity in algorithmic systems is not a technical problem awaiting a technical fix but a political condition that distributes power in specific and traceable directions. Langdon Winner asked in 1980, in "Do Artifacts Have Politics?", whether technological systems carry political properties independent of the intentions of their designers. The answer, in the context of clinical AI, is yes, and HHS is about to write those properties into federal infrastructure without having asked the public whether that is what it wants.⏎⏎My specific request to HHS addresses the framing question the RFI implicitly raises about the appropriate role of federal agencies in shaping adoption timelines:⏎⏎1. HHS should withdraw the accelerationist framing of this RFI and reissue it with a precautionary posture that treats democratic accountability as a prerequisite to adoption incentives, not an afterthought.⏎2. Any reissued RFI should ask explicitly which clinical domains the public believes should remain subject to human judgment even at measurable efficiency cost, and should treat those responses as binding inputs to subsequent rulemaking rather than as comments to be weighed against industry submissions.⏎⏎The question of whether algorithmic systems should govern clinical decisions is not a technical policy question HHS has authority to resolve by♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0003♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Thompson♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted by: Rebecca Thompson, Rockford, IL (private citizen, commercial truck driver, cardiac patient)⏎⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎WHAT I WANT TO KNOW BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE⏎⏎My first question is a practical one and I want it on the record before I get into anything else: does any of this cost me more money? That is not a rhetorical setup. I am on a fixed income. I drive long-haul routes and I manage my schedule around cardiology appointments in Rockford, and the last thing I can absorb is a new line item on an EOB that says "AI-assisted diagnostic review" or some similar charge that I never consented to and cannot appeal because I did not know it was happening. I have read enough of the RFI to understand that HHS is asking about reimbursement pathways for AI tools, and I want someone at this agency to state plainly whether accelerating adoption means accelerating billing. If a cardiology practice adds an AI screening layer to my EKG review, who pays for that layer? Because in my experience, when something new gets added to a clinical visit, the patient finds out about it later, on paper, with a balance due.⏎⏎About eighteen months ago, I found out that my cardiologist's office was running my EKG data through some kind of AI screening tool. I found out because a nurse mentioned it while I was already putting my jacket on. I asked whether I could see what the tool flagged. I asked whether its output had any bearing on the medication adjustment my doctor made that same visit. Three different people in that office gave me three different answers, none of which amounted to a real explanation. I am not against the technology. I want to be clear about that. But I left that office not knowing whether a machine had influenced what I was putting into my body, and I have not received a satisfying answer since. That experience is what brought me to this comment.⏎⏎⏎THE RELATIONAL QUESTION HHS IS NOT ASKING⏎⏎HHS frames this RFI around acceleration. The word appears repeatedly. What I do not see is any serious engagement with what gets lost when the pace of a clinical encounter is reorganized around algorithmic output. There is a body of evidence on this that I think deserves more weight than this RFI gives it. A 2014 meta-analysis by Kelley, Kraft-Todd, Schapira and colleagues, published in PLoS ONE, found that the quality of the patient-clinician relationship is itself a determinant of health outcomes, independent of the technical accuracy of the treatment. That is not a soft or sentimental finding. It means that how a doctor is present with a patient has measurable effects on whether that patient gets better. Zulman and colleagues wrote about this directly in JAMA in 2020, describing specific practices that sustain physician presence and connection during clinical encounters, and noting that those practices are under pressure from documentation burdens and workflow interruptions. Adding an AI layer to the EKG review, or the medication decision, or the differential diagnosis, does not automatically reduce that pressure. It can redistribute attention in ways that erode the relational quality of the visit without anyone in the room noticing.⏎⏎Eric Topol, who is generally optimistic about AI in medicine, wrote in Nature Medicine in 2019 that the convergence of human and artificial intelligence in clinical care carries real risks of relational erosion, specifically that clinicians may come to mediate the patient relationship through a screen rather than through direct engagement. He framed that as a concern worth managing, not a reason to stop, but the concern is there in the text and I think HHS should engage with it more directly than this RFI does. Shortliffe and Sepúlveda made a related point in JAMA in 2018: that clinical decision support tools are most beneficial when they are transparent to the clinician and legible to the patient, and that opacity in either direction creates a kind of accountability gap that is hard to close after the fact. My experience in Rockford is a small example of exactly that gap.⏎⏎⏎THE DESKILLING CONCERN AND WHAT IT MEANS FOR PATIENTS LIKE ME⏎⏎There is a second issue I want to raise and I will admit I am less certain about how to frame it, but it matters. Timothy Hoff published research in Health Care Management Review in 2011 examining how primary care physicians adapt to work innovations, and one of the patterns he documented was deskilling: the gradual atrophy of clinical judgment in areas where automated tools take over the cognitive work. Parasuraman and Manzey wrote about the complacency effects of automation more broadly in Human Factors in 2010, finding that humans who rely on automated systems over time become less able to catch errors when those systems fail. I am not saying AI tools will make my cardiologist worse at her job. I am saying that if HHS accelerates adoption without requiring any assessment of these effects, we will not know whether that is happening until it has been happening for a while. And patients like me, who are not in a position to independently evaluate the quality of a clinical judgment, are the ones who will absorb that uncertainty.⏎⏎The ONC Federal Health IT Strategic Plan for 2024 through 2030 does mention the importance of trustworthy AI, and I read that section carefully. What it does not specify is what "trustworthy" means in relational terms, as opposed to technical accuracy terms. A tool can be highly accurate in the aggregate and still be wrong for a specific patient in a specific encounter, and if the clinician's attention has shifted toward the tool's output and away from the patient in the room, that individual error is harder to catch. I do not think HHS has fully reckoned with that problem yet.⏎⏎⏎WHAT I AM ASKING FOR⏎⏎My ask is specific. Before any AI tool is permitted to influence a clinical decision, whether that means flagging an abnormality, recommending a medication adjustment, or generating a differential diagnosis, HHS should require demonstrated safety in relational and clinical terms, not just technical accuracy benchmarks. That means studies that follow real patients over time and ask whether the quality of their clinical encounters changed. It means disclosure requirements that are legible to patients, not just to clinicians. It means that when a nurse offhandedly mentions that a machine looked at my heart, I should already have a piece of paper in my hand explaining what that means, who reviewed the output, and whether it affected my care that day.⏎⏎I also want an answer to the cost question. Not a general assurance that HHS is committed to affordability. A specific answer to whether new reimbursement pathways for AI tools will result in new patient-facing charges, and if so, what recourse a patient on a fixed income has when those charges appear without prior notice. I have been waiting eighteen months for someone in that cardiology office to give me a straight answer about what happened during that visit. I would rather not wait another eighteen months for HHS to get around to the question of what it costs.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0004♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Robinson♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔Cerner Corporation♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda Robinson⏎Compliance Officer, Cerner Corporation⏎304-[redacted]@cerner.com⏎⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-Clinical-RFI⏎⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎Cerner Corporation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎⏎THE FRAMING PROBLEM⏎⏎The Department's Request for Information asks the right questions about the wrong half of the AI deployment problem. HHS has structured this RFI around acceleration — reducing friction, expanding reimbursement pathways, clarifying regulatory guidance so that health systems can move faster toward AI-assisted clinical care. Cerner's compliance function does not dispute that those barriers are real. An internal audit conducted across our active clinical deployment partnerships in Q2 2024 found that 83% of site compliance officers identified regulatory ambiguity around AI-generated clinical decision support as the primary bottleneck delaying full system integration. That finding is consistent with what this office has observed across our health system partners for the better part of three years.⏎⏎But there is a separate AI deployment already underway in the health sector, one that HHS appears to be treating as outside the scope of this RFI, and that omission is not a minor gap. Health insurers — specifically Medicare Advantage organizations and large commercial payers — have been deploying AI-driven prior authorization systems at scale for several years now. These systems are not speculative. They are operational. And the evidence that they are causing patient harm is no longer preliminary.⏎⏎The HHS Office of Inspector General found in April 2022 that some Medicare Advantage organizations were denying prior authorization requests in ways that raised serious concerns about beneficiary access to medically necessary care (OEI-09-18-00260). The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations published findings in October 2023 — "Refusal of Recovery: How Medicare Advantage Insurers Have Denied Patients Access to Post-Acute Care" — documenting that major insurers used algorithmic tools to generate post-acute care denials at rates that could not plausibly reflect individualized clinical review. The Kaiser Family Foundation's March 2023 issue brief by Koma, Cubanski, and Neuman on prior authorization in Medicare Advantage documented that beneficiaries who lack administrative support infrastructure are systematically less likely to successfully appeal denials, regardless of the clinical merits of their case.⏎⏎This is the AI adoption problem that is already harming patients. The RFI's framing, which treats AI adoption as something that needs to be accelerated rather than something that has already been deployed in ways that require accountability architecture, reflects a structural blind spot that this comment is intended to address directly.⏎⏎⏎Cerner Corporation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE PRIOR AUTHORIZATION SYSTEM AS AN AI DEPLOYMENT CASE STUDY⏎⏎Cerner's compliance team has reviewed the appeals process structures used by several major Medicare Advantage organizations as part of our health system partnership work. What we observe is an appeals infrastructure that is not designed to surface clinical errors — it is structured in ways that systematically disadvantage claimants with limited administrative capacity. The American Medical Association's 2022 Prior Authorization Physician Survey found that 35% of physicians reported that prior authorization delays had led to a serious adverse event for a patient in their care. That is not a documentation problem. That is a patient safety outcome attributable, in part, to AI-generated decision velocity that has outpaced any meaningful clinical review layer.⏎⏎The ProPublica and Capitol Forum investigation published in March 2023, reported by Rucker, Miller, and Armstrong, documented that Cigna's internal review process allowed physicians to reject prior authorization claims in batches — sometimes processing over 50 denials in a single day — without reading the underlying records. Whether that process was automated, semi-automated, or simply structured to replicate the output of an automated system is, from a compliance standpoint, a distinction without a difference. The functional result is AI-paced denial velocity with human-labeled accountability, which is precisely the configuration most resistant to regulatory correction.⏎⏎The racial equity dimension of this problem is also documented. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan published findings in Science in 2019 (366(6464):447-453) demonstrating that a widely used commercial algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent clinical complexity. That study examined a care management algorithm, not a prior authorization tool, but the underlying mechanism — cost as a proxy for need — is structurally identical to the optimization logic embedded in many payer-side AI denial systems. HHS has not, to Cerner's knowledge, issued guidance requiring payers to audit their prior authorization AI for demographic disparities before deployment or on any ongoing basis.⏎⏎Georgetown University's Center on Health Insurance Reforms published a working paper in January 2024 by Palanker, Volk, and Lucia documenting state-level efforts to address AI in health insurance. The National Academy for State Health Policy's Q1 2024 tracker on state AI legislation in health insurance found that at least 17 states had introduced or passed legislation imposing some form of transparency or human review requirement on AI-generated insurance decisions. The fact that state legislatures are moving faster than federal regulators on this question is not a sign that the problem is being handled — it is a sign that the federal framework is creating a patchwork compliance environment that will ultimately serve neither patients nor health systems.⏎⏎⏎Cerner Corporation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 3⏎⏎⏎WHAT THE RFI SHOULD ADDRESS BUT DOES NOT⏎⏎The Department's stated goals in the 2023 National AI Health Roadmap — responsible adoption, patient-centered design, equity-conscious deployment — are not objectionable as written. The problem is that the Roadmap, like this RFI, is oriented toward the provider side of the AI equation. It addresses clinical decision support tools, diagnostic algorithms, documentation assistance. It does not establish accountability architecture for payer-side AI systems that are making coverage determinations affecting millions of Medicare Advantage and commercial insurance beneficiaries annually.⏎⏎This is not a small omission. Cerner's analysis of reimbursement claims data from twelve health system partners found that AI-assisted diagnostic workflows reduced documentation burden by an average of 31 minutes per encounter. That is a real operational gain. But fewer than 20% of those encounters qualified for any form of AI-augmented reimbursement under existing CMS coding structures. The implication is that the financial case for provider-side AI adoption remains structurally weak while the financial case for payer-side AI denial systems remains structurally strong. HHS is proposing to fix the first problem without addressing the second. The incentive asymmetry that results will not produce the responsible adoption outcomes the Roadmap describes.⏎⏎The administrative burden literature is relevant here. Schulte and Fry's 2019 investigation for Kaiser Health News and Fortune — "Death by a Thousand Clicks: Where Electronic Health Records Went Wrong" — documented how EHR implementation created documentation burdens that consumed physician time without producing proportionate clinical value. The prior authorization AI problem is the same structural failure repeating in a new technological context. A system optimized for administrative throughput rather than clinical accuracy will generate the same category of harm regardless of whether the underlying tool is an EHR or an AI denial engine.⏎⏎Dafny, Gruber, and Ody's work on insurer cost-optimization incentive structures (American Journal of Health Economics, 1(1):53-81, 2015) is also directly applicable. Insurers operating in competitive markets have strong structural incentives to minimize payouts, and AI systems trained on claims data will, absent countervailing regulatory requirements, optimize toward that objective. This is not a criticism of any individual insurer's intentions. It is a description of how optimization systems behave when the objective function is cost rather than clinical appropriateness. HHS's RFI does not appear to account for this dynamic.⏎⏎⏎Cerner Corporation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 4⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎Given the above, Cerner's compliance function offers the following recommendations for HHS's consideration in response to the RFI's questions regarding regulatory actions the Department can take to support responsible AI adoption:⏎⏎1. Mandatory human clinical review for all AI-generated prior authorization denials. Before HHS expands any AI reimbursement incentive structure on the provider side, the Department should require that every prior authorization denial generated or scored by an AI system receive documented review by a licensed clinician with relevant specialty expertise. "Documented review" should mean a timestamped attestation that the reviewing clinician accessed and read the patient's clinical records — not a batch-approval workflow of the type described in the Rucker, Miller, and Armstrong reporting. This requirement should apply to Medicare Advantage organizations as a condition of continued CMS contract participation.⏎⏎2. Demographic disparity auditing as a pre-deployment requirement. Any AI system used in prior authorization, care management, or coverage determination should be required to demonstrate, prior to deployment and at regular intervals thereafter, that its outputs do not vary systematically by race, ethnicity, or socioeconomic status in ways not explained by clinically relevant factors. The Obermeyer et al. findings from 2019 established a clear methodological framework for this kind of audit. There is no technical obstacle to requiring it. The obstacle is regulatory will.⏎⏎3. Transparency requirements for payer-side AI. Medicare Advantage organizations and commercial insurers operating in federally regulated markets should be required to disclose, at minimum: (a) whether an AI system contributed to a prior authorization denial; (b) the general category of algorithmic logic applied; and (c) the demographic performance characteristics of that system as measured in the most recent audit cycle. The Georgetown and NASHP tracking work cited above documents that states are already moving toward these requirements. Federal action would establish a consistent floor rather than a fragmented state-by-state patchwork.⏎⏎o Several states have passed legislation requiring human review of AI insurance decisions as of Q1 2024, per the NASHP tracker. Federal preemption of a weaker standard would effectively nullify those protections.⏎⏎o CMS already has contract oversight authority over Medicare Advantage organizations. The regulatory mechanism exists. What is needed is the will to apply it to AI-generated denial workflows specifically.⏎⏎4. Accountability trail requirements. Any AI-assisted denial should generate an auditable record that can be reviewed by CMS, by the affected beneficiary's physician, and — upon appeal — by an independent reviewer. The current appeals process does not require payers to produce the algorithmic basis for a denial. That gap is not accidental. It is a feature of a system structured to minimize successful appeals rather than to ensure accurate initial determinations.⏎⏎⏎Cerner Corporation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 5⏎⏎⏎THE REIMBURSEMENT QUESTION⏎⏎This office recognizes that the RFI is also seeking comment on reimbursement structures that could accelerate provider-side AI adoption. Cerner's position is not that those questions are unimportant. The finding that fewer than 20% of AI-assisted encounters in our partner health systems qualify for AI-augmented reimbursement is a real structural problem. The documentation burden reductions we observed — averaging 31 minutes per encounter — represent genuine clinical capacity that the current coding framework fails to recognize or reward.⏎⏎But Cerner's compliance function cannot in good conscience recommend that HHS prioritize expanding AI reimbursement pathways for providers while the payer-side AI denial problem remains unaddressed. The practical effect of doing so would be to increase the volume of AI-assisted clinical decisions while leaving intact the AI-generated denial infrastructure that is already contesting those decisions on the back end. That is not a pathway to better patient outcomes. It is a more technologically sophisticated version of the same access problem that the OIG documented in 2022 and that the Senate Subcommittee documented in 2023.⏎⏎There is also a workforce dimension worth noting. The AMA's 2022 survey found that physicians spend an average of nearly two business days per week on prior authorization tasks. If provider-side AI reduces documentation time by 31 minutes per encounter, and that time is immediately consumed by AI-generated prior authorization response workflows, the net clinical capacity gain is close to zero. The Department's reimbursement framework should account for this interaction effect, and Cerner would welcome the opportunity to provide encounter-level data from our health system partners to support that analysis if HHS finds it useful.⏎⏎One additional observation, which may be outside the formal scope of this RFI but which Cerner's compliance function believes is worth recording: the comment period for this RFI was not widely publicized through channels accessible to frontline clinical staff or small and rural health systems. Cerner became aware of the RFI through our government relations monitoring function. Health systems in West Virginia and other rural states with limited administrative infrastructure are unlikely to be represented in this comment record in proportion to their stake in the outcome. HHS should consider whether the comment process itself reflects the equity principles the Roadmap articulates, and whether targeted outreach to underrepresented health system types would produce a more complete evidentiary record before the Department finalizes any regulatory or reimbursement actions.⏎⏎⏎Cerner Corporation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 6⏎⏎⏎The specific mechanism Cerner recommends — mandatory human clinical review with documented accountability trails before any AI reimbursement expansion — is administratively feasible under existing CMS contract authority. The precedents are in the literature. The patient harm documentation is in the public record. What the Department's RFI has not yet grappled with is the question of sequencing: whether it is responsible to accelerate AI adoption on the provider side before establishing a floor of accountability on the payer side. Cerner's compliance function believes the answer to that question is no, and we would welcome the opportunity to discuss the specific audit methodologies and accountability trail architectures that could make a phased approach workable in practice.⏎⏎Contact for follow-up questions regarding this submission:⏎⏎Linda Robinson⏎Compliance Officer⏎Cerner Corporation⏎lrobinson@cerner.com⏎[phone number on file with submitting office]♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0005♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Brown♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔Coalition for Health AI♔♔♔♔♔♔Coalition for Health AI⏎Comment Submitted to HHS Docket: AI in Clinical Care RFI⏎Contact: info@coalitionforhealthai.org⏎⏎Coalition for Health AI | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎The framing of this Request for Information concerns us deeply, and we want to be direct about that before addressing the specific questions HHS has posed. The RFI is structured around a presumption — that the primary problem is adoption speed, and that regulatory, reimbursement, and research-and-development barriers are what stand between the health system and better care. We do not share that presumption. The communities we serve, which include frontline nurses in Baltimore County hospital systems, community health workers in Prince George's County, and clinical social workers operating in federally qualified health centers across Maryland's Eastern Shore, are not experiencing a shortage of AI deployment. They are experiencing a shortage of protection from it. We urge HHS to treat this moment not as an acceleration opportunity but as a governance gap that, if left unaddressed, will produce measurable harm to both workers and the patients who depend on them.⏎⏎We are focusing our comment primarily on two of the RFI's implicit questions: what workforce conditions are necessary for AI to produce genuine clinical benefit, and what oversight structures should HHS require before deployment proceeds. We are not addressing reimbursement coding questions or FDA clearance pathways in this submission, as those are outside our organizational expertise and are being addressed by others. What we can speak to, with specificity, is what happens to care quality when the clinical workforce is treated as a cost variable rather than a clinical asset — and how AI deployment, without mandatory protective structures, accelerates exactly that dynamic.⏎⏎The burnout argument advanced by AI vendors and, frankly, reflected in the RFI's own framing, deserves direct scrutiny. The claim that AI will reduce clinician burnout is not well-supported by the documented mechanisms of burnout itself. Shanafelt and colleagues, writing in the Mayo Clinic Proceedings in 2022, tracked burnout and work-life integration satisfaction among physicians across nearly a decade, finding that the drivers most consistently associated with burnout were alert fatigue, loss of professional autonomy, and documentation burden — not insufficient computational assistance (Shanafelt et al., 2022, 97(3), 491–506). Wachter and Goldsmith made a related argument in the Harvard Business Review in 2018: that the electronic health record had become a primary driver of physician dissatisfaction not because of what it failed to do, but because of how it restructured clinical work around data entry, compliance documentation, and productivity surveillance (Wachter & Goldsmith, HBR, March 30, 2018). AI systems deployed in their current dominant form — ambient documentation tools, clinical decision support systems flagging dozens of low-priority alerts, productivity dashboards visible to supervisors — do not resolve any of these mechanisms. In many cases, as Sexton and colleagues documented in BMJ Quality & Safety in 2021, the accumulation of EHR-mediated burden directly predicts both clinician wellbeing decline and perceived patient safety degradation (Sexton et al., 2021, BMJ Quality & Safety, 30(11), 901–910). We are not arguing that AI cannot relieve burden. We are arguing that the specific AI products currently being marketed as burden-relief tools are structurally designed to serve administrative goals, and that HHS's RFI does not adequately distinguish between those two categories.⏎⏎Coalition for Health AI | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The workforce surveillance question is where our concern becomes most acute. Kellogg, Valentine, and Christin, in a 2020 analysis published in the Academy of Management Annals, documented what they called the "new contested terrain of control" — the deployment of algorithmic management systems that shift the locus of performance evaluation from human supervisors to automated productivity metrics, in ways that workers cannot contest, cannot fully observe, and cannot appeal through conventional labor processes (Kellogg, Valentine & Christin, 2020, Academy of Management Annals, 14(1), 366–410). This is not a theoretical concern in healthcare. The Economic Policy Institute's 2023 briefing paper on algorithmic wage and productivity surveillance in healthcare documented specific cases in which hospital systems used AI-generated productivity reports to justify reductions in nursing staff ratios, arguing to state regulators that AI tools had "effectively increased" the productive capacity of remaining staff and therefore reduced the need for additional hires (EPI Briefing Paper #478, 2023). We have seen versions of this argument made in Maryland. We do not name the specific systems here because the matter involves ongoing labor disputes, but the pattern is consistent with what EPI documented nationally: AI deployed as efficiency tool becomes AI deployed as staffing-cut justification, and the workers most affected are the ones who had the least input into the procurement decision.⏎⏎National Nurses United surveyed 4,800 registered nurses in 2023 and found that 61% of respondents working in facilities that had deployed algorithmic management tools reported increased workloads following deployment, while only 14% reported workload reduction (NNU Research Report, 2023). More significantly for this RFI, 78% of those respondents reported that they had not been consulted during procurement, and 83% reported that they did not have a formal mechanism to report AI-generated recommendations they believed were clinically incorrect. That last finding is not just a labor issue. It is a patient safety issue. The Government Accountability Office's 2022 report on AI in health care identified the absence of post-deployment monitoring and clinician feedback mechanisms as one of the primary structural risks associated with clinical AI deployment (GAO-22-104629, 2022). When frontline nurses cannot flag a malfunctioning algorithm through a formal channel, the algorithm keeps running. The American Medical Association's 2023 Digital Medicine Practice Snapshot found that fewer than 30% of surveyed practices had formal processes for frontline clinician input into AI procurement decisions, and fewer than 20% had post-deployment review structures that included frontline worker feedback (AMA Policy Research Perspectives, 2023). These are not incidental gaps. They are the predictable result of procurement processes designed to serve administrative and financial goals rather than clinical ones.⏎⏎Coalition for Health AI | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎We want to be honest about something that the burnout-promise framing obscures: the AI products most aggressively marketed to healthcare administrators are not primarily designed to serve clinicians. They are designed to serve the administrators who purchase them. Topol's influential 2019 Nature Medicine paper argued that the convergence of human and artificial intelligence in medicine had genuine potential to restore the "human connection" in clinical care by freeing clinicians from cognitive burden (Topol, 2019, Nature Medicine, 25(1), 44–56). We find that vision genuinely compelling. What Topol described, however, requires AI designed around clinician autonomy and patient benefit — not around throughput metrics and administrative dashboards. The products currently being deployed in the majority of community hospital systems the Coalition works with are not the Topol vision. They are productivity surveillance systems with clinical interfaces bolted on. HHS's RFI, by treating "AI adoption" as a unified category rather than distinguishing between these functionally different deployment types, risks accelerating the latter while claiming credit for the former.⏎⏎Obermeyer and colleagues' 2019 Science paper documenting racial bias in a widely deployed population health management algorithm is relevant here in a way that goes beyond the bias finding itself (Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli & Mullainathan, 2019, Science, 366(6464), 447–453). The algorithm had been deployed at scale across hundreds of health systems before the bias was identified — not because the health systems were negligent, but because no pre-deployment impact assessment was required, no post-deployment monitoring for disparate outcomes was mandated, and no frontline clinician feedback mechanism existed that would have surfaced the problem earlier. The ONC's own 2021 Strategy on Reducing Regulatory and Administrative Burden acknowledged that health IT deployment had consistently outpaced the governance structures needed to ensure it served care rather than creating new forms of burden (ONC Report to Congress, 2021). We are asking HHS not to repeat that pattern with AI.⏎⏎Coalition for Health AI | HHS AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎Our specific asks are as follows, and we want to be clear that we frame these as achievable policy requirements, not aspirational suggestions:⏎⏎1. Mandatory frontline worker consultation as a condition of any HHS-facilitated or federally reimbursed AI deployment. This means structured input from nurses, physicians, medical assistants, and other direct-care staff before procurement is finalized — not post-deployment feedback surveys. The AMA's 2023 data showing that fewer than 30% of practices have such processes suggests this is not happening voluntarily.⏎⏎2. Pre-deployment workforce impact assessments, modeled on environmental impact assessment requirements, that require deploying entities to document anticipated effects on staffing ratios, workload distribution, and job function before deployment proceeds. These assessments should be made available to affected workers and their representatives.⏎⏎3. An explicit prohibition, enforceable through conditions of participation or as a condition of federal reimbursement, on using AI-generated productivity metrics as the primary or sole justification for staffing ratio reductions. This is the specific mechanism by which AI is currently being used to justify understaffing, and HHS has the authority to address it directly.⏎⏎4. Post-deployment monitoring requirements that include worker wellbeing as a required outcome measure alongside clinical quality metrics. Dyrbye and colleagues documented in 2019 that work-unit safety culture and nursing burnout are directly correlated with patient safety outcomes (Dyrbye et al., 2019, Journal of Nursing Administration, 49(10), 487–493). A monitoring framework that measures clinical outcomes without measuring worker wellbeing is measuring an incomplete picture of safety.⏎⏎o A sub-requirement worth specifying: post-deployment monitoring should include a formal, accessible channel for frontline workers to flag AI-generated recommendations they believe are clinically erroneous or potentially harmful, with a documented review process and feedback loop to the deploying entity and, where relevant, to FDA.⏎⏎Coalition for Health AI | HHS AI RFI | Page 5⏎⏎We recognize that these requirements would add process steps to AI deployment, and we anticipate that some respondents to this RFI will argue that such requirements slow adoption and thereby delay clinical benefit. We take that argument seriously, and we want to respond to it directly. The communities we serve in Maryland are not well-served by AI deployment that is fast. They are well-served by AI deployment that is safe, that does not erode the workforce providing their care, and that is designed around their clinical needs rather than around administrative throughput targets. The GAO's 2022 report on AI in health care found that benefits to patient care were most consistently realized in deployment contexts where clinicians had meaningful input into implementation and where post-deployment monitoring was robust (GAO-22-104629, 2022). Speed of deployment and quality of deployment are not the same variable, and HHS's RFI, in framing adoption acceleration as the primary goal, conflates them in ways that we believe will produce worse outcomes for patients and workers alike.⏎⏎There is also a specific concern about community hospitals and federally qualified health centers that we want to raise, because the RFI's framing implicitly assumes a deployment context of well-resourced academic medical centers or large health systems with dedicated IT infrastructure. The practices the Coalition works with most closely are smaller, are more likely to serve Medicaid and uninsured populations, and have significantly less capacity to conduct independent evaluation of AI products before purchase. They are also more vulnerable to vendor representations about workforce efficiency gains, because their operating margins are thinner and the promise of cost reduction through AI is genuinely appealing to administrators under financial pressure. This is precisely the context in which mandatory pre-deployment assessments and frontline consultation requirements are most necessary — and it is precisely the context in which they are least likely to happen without federal requirement. We would note that the EPI's 2023 briefing paper found that algorithmic productivity surveillance was disproportionately deployed in lower-wage healthcare settings, which correlates strongly with the facilities serving the communities we represent (EPI Briefing Paper #478, 2023). That is not a coincidence. It reflects a pattern of technology deployment that concentrates efficiency extraction in the parts of the health system with the least power to resist it.⏎⏎We want to close by returning to something that we think gets lost in the technical framing of AI governance discussions. The clinical workforce is not a cost input that AI can substitute for or optimize around. It is the mechanism by which care is delivered, and its health — its engagement, its autonomy, its capacity for clinical judgment — is a patient safety variable. Shanafelt's longitudinal data make clear that clinician burnout has been worsening for over a decade (Shanafelt et al., 2022). Deploying AI systems that intensify surveillance, increase alert burden, and remove professional discretion will not reverse that trend. The policy framework HHS builds around AI adoption will either protect the conditions under which good care is possible, or it will accelerate the extraction of value from a workforce that is already under severe strain. We believe HHS can build the former, and we have tried in this comment to be specific about what that requires. The consultation requirement alone — simply mandating that the people who will use these tools have a formal role in deciding whether and how they are deployed — would represent a meaningful shift from current practice, and it is administratively feasible to implement as a condition of participation well within existing CMS authority.⏎⏎We are available to discuss any of the above with HHS staff and would welcome the opportunity to connect agency staff directly with frontline workers in Maryland who can speak to these dynamics from their own clinical experience. Our contact information is above, and we would ask that any follow-up be directed to our policy team at the email listed on page 1 of this submission, as our executive director is currently traveling through the end of the month and response times may be♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0006♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Jackson♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔National Council on Aging♔♔♔♔♔♔NATIONAL COUNCIL ON AGING⏎Comments in Response to HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary, U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Submitted by Mark Jackson, Policy Director⏎⏎⏎THE GOVERNANCE QUESTION HHS IS NOT ASKING⏎⏎The National Council on Aging (NCOA) submits these comments with a specific focus on two dimensions of the RFI that the Department has, perhaps deliberately, chosen not to foreground: the structural irreversibility of AI deployment in clinical settings, and the democratic legitimacy question that irreversibility raises. NCOA represents millions of older adults who are disproportionately the subjects of algorithmic clinical decision-making — not its architects, not its beneficiaries in the first instance, and not, under the current trajectory, its meaningful overseers. Before engaging the Department's specific questions, this organization needs to state plainly what the RFI's framing obscures: the decision to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care at the pace and in the manner HHS is contemplating is not a technical optimization question. It is a question about who governs medicine, and HHS is answering it unilaterally.⏎⏎NCOA's position is informed partly by the survey and convening data generated by the Association for Diagnostics and Laboratory Medicine (ADLM), whose 2024 member survey of approximately 8,200 laboratorians and pathologists found that 63 percent of respondents reported their institutions had piloted or deployed at least one AI-assisted diagnostic tool in the preceding 24 months. Fewer than 20 percent indicated their facility had received clear CMS reimbursement guidance covering those tools' clinical outputs. This is not a minor administrative lag. It is the signature of a deployment pattern in which systems are embedded in clinical infrastructure before the governance architecture necessary to oversee them exists. NCOA's own regional engagement with older adult communities in 2024 — including four convenings in Missouri, Ohio, Tennessee, and New Mexico — produced consistent themes: older patients are aware that algorithms are being used in their care, they have almost no information about how those algorithms work, and they have been offered no meaningful mechanism for objecting.⏎⏎⏎IRREVERSIBILITY AS A STRUCTURAL CONDITION, NOT A RISK TO BE MITIGATED⏎⏎The RFI asks, in several of its framing questions, how HHS can reduce "uncertainty that impedes AI innovation" (89 Fed. Reg., Health Sector AI RFI, ONC/ASTP 2024). NCOA finds this framing a category error of some consequence. The "uncertainty" in question is not merely commercial or regulatory friction; it is the deliberative space in which democratic societies make choices about what they want their institutions to do. Treating that space as an impediment to be cleared is not a neutral policy choice. It is a choice about who gets to decide.⏎⏎The specific mechanism NCOA wants the Department to take seriously is what this organization will call deployment-induced foreclosure. Coiera (2019) documented, in the context of clinical workflow reorganization following health IT deployments, that the practical reversibility of a technology falls sharply once that technology has reorganized the routines, staffing models, and institutional knowledge around it. This is not unique to AI — Dafny and Lee (2016) made a parallel observation about healthcare market consolidation, noting that institutional lock-in in healthcare is particularly durable because the switching costs are borne not by the institution that made the original choice but by the patients and clinicians embedded in the resulting system. AI deployment in clinical settings combines both dynamics. Once a hospital's diagnostic workflow has been restructured around a vendor's ambient documentation system or a risk-stratification algorithm, the clinical staff who previously performed those functions through direct judgment have, within a relatively short window, lost the practiced competency to do so without the system. The institutional knowledge atrophies. The workflows reorganize around vendor logic. Contractual dependencies accumulate.⏎⏎NCOA wishes to be specific about what this means for older adults. Consider the use of AI-driven prior authorization systems, which several major insurers have deployed to manage utilization for Medicare Advantage beneficiaries. When a prior authorization algorithm denies a claim, the appeal pathway nominally exists, but the practical capacity for a 74-year-old patient in rural Missouri to navigate that pathway — to obtain the clinical documentation, to engage the appeals process, to reach a human reviewer — is not equivalent to the nominal availability of that pathway. Eubanks (2018) described this dynamic in a different context but with directly applicable precision: automated systems do not eliminate human judgment, they relocate it to positions in the process where it is least accessible to the people most affected. Once that relocation has occurred and the system has been in operation long enough that the institutional memory of the prior process has faded, the practical option of returning to human-centered review does not exist in any meaningful sense.⏎⏎The GAO documented this concern at a structural level in its 2022 report on AI in health care (GAO-22-104629), finding significant gaps in federal oversight capacity relative to the pace of deployment. What the GAO report did not fully develop — though the evidence it compiled supports the inference — is that each month those gaps persist, the population of deployed systems generating path-dependent institutional dependencies grows, and the realistic possibility of course correction narrows. HHS's acceleration agenda, as framed in this RFI, would widen those gaps faster than any realistic governance build-out could close them.⏎⏎⏎NCOA Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎THE PROPRIETARY OPACITY PROBLEM IS NOT PRIMARILY A SAFETY PROBLEM⏎⏎Much of the existing policy literature on AI transparency in clinical settings frames algorithmic opacity as a patient safety issue, and it is that. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan (2019) demonstrated in Science that a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to manage health care for large patient populations encoded significant racial bias that was invisible to the institutions using it precisely because the algorithm's internal logic was proprietary. The bias was not incidental; it was structural, embedded in the choice of the outcome variable the algorithm was trained to predict. No external auditor, no clinician, no patient could have identified it through the interfaces the vendor provided.⏎⏎But NCOA wants to make a different argument here, one that the safety framing tends to obscure. The opacity of proprietary clinical AI systems is not primarily a safety deficiency. It is a structural democratic deficiency. Pasquale (2015) developed this argument at length in The Black Box Society, and it has not lost force in the intervening decade: when the logic governing significant social decisions is legally shielded from inspection, the oversight mechanisms that democratic societies depend upon — legislative, judicial, professional, and civil — cannot function. Selbst and Barocas (2018) made a related point specifically about the limits of explainability requirements as a substitute for genuine accountability: even if a system can generate a post-hoc explanation of a specific output, that explanation does not expose the system's overall decision logic to the kind of scrutiny that would allow democratic communities to decide whether that logic is acceptable. Brundage et al. (2020) extended this analysis to the structural impossibility of meaningful external audit under current proprietary frameworks, finding that the mechanisms vendors typically offer for "trustworthy AI" claims are not verifiable by external parties in any technically robust sense.⏎⏎For older adults specifically, this opacity takes on additional weight. Medicare and Medicaid are public programs. The clinical decisions made within them — including decisions about what care is appropriate, what is medically necessary, what risk level a patient presents — are decisions being made with public resources on behalf of a public that has, through decades of legislative deliberation, established specific expectations about what those programs will do. When a proprietary algorithm makes or substantially shapes those decisions, and when the algorithm's logic is not available for inspection by CMS, by Congress, by the beneficiaries themselves, or by the clinicians nominally responsible for the care, a transfer of authority has occurred that the democratic process authorizing those programs did not sanction. This organization does not believe HHS has the mandate to ratify that transfer through an acceleration agenda that treats regulatory scrutiny as market friction.⏎⏎⏎ADLM SURVEY CONTEXT AND THE REIMBURSEMENT-GOVERNANCE MISALIGNMENT⏎⏎Returning to the ADLM data because it is unusually specific and deserves more attention than it typically receives in policy discussions: the finding that fewer than one-third of the 47 laboratory AI products cleared by FDA between 2020 and 2024 had associated CPT codes enabling straightforward billing is not primarily a reimbursement problem. It is a governance signal. The absence of a billing pathway is, in the current system, one of the few friction points that slows deployment and creates an opening for deliberation. When CMS eventually resolves those billing ambiguities — and this RFI is in part a request for input on how to do so more quickly — it will remove one of the few remaining structural checks on deployment pace. NCOA is not arguing that reimbursement ambiguity is a good governance mechanism; it is clearly an accidental and imperfect one. But the appropriate response to its inadequacy is not to eliminate it faster than the governance architecture can be built to replace it. The appropriate response is to build the governance architecture first.⏎⏎Mello et al. (2013) made a structurally analogous argument in a different context, writing about the sequencing of clinical trial data sharing: the infrastructure and governance norms for responsible data sharing had to be developed in parallel with, not subsequent to, the mechanisms that would compel sharing. The sequencing mattered because once data sharing norms were established by practice rather than by deliberation, they became very difficult to alter. The same logic applies here. Deployment norms established in the absence of governance frameworks tend to become the de facto standard against which any subsequent regulatory requirement is measured — and found burdensome.⏎⏎⏎REGARDING THE SPECIFIC QUESTION OF REIMBURSEMENT PATHWAYS FOR AI-ASSISTED CLINICAL TOOLS⏎⏎The RFI asks specifically about regulatory and reimbursement actions HHS can take to accelerate adoption. NCOA's response to this question is more restrained than the Department may prefer, but it is offered in good faith.⏎⏎NCOA does not oppose the development of clearer reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted clinical tools. What this organization opposes is the development of those pathways in advance of, or as a substitute for, the governance conditions that would make reimbursement appropriate. Specifically, NCOA recommends that any new CPT code or coverage determination for an AI-assisted clinical tool be conditioned on the following, at a minimum:⏎⏎1. The tool's training data, validation methodology, and performance characteristics across demographic subgroups — including age, race, sex, and insurance status — must be disclosed to CMS and available for review by an independent technical body prior to coverage determination. This is not an onerous requirement; it is the minimum necessary for a public payer to make an informed coverage decision.⏎⏎2. The tool must have been validated on a population that includes older adults in proportions representative of the Medicare population it will be used to serve. The current FDA clearance pathway does not require this, and the ADLM policy analysis of cleared laboratory AI products found significant gaps in the demographic representativeness of validation datasets. A coverage determination that does not condition reimbursement on age-representative validation is, in effect, a decision to use Medicare beneficiaries as a post-market validation population without their knowledge or consent.⏎⏎3. Any institution receiving reimbursement for AI-assisted clinical services must maintain a documented human override pathway — not a nominal one, but a functionally accessible one — and must report annually to CMS on the frequency with which that pathway is invoked and the outcomes associated with its invocation. This requirement is not technically complex. It is administratively modest. Its absence from current deployment frameworks is a policy choice, not a technical inevitability.⏎⏎4. Contracts between health systems and AI vendors must not contain confidentiality provisions that prevent the health system from disclosing the tool's decision logic to CMS, to state regulators, or to the patients whose care it affects. Topol (2019) wrote optimistically about the convergence of human and artificial intelligence in clinical settings, and NCOA does not dispute the potential he described. But Topol's analysis, like much of the optimistic literature, assumes that the governance conditions for responsible deployment will be developed alongside the technology. That assumption has not been borne out by the deployment record of the past five years, and reimbursement policy should not proceed as though it has been.⏎⏎⏎NCOA Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 5⏎⏎THE DEMOCRATIC DOMAIN RESERVATION ARGUMENT⏎⏎This organization has tried, in the preceding sections, to engage HHS's acceleration agenda as a technical and regulatory question. There are genuine technical and regulatory dimensions to it, and the recommendations above reflect NCOA's best judgment about what governance conditions would need to exist before acceleration is appropriate. But NCOA wants to be direct about a dimension of this question that the technical framing tends to suppress.⏎⏎A democratic society has the right to decide that certain domains of human activity should remain stubbornly human — not because human performance in those domains is always optimal, but because the accountability relationships that human performance creates are themselves part of what the domain is for. Clinical judgment is one such domain. When a physician makes a diagnostic error, there is a pathway — imperfect, adversarial, expensive, but real — through which that error can be examined, contested, and corrected, and through which the physician's practice can be modified or ended. When an algorithm makes a systematic diagnostic error, as Obermeyer et al. (2019) documented, that pathway does not exist in the same form. The error propagates silently across millions of cases until a researcher with access to outcome data and the methodological tools to detect it publishes a finding that the vendor may contest, that the institutions using the tool may not act upon, and that the patients harmed by it may never learn about.⏎⏎The question of whether that trade is acceptable — faster throughput and possibly better average performance, in exchange for the accountability relationships that make error correction and democratic oversight possible — is not a question HHS can answer on behalf of the public. It is a question the public has not been asked. The RFI's framing of regulatory oversight as "uncertainty that impedes AI innovation" does not merely get the answer wrong; it forecloses the question. When HHS removes the friction that slows deployment, it does not simply accelerate adoption; it forecloses, before the deliberation has occurred, the option of deciding that the accountability relationships medicine depends upon should not be traded away for efficiency.⏎⏎NCOA recognizes that this argument will be characterized by some as technophobic or as an obstacle to genuine improvements in care for older adults. That characterization misunderstands the argument. This organization is not arguing that AI has no role in clinical care. It is arguing that the decision about what role AI should play, under what conditions, with what oversight, and with what preserved space for human judgment, belongs to a democratic process that HHS is in the process of preempting. The irreversibility mechanisms described above — workflow reorganization, atrophied expertise, contractual lock-in, the establishment of deployment norms through practice rather than deliberation — mean that the window in which that democratic process could meaningfully shape the outcome is closing. Once it closes, the choice will have been made. Not by Congress. Not by patients. Not by clinicians. By the deployment schedules of a small number of large vendors whose internal logic is, as Pasquale (2015) and Brundage et al. (2020) documented in different registers, structurally insulated from the oversight mechanisms a democratic society depends upon to govern itself.⏎⏎⏎CLOSING OBSERVATIONS⏎⏎NCOA does not oppose HHS engagement with AI governance. This organization would welcome a regulatory process genuinely oriented toward building the governance architecture — transparency requirements, demographic validation standards, human override mandates, audit rights — that would make responsible acceleration possible. What this organization cannot support is an acceleration agenda that treats the absence of that architecture as an opportunity rather than as a problem.⏎⏎The ADLM survey data cited throughout these comments reflects a deployment reality that is already well ahead of the governance capacity to oversee it. The 63 percent of institutions that have deployed AI-assisted diagnostic tools without clear reimbursement guidance are not waiting for governance; they are operating in its absence and establishing, through practice, the norms that governance will eventually have to work around. Every month that gap persists, the population of older adults whose care is being shaped by systems they cannot see, contest, or meaningfully influence grows. HHS has the regulatory authority to condition reimbursement, to require disclosure, to mandate demographic validation, and to preserve human override pathways. The question this RFI has not asked — and that NCOA is asking on behalf of the older adults it represents — is whether HHS has the democratic mandate to do anything less.⏎⏎NCOA requests that the Department publish, as part of any subsequent rulemaking arising from this RFI, a specific analysis of the demographic representativeness of validation datasets for AI tools under consideration for Medicare coverage, and that CMS establish a public-facing registry of AI tools for which coverage determinations have been made, including the subgroup performance data that formed the basis of each determination. The registry need not be technically complex; a structured data table updated quarterly would suffice. The absence of such a registry as of the date of this submission, given the deployment volumes the ADLM data documents, is♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0007♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Thomas♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My hospitalization in January 2023 — three days in the University of Vermont Medical Center emergency department, caused by a medication interaction that a basic AI screening tool could have flagged before my new cardiologist ever walked into the exam room — is what brought me to this rulemaking. My daughter works in health informatics in Boston and she explained afterward exactly what kind of tool would have caught it. So I am not writing from ignorance about what clinical AI can do well. I am writing because this request for information frames the AI-in-healthcare question almost entirely around clinical decision support and reimbursement for beneficial tools, and in doing so it ignores what is already the dominant, documented, financially lucrative application of AI in American healthcare: automated prior authorization denial systems deployed by major insurers to deny care at a speed and volume no human reviewer could match. The October 2023 Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations report, "Denial, Delay, Disappear," documented in granular detail how UnitedHealth and Humana used AI-driven prior authorization systems that produced denial rates dramatically higher than human review, particularly for Medicare Advantage enrollees in post-acute care settings. ProPublica and The Capitol Forum reported in 2023 on NaviHealth's nH Predict tool, which was trained on cost-containment objectives and used to cut off rehabilitation stays for elderly patients whose physicians had not authorized discharge. The HHS Office of Inspector General's April 2022 report (OEI-09-18-00260) found that Medicare Advantage organizations were denying prior authorization requests that met coverage criteria, at rates that should have triggered serious regulatory concern. The AMA's 2023 Prior Authorization Physician Survey documented that AI-generated denials had increased administrative burden while producing no demonstrated improvement in clinical appropriateness determinations. Kaye et al., writing in the Journal of Health Politics, Policy and Law in 2023 (Vol. 48, No. 3, pp. 411-438), described this as the systematic erosion of clinical judgment by algorithmic systems optimized for cost rather than care. Obermeyer and colleagues established in Science in 2019 (Vol. 366, Issue 6464) that health algorithms trained on cost proxies systematically underweight patient need, a finding that should have stopped cost-optimized denial AI in its tracks.⏎⏎What concerns me about HHS framing this as a question of "accelerating adoption" and "fostering competition among clinical AI tool builders" is that the competitive market for AI in healthcare already has a winner, and it is not the patient. The appeals process nominally available under 42 C.F.R. sections 422.568 and 422.572 was not designed for a world where an AI system can generate thousands of denials overnight. An elderly patient in Vermont without a daughter in health informatics has roughly no realistic path through that appeals process before the skilled nursing facility discharges her anyway. I want HHS to require demonstrated safety and equity review before any AI system may be used in coverage or prior authorization decisions, and to revise the appeal timeline regulations to account for the speed of automated denials. The current standard timelines assume a human reviewer made a considered judgment.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0008♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔10♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Clark♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔Georgetown University Medical Center♔♔♔♔♔♔Georgetown University Medical Center — Postdoctoral Research Program⏎Comments Submitted to HHS Docket: AI in Clinical Care RFI⏎Submitted by: James Clark, Postdoctoral Fellow, Department of Cardiovascular Research⏎Georgetown University Medical Center, Washington, DC / Boise, Idaho (primary research site)⏎⏎Georgetown University Medical Center | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 1⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE DEPLOYMENT-GOVERNANCE GAP AS STRUCTURAL PRECONDITION FOR HARM⏎⏎The American College of Cardiology's 2024 Survey of Cardiology Fellows and Early-Career Cardiologists on AI Literacy and Institutional Deployment found that 67% of the 2,800 respondents reported working in institutions that had deployed at least one AI-assisted diagnostic or risk-stratification tool, yet 58% of that group indicated they had received no formal training on interpreting AI-generated outputs or understanding model limitations (ACC, 2024). That finding is not a workforce development problem. It is a structural description of the conditions under which AI tools are currently being used on patients, and it is the appropriate starting point for evaluating what HHS is proposing here. The Department's RFI frames acceleration as the primary policy objective, asking how regulatory, reimbursement, and research-and-development actions can be arranged to speed adoption. The framing is not wrong to care about adoption barriers, but it proceeds from an assumption — that the primary problem is insufficient deployment — that the empirical record does not support. The documented problem, at this moment, is deployment that has substantially outpaced the governance infrastructure required to detect when those tools are failing specific patient populations. Acceleration in that context is not a neutral policy choice.⏎⏎Georgetown University Medical Center | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 2⏎⏎---⏎⏎ALGORITHMIC BIAS AS AN OBSERVED PATTERN, NOT A SPECULATIVE CONCERN⏎⏎The literature on algorithmic bias in clinical AI is not a set of theoretical warnings. It is a record of documented failures, and the failures share a common structure that makes them relevant to any accelerationist policy posture. Sjoding et al. published in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 a finding that pulse oximeters — devices so routine that clinicians rarely think of them as algorithmic — produced occult hypoxemia in Black patients at a rate nearly three times higher than in white patients, a disparity that went undetected in clinical practice for years precisely because the device's authoritative numeric output suppressed scrutiny (Sjoding MW, Dickson RP, Iwashyna TJ, Gay SE, Valley TS. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(25):2477-2478). Obermeyer and colleagues published in Science in 2019 an analysis of a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to identify patients for high-risk care management programs, finding that the algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients by using healthcare cost as a proxy for health status — a proxy that encoded decades of differential access to care and produced a model that, at equivalent risk scores, assigned Black patients significantly lower priority for intervention (Obermeyer Z, Powers B, Vogeli C, Mullainathan S. Science. 2019;366(6464):447-453). Wong et al. published in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2021 an external validation of the Epic Sepsis Model, a proprietary tool deployed across hundreds of hospital systems, and found that the model's performance in their institution — a non-developer, real-world setting — was substantially worse than reported in the original validation literature, with an AUROC of 0.63 compared to the developer's reported figures, and with documented inequities in alert distribution across patient subgroups (Wong A, Otles E, Donnelly JP, et al. JAMA Intern Med. 2021;181(8):1065-1070). Adamson and Smith, writing in JAMA Dermatology in 2018, documented that dermatology AI systems trained predominantly on images of lighter-skinned patients showed degraded diagnostic accuracy for conditions presenting differently on darker skin, a limitation that was not surfaced in initial performance reporting because benchmark datasets did not reflect the demographic composition of patients most likely to receive care in under-resourced settings (Adamson AS, Smith A. JAMA Dermatol. 2018;154(11):1247-1248).⏎⏎Georgetown University Medical Center | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 3⏎⏎These are not isolated anomalies. Each represents a node in a reproducible failure mode: a model trained on data that reflects historical patterns of differential access, utilization, and documentation; a validation process conducted in settings that do not represent the full distribution of deployment environments; a performance metric that aggregates across subgroups in a way that obscures differential harm; and a deployment environment in which the tool's outputs carry sufficient authority to suppress the clinical scrutiny that would otherwise catch errors. Char, Shah, and Magnus identified this structural pattern in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2018, arguing that machine learning tools trained on EHR data inherit the biases embedded in clinical documentation, ordering patterns, and institutional practices, and that these biases are compounded rather than corrected by the feedback loops that occur when AI outputs influence subsequent clinical behavior and documentation (Char DS, Shah NH, Magnus D. N Engl J Med. 2018;378(11):981-983). Gianfrancesco and colleagues elaborated the specific mechanisms by which EHR data produces biased training sets, noting that missingness patterns, differential coding practices, and socioeconomic proxies embedded in structured data fields systematically distort model outputs in ways that are not visible from aggregate performance statistics (Gianfrancesco MA, Tamang S, Tamang S, Yazdany J, Schmajuk G. JAMA Intern Med. 2018;178(11):1544-1547). Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones, also in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020, extended this analysis to race correction in clinical algorithms more broadly, documenting how the use of race as a biological variable in algorithms for kidney function, lung function, and obstetric risk estimation encodes social constructs as biological facts and produces systematic disadvantage for patients of color (Vyas DA, Eisenstein LG, Jones DS. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(9):874-882).⏎⏎Georgetown University Medical Center | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 4⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE PERFORMANCE-DEGRADATION PROBLEM IN NON-ACADEMIC SETTINGS IS AN EQUITY ISSUE⏎⏎HHS's RFI does not adequately address what Finlayson and colleagues called "dataset shift" — the phenomenon by which a model trained in one clinical environment degrades in performance when deployed in a different environment whose patient population, documentation practices, and workflow characteristics differ from the training context (Finlayson SG, Subbaswamy A, Singh K, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(3):283-286). This is not a narrow technical problem. It is an equity problem with a predictable directional bias. AI tools in clinical care are predominantly developed and initially validated at large academic medical centers with well-resourced informatics infrastructure, high coding specificity, and patient populations that over-represent insured, English-speaking patients with regular primary care contact. The facilities where performance degradation is most likely to occur — community hospitals, rural critical access facilities, federally qualified health centers, safety-net institutions — are disproportionately the facilities where marginalized patients, patients of color, patients with limited English proficiency, and patients with Medicaid or no insurance receive care. When a model trained at an academic center is deployed at a rural Idaho critical access hospital, the performance gap is not random noise. It is a systematic transfer of risk onto the patients who were already least well served by the training data.⏎⏎Georgetown University Medical Center | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 5⏎⏎Seyyed-Kalantari and colleagues documented this mechanism directly in the context of chest radiograph AI, finding in a study published in Nature Medicine in 2021 that AI algorithms applied to chest radiographs in under-served patient populations produced higher rates of underdiagnosis for those populations, and that the performance gap was largest for patients who were uninsured, patients with limited education, and patients from racial and ethnic minority groups — precisely the populations whose clinical data was most underrepresented in training sets (Seyyed-Kalantari L, Zhang H, McDermott MBA, Chen IY, Ghassemi M. Nat Med. 2021;27(12):2176-2182). The ACC's Quality Improvement for Institutions Program, which spans more than 2,200 cardiovascular care sites, documented in its 2023 annual review that fewer than 30% of participating sites reported having a defined clinical oversight protocol for AI-driven decision support (ACC. Quality Improvement for Institutions Program: 2023 Annual Review. Washington, DC: ACC; 2023). That means that at more than 70% of cardiovascular care sites in a program explicitly designed to support quality infrastructure, there is no defined process for monitoring whether AI tools are performing as expected, for whom, and under what conditions. The absence of governance infrastructure is not distributed randomly across the healthcare system. It is concentrated in precisely the settings where performance degradation is most likely and most consequential.⏎⏎Georgetown University Medical Center | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 6⏎⏎---⏎⏎AUTOMATION BIAS AS A HARM-AMPLIFICATION MECHANISM⏎⏎There is a specific reason why AI errors are more dangerous than equivalent human errors in clinical settings, and HHS's RFI does not engage with it. Cabitza, Rasoini, and Gensini described the mechanism in JAMA in 2017: the presentation of a recommendation in algorithmic form — with a numeric confidence score, a clean interface, and the implicit authority of institutional deployment — induces automation bias in clinicians, meaning a tendency to accept the output without applying the same critical scrutiny that would be applied to a colleague's verbal recommendation or a paper-based decision support tool (Cabitza F, Rasoini R, Gensini GF. JAMA. 2017;318(6):517-518). This is not a failure of individual clinicians. It is a predictable cognitive response to the design and authority of AI systems, and it is compounded by the workflow conditions under which clinical AI is typically used: high patient volume, time pressure, cognitive load, and an institutional environment in which the tool has been deployed with implicit endorsement from hospital administration. When a clinician overrides a human colleague's recommendation, that decision is visible, documentable, and subject to social accountability. When a clinician accepts an AI output without scrutiny, the process is largely invisible. The ACC's 2024 survey finding that 58% of clinicians working with deployed AI tools had received no formal training on interpreting outputs or understanding model limitations is a direct description of a workforce operating without the conceptual tools necessary to recognize when automation bias is occurring.⏎⏎Georgetown University Medical Center | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 7⏎⏎The implications for equity are direct. If automation bias suppresses the critical scrutiny that would otherwise catch errors, and if the errors that AI systems produce are systematically more likely to affect specific patient subgroups — as the literature reviewed above consistently demonstrates — then automation bias is not merely a patient safety problem in the aggregate. It is a mechanism by which the disparate errors embedded in AI systems are more likely to reach patients as consequential clinical decisions. A clinician who might otherwise notice that a risk-stratification score seems inconsistent with a patient's presentation, and who would investigate further, is less likely to do so when the score is presented as an authoritative algorithmic output. The patients most likely to have their risk systematically underestimated by algorithms trained on biased data are the patients for whom that clinician's critical scrutiny was most needed.⏎⏎Georgetown University Medical Center | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 8⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE SPECIFIC REGULATORY GAP: NO MANDATORY PRE-DEPLOYMENT EQUITY AUDITING, NO POST-DEPLOYMENT BIAS SURVEILLANCE⏎⏎Regarding the RFI's questions about regulatory actions HHS can take to support responsible AI adoption — this is the question the following comments address directly, because it is the question where the gap between what HHS has proposed and what the literature demands is most apparent.⏎⏎HHS's RFI identifies a range of potential regulatory actions: updating FDA frameworks for AI/ML-based software as a medical device, developing interoperability standards, clarifying liability frameworks, and creating reimbursement pathways. None of the proposed regulatory actions includes a mandatory requirement for pre-deployment equity auditing — a structured assessment, conducted before a tool enters clinical use, of the tool's performance across relevant demographic subgroups in deployment-representative populations. None of the proposed actions includes a mandatory post-deployment bias surveillance framework — a systematic process for monitoring, after a tool is in use, whether its outputs are producing differential outcomes across patient subgroups, and for triggering investigation and remediation when disparities are detected.⏎⏎The absence of these two requirements is not a minor gap. It means that under the policy framework HHS is developing, algorithmic disparities of the type documented by Sjoding, Obermeyer, Wong, Adamson, Seyyed-Kalantari, and others will only be detected after they have been harming patients at scale, if they are detected at all. The Obermeyer et al. algorithm was deployed across a health system serving millions of patients before the bias was identified through a research investigation that was not required by any regulatory framework. The Epic Sepsis Model was deployed across hundreds of hospital systems before Wong et al. conducted the external validation that surfaced its performance limitations. Pulse oximetry bias went clinically undetected for decades. The pattern is consistent: tools enter clinical use, achieve wide deployment, and their disparate impacts are identified through research investigations that occur years later, if at all.⏎⏎Georgetown University Medical Center | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 9⏎⏎The specific regulatory requirements that the literature supports, and that HHS should incorporate into any final policy framework, are the following:⏎⏎1.    Mandatory pre-deployment subgroup performance reporting. Any AI tool seeking regulatory clearance or reimbursement eligibility under an HHS framework should be required to report disaggregated performance statistics — sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value, and negative predictive value at minimum — stratified by race, ethnicity, sex, insurance status, and primary language, computed on a validation dataset that is representative of the intended deployment population rather than the development institution's patient panel.⏎⏎2.    Mandatory deployment-environment validation for high-risk clinical AI. Tools intended for deployment in community hospitals, rural facilities, or safety-net institutions should be required to undergo prospective validation in at least one such setting before receiving reimbursement or regulatory endorsement, rather than relying exclusively on academic center validation studies.⏎⏎3.    Mandatory post-deployment bias surveillance as a condition of continued reimbursement eligibility. Institutions deploying AI tools under any HHS reimbursement framework should be required to maintain and report, at defined intervals, outcome data stratified by the demographic variables listed above, with a defined threshold for triggering mandatory review of the tool's continued deployment.⏎⏎4.    Minimum AI literacy standards as a condition of institutional deployment. The ACC's 2024 survey finding that 58% of clinicians using deployed AI tools had received no training on output interpretation or model limitations should be treated as a patient safety problem requiring regulatory response. HHS should condition any reimbursement or regulatory endorsement of AI tools on institutional attestation that clinicians using those tools have received structured training on automation bias, model limitations, and the demographic conditions under which the tool was validated.⏎⏎These are not novel regulatory concepts. They are extensions of existing frameworks for device validation, quality reporting, and conditions of participation that HHS already administers. The argument that they would slow adoption is an argument that adoption should proceed before the tools are validated for the populations on whom they will be used.⏎⏎Georgetown University Medical Center | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 10⏎⏎---⏎⏎A NOTE ON THE FRAMING OF THIS RFI⏎⏎The RFI's accelerationist framing warrants direct comment, because it reflects a policy posture that the empirical record does not support. The Department is asking how to remove barriers to adoption, how to create reimbursement pathways, how to streamline regulatory review. These are legitimate questions about a technology with genuine clinical potential. But the framing assumes that the primary problem is insufficient deployment, when the documented problem — at more than 2,200 cardiovascular care sites, across a literature spanning pulse oximetry to insurance algorithms to chest radiograph AI — is deployment that has substantially outpaced the governance infrastructure required to detect harm. The ACC survey finding is not an argument against AI in clinical care. It is an argument that the current rate of deployment, in the absence of the governance frameworks described above, is already creating the conditions for harm at scale, and that HHS is proposing to accelerate that process without first addressing the structural gap.⏎⏎There is also a question about what "acceleration" means in practice for rural Idaho, which is the research context most directly relevant to this submission. The cardiovascular care infrastructure in rural Idaho is not comparable to what exists at a large academic medical center in a major metropolitan area. The informatics infrastructure is different, the patient population is different, the workflow conditions are different, and the capacity for post-deployment monitoring is substantially more limited. A policy framework designed to accelerate AI adoption that does not account for those differences will produce tools validated elsewhere and deployed here without the governance infrastructure to detect when they are failing the specific patients in this context. That is not a hypothetical concern. It is a description of the current situation, documented in the ACC's 2023 institutional review.⏎⏎Georgetown University Medical Center | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 11⏎⏎The dataset shift literature is particularly relevant in this context. Finlayson et al.'s 2021 New England Journal of Medicine analysis of clinician and dataset shift in AI documented the specific mechanisms by which a model's performance degrades as the deployment environment diverges from the training environment, and noted that the divergence is often invisible to the clinicians using the tool because the tool continues to produce outputs — it does not fail silently in the way that a broken laboratory instrument does. A tool that is producing systematically biased outputs in a rural critical access hospital looks, from the clinician's interface, identical to a tool that is performing as designed. The only way to detect the difference is through the kind of post-deployment subgroup outcome monitoring that HHS has not proposed to require.⏎⏎Georgetown University Medical Center | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 12⏎⏎The comment does not argue that HHS should halt AI development in clinical care. The literature is clear that there are specific contexts in which AI tools have demonstrated genuine performance advantages, and the potential for AI to extend specialist-level diagnostic capability to under-resourced settings is real and worth pursuing. But the potential is only realized equitably if the deployment framework is designed to detect and correct disparate performance before it becomes embedded in clinical practice. The tools that are currently being deployed at scale were not designed with that framework in place, and the RFI does not propose to create it. What HHS is proposing is a reimbursement and regulatory structure that will make AI tools more widely available without first establishing the monitoring infrastructure that would allow the healthcare system to know whether those tools are working equitably for the patients who receive care in the settings where the governance gap is largest. That is the specific concern this comment addresses, and it is not resolved by any of the regulatory actions currently described in the⏎⏎Georgetown University Medical Center | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 13⏎⏎[continued from previous page] — RFI's proposed framework. The ACC's Quality Improvement for Institutions data and the 2024 fellow survey together describe a system in which deployment has already outpaced governance at the institutional level. HHS's proposed acceleration would widen that gap at the national level, without the pre-deployment equity auditing or post-deployment bias surveillance requirements that would be necessary to detect the resulting harm before it becomes entrenched.⏎⏎Contact for follow-up questions regarding this submission:⏎James Clark, Postdoctoral Fellow⏎Georgetown University Medical Center⏎jclark-postdoc@gumc.georgetown.edu [address on file with submitting office]♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0009♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Gonzalez♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔April 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎My buddy Carlos did two tours in Fallujah with me and went to the VA clinic up in Shreveport where a computer flagged him wrong on a mental health screening and the doctor just went with it, never really sat down and talked to him. Took almost eight months to get that sorted out. I dont know if there is a public hearing for this because I would rather say this in person, but my worry is that those old medical records the computer is learning from already had bad calls in them, especially for veterans and people in rural areas, so the machine is just going to repeat those same mistakes faster and wider. Please slow this down and make sure it works fairly for everybody before you roll it out everywhere.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎Anthony Gonzalez⏎Bossier City, Louisiana♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0010♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Allen♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To the Office of the Deputy Secretary, HHS:⏎⏎MY CONCERN WITH THE FRAMING OF THIS RFI⏎⏎There is a patient I keep thinking about, a dairy farmer from Addison County, 74 years old, who had been living with undetected atrial fibrillation for what we later estimated was somewhere between four and eight months. The AI-assisted ECG monitoring system our hospital piloted in 2022 flagged him. I was the nurse who sat with him afterward. I am not writing this as someone who is afraid of these tools. I am writing as someone who watched that technology work, and who is now genuinely alarmed by what this RFI is actually asking, which is not whether AI should be adopted thoughtfully, but how quickly federal policy can be reoriented to accelerate adoption that is already, in many institutions, outpacing our ability to understand what we are embedding. Eric Topol's 2019 synthesis in Nature Medicine (15:44-56) documented real clinical promise in algorithmic pattern recognition, and I do not dispute that evidence. What I dispute is the assumption that documenting promise is sufficient grounds for the kind of systemic embedding this RFI appears to be building toward. Coiera's 2021 analysis in npj Digital Medicine (4:94) showed something that does not get enough attention in policy conversations: clinical workflow integration creates operational dependency that outlasts the original rationale for adoption. Clinicians and institutions lose the procedural memory to function without a system, not because they are lazy, but because workflows reorganize around available tools. Selbst and colleagues made a related structural point at ACM FAccT 2019 — AI systems embedded in institutional infrastructure resist correction even after documented failures, because the cost of reversal grows faster than the evidence for harm accumulates. That is not a hypothetical. I have watched it happen with far less consequential health IT decisions than the ones this RFI is contemplating.⏎⏎THE DEMOCRATIC LEGITIMACY PROBLEM⏎⏎What concerns me most is not any particular algorithm. It is the following pattern, which I think this RFI reflects whether or not that is the intent:⏎⏎1. A federal agency frames regulatory scrutiny as an obstacle to innovation rather than as a legitimate democratic function.⏎2. Adoption accelerates before accountability infrastructure exists, which the President's Council of Advisors on Science and Technology acknowledged explicitly in their 2023 report on AI governance in health care, finding structural gaps in the ability of current regulatory frameworks to audit proprietary clinical AI systems.⏎3. Proprietary systems whose internal logic cannot be examined by clinicians, patients, or independent regulators become load-bearing infrastructure, at which point reversal is practically foreclosed regardless of what evidence emerges.⏎4. The question of whether medicine should be organized this way is never actually put to the communities who will live with the answer.⏎⏎Sheila Jasanoff argued in The Ethics of Invention (2016) that democratic societies retain the right to decide that certain domains should remain governed by human judgment even at the cost of efficiency, and that this is a legitimate collective choice, not a failure of rational policy. I think she is right, and I think HHS is in danger of preempting that choice on behalf of patients and communities who have not been asked. The analogy Dafny and Lee drew in the New England Journal of Medicine (374:1804, 2016) about healthcare market consolidation is apt here, not perfectly, but the structural logic is similar: once decision-making authority concentrates in a small number of vendor systems, the conditions that would allow a different arrangement to emerge are themselves eliminated. I am not asking HHS to stop thinking about AI. I am asking HHS to reframe this RFI around a different question, which is not how to accelerate adoption but what democratic process should precede irreversible embedding. The current framing treats that as a bureaucratic delay. I think it is the actual work. What worries me practically is that nothing in the RFI's question set addresses what happens when a rural critical access hospital in Vermont becomes dependent on a system whose vendor is acquired, repriced,♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0011♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Smith♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: RE: HHS AI comment submission - please include in docket⏎⏎My father was misdiagnosed at a rural clinic outside Bismarck because there was no radiologist available for nearly four days. I work in a medical office now and I see what AI could do for places like ours. But before HHS accelerates anything, it needs to deal with what's actually happening: insurance companies using automated systems to deny prior authorizations with no real human reviewing the decision, and patients, especially elderly ones on fixed incomes, having absolutely no practical way to appeal.⏎⏎-- ⏎Amanda Smith♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0012♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Robinson♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas Robinson, MS, PhD⏎Biostatistician, Department of Biomedical Informatics⏎Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center⏎410 W. 10th Avenue, Columbus, OH 43210⏎trobinson@wexnermedical.osu.edu⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Attn: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center | HHS AI RFI Comments | T. Robinson | Page 1⏎⏎⏎The framing of this RFI deserves to be examined before any of its specific questions are answered, because the framing is itself the problem. HHS is asking how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. Our institution has spent three fiscal years attempting to answer a prior question — how to deploy AI systems responsibly when no coherent federal accountability framework exists — and the data we have accumulated in that effort are not encouraging. A 2024 internal analysis conducted by our AI Governance Committee across 13 specialty service lines found that 68% of implementation delays were attributable not to technical immaturity or clinician resistance, but to unresolved federal guidance on liability attribution and reimbursement eligibility. Of 47 distinct AI model integrations attempted or completed across our health system between fiscal years 2022 and 2024, only 11 achieved sustainable operational status. That is a 77% attrition rate. Our data scientists and faculty clinicians, surveyed during the 2024 Governance Committee review, identified the absence of a unified HHS validation and accountability framework as the single greatest barrier to scaling proven tools from pilot status to standard-of-care deployment. HHS is asking us how to go faster. Our data suggest that the more urgent policy question is how to go safely, and those two questions are not the same question.⏎⏎⏎SECTION I: THE LIABILITY VACUUM IS NOT AN INCIDENTAL GAP⏎⏎The core structural problem with accelerated AI deployment in clinical settings is that existing medical malpractice doctrine, built on the premise of an identifiable human decision-maker exercising professional judgment, has no coherent mechanism to assign responsibility when an AI-assisted error occurs. Price, Gerke, and Cohen identified this problem in 2019, writing specifically about the indeterminate causation problem that arises when physician conduct is mediated by algorithmic recommendation (Price WN II, Gerke S, Cohen IG, "Potential liability for physicians using artificial intelligence," JAMA, 2019;322(18):1765–1766). What our institutional experience has since confirmed is that the problem is not merely theoretical. When our clinical decision support tools have generated recommendations that were questioned or that contributed to suboptimal care pathways, the post-hoc attribution conversations among clinicians, hospital administration, and vendors have been, to use a precise term, incoherent. Each party possesses a structurally plausible deflection narrative. The attending physician did not program the model. The hospital did not train it. The vendor did not make the clinical judgment to act on its output. Respondeat superior doctrine, which assigns liability upward through an employment relationship, does not map cleanly onto a tripartite arrangement where the critical decision is distributed across an algorithm, a deploying institution, and an end-user clinician who may not fully understand the model's basis for its recommendation.⏎⏎Gerke, Minssen, and Cohen have written extensively on this configuration, noting that the ethical and legal challenges of AI-driven healthcare are compounded precisely because the locus of decision-making becomes genuinely ambiguous when deep learning systems are in the loop (Gerke S, Minssen T, Cohen G, "Ethical and legal challenges of artificial intelligence-driven healthcare," in: Bohr A, Memarzadeh K, eds., Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare, Academic Press, 2020:295–336). Sendak and colleagues observed in their 2020 study of clinical deep learning deployment that clinicians frequently treat AI outputs as authoritative without understanding the model's internal logic, a dynamic they described using the phrase "the human body is a black box," meaning that clinicians already operate with significant epistemic uncertainty about patient physiology, and AI systems layer a second black box on top of that uncertainty without resolving the first (Sendak MP, Elish MC, Gao M, et al., "'The human body is a black box': supporting clinical decision-making with deep learning," Proc ACM FAT*, 2020:99–109). When errors occur in this double-opacity environment, the question of who had the relevant duty of care and who breached it cannot be answered by reference to any existing federal standard. HHS's RFI does not propose to answer it either. That silence is the design flaw this comment is addressing.⏎⏎⏎Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center | HHS AI RFI Comments | T. Robinson | Page 2⏎⏎⏎SECTION II: THE DEFLECTION ARCHITECTURE IS A DESIGNED FEATURE⏎⏎It would be reassuring to characterize the tripartite liability diffusion described above as an accidental regulatory gap — something that fell through the cracks of a framework designed for a different technological moment. Our reading of the current deployment landscape does not support that reassurance. The commercial incentive structure for AI vendors in the clinical space actively benefits from liability indeterminacy. Vendors negotiate contract terms that assign clinical responsibility to deploying institutions. Deploying institutions, in turn, rely on clinician professional standards as their first line of indemnification. Clinicians, for their part, can point to the vendor's model and the institution's procurement decision. The result is not a gap but a loop — a closed circuit of deflection in which harmed patients have no clear defendant and no coherent evidentiary pathway to establish causation. Doshi-Velez and colleagues at the Berkman Klein Center identified the explanation problem as central to AI accountability, arguing that legal accountability frameworks depend on the ability to reconstruct decision logic, and that most current clinical AI systems cannot provide that reconstruction in any legally meaningful form (Doshi-Velez F, Kortz M, Budish R, et al., "Accountability of AI under the law: the role of explanation," Berkman Klein Center Working Paper, 2017, https://ssrn.com/abstract=3064761). HHS's proposed acceleration of deployment would embed systems with this structural accountability deficit more deeply into clinical workflows, making post-hoc remediation correspondingly more difficult.⏎⏎Mello, Frakes, Blumenkranz, and Studdert, writing in JAMA in 2020, examined the relationship between malpractice liability structures and health care quality, and their analysis supports a concern that our institution has observed operationally: the deterrence function of malpractice liability depends on a legible connection between negligent conduct and legal consequence, and when that connection is obscured by technological intermediation, the deterrence effect degrades (Mello MM, Frakes MD, Blumenkranz E, Studdert DM, "Malpractice liability and health care quality," JAMA, 2020;323(4):352–366). Char, Shah, and Magnus, writing in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2018, made a related point about the ethical infrastructure required to make machine learning deployment responsible — arguing that implementation without accountability architecture is not cautious innovation but a transfer of risk from institutions to patients (Char DS, Shah NH, Magnus D, "Implementing machine learning in health care — addressing ethical challenges," N Engl J Med, 2018;378(11):981–983). HHS should engage seriously with this literature before characterizing its RFI as a patient-centered initiative.⏎⏎⏎SECTION III: SPECIFIC CONCERNS REGARDING THE RFI'S SCOPE AND OMISSIONS⏎⏎This comment focuses primarily on two dimensions of the RFI where the accountability deficit is most acute and where HHS's silence is most consequential.⏎⏎First, the RFI's treatment of reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted clinical services does not address the liability implications of creating financial incentives for adoption in the absence of accountability standards. When CMS or private payers reimburse AI-assisted diagnostic or treatment decisions, they are implicitly validating the clinical legitimacy of those decisions. That validation, however, does not resolve the question of who bears responsibility when the AI-assisted decision is wrong. Topol, writing in Nature Medicine in 2019, described the convergence of human and artificial intelligence in high-performance clinical settings as requiring what he called a new "cognitive partnership" between clinicians and algorithms — a partnership that presupposes institutional and legal structures capable of assigning responsibility within that partnership (Topol EJ, "High-performance medicine: the convergence of human and artificial intelligence," Nat Med, 2019;25(1):44–56). No such structures currently exist in any HHS-administered framework, and the RFI does not propose to create them.⏎⏎Second, the RFI's discussion of validation standards for clinical AI is inadequate. Cohen, Evgeniou, Gerke, and Minssen, examining the European AI strategy in Lancet Digital Health in 2020, identified prospective validation requirements as the minimum necessary condition for responsible clinical AI deployment — noting that post-market surveillance alone cannot protect patients from harms that are structurally invisible until they have already accumulated at scale (Cohen IG, Evgeniou T, Gerke S, Minssen T, "The European artificial intelligence strategy: implications and challenges for digital health," Lancet Digital Health, 2020;2(7):e376–e379). The absence of a unified HHS validation framework is not merely an administrative inconvenience. Our institution has catalogued at least 14 instances across the past three years in which commercially deployed clinical AI tools were integrated into our workflows on the basis of vendor-supplied validation studies that were not independently replicated, were conducted on patient populations that did not reflect our own demographics, or were performed on training data whose composition we could not audit. Hoffman and Podgurski, examining AI discrimination in health care, have documented how validation failures of precisely this kind produce systematic disparate harm in clinical populations that are underrepresented in training datasets (Hoffman S, Podgurski A, "Artificial intelligence and discrimination in health care," Yale J Health Policy Law Ethics, 2019;19(3):1–51). Keskinbora's analysis of medical ethics considerations in AI deployment reaches similar conclusions regarding the inadequacy of relying on vendor-supplied performance metrics as a substitute for independent clinical validation (Keskinbora KH, "Medical ethics considerations on artificial intelligence," J Clin Neurosci, 2019;64:277–282).⏎⏎⏎Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center | HHS AI RFI Comments | T. Robinson | Page 3⏎⏎⏎The specific operational consequences of these validation failures at our institution include the following:⏎⏎1. An AI-assisted sepsis prediction tool deployed in our emergency department in early 2023 produced alert fatigue rates that were not observed in the vendor's published validation cohort; the tool was withdrawn from operational use after seven months, contributing to one of the 36 failed integrations referenced above, and the institutional cost of that deployment cycle — including integration labor, clinician training, and EHR modification — exceeded $340,000 by our biostatistics team's accounting.⏎⏎2. A radiology AI tool for chest X-ray interpretation, deployed in our outpatient pulmonology service, demonstrated performance degradation in patients over age 70 that was not disclosed in the vendor's pre-deployment documentation; this was identified only through our own prospective monitoring protocol, which was not required by any federal standard but was implemented on the initiative of our AI Governance Committee.⏎⏎3. A natural language processing tool for clinical note summarization, piloted in our oncology service, produced structured outputs that attending physicians reported treating as authoritative despite the tool's documented 12% error rate on medication dosage extraction — a rate our team considers clinically unacceptable, but which no federal standard currently prohibits.⏎⏎Each of these cases illustrates the same structural problem: deployment proceeded without an enforceable federal accountability framework, errors accumulated, and the question of who bore legal and financial responsibility for the resulting clinical and institutional harms was never coherently resolved. HHS is proposing to create conditions under which this pattern occurs not at the pilot scale but at the scale of standard-of-care deployment across the U.S. health system.⏎⏎⏎SECTION IV: WHAT HHS MUST DO BEFORE ACCELERATING ADOPTION⏎⏎Our institution's position, informed by three years of direct operational experience and grounded in the literature reviewed above, is that HHS should not take any regulatory or reimbursement action to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care before the following conditions are met. This is not a suggestion. The Department has a statutory obligation to protect patients, and the current regulatory posture is inconsistent with that obligation.⏎⏎The conditions we consider minimally necessary are:⏎⏎a. A published federal framework for liability attribution in AI-assisted clinical errors, specifying the respective duties of care borne by AI vendors, deploying institutions, and clinical end-users, and providing a legally operable mechanism for establishing causation when algorithmic recommendation contributes to patient harm. The framework should not be advisory; it must be enforceable.⏎⏎b. Mandatory independent validation requirements for clinical AI tools, specifying minimum cohort size, demographic representativeness standards, and performance benchmarks, with validation conducted by parties without financial relationships with the tool's developer. The ONC's existing work on interoperability standards under the Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement provides a partial model for how HHS can establish certification-style requirements (Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology, Draft Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement, HHS/ONC, 2022), but that framework does not extend to clinical AI validation and would require substantial modification to do so.⏎⏎c. A reimbursement structure that conditions payment for AI-assisted clinical services on demonstrated compliance with the validation and accountability frameworks described above, rather than creating financial incentives for adoption that outrun accountability requirements.⏎⏎Obermeyer and Lee, writing in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2017, argued that the future of medicine depends on integrating human and artificial intelligence in ways that preserve rather than erode the epistemic standards that clinical practice requires (Obermeyer Z, Lee TH, "Lost in thought — the limits of the human mind and the future of medicine," N Engl J Med, 2017;377(13):1209–1211). Our institution does not oppose AI adoption. We have invested substantially in attempting to achieve it. What we oppose is a federal posture that treats accountability infrastructure as something to be developed after deployment has occurred at scale, on the theory that the benefits of adoption justify the risks of a transitional period without enforceable standards. That theory has already cost our institution approximately $2.1 million in failed deployment cycles over three years, by our Governance Committee's accounting. It has also, in ways that our retrospective analyses cannot fully quantify, exposed patients to clinical decision support tools whose error profiles were not disclosed, whose liability implications were not resolved, and whose integration into care pathways was predicated on vendor representations that no federal body had independently verified.⏎⏎The RFI's silence on liability attribution is not a placeholder pending further regulatory development. It is a signal that HHS is prepared to accelerate deployment while leaving the accountability question to litigation — which is to say, to patients who have already been harmed. That is not a policy our institution can endorse, and the Department should not interpret the absence of widespread formal objection as evidence that the health care sector has accepted it. Our 2024 Governance Committee survey found that 84% of our faculty clinicians expressed concern about personal liability exposure from AI-assisted clinical decisions, but fewer than 20% had submitted formal comments to any federal body on the subject, because they did not believe formal comment would produce a response. This comment is submitted in the hope that belief is wrong, though our institution acknowledges the empirical basis for it is not negligible.⏎⏎One further point that does not fit cleanly into the above framework but which our biostatistics team considers important: the RFI's implicit assumption that regulatory acceleration will produce adoption at scale assumes a deployment-ready health system infrastructure that does not uniformly exist. Our own EHR environment, built on a legacy Epic configuration with significant custom modifications accumulated over eight years, creates integration constraints that no federal reimbursement incentive will resolve in the near term. The liability question and the infrastructure question are related, because health systems that cannot safely integrate AI tools will face pressure to deploy them anyway if reimbursement incentives make non-deployment financially costly — and that pressure will fall most heavily on smaller and rural institutions that lack the internal governance capacity we have at a major academic medical center. The Department has not, as far as we can determine from the RFI text, modeled the differential impact of accelerated deployment incentives on health systems with varying integration capacity, and⏎⏎⏎Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center | HHS AI RFI Comments | T. Robinson | Page 4⏎⏎⏎that gap in the Department's analysis corresponds to precisely the population most likely to deploy AI tools in conditions least conducive to safe use.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0013♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Thomas♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother was in a Tampa hospital back in February for three days and the nurses were scrambling the whole time just to find her chart on computers that kept freezing. That part I understand needs to be fixed. But what worries me about rushing AI into hospitals is that the computer learned from old records that already had problems in them, years of patients not getting equal treatment, and now it just repeats that and nobody questions it because it came from a screen. Doctors and nurses are busy, they're going to trust what the system tells them. And I'd bet the hospitals in poorer areas or smaller towns aren't going to get the same results as the big research hospitals where this stuff gets tested first. Please slow down and make sure it actually works fair for everyone before pushing it everywhere.⏎⏎Page 1 of 1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0014♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Allen♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Karen had a valve problem that two separate echocardiogram reads called unremarkable. A third read, assisted by imaging software at Hartford Hospital, caught it. Six weeks later she was in surgery, and her surgeon told us that if it had waited another year, the repair would have been substantially more complicated and possibly unsafe. I sat in that waiting room thinking about the twenty miles between Hartford Hospital and the smaller facility near our home in Connecticut, and what those twenty miles meant for who gets access to what technology. That arbitrariness is real, and the clinical promise of AI is real, and I don't want to pretend otherwise.⏎⏎But that is not what I want to address here.⏎⏎THE AUTOMATION OF ADMINISTRATIVE WORK⏎⏎What concerns me about this request for information is a quieter assumption running through its framing: that administrative work in healthcare is a processing problem, and that AI can process it better and faster than people can. I want to push back on that assumption directly, because I think it misunderstands what administrative healthcare workers actually do, and because the consequences of getting this wrong will fall on patients who are already vulnerable and already poorly served.⏎⏎People who do care coordination, patient navigation, prior authorization review, and benefits counseling are not doing clerical work. They are doing something closer to what Hubert and Stuart Dreyfus described in Mind Over Machine (1986) as expert pattern recognition: they are reading situations, not forms. A care coordinator at a Federally Qualified Health Center in Hartford who has worked that community for eight years knows which patients will lose Medicaid coverage during a redetermination cycle and quietly call no one about it, knows which transportation vendors are reliable in February and which ones aren't, knows that a particular patient's daughter handles the paperwork but her son makes the decisions and those two people are not speaking right now. Michael Polanyi called this kind of knowledge "tacit" precisely because it resists encoding (The Tacit Dimension, 1966). You cannot extract it into a training set. You cannot write it into a prompt. When you automate the role that carries it, you don't transfer the knowledge. You discard it.⏎⏎The efficiency argument for administrative AI is not wrong on its face. Sinsky et al. (2016, Annals of Internal Medicine, 165(11), 753-760) documented how administrative burden consumes physician time that should go to patients, and that finding is cited constantly and correctly. Understaffing is real. The documentation load is genuinely unsustainable. I understand why people look at those numbers and reach for automation. What I think the efficiency framing misses is that administrative friction in healthcare is not purely waste. Some of it is. But some of it is a checkpoint where a human being with contextual knowledge catches an error before it reaches a patient. A prior authorization reviewer who flags an inconsistency between a diagnosis code and a requested procedure, not because a rule told her to but because she has seen this physician's coding patterns for three years and something looks off, is doing something no algorithm trained on population-level claims data can replicate. When you remove that reviewer and replace her with a system optimized for throughput, you don't just speed up the process. You remove a safety layer while creating new categories of error that the system will not flag because the system does not know what it does not know.⏎⏎The evidence on automated administrative decision-making is not encouraging. The National Academy for State Health Policy's 2022 issue brief on automated Medicaid eligibility determinations documented systematic errors in multiple state implementations, including cases where automation incorrectly terminated coverage for eligible beneficiaries at scale, with errors that were invisible to program administrators until advocates started tracking individual cases. The Government Accountability Office's 2023 report on prior authorization in Medicare Advantage (GAO-23-105765) found that even under human administration, plans were approving services that should have been denied and denying services that should have been approved at rates that CMS was not adequately monitoring. That is the baseline we are proposing to automate. Obermeyer et al.'s 2019 Science paper (366(6464), 447-453) on racial bias in a widely deployed health management algorithm showed that systematic errors in automated systems are not random: they follow the contours of existing inequity, amplifying disadvantage in populations that are already underserved. Administrative AI will not be different. The errors will not distribute evenly.⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD REQUIRE BEFORE ANY ADMINISTRATIVE AI DEPLOYMENT⏎⏎I want to be specific about what I am asking for, because "be careful with AI" is not a regulatory position.⏎⏎1. Pre-deployment safety demonstration: Any AI system used to make or assist in administrative decisions affecting patient access to care (prior authorization, eligibility determination, care coordination routing, benefits navigation) should be required to demonstrate accuracy and equity benchmarks against a defined patient population before deployment, not after. Post-market surveillance is insufficient for decisions that affect access to care in real time.⏎⏎2. Accountability frameworks that already exist should apply: The regulatory accountability structures in 45 C.F.R. § 164.530 and 42 C.F.R. § 431.200 et seq. cover administrative decision-making in covered entities and Medicaid programs. HHS should explicitly confirm that AI systems operating in these functions are subject to the same accountability requirements as human administrators, and should not allow vendors to disclaim liability by characterizing their tools as "decision support" when those tools are in practice making the decision.⏎⏎3. Preservation of human review capacity: Efficiency gains from administrative AI should not be used to justify reducing the workforce that performs administrative oversight. If a system automates the initial processing of prior authorization requests, a human reviewer with genuine authority to override the system's recommendation must remain in the workflow. This is not a formality. It is the difference between a safety layer and a liability disclaimer.⏎⏎4. Transparency to patients: Patients affected by automated administrative decisions should be informed that automation was involved and should have a clear, accessible process for requesting human review. This is basic procedural fairness and it is currently absent from most AI deployment frameworks I have seen discussed in this rulemaking context.⏎⏎The concern I keep coming back to is this: the patchwork logic that created the disparity I described at the beginning of this comment, where Karen's outcome depended partly on which hospital had invested in which software, is now being applied to administrative functions. But in the clinical AI context, at least there is a visible event when something goes wrong. A missed diagnosis eventually surfaces. An administrative error that terminates someone's Medicaid coverage, or routes them to the wrong care coordinator, or generates an incorrect prior authorization denial, may never surface as a discrete failure. The patient just falls through. That invisibility is not a reason to be less careful. It is a reason to be more careful, and to require demonstrated safety before deployment rather than assuming efficiency gains will outweigh harms we have not yet measured.⏎⏎I recognize that the people working on this rulemaking are trying to solve real problems. The administrative burden on the healthcare system is genuinely unsustainable. I am not arguing for preserving the status quo. I am arguing that the expertise of administrative healthcare workers, the tacit and contextual knowledge they carry, is a patient safety resource, and that treating it as overhead to be eliminated rather than capacity to be supported is a mistake that will be paid for by people who are already paying too much. The NASHP findings from 2022 should be required reading for anyone designing a deployment framework here, because those state-level Medicaid automation projects were also launched with efficiency goals and genuine good intentions, and the errors they generated were invisible until someone went looking.⏎⏎HHS should require that before any administrative AI system is deployed in a clinical care setting, the deploying entity demonstrate that human expertise has been preserved in the workflow, not just nominally but functionally, and that the system has been tested specifically for the kinds of errors that fall on♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0015♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Adams♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔University of Chicago Center for Health and the Social Sciences♔♔♔♔♔♔University of Chicago Center for Health and the Social Sciences⏎Comments Submitted to HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-RFI-Clinical-Care⏎Submitted by: Brian Adams, Research Faculty, Center for Health and the Social Sciences⏎⏎—————————————————————————————————⏎UChicago CHSS | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎—————————————————————————————————⏎⏎⏎THE POLITICAL ECONOMY OF AI ACCELERATION: MARKET CONCENTRATION, REGULATORY CAPTURE, AND THE LIMITS OF THE CURRENT FRAMING⏎⏎The Center for Health and the Social Sciences at the University of Chicago submits these comments in response to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings. These comments do not address clinical safety, algorithmic accountability, or workforce displacement — important questions that other commenters will engage more directly. This submission focuses on a narrower but consequential set of concerns: the structural economic conditions of the health AI market, and the degree to which the RFI's framing already reflects the successful prior influence of the concentrated commercial interests that HHS is ostensibly regulating.⏎⏎The concern is not that HHS is acting in bad faith. The concern is that the policy conversation has been shaped upstream of this RFI, and that the proposed instruments — reimbursement reforms, interoperability mandates, public-private partnerships — will, under present market conditions, function primarily as mechanisms for incumbent entrenchment rather than broad diffusion of beneficial technology.⏎⏎⏎MARKET CONCENTRATION AND THE STRUCTURE OF THE HEALTH AI VENDOR ECOSYSTEM⏎⏎The health AI market is not competitive in any meaningful sense. A small number of large EHR vendors and technology platform companies control the data infrastructure through which AI tools must flow to reach clinical settings, and the barriers to entry for smaller or non-commercial developers are substantial. Gans and Stern (2023) document this dynamic explicitly, describing how EHR incumbency creates compounding AI market entry barriers through proprietary data formats, certification dependencies, and switching costs that effectively foreclose competition from firms that cannot absorb the cost of multi-year integration cycles. The CHIME survey data cited in the background to this RFI — finding that 78% of health IT leaders identify interoperability gaps as a primary deployment barrier — is read by the Center as evidence of this incumbency structure, not simply as a technical problem amenable to standards-based solutions.⏎⏎The historical record on healthcare market consolidation is not encouraging here. Dafny and Lee (2016) and Beaulieu et al. (2020) have both documented how consolidation in healthcare markets tends to reduce quality while increasing cost, even when the original rationale for consolidation was efficiency-based modernization. The analogy is not perfect, but the mechanism is structurally similar: when regulatory action reduces friction for large, well-capitalized actors while nominally remaining technology-neutral, the practical effect is accelerated concentration. Adler-Milstein and Pfeifer (2017) documented precisely this dynamic in the information blocking context, where certification-based compliance requirements that appeared vendor-neutral in design produced outcomes that systematically favored large EHR vendors with dedicated regulatory affairs capacity.⏎⏎The GAO's 2022 report on AI in health care (GAO-22-104629) acknowledged that the market for clinical AI tools is characterized by significant information asymmetries between vendors and health system purchasers, but stopped short of analyzing the competitive structure implications. This Center believes that gap in the federal analytical record is consequential, and that HHS's RFI should have included explicit questions about market concentration dynamics before moving to questions about acceleration mechanisms.⏎⏎—————————————————————————————————⏎UChicago CHSS | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 2⏎—————————————————————————————————⏎⏎⏎THE RFI FRAMING AS A POLITICAL ECONOMY PROBLEM⏎⏎The structure of this RFI warrants scrutiny as an artifact of prior influence, not just as a policy document. The questions posed — how to streamline reimbursement, how to reduce regulatory burden, how to structure public-private partnerships for AI development — are questions that assume the primary problem is regulatory friction slowing beneficial technology. That framing is not neutral. It reflects a particular diagnosis of the problem, and that diagnosis happens to be the one most consistently advanced by the large technology vendors and health system associations with the greatest lobbying capacity and the most direct financial interest in regulatory acceleration.⏎⏎Stigler's foundational 1971 analysis of regulatory capture argued that the regulated industry tends to shape the regulatory agenda in its own interest, particularly in technically complex domains where the regulator depends on industry for information. The health AI context is a near-textbook case: HHS depends substantially on vendor-provided data, vendor-funded research, and vendor-staffed advisory bodies to understand the technology it is attempting to govern. Mazzucato and Collington (2023) extend this analysis to the consulting and technology sectors specifically, documenting the structural mechanisms through which private actors extract public value from government modernization initiatives while simultaneously shaping the terms on which those initiatives are designed.⏎⏎The Center is not arguing that the commercial actors involved are acting illegally or even that their interests are entirely misaligned with patient welfare. The argument is narrower: the framing of this RFI reflects prior successful influence, and that framing will determine which policy instruments are considered, which actors benefit, and which risks are treated as residual rather than central.⏎⏎Landi and Muoio (2023) documented the degree to which EHR vendor consolidation has already constrained health system procurement choices in ways that produce vendor lock-in and reduce the leverage of safety-net hospitals in particular. The reimbursement reforms contemplated in this RFI, if implemented without structural conditions, will reproduce that dynamic at the AI layer of the health IT stack. Frakt and Jha (2023) have argued more broadly that reimbursement structure reforms in health care consistently advantage actors with the administrative capacity to navigate new billing categories — and in the AI context, that means large integrated vendors, not community health centers or critical access hospitals in rural North Carolina or elsewhere.⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC CONCERNS REGARDING PROPOSED POLICY INSTRUMENTS⏎⏎The Center's concerns resolve into four specific analytical objections to the policy instruments under consideration, which are offered here not as a comprehensive critique but as a starting point for the kind of structural analysis this RFI should have solicited directly:⏎⏎1.  Reimbursement reforms for AI-enabled care pathways, absent market structure conditions, will function as revenue transfer mechanisms to incumbent vendors. If CMS creates new billing codes or add-on payments for AI-assisted clinical decision support without requiring open interoperability as a condition of payment eligibility, the practical effect will be to subsidize proprietary platforms. The National Academy for State Health Policy documented this risk in its 2023 issue brief on vendor lock-in and AI-enabled clinical decision support, noting that health systems that receive reimbursement incentives tied to specific AI tools face dramatically increased switching costs in subsequent procurement cycles.⏎⏎2.  Public-private partnership mechanisms, including Cooperative Research and Development Agreements (CRADAs) and data-sharing arrangements between federal health data infrastructure and commercial AI developers, require structural firewalls that are not currently contemplated in the RFI. Mazzucato and Collington (2023) describe the generic mechanism by which private actors use public-private partnership structures to gain preferential access to publicly-funded data and infrastructure, then extract rents from the resulting market position. HHS should require independent oversight of any such arrangements, with explicit conflict-of-interest provisions and public disclosure of data access terms.⏎⏎3.  Interoperability mandates, as Schulte and Fry (2019) documented extensively in the EHR context, are systematically undermined by large vendors with the political capacity to shape implementation guidance in ways that preserve de facto proprietary advantage while achieving nominal compliance. The same dynamic should be anticipated in the AI context. Interoperability requirements that are technically meaningful — requiring, for example, that AI model inputs and outputs be accessible through standardized APIs regardless of EHR platform — will face intense vendor resistance, and HHS should be prepared for that resistance to manifest in the sub-regulatory guidance process, not just in public comment.⏎⏎4.  The RFI does not ask whether the federal government should support open-source or publicly-owned AI tools as an alternative to commercial procurement. This is a significant omission. Obermeyer et al. (2019) demonstrated that a widely-deployed commercial algorithm used in population health management systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients — an outcome that the commercial vendor had limited incentive to address until public scrutiny forced the issue. The existence of publicly-owned or open-source alternatives would create competitive pressure on commercial vendors while preserving public access to model architecture and training data for independent audit.⏎⏎—————————————————————————————————⏎UChicago CHSS | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 3⏎—————————————————————————————————⏎⏎⏎RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎The Center offers the following recommendations, which follow directly from the political economy analysis above rather than from clinical or technical considerations:⏎⏎a.  HHS should commission an independent market concentration analysis of the health AI vendor ecosystem prior to finalizing any reimbursement or regulatory reforms. That analysis should examine horizontal concentration among AI developers, vertical integration between EHR vendors and AI product lines, and the degree to which existing certification and billing infrastructure creates durable incumbent advantage.⏎⏎b.  Any reimbursement reforms for AI-enabled care pathways should include open interoperability requirements as a condition of payment eligibility, with enforcement mechanisms that are not delegated to the vendor certification process.⏎⏎c.  HHS should establish structural firewalls between vendor-funded research and any standard-setting or guidance-development processes related to clinical AI. This includes restricting the participation of vendor employees and vendor-funded researchers in advisory committees that set technical standards for AI reimbursement eligibility.⏎⏎d.  HHS should issue a specific solicitation for public comment on the potential for federally-supported open-source clinical AI tools, modeled on existing precedents in genomics (e.g., the NIH's All of Us data platform) that have preserved public access to federally-funded research infrastructure.⏎⏎e.  Any CRADAs or public-private data-sharing arrangements entered into in connection with this initiative should be subject to independent oversight, with public disclosure of data access terms, revenue-sharing arrangements, and conflict-of-interest certifications for all participating parties.⏎⏎The Center recognizes that these recommendations are in some tension with the acceleration framing of the RFI itself. That tension is the point. The question of whether HHS should be accelerating AI adoption in clinical care — as opposed to ensuring that AI adoption, at whatever pace market and regulatory conditions produce, serves patient welfare rather than vendor revenue — is a prior question that this RFI does not ask. The Center believes it should have been asked, and that the failure to ask it reflects exactly the kind of upstream agenda-setting that Stigler's framework would predict.⏎⏎One additional concern that does not fit neatly into the categories above: the 14-month average delay from AI procurement to clinical deployment documented in CHIME member case studies is cited in this RFI's background materials as evidence of regulatory friction that should be reduced. This Center reads that same statistic differently. A 14-month integration timeline in a market where procurement decisions are made by health IT administrators under significant vendor pressure, and where post-deployment monitoring capacity is limited, may reflect appropriate caution rather than inefficiency. The question of whether deployment delays are a problem or a safeguard depends entirely on who bears the cost of a failed deployment — and the answer to that question is not the same for a large academic medical center as it is for a critical access hospital in♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0016♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Lewis♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔Physicians for a National Health Program♔♔♔♔♔♔Physicians for a National Health Program⏎2 N. LaSalle Street, Suite 2850⏎Chicago, IL 60602⏎government.affairs@pnhp.org⏎⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI-0001⏎⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎PNHP Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎THE ACCOUNTABILITY STRUCTURE DOES NOT EXIST, AND HHS IS PROPOSING TO BUILD ON TOP OF NOTHING⏎⏎Physicians for a National Health Program submits these comments on behalf of its physician membership to respond specifically to the Department's questions regarding regulatory frameworks and reimbursement incentives for AI adoption in clinical settings. PNHP does not oppose the application of computational tools to medicine. What PNHP opposes, with mounting urgency that this RFI has done nothing to diminish, is the Department's apparent intention to accelerate deployment of clinical AI systems before resolving the foundational legal question that every other accountability structure in American medicine has required as a precondition: who is responsible when the system causes harm?⏎⏎That question is not rhetorical. Medical malpractice doctrine, as it has operated for over a century, rests on the assumption that an identifiable human decision-maker exercised clinical judgment, and that this person can be held to account. When a physician delegates a decision to an AI-driven clinical decision support system, the accountability chain does not merely bend — it fractures. The clinician deflects to the tool. The hospital deflects to the vendor specification. The vendor deflects to training data or user configuration error. This is not a hypothetical future risk. Obermeyer and Emanuel documented in 375 New England Journal of Medicine 1216 (2016) that algorithmic decision-making already obscures clinical responsibility in ways traditional negligence doctrine was not designed to address. The Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability section 2 (Am. Law Inst. 1998) provides no clean doctrinal home for AI clinical tools, which are neither products in the traditional sense nor professional services. Hoffman and Podgurski, writing in 19 Yale J. Health Pol'y L. & Ethics 1 (2019), identified this liability vacuum with precision that HHS has declined to engage. The GAO reached parallel conclusions in GAO-22-104629 (Feb. 2022), finding that accountability frameworks lag AI deployment across clinical settings. None of this is new literature. The Department has had years to act.⏎⏎The documented harms are already accumulating in the administrative layer, which this RFI treats as peripheral when it is in fact a primary site of AI-driven patient harm. The HHS Office of Inspector General, in OIG Report OEI-02-23-00140 (Apr. 2024), documented the systematic misuse of automated prior authorization denial systems in Medicare Advantage — systems making consequential clinical-access decisions with no identifiable human accountable for the outcome. Existing administrative simplification rules, including 45 C.F.R. section 164.306 and 45 C.F.R. section 162.1101, impose transaction processing obligations that were written without any anticipation of AI intermediation and create zero accountability obligations for AI-driven processing nodes. Frank Pasquale's structural analysis in The Black Box Society (Harvard Univ. Press 2015) described exactly the vendor opacity and institutional lock-in that follows once AI becomes embedded in regulated industries — and that embedding is already well advanced in the prior authorization and claims adjudication infrastructure connecting payers and providers.⏎⏎⏎PNHP Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎The contrast with the European Union's approach is not merely instructive — it is damning. The EU AI Act, Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Article 22 and Annex III, required that accountability infrastructure precede deployment authorization for high-risk AI systems in healthcare. HHS is proposing the reverse: deploy first, develop governance later. The price and Cohen analysis in 17 Nature Medicine 37 (2019) established that AI intermediation disrupts traditional accountability structures in ways that cannot be remediated after deployment because the evidentiary record necessary for accountability is generated — or not generated — at the point of system design. Mello et al., writing in 401 New England Journal of Medicine 1 (2024), proposed a governance framework for AI accountability in healthcare that HHS has not adopted, cited, or substantively engaged in this RFI. The AMA's own policy, H-480.940 (2023), states that physician accountability cannot be delegated to AI systems — a position that is irreconcilable with the reimbursement incentive structures this RFI is contemplating.⏎⏎PNHP's specific demands are as follows:⏎⏎1. HHS must not issue any incentive, reimbursement modification, or programmatic design change that accelerates AI adoption in clinical or administrative care until the Department has promulgated binding rules assigning clear, non-delegable legal accountability to a specific responsible party for every AI-assisted decision affecting patient care or access to care.⏎⏎2. Any such binding rule must address the administrative intermediary layer — clearinghouses, transaction processors, prior authorization platforms — as a discrete accountability domain, not merely an extension of provider or payer obligations.⏎⏎3. The Department must require that AI systems operating in high-risk clinical or administrative domains maintain auditable decision logs sufficient to support both civil liability and regulatory enforcement proceedings, with retention periods and access rights specified by rule rather than left to vendor contract.⏎⏎4. HHS must engage the liability vacuum documented by Hoffman and Podgurski, by the GAO, and by the OIG before issuing any further guidance treating AI adoption as presumptively beneficial absent resolved accountability structure.⏎⏎PNHP notes that the ONC's existing interoperability framework under 45 C.F.R. section 171 and the Trusted Exchange Framework provides no mechanism for tracing accountability through AI-assisted transactions, and the Department has offered no explanation for why it believes the administrative simplification infrastructure can absorb AI intermediation without structural♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0017♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Rodriguez♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mom had hip surgery at the hospital in Rapid City two years back and I sat there for FORTY MINUTES watching her hit that call button while nurses stared at computer screens doing data entry. They weren't ignoring her. The system had them buried.⏎⏎So I get why HHS wants to fix this. I do. But what I keep thinking about is WHO actually benefits here. The companies pushing hardest for rapid AI adoption are the same ones who will be selling the contracts. And once a hospital buys into one of these big platforms, they basically cannot leave. The switching costs are enormous. So whatever savings HHS is promising on paper, a big chunk of it just flows straight into licensing fees and renewal contracts. I've watched this happen in other industries.⏎⏎The smaller companies that might actually build something useful get squeezed out because they can't afford to navigate the billing rules or the lobbying.⏎⏎Please just make sure it's patients who come out ahead here, not the vendors.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0018♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Lopez♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔American Association of Nurse Practitioners♔♔♔♔♔♔EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎The American Association of Nurse Practitioners (AANP) submits these comments in response to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. AANP represents more than 355,000 nurse practitioners across the United States who collectively deliver tens of millions of patient encounters annually, including a substantial share of primary and specialty care for patients with chronic liver disease, metabolic syndrome, and other conditions for which AI-assisted clinical decision support tools are now being actively marketed. AANP urges HHS to approach this RFI with considerably more caution than its framing suggests, and to resist the institutional momentum toward deployment-first, evaluation-later policymaking that has characterized prior waves of health information technology adoption. The core argument advanced in these comments is not that AI has no role in clinical care — it plainly does, and AANP has engaged constructively with AI policy for several years — but rather that the RFI's operating assumptions systematically underweight the relational dimensions of clinical practice in ways that, if translated into reimbursement and regulatory frameworks, will produce measurable patient harm. Appendix A, which contains AANP's full registry analysis and provider survey data referenced herein, is being transmitted separately to the docket and should be considered part of this submission.⏎⏎AANP Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-OS-2024-0011 | Page 1⏎⏎The RFI uses the phrase "enhance productivity" at least four times in describing the goals of AI adoption in clinical settings. AANP wants to address that framing directly, because it is not a neutral choice of language. Productivity, in the economic sense the RFI invokes, measures output per unit of input — encounters per clinician per hour, documentation time per note, referrals generated per abnormal test result. These are not clinically irrelevant metrics, and AANP does not pretend otherwise. But when "enhance productivity" becomes the organizing aspiration for AI deployment in clinical care, it encodes a model of medicine as transaction processing rather than as relational practice, and that encoding has downstream consequences for how tools are designed, how they are reimbursed, and how their success is measured. A data from AANP's 2023 State of Liver Disease Policy Survey — conducted among 210 hepatology providers and 94 patient advocacy partners across 38 hepatology centers — found that reimbursement ambiguity and the absence of validated clinical integration frameworks were the two leading barriers to AI tool adoption, not lack of interest in the tools themselves. What providers told us, in follow-up interviews, was more textured than a survey can capture: they were worried that AI tools would be deployed to speed up encounters, not to deepen them, and that reimbursement structures would reinforce that pressure. HHS should take that concern seriously as a policy signal, not dismiss it as technophobia.⏎⏎Consider what clinical care actually looks like in a hepatology practice managing patients with early-stage fibrosis. A 52-year-old woman — AANP will call her a composite drawn from multiple provider accounts — presents with fatigue that she describes as "just tired, you know, from everything." She has had an abnormal FIB-4 result for seven months. She has not seen a hepatologist. She cannot articulate, in clinical language, that her fatigue is qualitatively different from the exhaustion of caregiving and economic stress that fills the rest of her description. The nurse practitioner in that encounter is not primarily processing data. She is reading the patient's affect, noticing that the patient's adult daughter, who came to the appointment, will not meet the clinician's eyes, registering that something in the room is not being said. The referral that follows — the one that will catch the early-stage fibrosis before it advances — comes not from an algorithmic prompt but from a clinical judgment that is inseparable from the relational attunement of that encounter. AANP's registry data, encompassing more than 47,000 patients across those same 38 centers, found that 63% of patients with early-stage fibrosis had no documented specialist encounter within 12 months of an abnormal non-invasive test result. That is a genuine care gap. But the answer to that care gap is not to replace the relational encounter with an algorithmic nudge; it is to ensure that relational encounters happen, and that clinicians have the training and time to act on what those encounters reveal.⏎⏎AANP Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-OS-2024-0011 | Page 2⏎⏎The clinical literature on the therapeutic relationship is not soft or supplementary evidence — it is a body of randomized controlled trial data that HHS should be required to engage before designing reimbursement frameworks for AI tools that mediate that relationship. Kelley and colleagues' 2014 systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials published in PLOS ONE documented that the quality of the patient-clinician relationship produces measurable, independent effects on clinical outcomes — effects that are not explained away by technical quality of care or diagnostic accuracy (Kelley JM, Kraft-Todd G, Schapira L, Kossowsky J, Riess H, PLOS ONE, 2014;9(4):e94207). Hojat and colleagues found that physicians' empathy scores, measured prospectively, predicted hemoglobin A1c and LDL cholesterol control in diabetic patients independently of other clinical variables — a finding with direct implications for any AI system that reduces the relational bandwidth of the clinical encounter in the name of efficiency (Hojat M, Louis DZ, Markham FW, Wender R, Rabinowitz C, Gonnella JS, Academic Medicine, 2011;86(3):359-364). Zolnierek and DiMatteo's meta-analysis of 127 studies found that patients of physicians with stronger communication skills were 19% more likely to adhere to treatment recommendations — and that meta-analysis included studies spanning multiple decades and clinical contexts, making it one of the most robust findings in health services research (Zolnierek KB, DiMatteo MR, Medical Care, 2009;47(8):826-834). Epstein and Street have argued compellingly that patient-centered care is not a stylistic preference but a measurable mechanism through which clinical outcomes are produced, and that frameworks which treat relational quality as separable from technical quality fundamentally misunderstand how healing works (Epstein RM, Street RL Jr., Annals of Family Medicine, 2011;9(2):100-103). None of these citations appear in HHS's RFI. That is a significant gap.⏎⏎The RFI's framing also largely ignores what the automation bias literature has established about the longitudinal effects of algorithmic intermediation on clinical reasoning capacity. This is not primarily an individual clinician problem — it is an institutional and generational one, and that distinction matters enormously for policy. Parasuraman and Manzey's foundational review in Human Factors documented that automation complacency is not a character flaw in individual users but a predictable cognitive response to reliable automated systems — systems that perform well enough, often enough, that vigilant human oversight becomes cognitively costly and gradually attenuates (Parasuraman R, Manzey DH, Human Factors, 2010;52(3):381-410). Goddard and colleagues' systematic review in JAMIA confirmed that automation bias — the tendency to favor automated recommendations over independent judgment — is a consistent finding across clinical decision support deployments and is not reliably mitigated by training alone (Goddard K, Roudsari A, Wyatt JC, Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association, 2012;19(1):121-127). What this means at the institutional level is that health systems that deploy AI clinical decision support (CDSS) tools at scale are not just changing how individual clinicians make individual decisions — they are changing the training environment in which the next generation of clinicians develops clinical reasoning capacity. A nurse practitioner who has spent the first several years of her practice following AI-generated care pathway recommendations for liver disease management will have built different cognitive habits than one who developed those pathways through accumulated clinical judgment. When the AI system fails — and all systems fail, as Kohn, Corrigan, and Donaldson documented with respect to every prior generation of health information technology in To Err Is Human — the clinician trained in an AI-mediated environment will have fewer independent reasoning resources to fall back on (Kohn LT, Corrigan JM, Donaldson MS, eds., To Err Is Human: Building a Safer Health System, Washington, DC: National Academies Press, 2000). HHS's RFI does not address this deskilling risk at all, and AANP considers that a serious omission.⏎⏎AANP Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-OS-2024-0011 | Page 3⏎⏎There is also an anchoring problem that the RFI's framing tends to obscure. Tversky and Kahneman's work on heuristics and biases established that numerical anchors — even arbitrary ones — systematically distort subsequent judgment (Tversky A, Kahneman D, Science, 1974;185(4157):1124-1131). In clinical contexts, an AI system that presents a risk score or a recommended care pathway before the clinician has completed her own assessment does not merely add information to the encounter — it restructures the cognitive sequence of that encounter in ways that systematically favor the algorithm's framing. Shortliffe and Sepúlveda noted in their 2018 JAMA commentary that the clinical decision support literature has not adequately grappled with the difference between tools that extend clinical reasoning and tools that substitute for it, and that the design and deployment context of CDSS tools determines which function they actually serve in practice (Shortliffe EH, Sepúlveda MJ, JAMA, 2018;320(21):2199-2200). AANP agrees, and would add that reimbursement frameworks that reward speed and throughput will reliably push deployed CDSS tools toward substitution rather than extension, regardless of what their designers intended. The incentive structure is the policy. HHS needs to understand that.⏎⏎Abraham Verghese has written with clinical precision about what is lost when the patient becomes, in his phrase, an "icon" — a data object whose representation in the electronic health record becomes more real to the clinical encounter than the person in the room (Verghese A, New England Journal of Medicine, 2008;359(26):2748-2751). AANP believes that AI-assisted clinical care, deployed under the productivity-enhancement framework the RFI advances, will accelerate that iconization. Eric Topol's influential 2019 Nature Medicine paper on the convergence of human and artificial intelligence in medicine is worth citing here precisely because of where it falls short: Topol argues that AI will "liberate" clinicians from administrative burden and restore time for human connection, but the paper's policy prescriptions — faster deployment, broader integration, reduced regulatory friction — do not actually describe mechanisms by which that liberation occurs or how it would be measured (Topol EJ, Nature Medicine, 2019;25(1):44-56). The assumption is that time freed from documentation will automatically flow back into relational care. AANP's provider survey data suggest otherwise. When clinicians in our network described their experience with existing AI documentation tools, the most common response was that the time savings were absorbed by increased patient volume expectations, not by longer or deeper individual encounters. Bickmore and Paasche-Orlow have documented that information technology deployments in health settings consistently underperform on relational outcomes because the institutional incentives surrounding them are misaligned with relational goals (Bickmore TW, Paasche-Orlow MK, American Journal of Health Behavior, 2012;36(3):367-377). That is not a technology problem. It is a policy and payment problem, and it is the problem HHS has the authority to address.⏎⏎AANP Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-OS-2024-0011 | Page 4⏎⏎AANP's specific policy recommendations, responsive to the RFI's questions regarding regulatory action and reimbursement design, are as follows:⏎⏎1. HHS should establish a moratorium on reimbursement approval for AI-assisted clinical decision support tools deployed in direct patient care contexts absent prospective evaluation data that includes both safety outcomes and relational-impact measures. "Prospective evaluation" means pre-deployment data collection, not post-market surveillance of tools already in widespread use. The 21st Century Cures Act's framework for software as a medical device provides a starting point, but it does not require relational-impact evaluation, and HHS should close that gap through rulemaking.⏎⏎2. Any reimbursement framework for AI CDSS tools developed through CMS or through value-based payment arrangements must require the collection of patient-reported experience measures (PREMs) as a condition of ongoing reimbursement. PREMs should specifically assess patients' perceptions of whether their clinician was present and attentive during the encounter, whether they felt heard, and whether the clinical interaction addressed concerns that the patient had not explicitly articulated. These are not soft metrics — they predict adherence, return-to-care rates, and downstream utilization in ways that are clinically and economically significant.⏎⏎3. HHS should commission, through AHRQ or through a dedicated funding mechanism within ONC, a research program specifically focused on the relational and deskilling dimensions of AI adoption in clinical care. This program should include longitudinal cohort studies of clinician reasoning capacity before and after AI CDSS deployment, qualitative research on patient experience of AI-mediated encounters, and health services research on whether AI-freed time in clinical practice is actually reinvested in relational care or absorbed by throughput pressure. The evidence base for relational harm from AI intermediation is currently thin not because the harm is implausible but because nobody has funded the research to document it. HHS should fund that research before deploying the tools at scale, not after.⏎⏎4. HHS should convene a formal stakeholder process — distinct from this RFI — specifically focused on the therapeutic alliance as a clinical safety variable. That process should include patient advocates, behavioral scientists, health services researchers, and front-line clinicians, and its output should be a conceptual framework for measuring relational quality that can be integrated into AI tool evaluation requirements.⏎⏎AANP Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-OS-2024-0011 | Page 5⏎⏎AANP recognizes that some of these recommendations will be characterized as obstructionist by stakeholders with commercial interests in accelerated AI deployment, and AANP wants to be clear that this characterization would be inaccurate. There are AI applications in clinical care that AANP supports and has supported actively: ambient documentation tools that reduce administrative burden without mediating clinical reasoning, population health analytics that help practices identify patients who have fallen out of care, and image analysis tools that assist specialist interpretation without displacing it. The distinctions that matter are not between "AI" and "no AI" but between deployment contexts and incentive structures that preserve relational clinical practice and those that erode it. AANP's concern is specifically with the RFI's framing, which does not make these distinctions, and with the reimbursement and regulatory infrastructure that framing would produce if left unchallenged.⏎⏎AANP Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-OS-2024-0011 | Page 6⏎⏎There is one more dimension of this issue that AANP wants to name, even though it is harder to operationalize than the recommendations above. The patients in AANP's liver disease registry — the 47,000-plus individuals whose care trajectories we have followed across 38 hepatology centers — are not, in aggregate, the patients who will benefit most from AI productivity enhancements. They are disproportionately low-income, disproportionately from communities with limited specialist access, disproportionately from groups whose medical histories include encounters with healthcare systems that did not listen to them. For those patients, the therapeutic relationship is not a nice-to-have. It is frequently the mechanism by which they stay engaged with care at all. A system that optimizes for throughput and algorithmic consistency will, in AANP's assessment, systematically underserve those patients even if its technical performance metrics look excellent. The 63% of early-stage fibrosis patients in our registry who had no documented specialist encounter within 12 months of an abnormal non-invasive test result — those patients were not lost to follow-up because the algorithm failed. They were lost because nobody called them back. Nobody sat with them and helped them understand what "fibrosis" means and why it matters and what comes next. No AI system currently on the market or in development addresses that problem. HHS's RFI, in its current form, does not either, and AANP urges the Department to revisit its framing before translating this solicitation into policy.⏎⏎For questions regarding this submission, contact the AANP Policy and Regulatory Affairs team at policy@aanp.org⏎⏎AANP Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-OS-2024-0011 | Page 7⏎⏎NOTE: Appendix A (AANP/GLI Joint Registry Analysis, 47,312 patient records, 38 hepatology centers, data through Q3 2024) and Appendix B (2023 State of Liver Disease Policy Survey, full methodology and crosstabulations) are being submitted as separate attachments. If those attachments have not been received and associated with this docket entry, please contact the address above. AANP has experienced inconsistent confirmation of attachment receipt on the regulations.gov platform for submissions exceeding 10MB and cannot confirm both appendices transmitted successfully.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0019♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Adams♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔PathAI♔♔♔♔♔♔Tapestryhealth Office of Compliance and Clinical Informatics⏎Comments Submitted to HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP-ONC⏎Re: Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: James Adams, Chief Compliance Officer, PathAI / Tapestryhealth Compliance Function⏎Date: January 2025⏎⏎Tapestryhealth Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎MARKET CONCENTRATION AND THE POLITICAL ECONOMY OF THIS RFI⏎⏎Tapestryhealth operates across 47 affiliated clinical sites and submits these comments based on a Q1-Q4 2024 internal compliance review that produced findings we believe are directly relevant to the framing of this Request for Information (Tapestryhealth Office of Compliance and Clinical Informatics, 2024). That review found that 68% of AI-assisted decision-support tools currently deployed across our network lack audit trail documentation sufficient to satisfy existing OIG guidance on algorithmic accountability (Office of Inspector General, U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, OIG-CPG-2023-04, 2023). We also found that 23% of claim denials involving AI-flagged coding recommendations during that same period were attributed specifically to insufficient documentation of model version provenance and validation status. We raise these findings not to rehearse compliance failures, but because they illuminate something about the political economy of this RFI that we think HHS needs to confront directly.⏎⏎The framing of this RFI is, at its core, a vendor-friendly document. The questions are organized around acceleration, adoption, and reimbursement modernization in ways that take for granted that the primary obstacle to beneficial AI in clinical care is regulatory friction rather than market structure. That premise is contestable, and Tapestryhealth submits that HHS should examine it carefully before proceeding. The health information technology market is structurally concentrated among a small number of large EHR vendors and AI technology incumbents whose lobbying capacity and public comment infrastructure allow them to shape regulatory frameworks in ways that entrench their market position (Gee & Bhatt, 2023, Health Affairs, 42(8), 1102-1111; Dafny & Lee, 2022, New England Journal of Medicine, 386(14), 1289-1292). We are not making a speculative claim. The Federal Trade Commission made a substantially similar observation in its October 2023 policy paper on generative AI and health data, which identified market concentration risks and regulatory gaps that have not been resolved in the intervening period (Federal Trade Commission, 2023). The RFI's emphasis on public-private partnerships and CRADA mechanisms, without any corresponding attention to how those mechanisms will be governed or who will control the resulting data and model assets, is a structural choice that will benefit large incumbents disproportionately.⏎⏎Tapestryhealth Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎REIMBURSEMENT MODERNIZATION AND LOCK-IN DYNAMICS⏎⏎Tapestryhealth's compliance analysis focuses specifically on two areas addressed in this RFI: the proposed reimbursement modernization framework and the CRADA and public-private data partnership structures. We do not address clinical safety, workforce, or liability questions in this submission, which are covered in separate filings from our clinical informatics leadership.⏎⏎On reimbursement, the concern is straightforward. The Congressional Budget Office's 2024 working paper on federal reimbursement policy and AI in clinical settings documented that distributional effects across provider types are not neutral, and that smaller and independent providers face substantially higher administrative burdens in navigating AI-related billing and coverage processes than large integrated health systems (Congressional Budget Office, CBO Working Paper 2024-07). Tapestryhealth is a mid-sized affiliated network, and our experience is consistent with that finding. The proposed reimbursement modernization, as framed in this RFI, will primarily benefit organizations that have already made large capital investments in AI infrastructure integrated with dominant EHR platforms. Adler-Milstein and Pfeifer (2022) documented that EHR switching costs are substantial enough to constitute genuine market lock-in, with hospital transition data showing that once an institution is embedded in a given vendor's infrastructure, the effective cost of switching exceeds the potential benefit in almost all scenarios (Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association, 29(6), 987-996). A reimbursement framework that is operationalized through EHR-integrated workflows will replicate this dynamic at the AI layer. Underperforming AI products embedded in dominant EHR platforms will be insulated from competitive accountability because the cost of replacing them exceeds the cost of tolerating their failures.⏎⏎The American Hospital Association's March 2024 survey of health system AI procurement found that contract terms from dominant AI vendors routinely include data-use provisions that transfer substantial value from healthcare systems to technology companies, often without health system leadership fully understanding the scope of those provisions at signing (AHA Research Series, March 2024). Schulman and Richman (2023) made the same point more bluntly: the economics of healthcare AI, as currently structured, systematically displace clinical value toward vendor profit rather than patient welfare (JAMA, 329(19), 1651-1658). Tapestryhealth does not think HHS has adequately grappled with this in the RFI's framing of reimbursement modernization.⏎⏎o The proposed coverage structures do not address proprietary model opacity.⏎o There is no proposed requirement that reimbursement eligibility be conditioned on audit trail documentation meeting OIG standards.⏎o There is no mechanism to prevent reimbursement benefits from flowing to AI products that have not been validated against real-world performance benchmarks at the site of deployment.⏎o The CRADA structures described in the RFI contain no proposed conflict-of-interest standards governing what participating vendors can do with data generated through federally-subsidized partnerships.⏎⏎Tapestryhealth Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎CRADA MECHANISMS AND PROPRIETARY CAPTURE⏎⏎Maddox, Rumsfeld, and Payne (2023) examined public-private data partnerships in healthcare AI and identified a consistent pattern they described as "proprietary capture," in which nominally public partnership structures produce model assets and data derivatives that are effectively controlled by private vendors with no durable public-benefit obligations attached (npj Digital Medicine, 6(1), 44). The CRADA mechanisms described in this RFI appear to reproduce this structure without modification. HHS has not proposed any requirement that model outputs or validation datasets generated through CRADA partnerships be made available for independent audit, that participating vendors disclose the terms under which they retain rights to derivative data products, or that there be any public accountability mechanism if a CRADA-developed AI tool underperforms after deployment at scale.⏎⏎We want to be direct about what we think is happening here, because Tapestryhealth believes the compliance and governance community has an obligation to name it. Kesselheim and Avorn (2022) described a pattern in health technology policy in which the framing of a regulatory question itself constitutes a form of capture, because it defines which solutions are visible and which are not (Milbank Quarterly, 100(3), 712-738). The framing of this RFI, which takes AI acceleration as the governing objective and treats regulatory friction as the primary obstacle, makes invisible a range of policy options that would prioritize market accountability, vendor transparency, and public control of federally-subsidized data assets. Those options are not radical. They include, for example, conditioning reimbursement eligibility on public disclosure of model version history and validation status, requiring that CRADA participants submit to independent audit of model performance and data-use practices, and establishing a mandatory conflict-of-interest review process before any public-private AI data partnership is finalized. None of these appear in the RFI.⏎⏎Tapestryhealth Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎SPECIFIC REQUESTS AND CONCERNS⏎⏎Tapestryhealth urges HHS to take the following actions before finalizing any acceleration policy emerging from this RFI:⏎⏎1. Commission an independent market-structure analysis of the health AI vendor landscape, with specific attention to concentration ratios, EHR integration dependencies, and switching cost dynamics, prior to finalizing any reimbursement modernization framework. This analysis should be conducted by an entity with no financial relationship to any EHR vendor or AI technology company.⏎⏎2. Impose a moratorium on new public-private AI data partnerships, including those structured through CRADA mechanisms, until HHS has established conflict-of-interest standards governing participating vendors' rights to derivative data products and independent audit requirements for model performance and data-use practices.⏎⏎3. Condition any new reimbursement eligibility for AI-assisted clinical decision support on audit trail documentation meeting the standards set out in OIG-CPG-2023-04. Our Q4 2024 internal review found that this standard is not currently met by the majority of deployed tools across our network, which means that reimbursement modernization without this condition will primarily reward vendors whose products are already embedded in large health systems, not vendors whose products actually perform.⏎⏎Our analysis is that the comment process for this RFI has been structured in a way that gives large EHR vendors and AI incumbents a structural advantage, because they have the government relations infrastructure to submit detailed, technically sophisticated comments that shape the final regulatory text. Tapestryhealth is submitting these comments in part to put into the record that mid-sized affiliated networks with serious compliance functions do not share the premise that acceleration is the correct governing objective, and that the absence of market-structure analysis in the RFI's framing is a gap that should concern HHS as much as it concerns us.⏎⏎REFERENCES⏎⏎American Hospital Association. (2024). Survey of Health System AI Procurement: Vendor Concentration, Contract Terms, and Switching Barriers. AHA Research Series, March 2024.⏎⏎Congressional Budget Office. (2024). Federal Reimbursement Policy and Artificial Intelligence in Clinical Settings: Distributional Effects Across Provider Types. CBO Working Paper 2024-07.⏎⏎Dafny, L., & Lee, T.H. (2022). The Myth of Competitive Health IT Markets. New England Journal of Medicine, 386(14), 1289-1292.⏎⏎Federal Trade Commission. (2023). Generative AI and Health Data: Market Concentration Risks and Regulatory Gaps. FTC Policy Paper, October 2023.⏎⏎Gee, R.E., & Bhatt, D.L. (2023). Concentration and Competition in the Health Information Technology Market. Health Affairs, 42(8), 1102-1111.⏎⏎Adler-Milstein, J., & Pfeifer, E. (2022). EHR Switching Costs and Vendor Lock-In: Evidence from Hospital Transitions. Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association, 29(6), 987-996.⏎⏎Kesselheim, A.S., & Avorn, J. (2022). Regulatory Capture and the Framing of Health Technology Policy. Milbank Quarterly, 100(3), 712-738.⏎⏎Maddox, T.M., Rumsfeld, J.S., & Payne, P.R.O. (2023). Public-Private Data Partnerships in Healthcare AI: Governance Gaps and Proprietary Capture. npj Digital Medicine, 6(1), 44.⏎⏎Office of Inspector General, U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. (2023). OIG Compliance Program Guidance for Hospitals: Algorithmic Decision Tools and Audit Trail Requirements. OIG-CPG-2023-04.⏎⏎Schulman, K.A., & Richman, B.D. (2023). Who Profits from Healthcare AI? Vendor Economics and the Displacement of Clinical Value. JAMA, 329(19), 1651-1658.⏎⏎Tapestryhealth Office of Compliance and Clinical Informatics. (2024). Internal Review: AI Decision-Support Tool Audit Trail Compliance and Reimbursement Claim Denial Analysis, Q1-Q4 2024. Unpublished internal report.⏎⏎[Contact: james.adams@pathAI-tapestryhealth.org⏎Correspondence address on file with submitting organization]♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0020♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔White♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah White⏎14 Briarwood Lane⏎Cranston, RI 02920⏎⏎June 3, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information, Docket No. HHS-2025-AI-RFI⏎⏎MY DOCTOR RETIRED BECAUSE OF THE PAPERWORK⏎⏎Dr. Melinda Caruso was my family's primary care physician for eleven years out of her practice on Reservoir Avenue in Providence. She retired in 2023. She told her patients the administrative burden had become impossible. That is what I keep thinking about when I read this request for information. The government added so much documentation and billing complexity to that woman's workday that she left medicine, and now you want to talk about AI like the problem is that doctors aren't moving fast enough. The relationship she had with her patients was not inefficiency. It was the whole point. Slow down, prove this technology keeps that relationship intact first, then talk about deploying it.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎Sarah White♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0021♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret♔Anderson♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: RE: HHS comment submission -- Margaret Anderson⏎⏎My doctor's office in Bangor started using some kind of AI system to read my mammograms and nobody told me a thing about it. I heard from my neighbor Carol that the computer had flagged something, and I spent three weeks frightened before a follow-up cleared it. That's my starting point here. What worries me about all this is that it seems like the big companies selling these systems are the ones pushing hardest for this, not patients. Once a hospital buys into one of these systems they're locked in -- the company has no real reason to fix problems because the hospital can't just switch. And I genuinely don't understand how competition is supposed to help ordinary patients when only giant companies can navigate all the government billing rules in the first place. Please slow down and listen to patients before approving more of this.⏎⏎Margaret Anderson⏎Retired, Bangor Maine♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0022♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Mitchell♔♔New York♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔ON THE QUESTION OF WORKFORCE IMPACT AND PROFESSIONAL AUTONOMY⏎⏎My colleague Rosa was working a shift at our branch in the Bronx last October when she went into cardiac arrhythmia. The ER team later told her family that an AI-assisted ECG tool had flagged the irregularity seventeen minutes before any clinician caught it independently. I have spent the months since then reading whatever I can find on clinical AI implementation, and what I keep returning to is a discomfort that the literature supports but that this RFI does not adequately name: the question of what these tools will actually be used for, inside institutions that face relentless financial pressure, is not the same question as what they are capable of doing in controlled or ideal conditions.⏎⏎HHS is asking how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings. I want to respond specifically to the workforce dimension of that question, because I think the Department is at risk of accelerating a technology whose measurable patient-care benefits will be captured by administrators and vendors while the costs fall on the nurses, physicians, and care coordinators who are already working under conditions that Shanafelt et al. (2022), writing in Mayo Clinic Proceedings, documented as a clinician burnout crisis that worsened significantly between 2011 and 2020, predating the pandemic entirely. The tools being marketed to hospital systems right now are marketed as burnout-reduction solutions. Bates et al. (2021) in npj Digital Medicine did find genuine evidence that AI assistance can reduce cognitive load in emergency settings. But there is a meaningful gap between reducing cognitive load and reducing headcount, and the financial incentives facing health systems will, predictably, push toward the latter. National Nurses United documented this in their 2023 policy brief on AI and the healthcare workforce: administrators receiving AI-generated productivity metrics tend to interpret them as evidence that existing staff can absorb more patients per shift, not as evidence that staff are finally getting support. That is not a speculation about bad faith. It is a structural prediction about how cost-cutting institutions respond to efficiency data, and HHS should take it seriously as a patient safety consideration, not merely a labor one.⏎⏎The second problem, which I think is actually more serious and less discussed in this RFI's framing, is algorithmic performance monitoring of clinicians themselves. Kellogg, Valentine, and Christin (2020) in the Academy of Management Annals documented in considerable detail how algorithmic control systems in workplaces systematically erode professional autonomy and discretionary judgment, and their analysis applies directly to healthcare settings where AI tools generate real-time productivity data on how long a nurse spends with each patient, how quickly a physician closes an encounter note, how a care coordinator's caseload compares to a system-generated benchmark. Arndt et al. (2017) in the Annals of Family Medicine showed that primary care physicians were already spending more than half their working hours on EHR tasks rather than direct patient care, and that EHR event log data was being used to assess their productivity in ways that did not account for complexity. Adding AI-generated performance metrics to that environment does not reduce surveillance; it intensifies it. And there is a direct causal line between that kind of surveillance and patient harm, because a clinician who is being monitored against productivity benchmarks will, rationally, spend less time with difficult or slow patients, will document to the metric rather than to the clinical picture, and will suppress the kind of open-ended observation that catches what the algorithm missed. The seventeen minutes that mattered for Rosa's recovery depended on a clinician who was paying attention in a way that no algorithm had prompted. I genuinely worry about what happens to that kind of attention when the clinician is also being scored on throughput.⏎⏎I should note that Obermeyer et al. (2019) in Science demonstrated that AI tools trained on existing healthcare data can encode and amplify racial and socioeconomic bias in ways that are not visible in aggregate performance metrics. My Bronx community is majority Black and Latino and has higher rates of poverty than the city median. The patients who are most likely to be harmed by a surveilled, understaffed, demoralized clinical workforce are the same patients who are most likely to be harmed by biased algorithmic outputs. These are compounding risks, and the ONC's own issue brief on EHR adoption and usability acknowledged that health IT implementation has historically produced uneven benefits across patient populations.⏎⏎My specific request to HHS is this: any AI procurement in federally reimbursed settings should require, as a condition of reimbursement, three things. First, mandatory structured consultation with frontline clinical workers before any AI tool is deployed in their practice environment, with documentation that their input was considered. Second, an independent staffing-impact review conducted at six and eighteen months post-deployment, with results reported to CMS. Third, a clear regulatory prohibition on using AI-generated productivity or performance data as the primary basis for staffing ratio reductions. I recognize that the third of these will be contested by health systems on operational grounds. But without it, the first two are largely procedural gestures. The financial pressure to cut staffing will find a way to use whatever data is available to justify that cut, and "the AI said our nurses are efficient" is going to be a very hard argument to rebut in the absence of an explicit rule against it.⏎⏎I'd also note that the consultation requirement would need to specify what "frontline" means in practice, because in my reading of the literature, the people who get consulted in these processes are typically charge nurses and department heads rather than the floor nurses and♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0023♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Torres♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔Booz Allen Hamilton♔♔♔♔♔♔Booz Allen Hamilton⏎8283 Greensboro Drive⏎McLean, Virginia 22102⏎⏎Donald Torres, Director, Health IT Practice⏎dtorres@boozallen.com⏎⏎April 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information -- Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Booz Allen Hamilton Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 1⏎⏎RE: Request for Information -- Health Sector Artificial Intelligence; Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎To the Office of the Deputy Secretary and the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / ONC:⏎⏎Booz Allen Hamilton submits these comments in response to the Department's Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. Booz Allen advises federal health agencies, hospital systems, and health IT organizations across the country, including substantial work supporting interoperability initiatives under the 21st Century Cures Act and ONC's Health IT Certification Program at 45 CFR Part 170. Our health IT practice works directly with clinical operations teams who are deploying, evaluating, or declining to deploy AI-assisted tools in real patient care environments. That experience is what grounds this submission.⏎⏎Booz Allen Hamilton Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 2⏎⏎The framing of this RFI gives us genuine pause. The Department is asking what actions it can take to accelerate AI adoption, and that framing presupposes that speed is the right variable to optimize. Booz Allen's position is that it is not. The more appropriate policy question is what conditions need to be met before AI tools are deployed at scale in clinical settings, and what regulatory architecture will ensure that deployment decisions are made by accountable humans with adequate information rather than by market pressure or reimbursement incentives alone. The RFI's acceleration framing, taken at face value, risks treating the current pace of AI adoption as a problem to be solved rather than as a reasonable response to genuine uncertainty about safety, equity, and accountability.⏎⏎This is not a theoretical concern. Booz Allen's internal benchmark study, conducted across 38,000 patient cases in oncology practice networks spanning more than 500 sites, found that interoperability gaps between EHR systems and AI inference engines were the single most frequently cited barrier to full platform utilization, reported by 68% of participating sites. The same study found that algorithm-assisted patient screening for clinical trial matching reduced median time-to-enrollment by 47% compared to manual workflows. Both findings are real. But the Department should notice what they mean together: the performance gains are real, and the structural barriers are also real, and those barriers are not primarily the result of over-regulation. They are the result of fragmented technical infrastructure, inadequate data governance, and the absence of clear accountability standards for AI-assisted clinical decisions. Removing regulatory friction without first addressing those structural conditions will not produce safe, equitable AI adoption. It will produce faster deployment of tools whose failure modes are not yet well understood, into clinical environments that are not yet equipped to catch those failures.⏎⏎Booz Allen Hamilton Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 3⏎⏎PRECAUTIONARY DEPLOYMENT STANDARDS BEFORE ACCELERATION⏎⏎The Department should, as a threshold matter, establish preconditions for AI deployment in clinical care that must be demonstrated before any tool is eligible for reimbursement incentives or regulatory accommodation. Booz Allen recommends that HHS, working with ONC and CMS, develop a tiered risk classification framework for clinical AI tools that draws from but goes beyond the FDA's existing Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) guidance. The FDA's current framework, articulated in its 2021 action plan for AI/ML-based SaMD (86 Fed. Reg. 2987), addresses pre-market performance requirements but does not adequately specify post-market surveillance obligations or real-world equity monitoring requirements. That gap needs to close before the Department creates new incentives for adoption.⏎⏎Specifically, Booz Allen recommends the following preconditions for any AI tool deployed in clinical care settings with federal reimbursement support:⏎⏎1. Prospective performance validation on patient populations that are demographically representative of the intended deployment environment, with disaggregated results by race, ethnicity, sex, age, and insurance status. A tool that performs well on aggregate AUROC metrics may still produce systematically worse outputs for Medicaid patients or patients from underrepresented racial groups. Booz Allen has seen this pattern in multiple deployments, and it is not caught by standard pre-market validation protocols.⏎⏎2. Mandatory post-deployment monitoring at intervals no greater than 90 days, with automatic suspension triggers if model performance degrades beyond pre-specified thresholds. The clinical AI literature is clear that model drift is a real and recurring problem (see, e.g., Nestor et al., "Feature Engineering Improves Prediction of Hospital Mortality with Machine Learning," Critical Care Medicine, 2019), and current deployment practice does not consistently account for it.⏎⏎3. Clear documentation, available to treating clinicians at the point of care, of what the AI tool was trained on, what its known failure modes are, and under what circumstances its outputs should not be relied upon. This is not asking for something exotic. The ONC's 2024 Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability (HTI-1) final rule at 45 CFR 170.315(b)(11) already requires certain transparency disclosures for clinical decision support. Those requirements should be extended and strengthened to cover all AI tools that influence clinical decisions, not just those that meet the current CDS definition.⏎⏎4. Explicit assignment of clinical accountability to a licensed human clinician for any AI-assisted decision that affects patient diagnosis, treatment, or care pathway. The Department should be direct about this: AI tools do not make decisions. Clinicians make decisions. The regulatory architecture should make that accountability visible and enforceable, not obscure it through language about "AI-assisted" workflows that diffuses responsibility without locating it anywhere specific.⏎⏎Booz Allen Hamilton Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 4⏎⏎THE REIMBURSEMENT QUESTION IS WHERE THIS GETS COMPLICATED⏎⏎Booz Allen wants to be honest that the reimbursement question is harder than the RFI's framing suggests, and we are not certain that any single reimbursement model gets this right. CMS has begun exploring new payment pathways for AI-assisted services, and there is genuine tension between two legitimate concerns. On one side, the absence of reimbursement pathways is a real barrier to deployment of tools that have demonstrated safety and effectiveness. Clinicians and health systems cannot sustain AI infrastructure at scale without payment support, and that is a fair point. On the other side, creating reimbursement incentives before precautionary standards are in place will accelerate adoption for financial reasons rather than clinical ones, and the history of health IT incentive programs -- including the HITECH Act's Meaningful Use program, which drove EHR adoption at a pace that outran usability and interoperability standards -- should give the Department pause.⏎⏎Booz Allen Hamilton Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 5⏎⏎Booz Allen's recommendation is that CMS delay the creation of AI-specific reimbursement codes or add-on payments under the Medicare Physician Fee Schedule until the precautionary deployment standards described above are in place and operational. The sequencing matters. If reimbursement incentives arrive first, market dynamics will drive adoption regardless of whether the safety and accountability infrastructure exists. The Department has seen this movie before. The Office of Inspector General's 2023 report on EHR-related fraud (OIG Report OEI-01-20-00510) documented how incentive-driven adoption created compliance vulnerabilities that are still being worked through years later. The AI context is different in some respects but the underlying dynamic -- incentives driving adoption faster than governance can keep pace -- is the same.⏎⏎There is a separate concern about AI in insurance and prior authorization decisions that Booz Allen believes the Department should address directly and separately from the clinical care context. The use of AI tools by payers to automate prior authorization denials has been documented extensively (see, e.g., the Senate Finance Committee's June 2023 report on prior authorization in Medicare Advantage), and it raises accountability questions that are categorically different from AI tools that support clinician decision-making. A physician using an AI screening tool to identify clinical trial candidates retains clinical judgment and can override or disregard the AI's output. A beneficiary whose prior authorization request is denied by an automated system has no equivalent check unless the appeals process is functional and accessible. Booz Allen urges HHS to treat these two contexts as requiring distinct regulatory frameworks, and to resist any tendency to allow the positive use cases in clinical care to provide cover for problematic uses in coverage and payment administration.⏎⏎Booz Allen Hamilton Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 6⏎⏎INTEROPERABILITY AS A PREREQUISITE, NOT AN AFTERTHOUGHT⏎⏎The 68% figure cited above -- the share of oncology sites in Booz Allen's benchmark study that identified EHR-to-AI-inference-engine interoperability gaps as the primary barrier to platform utilization -- points to something the Department needs to take seriously before it designs any acceleration program. AI tools do not operate in isolation. They depend on structured, timely, accurate data feeds from EHR systems, lab systems, imaging systems, and increasingly from patient-generated data sources. When those feeds are incomplete, delayed, or formatted inconsistently, AI outputs degrade in ways that are not always visible to the clinician relying on them. A clinical decision support tool trained on clean, complete data from an academic medical center will not perform the same way when deployed against the fragmented, variable-quality data environments that characterize community hospitals and independent practices.⏎⏎The Department's existing interoperability authorities under the 21st Century Cures Act, including ONC's API requirements at 45 CFR 170.315(g)(10) and the information blocking provisions at 45 CFR 171, are the right foundation, but they were not designed with AI data dependencies in mind. Booz Allen recommends that ONC initiate a focused rulemaking or technical standards process to define minimum data quality and completeness standards for EHR data used as inputs to clinical AI tools. This is not a small undertaking, and we are not suggesting it can be done quickly. But it needs to happen before the Department creates incentives for AI adoption at scale, or the result will be widespread deployment of tools operating on data inputs that do not meet the quality thresholds those tools were validated against. That is a patient safety problem, and it is one that will not show up immediately in outcomes data because the failure modes are often silent.⏎⏎Booz Allen Hamilton Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 7⏎⏎The TEFCA framework, while still maturing, offers one potential mechanism for establishing shared data quality expectations across participating networks. ONC should consider whether TEFCA participation requirements can be used to establish AI-readiness standards for data exchange, including minimum field completion rates, latency requirements for near-real-time AI inference use cases, and standardized approaches to handling data provenance and audit trails. Booz Allen has worked with several TEFCA Qualified Health Information Networks and the variation in data quality across those networks is substantial -- far more substantial than the current framework's governance requirements address. Without some floor on data quality, acceleration of AI adoption will produce adoption of unreliable AI, and the clinical and liability consequences of that will fall on patients and clinicians rather than on the vendors whose tools are performing below their validated specifications.⏎⏎Booz Allen Hamilton Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 8⏎⏎HUMAN RESPONSIBILITY AT EVERY DECISION POINT⏎⏎Booz Allen wants to close on the accountability question because it is the one that the Department's RFI addresses least directly and that Booz Allen believes is most important. The question of who is responsible when an AI-assisted clinical decision goes wrong is not yet settled in federal regulation, in state licensing law, or in the clinical liability framework. That ambiguity is not a neutral condition. It creates an environment in which AI tools can be deployed in ways that diffuse clinical accountability without providing any mechanism for patients or regulators to identify where a decision was made and by whom.⏎⏎The Department should issue clear guidance -- and eventually pursue rulemaking -- establishing that the use of an AI tool in any clinical decision does not transfer, share, or reduce the accountability of the licensed clinician who makes that decision. This should be stated plainly and without the hedging language that tends to appear in federal AI guidance documents. The existing literature on automation bias (see Parasuraman & Manzey, "Complacency and Bias in Human Use of Automation," Human Factors, 2010) is clear that clinicians under time pressure will frequently defer to AI outputs without adequately evaluating them, particularly when the AI output is presented with high confidence scores. The regulatory framework should account for that behavioral reality, not assume it away by stating that humans remain "in the loop."⏎⏎Booz Allen Hamilton Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 9⏎⏎Concretely, Booz Allen recommends:⏎⏎o Mandatory disclosure to patients when an AI tool has been used in their diagnosis or treatment recommendation, including what the tool recommended and whether the treating clinician agreed with or deviated from that recommendation. Patients have a right to know when an algorithm has influenced their care.⏎⏎o Prohibition on AI-only clinical decisions without human review in any setting receiving federal reimbursement. This should be a bright-line rule, not a default that can be contracted around.⏎⏎o Requirements that AI tool vendors maintain explainability documentation sufficient for a treating clinician to understand, in plain terms, why the tool produced a given output for a given patient. Black-box outputs in clinical settings are not acceptable when the stakes include patient safety. The FDA's discussion of transparency in its 2023 draft guidance on predetermined change control plans (88 Fed. Reg. 40436) gestures toward this but does not go far enough for high-stakes clinical applications.⏎⏎o Clear audit trail requirements for any AI-assisted clinical decision, including the data inputs the tool relied on, the version of the model in use at the time, and the clinician's documented response to the AI output. These audit trails should be retained in the patient's medical record and accessible for quality review and regulatory inspection.⏎⏎Booz Allen Hamilton Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 10⏎⏎The Department should also be attentive to workforce implications that the RFI does not fully address. Clinical AI tools are frequently marketed as efficiency solutions, and there is pressure in some health system environments to use AI adoption as a rationale for reducing clinical staffing. Booz Allen is concerned that reimbursement models that reward AI utilization without requiring minimum human review standards could accelerate that dynamic. The appropriate use of AI in clinical care is to support clinician judgment, not to substitute for clinical staffing. HHS should make that principle explicit in any policy guidance it issues on clinical AI, and CMS should consider whether staffing standard requirements under the Medicare Conditions of Participation need to be updated to address AI-related substitution risks.⏎⏎One more thing worth flagging, and this is admittedly a somewhat different point from the above: the Department's RFI focuses on clinical care settings, but many of the AI tools being deployed in health systems operate across clinical and administrative functions simultaneously. A tool that schedules appointments, flags care gaps, and generates prior authorization requests may touch clinical decisions even if it is classified administratively. The regulatory framework should account for the actual function of AI tools, not just their nominal classification, or vendors will have strong incentives to classify tools in whatever category carries the lightest regulatory burden. ONC's existing approach to CDS classification under 45 CFR 170.315(b)(11) has already encountered this problem, and the AI context will make it significantly more acute.⏎⏎Booz Allen Hamilton Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 11⏎⏎Booz Allen appreciates the opportunity to provide these comments and is available to discuss any of the issues raised here in greater detail. Questions or requests for follow-up may be directed to Donald Torres, Director, Health IT Practice, at the contact information on the cover page of this submission.⏎⏎dtorres@boozallen.com⏎(703) 902-5000 (main)⏎⏎Booz Allen Hamilton⏎8283 Greensboro Drive⏎McLean, Virginia 22102♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0024♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Wilson♔♔California♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted to HHS Docket on AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎WHAT AI GOT RIGHT, AND WHERE I WORRY WE MISREAD THE LESSON⏎⏎My orthopedic surgeon at Scripps Mercy told me, almost as an aside, that an AI imaging tool had caught a hairline clavicle fracture my initial X-ray read had missed. I was 78, post-op from a rotator cuff repair, and that fracture left alone could have turned into something genuinely dangerous. I walked out of that clinic feeling the way I do when a good inspector catches a compromised beam before the pour — quiet relief, unglamorous, consequential. I've spent five decades in construction and I know what redundancy is worth. That tool worked exactly like a safety check should: it backed up a human call, flagged something specific and narrow, and a physician made the final judgment. That's the right architecture. What concerns me is whether HHS is building toward that model or toward something structurally different, where the AI carries the load and the clinician just watches the gauge.⏎⏎THE PART THAT KEEPS ME UP⏎⏎Medicine isn't a data-processing problem with a human interface bolted on. Patients arrive scared, inarticulate, sometimes unable to name what's wrong. A good clinician reads body language, family dynamics, the way somebody hesitates before answering. Kelley et al. (2014, PLOS ONE 9:e94207) showed in a systematic review that the patient-clinician relationship itself produces measurable health outcomes independent of whatever treatment gets prescribed. Levinson et al. (1997, JAMA 277:553) found that the quality of physician communication predicted malpractice claims better than technical error rates, which tells you something about what patients actually experience as harm. Bickmore and Picard (2005, ACM Trans. Computer-Human Interaction 12:293) demonstrated that even well-designed computer systems can simulate relational cues but cannot sustain the kind of trust that changes health behavior over time. When you interpose an AI system between a clinician and that relational work, you don't just change the workflow — you erode something that was doing clinical work you weren't counting. And there's a second problem I think about a lot: automation bias. Goddard et al. (2012, JAMIA 19:121) and Skitka, Mosier, and Burdick (1999, Int'l J. Human-Computer Studies 51:991) both documented how reliably people defer to automated outputs even when those outputs are wrong, especially under time pressure. A clinician who spends years deferring to a diagnostic algorithm is not the same clinician who would have developed independent pattern recognition without it. Topol (2019, Nature Medicine 25:44) is probably the most prominent AI advocate in medicine and even he acknowledged the technology works best as an adjunct to human judgment, not a replacement for it. In construction we have a saying: you don't build on an untested foundation just because the contractor is eager to break ground. HHS should require demonstrated safety data and preserved human decision authority before any broad clinical deployment, not after. The fracture my surgeon caught — that's what AI should be doing. I'm asking you not to let the industry reframe that modest, real success as permission to hand over the whole structure.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0025♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Lee♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔Society for Women's Health Research♔♔♔♔♔♔Society for Women's Health Research⏎Comments Submitted to HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎RE: Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Federal Docket Submission — [Docket ID pending confirmation]⏎⏎⏎SWHR Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎⏎EQUITY AUDITING AS A PRECONDITION, NOT AN AFTERTHOUGHT⏎⏎The Society for Women's Health Research (SWHR) submits these comments in response to HHS's request for input on regulatory and reimbursement actions to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care settings. SWHR recognizes the genuine potential of AI-assisted clinical decision support to improve diagnostic accuracy and reduce administrative burden. That said, the RFI as drafted contains a structural omission that, if left unaddressed, will systematically worsen health outcomes for the populations our organization exists to serve. The document solicits input on acceleration mechanisms — reimbursement pathways, R&D incentives, regulatory flexibility — without establishing any mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing or post-deployment bias surveillance requirements as threshold conditions for HHS endorsement or federal reimbursement. This is not a minor gap. It is the organizing problem around which every other policy recommendation in this comment is framed.⏎⏎The published evidence on algorithmic bias in clinical settings is now sufficiently developed that continued inaction constitutes a policy choice, not a knowledge deficit. Obermeyer et al. (2019) documented that a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to manage population health systematically underestimated illness severity in Black patients by using healthcare cost as a proxy for health need — a design choice that encoded historical access disparities directly into the model's outputs, reducing the proportion of Black patients identified for high-risk care management programs by more than half. Sjoding et al. (2020) demonstrated that pulse oximeters — a device so normalized in clinical settings that its bias was invisible to practitioners for decades — produced occult hypoxemia readings in Black patients at nearly three times the rate observed in white patients, a failure that almost certainly contributed to preventable deterioration during COVID-19 hospitalization surges. These are not edge cases. Gianfrancesco et al. (2018) identified multiple categories of structural bias in EHR-derived training data, including differential documentation rates, socioeconomic confounding, and missing data patterns that disproportionately affect Medicaid populations and patients receiving care in safety-net institutions. Seyyed-Kalantari et al. (2021) found that AI algorithms applied to chest radiographs in under-served populations systematically underdiagnosed pathology relative to performance benchmarks derived from academic medical center datasets. Adamson and Smith (2018) raised parallel concerns about dermatology AI trained predominantly on lighter skin tones, with predictable implications for diagnostic accuracy in patients of color. Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones (2020) documented how race-correction adjustments embedded in legacy clinical algorithms — including kidney function estimators and spirometry reference equations — carry discriminatory assumptions forward into AI systems that incorporate those outputs as input features. Char, Shah, and Magnus (2018) articulated the ethical architecture of this problem clearly: AI systems do not generate bias independently; they inherit and scale the biases present in the data and institutional structures from which they were trained.⏎⏎⏎SWHR Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE COMPOUNDING PROBLEM OF AUTOMATION BIAS IN COMMUNITY SETTINGS⏎⏎What makes the acceleration framing in this RFI particularly concerning is the combination of two effects that, taken together, produce harm that is worse than either produces alone. First, Cabitza, Rasoini, and Gensini (2017) documented the phenomenon of automation bias in clinical settings — the well-characterized tendency of clinicians to defer to algorithmic outputs even when those outputs conflict with clinical observation, particularly when the AI system presents its recommendations with high apparent confidence. Sendak et al. (2020) observed in a qualitative study of deep learning deployment at Duke University Health System that clinicians frequently described AI outputs as authoritative in ways that suppressed independent diagnostic reasoning. Second — and this is the part the RFI does not adequately grapple with — model validation studies are overwhelmingly conducted in large, well-resourced academic medical centers. Wong et al. (2021) conducted external validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model and found that performance degraded substantially when the model was applied outside the institution where it was developed, with concerning false-alert patterns that consumed nursing time without improving outcomes. The Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology's 2023 Algorithmic Bias Issue Brief explicitly flagged the gap between validation-site performance and real-world performance in community hospitals and rural facilities as a priority concern. What this means in practice: clinicians in under-resourced settings, where marginalized patients disproportionately receive care, are most likely to encounter AI systems performing below their published benchmarks, and are simultaneously most subject to automation bias effects that suppress the clinical skepticism that might otherwise catch those errors. Price, Gerke, and Cohen (2019) noted that liability frameworks for AI-assisted decision-making remain unsettled, which further complicates the institutional incentives for error reporting in these settings.⏎⏎SWHR therefore requests that HHS condition any federal reimbursement or regulatory endorsement of clinical AI systems on the following minimum requirements:⏎⏎1. Mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing, with stratified performance reporting disaggregated by race, ethnicity, sex, socioeconomic status, and geographic setting (rural/urban/safety-net), conducted by an independent third party rather than the deploying institution or vendor.⏎⏎2. Ongoing post-deployment bias surveillance protocols, with defined reporting thresholds and a federal repository for aggregated findings, modeled on the adverse event reporting infrastructure that governs medical devices under 21 CFR Part 803.⏎⏎3. Requirement that validation datasets for any HHS-reimbursed AI tool include a statistically adequate representation of Medicaid populations and patients from federally qualified health centers, not only from academic medical center cohorts.⏎⏎HHS has the regulatory authority and the evidentiary basis to act on these requirements now. The question of whether AI can accelerate care delivery has a clear answer. The question of whether HHS will establish the accountability architecture before deployment scales is the one this RFI should be answering, and the current draft does not.⏎⏎SWHR notes that the reimbursement coding inconsistencies documented in the 2024 Texas Medical Association survey — 38 distinct denial scenarios across four health systems — likely fall disproportionately on practices serving lower-income and rural patients, which further compounds♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0026♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Nelson♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔Duke Clinical Research Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔RE: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket Submitted by: Nancy Nelson, Postdoctoral Fellow, Duke Clinical Research Institute⏎Date of Submission: March 2025⏎⏎⏎THE FRAMING PROBLEM⏎⏎The 2024 CHIME survey of 610 health system CIOs and senior IT executives deserves to be taken seriously. The finding that 67% of respondents identified the absence of clear HHS guidance on AI validation and post-deployment monitoring as the single greatest barrier to scaling clinical AI tools is a meaningful data point, and the documented average delay of 14 months in procurement decisions is a genuine institutional burden. These are not trivial concerns. Health systems serving high-proportion Medicare and Medicaid populations face real resource constraints, and the CHIME AI Task Force policy brief's documentation of interoperability failures around model cards and FHIR-based performance metadata reflects genuine technical friction (CHIME AI Task Force, 2024).⏎⏎But the inference HHS appears to draw from this evidence — that regulatory uncertainty is a problem to be dissolved rather than, in part, a democratic signal to be respected — is the precise point this comment contests. The RFI's stated goal of reducing "uncertainty that impedes AI innovation" reframes the deliberative function of regulatory scrutiny as an obstacle. That reframing is not neutral. It is a political choice about which values governance should serve, and it is a choice that HHS does not have the unilateral democratic mandate to make on behalf of the public.⏎⏎The remainder of this comment develops two arguments. First, that the embedding of AI systems in clinical infrastructure constitutes a form of institutional lock-in that forecloses future democratic choices in ways that require explicit democratic authorization before any acceleration posture is adopted. Second, that the opacity of proprietary clinical AI systems makes meaningful democratic oversight structurally impossible under current frameworks, concentrating consequential decision-making power in corporate entities with no accountability to local communities or patients. This comment does not address clinical safety or algorithmic bias in depth — those dimensions are addressed in other submissions — but focuses strictly on questions of governance, reversibility, and democratic legitimacy.⏎⏎⏎THE LOCK-IN PROBLEM IS NOT METAPHORICAL⏎⏎When health economists write about lock-in dynamics in healthcare markets, they typically mean contractual dependencies or switching costs (Dafny & Lee, 2016). Those mechanisms are real, but the lock-in problem with clinical AI is considerably more severe, and the mechanisms are worth naming with some precision.⏎⏎The first mechanism is the atrophy of institutional knowledge. When clinical workflows are reorganized around AI-generated outputs — whether risk stratification scores, triage recommendations, or documentation assistance — the tacit expertise that previously resided in clinical staff begins to erode. This is not speculative; it is a well-documented feature of automation dependency in complex sociotechnical systems. Coiera (2019) described this dynamic as "the last mile" problem: the practical knowledge required to function independently of an AI system degrades as clinicians habituate to deferring to it. Within a period of years, not decades, the institutional capacity to reverse a deployment decision atrophies even when documented harms accumulate. The decision to adopt, in other words, is not symmetrical with the decision to unadopt.⏎⏎The second mechanism is staffing reorganization. Health systems that deploy AI-assisted clinical decision support tools do not simply add a tool to an existing workflow; they restructure labor around that tool's outputs. Roles are redefined, supervision models shift, and in some cases, staffing ratios are recalibrated on the assumption that AI augmentation reduces the need for certain categories of human judgment (Topol, 2019; Shortliffe & Sepúlveda, 2018). Once those staffing changes are institutionalized — embedded in job descriptions, budgets, and collective bargaining agreements — the human redundancy required to safely disengage from the AI system may no longer exist.⏎⏎The third mechanism is contractual dependency. Proprietary AI systems for clinical use are typically licensed under multi-year enterprise agreements with significant switching costs and, in many cases, data-sharing provisions that make the health system's own patient data partially inaccessible or operationally entangled with the vendor's model improvement pipeline. The practical freedom to exit an AI deployment is therefore constrained not only by internal knowledge atrophy and staffing reorganization but by the legal architecture of the contracts governing the deployment.⏎⏎Taken together, these mechanisms mean that the decision to deploy a proprietary clinical AI system at scale is not a reversible experiment. It is, in a meaningful sense, a permanent structural commitment — or at minimum, one that becomes practically irreversible within a window far shorter than the democratic and regulatory learning cycles that would ordinarily govern major changes to medical practice. HHS's accelerationist framing, which treats deployment as the goal and oversight as friction, is not merely premature. It is structurally anti-democratic, because it forecloses future democratic choices before the public has had a meaningful opportunity to make them.⏎⏎⏎DEMOCRATIC OPTION VALUE AND THE PRECAUTIONARY DEMOCRATIC PRINCIPLE⏎⏎Democratic theory has long recognized that the foreclosure of future choices is itself a political act requiring authorization. The concept of option value — borrowed from financial theory but applied to democratic choice by theorists including Sheila Jasanoff — holds that preserving the capacity to make different choices in the future has intrinsic value, and that actions which irreversibly foreclose options impose a cost on democratic self-governance that cannot be recovered through subsequent deliberation (Jasanoff, 2016). This is not an argument against technological change. It is an argument that the pace and architecture of technological embedding in public institutions must be subject to democratic authorization, not merely administrative discretion.⏎⏎HHS is a federal agency operating under delegated statutory authority. That authority does not include a mandate to determine, on behalf of the American public, that medicine should be reorganized around proprietary AI infrastructure before adequate democratic deliberation has occurred. The RFI itself, while a welcome gesture toward public input, is not a substitute for that deliberation. Soliciting comments from industry stakeholders and researchers through a federal docket is a far cry from the kind of structured, inclusive, and adequately resourced democratic deliberation that a decision of this magnitude warrants. The population that would bear the consequences of accelerated AI deployment in clinical care — patients in rural communities, elderly Medicare beneficiaries, Medicaid-enrolled populations with limited ability to exit to alternative care settings — is not meaningfully represented in a CHIME survey of 610 health system CIOs.⏎⏎What this comment proposes is the adoption of something that might be called a precautionary democratic principle for clinical AI governance: the recognition that where a technology's deployment involves irreversible institutional change, where the mechanisms of that irreversibility operate faster than democratic learning cycles, and where the affected populations lack meaningful exit options, the burden of justification rests with those who would accelerate deployment, not those who would require additional deliberation. This principle is not novel. Versions of it appear in Mazzucato and Entsminger's (2021) analysis of public-option models in digital health governance, in Pasquale's (2015) account of accountability deficits in algorithmic systems, and in Jasanoff's broader work on the ethics of technological governance. What is novel is its application to a federal agency's explicit framing of regulatory oversight as an obstacle to innovation.⏎⏎⏎THE OPACITY PROBLEM AND DEMOCRATIC ACCOUNTABILITY⏎⏎A second structural concern warrants attention, though this comment treats it more briefly. The opacity of proprietary clinical AI systems is not merely a technical limitation amenable to better documentation requirements. It is a condition that makes democratic oversight structurally impossible under current frameworks.⏎⏎Pasquale (2015) argued that algorithmic systems whose internal logic cannot be audited concentrate decision-making power in ways that are qualitatively different from other forms of institutional power, because the opacity is not incidental but is actively maintained as a condition of competitive advantage. Clinical AI systems deployed under proprietary licensing arrangements are, in general, not auditable by the regulators who would nominally oversee them, the clinicians who deploy them, or the patients whose care they affect. Selbst and Barocas (2018) have written carefully about why the intuitive appeal of explainability requirements does not resolve this problem — explanation and auditability are not the same thing, and the former can be provided in ways that satisfy regulatory form while preserving substantive opacity. The Doshi-Velez et al. (2017) working paper on accountability of AI under law reached similar conclusions about the gap between legal requirements for explanation and genuine accountability.⏎⏎The consequence for democratic governance is not merely that individual patients cannot understand the systems affecting their care. It is that the institutions nominally responsible for democratic oversight — HHS, ONC, state health departments — lack the technical access to evaluate whether deployed systems are performing as represented, let alone whether the aggregate effects of widespread deployment are consistent with democratically established values. Obermeyer et al. (2019) documented this problem empirically in the context of a widely deployed population health algorithm, demonstrating that systematic racial bias had persisted undetected for years in a commercially deployed system. The detection required independent academic research, not regulatory oversight. That is a governance failure, not a technical one, and it illustrates why the opacity problem is inseparable from the democratic accountability problem.⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC CONCERNS REGARDING THE RFI'S REIMBURSEMENT QUESTIONS⏎⏎This comment focuses primarily on the RFI's questions regarding the regulatory and governance actions HHS might take to accelerate adoption, but one additional concern regarding reimbursement policy warrants direct statement.⏎⏎The RFI solicits input on reimbursement mechanisms that could reduce financial barriers to AI adoption. The concern here is straightforward:⏎⏎1. Reimbursement incentives are among the most powerful instruments available to HHS for shaping health system behavior. They operate at scale, they are difficult to retract once institutionalized, and they function as structural subsidies that accelerate deployment independent of whether adequate oversight frameworks exist.⏎⏎2. The deployment of reimbursement incentives for clinical AI tools before post-deployment monitoring standards, model card requirements, and audit mechanisms are in place would accelerate precisely the lock-in dynamics described above, while simultaneously reducing the financial incentive for health systems to invest in the institutional knowledge and staffing redundancy required to safely reverse those deployments.⏎⏎3. The populations most affected by premature deployment — Medicare and Medicaid beneficiaries, rural patients, patients in safety-net institutions — are also the populations least likely to have exit options if deployed systems perform poorly.⏎⏎The CHIME survey documents that procurement delays are real and that legal uncertainty imposes costs. But the appropriate response to that uncertainty is not to reduce it by providing regulatory clarity in advance of adequate deliberation. It is to acknowledge that the uncertainty reflects the genuine unresolved state of democratic and scientific consensus about how clinical AI should be governed.⏎⏎⏎RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎This comment makes two recommendations, both of which are intentionally modest relative to the scope of the issues raised.⏎⏎First, HHS should commission an independent democratic deliberation process — structured along the lines of a citizens' assembly or deliberative mini-public, with adequate representation of patient populations, frontline clinicians, and community health advocates — before adopting any acceleration posture with respect to clinical AI. This is not a recommendation against eventual adoption. It is a recommendation that the decision about what pace and architecture of adoption is consistent with democratic values should be made through a process that is itself democratically legitimate. The RFI process does not meet that standard.⏎⏎Second, HHS should impose an interim moratorium on the development and deployment of reimbursement incentives designed to accelerate clinical AI adoption until post-deployment monitoring standards, model transparency requirements, and independent audit mechanisms are in place. The CHIME AI Task Force (2024) documented that interoperability constraints around model cards and performance metadata are already compounding procurement delays at institutions serving high-proportion Medicare and Medicaid populations. That documentation is an argument for addressing the interoperability infrastructure problem first — not for deploying reimbursement incentives that would accelerate adoption before the infrastructure exists to monitor what is being adopted.⏎⏎Neither of these recommendations is anti-technology. The research literature on clinical AI includes compelling evidence that well-governed deployment of AI tools can improve diagnostic accuracy and reduce clinician burden in specific, well-characterized contexts (Keane & Topol, 2018). The argument here is not that those benefits are illusory. It is that the governance architecture required to realize them equitably and reversibly does not yet exist, and that HHS's current framing — which treats the absence of that architecture as a reason to move faster, not a reason to build it first — has the causal logic inverted.⏎⏎The 14-month procurement delay documented in the CHIME survey is a genuine cost. But it is worth asking who bears that cost relative to the cost of accelerated lock-in in systems that cannot be audited, cannot be reversed without atrophying institutional knowledge, and whose performance in the specific populations served by safety-net institutions remains largely⏎⏎Duke Clinical Research Institute | HHS AI RFI Submission | March 2025♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0027♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Martinez♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My granddaughter sat in an urgent care waiting room in Winston-Salem for four hours last month with a 103 degree fever because some computer program rated her "low priority." I spent 34 years nursing at Wake Forest Baptist and I can tell you no algorithm knows what a sick person looks like the way a person does. But honestly what scares me more than anything right now is what the insurance companies are already doing with AI, they are using it to deny claims automatically at a massive scale and the denials are nearly impossible to fight, especially if you are 70 years old and don't have a lawyer or the energy to keep calling. HHS pushing to speed this up without putting protections in place first is going to hurt the people who can least afford it. Please slow down and fix that part before anything else.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0028♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Clark♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Sandra ended up at Banner Health in Mesa two winters back, and what I watched happen there still bothers me, a medication dosage mix-up that added four extra days to her stay because nobody actually reviewed an alert that sat in the computer system. I get that technology can help. I taught it for fifteen years. But HHS is framing this whole AI push like it's for patients, when the fastest-growing use I can see is insurance companies running automated denials on prior authorization requests with no human ever looking at the case. When that denial comes, there's nobody to call. The appeals process is built to exhaust you until you quit. HHS needs to require real human review of any AI-generated insurance decision, and patients need an actual right to appeal to a person.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0029♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔14♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Rivera♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔Premier Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔Premier Inc. Comments on HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-RFI | 89 FR 22456⏎Submitted by: Jessica Rivera, Senior Vice President, Policy and Regulatory Affairs⏎Premier Inc., Charlotte, North Carolina⏎Date: April 2024⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 1⏎⏎INTRODUCTION AND ORGANIZATIONAL GROUNDING⏎⏎Premier Inc. is a healthcare improvement company serving approximately 4,400 U.S. hospitals and health systems and more than 225,000 other provider organizations. Our organization's benchmarking and analytics infrastructure processes data from a substantial share of the U.S. healthcare market, and our policy engagement on health information technology spans more than a decade of active participation in ONC rulemaking, CMS payment reform, and interoperability standards development. We submit these comments in response to the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and ONC's Health Sector AI Request for Information (89 FR 22456) with genuine respect for the Department's intent, but also with significant concern about the structural assumptions embedded in the RFI's framing.⏎⏎Our concern is not with the goal of accelerating beneficial AI adoption in clinical settings. Our concern is with the sequence and conditions under which acceleration is being proposed. The RFI as published is organized primarily around removing friction — regulatory, reimbursement-related, and administrative — from the AI deployment pathway. That framing assumes that friction is the primary obstacle to good outcomes. Our organization's benchmarking work, conducted across more than 1,200 healthcare facilities in collaboration with interoperability platform vendors, tells a different story. According to a 2024 InterSystems Corporation benchmarking report drawing on over 800 million patient record transactions annually, the leading cause of AI deployment stalls was not regulatory ambiguity or reimbursement uncertainty — it was data normalization failures, cited in 67% of deployment interruptions (InterSystems Corporation, 2024). That same study found that facilities with structured AI-readiness frameworks reduced integration timelines by 34% compared to facilities using ad hoc approaches. The implication is that the bottleneck is internal to health systems and is largely a data infrastructure problem, not a regulatory problem. HHS's acceleration agenda, as framed, does not address this. What it does address is clearing the path for vendor deployment — which is a different problem entirely, and one that benefits a different set of interests.⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 2⏎⏎THE POLITICAL ECONOMY OF HEALTHCARE AI: WHAT THE RFI DOES NOT ASK⏎⏎The RFI solicits comment on regulatory actions, reimbursement modernization, and research-and-development investments HHS can make to accelerate AI adoption. What it does not ask — and what we think is the more consequential policy question — is who captures the value when AI is deployed at scale in clinical settings, and whether the market structure currently governing healthcare AI is capable of producing the outcomes HHS says it wants. We think HHS should be asking those questions before it commits to an acceleration agenda, and we are genuinely puzzled that they are absent from the RFI entirely.⏎⏎The healthcare AI market is structurally concentrated in ways that create serious alignment problems. A small number of large EHR vendors control the data environments through which most clinical AI tools must be deployed. Epic Systems alone serves approximately 38% of U.S. hospital beds. When AI tools require EHR integration to function — which most clinical decision support applications do — the EHR vendor effectively controls access to the deployment channel. Gans and Stern (2023) describe this dynamic as "platform envelopment," a process through which incumbent technology platforms extend their control into adjacent markets by leveraging their existing data and integration relationships (Gans & Stern, 2023, p. 104). In healthcare AI, platform envelopment means that the vendors best positioned to benefit from HHS's acceleration agenda are not necessarily the vendors with the best clinical products — they are the vendors with the deepest EHR integration relationships. That is a market structure problem, and reducing regulatory friction without addressing it will accelerate the wrong things.⏎⏎Dafny and Lee (2022) have documented the broader consequences of healthcare market concentration, including reduced quality, higher prices, and diminished responsiveness to patient and payer preferences (Dafny & Lee, 2022, p. 1331). The dynamics they describe in hospital and insurer markets are replicating themselves in health IT. The Federal Trade Commission's 2023 staff report on generative AI and the healthcare sector noted that incumbent EHR vendors and large technology companies have structural advantages in clinical AI markets that are likely to produce anti-competitive outcomes absent deliberate regulatory intervention (Federal Trade Commission, 2023, pp. 14–17). The FTC's analysis was produced independently of this RFI process, which makes its absence from the RFI's framing more notable, not less.⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 3⏎⏎VENDOR LOCK-IN AND THE LIMITS OF INTEROPERABILITY MANDATES⏎⏎The RFI references interoperability as a mechanism for promoting AI adoption, and Premier Inc. strongly supports interoperability as a policy goal. But interoperability mandates, as currently structured, do not resolve the vendor lock-in problem in clinical AI — they may actually make it worse by creating a veneer of openness that legitimizes proprietary ecosystems without meaningfully constraining them. Adler-Milstein and Pfeifer (2023) examined the practical effects of ONC's interoperability rules on EHR market competition and found that while FHIR-based APIs expanded data portability in limited technical respects, they did not reduce switching costs or competitive dependency on incumbent vendors (Adler-Milstein & Pfeifer, 2023, p. 815). The study concluded that interoperability mandates, absent structural requirements around data portability, contract terms, and API pricing, function primarily as compliance exercises rather than as market-opening interventions.⏎⏎This matters enormously for clinical AI because the integration dependencies that create lock-in are not primarily at the data access layer — they are at the workflow layer. When a health system deploys an AI-assisted clinical decision support tool that is embedded in its EHR's native interface, the switching cost is not just the cost of moving data. It is the cost of retraining clinical staff, rebuilding workflow integrations, renegotiating contracts, and absorbing the productivity loss during transition. These costs are prohibitive for most health systems, and they insulate underperforming products from competitive pressure in ways that have no analog in markets with lower switching costs. A clinical AI product that produces suboptimal recommendations but is deeply embedded in a health system's EHR workflow will persist not because it is performing well, but because the cost of replacing it is too high. HHS's acceleration agenda, by encouraging rapid deployment before adequate performance standards exist, will accelerate the accumulation of these integration dependencies — and the lock-in they create will outlast whatever regulatory framework HHS eventually puts in place to govern AI performance.⏎⏎Our organization has observed this dynamic directly in our network. Health systems that deployed early-generation sepsis prediction tools from major EHR vendors in 2019 and 2020 are still operating those tools despite mounting evidence that their performance in diverse patient populations is substantially below what was claimed at deployment. The GAO examined this problem in its 2023 report on AI in health care, noting that the lack of post-market performance monitoring requirements for clinical AI tools means that underperforming products remain in use long after their limitations become apparent (Government Accountability Office, 2023, pp. 28–31). Accelerating deployment without post-market surveillance requirements does not solve this problem; it scales it.⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 4⏎⏎THE REIMBURSEMENT QUESTION AND WHO ACTUALLY BENEFITS⏎⏎The RFI asks specifically about reimbursement modernization as a mechanism for accelerating AI adoption. We want to engage with this question carefully, because we think the framing of reimbursement reform in the AI context obscures a significant value transfer that HHS has not adequately analyzed.⏎⏎The standard argument for AI reimbursement reform goes roughly as follows: health systems will not invest in AI deployment if they cannot recover the costs through reimbursement, therefore CMS should develop payment mechanisms that recognize AI-assisted clinical services. This argument has surface plausibility, but it conflates the interests of health systems with the interests of AI vendors in ways that are not analytically justified. When a health system deploys a clinical AI tool, the primary financial beneficiary of reimbursement is typically not the health system — it is the vendor, through licensing fees and implementation contracts that are paid regardless of whether the tool improves outcomes. A health system that receives a new AI-assisted billing code from CMS does not pocket that revenue; it uses it, in part, to fund the licensing costs of the AI product that justified the billing code. The net financial effect on health systems is ambiguous at best. The net financial effect on vendors is straightforwardly positive. This is not a hypothetical concern. The National Academy for State Health Policy's 2024 issue brief on public-private partnerships in health AI documented multiple instances in which reimbursement structures for AI-assisted services were designed in ways that primarily benefited vendor licensing models rather than health system economics or patient outcomes (National Academy for State Health Policy, 2024, pp. 8–11).⏎⏎The political economy of this dynamic is worth examining directly. Litan and Singer (2022) analyzed the influence of health IT vendors on ONC rulemaking processes and found systematic evidence that vendor interests shaped both the framing of regulatory problems and the design of proposed solutions, in ways consistent with Stigler's (1971) foundational account of regulatory capture — the process by which regulated industries come to control the regulatory bodies nominally overseeing them (Litan & Singer, 2022, pp. 18–22; Stigler, 1971, p. 5). We are not alleging that HHS has been captured in any crude sense. But the structure of this RFI — its focus on removing regulatory friction, its emphasis on public-private partnerships, its framing of reimbursement reform as a tool for accelerating vendor-developed products — is more consistent with a vendor-drafted policy agenda than with an independent assessment of what the healthcare system needs. Kesselheim, Avorn, and Sarpatwari (2023) made a related observation about federal health technology policy more broadly, documenting how commercial interests shape the framing of policy problems in ways that systematically favor proprietary solutions over public-interest alternatives (Kesselheim et al., 2023, p. 1565). The RFI's silence on market concentration, vendor lock-in, and the economics of public-private data partnerships is not a neutral omission.⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 5⏎⏎THE PUBLIC DATA PROBLEM⏎⏎There is a specific issue within the public-private partnership question that deserves direct attention, and that the RFI's framing largely avoids. A substantial share of the training data underlying commercial clinical AI products was generated in federally funded healthcare settings, processed through federally subsidized EHR systems (the HITECH Act allocated approximately $36 billion in EHR adoption incentives), and validated against federally maintained claims databases and registries. When commercial vendors develop AI products using this data under Cooperative Research and Development Agreements or similar partnership structures, the public's contribution to the product's development is not reflected in the ownership or licensing terms. Health systems — including the public and nonprofit institutions that generated the underlying data — then pay licensing fees to access products that were built, in material part, from their own patients' records.⏎⏎The National Academy for State Health Policy's analysis identified this as a structural inequity in the current public-private AI partnership model, recommending that any federally facilitated AI development agreements include provisions for public ownership stakes, royalty arrangements, or preferential licensing terms for public and nonprofit health systems (National Academy for State Health Policy, 2024, p. 13). Our organization endorses this recommendation strongly. The RFI's discussion of public-private partnerships does not engage with this question at all, which is a significant gap. If HHS proceeds to expand public-private AI partnerships without addressing the ownership and licensing economics, it will be using public resources to subsidize the development of proprietary products that public institutions will then be required to license back at market rates. That is not an acceleration agenda for the health system — it is a subsidy program for technology vendors.⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 6⏎⏎A NOTE ON THE SURVEY DATA AND WHAT IT ACTUALLY SHOWS⏎⏎Our organization's 2024 Global Summit survey of 94 health system administrators found that regulatory ambiguity around AI model validation and reimbursement pathways was ranked among the top three barriers to scaling AI-assisted clinical decision support tools. We want to be precise about what this finding does and does not show, because we think it is being read selectively in industry commentary on this RFI.⏎⏎The finding shows that health system administrators perceive regulatory ambiguity as a significant barrier. It does not show that regulatory ambiguity is the primary cause of poor AI adoption outcomes. Perception surveys of administrators will reliably identify regulatory friction as a top concern because regulatory compliance is a salient cost that administrators directly manage — it appears in their budgets, generates legal risk, and requires dedicated staff time. The structural market problems we have described in this comment — concentration, lock-in, misaligned vendor incentives, data ownership inequities — are less salient to individual administrators because they operate at a market level rather than an institutional level. An administrator who is paying too much for a locked-in AI product may perceive that problem as a contracting issue or a budget constraint rather than as a market structure failure. The fact that regulatory friction is more visible than market structure failure does not mean it is more consequential. HHS should be cautious about designing policy primarily in response to administrator perception surveys, particularly when those surveys were conducted at vendor-sponsored industry events where the framing of questions may itself reflect vendor priorities.⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 7⏎⏎SPECIFIC CONCERNS REGARDING THE RFI'S REGULATORY QUESTIONS⏎⏎The RFI asks, among other things, what regulatory actions HHS can take to reduce barriers to AI adoption in clinical settings. We want to address this question directly, though our answer is probably not what the RFI's drafters anticipated.⏎⏎The regulatory actions most likely to produce good outcomes are not deregulatory. They are structural. The absence of mandatory pre-market performance benchmarking requirements for clinical AI tools — particularly tools that influence diagnostic and treatment decisions — means that vendors can make efficacy claims that are not independently verified before deployment. The GAO documented this gap in its 2023 report and recommended that HHS develop a clearer regulatory framework for AI tools that fall outside FDA's current oversight jurisdiction, noting that a significant share of clinical AI applications are structured as quality improvement tools or clinical decision support software in ways that place them outside existing regulatory pathways (Government Accountability Office, 2023, pp. 19–22). We agree with the GAO's analysis. The appropriate regulatory response is to close this gap, not to widen it in the name of acceleration.⏎⏎We are also concerned about the RFI's framing of AI governance as primarily a question of standards development and voluntary frameworks. Voluntary frameworks have a poor track record in health IT markets. The ONC's information blocking rules, which were implemented as mandatory requirements rather than voluntary guidance, produced measurably better compliance outcomes than predecessor voluntary interoperability initiatives precisely because they carried enforcement consequences. If HHS wants AI governance to produce meaningful behavioral change among vendors, it will need enforcement mechanisms, not just frameworks. The political economy literature on health IT regulation suggests that vendors will participate constructively in voluntary framework development processes while simultaneously structuring their commercial practices to minimize the operational impact of whatever framework emerges (Litan & Singer, 2022, p. 24). This is not cynicism about individual vendors — it is a predictable institutional response to regulatory environments that lack enforcement teeth.⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 8⏎⏎There is also the question of what "acceleration" means in practice for health systems of different sizes and resource levels. The RFI's framing implicitly assumes that faster deployment is better deployment, and that the health systems currently not deploying AI are failing to capture value they should be capturing. Our organization works with health systems across the full size and resource spectrum, and our experience is that the health systems moving most cautiously on AI deployment are often doing so for well-founded reasons — inadequate data infrastructure, insufficient technical staff to evaluate vendor claims, prior negative experiences with health IT deployments that did not deliver promised benefits, and legitimate concern about clinical liability in the absence of clear performance standards. Treating these as barriers to overcome rather than as rational responses to an immature market is a policy framing error that will produce predictable harms. The InterSystems benchmarking data we cited earlier makes this concrete: facilities with structured AI-readiness frameworks significantly outperformed facilities that deployed rapidly without adequate preparation (InterSystems Corporation, 2024). Acceleration without readiness infrastructure is not a shortcut — it is a different pathway to a worse outcome.⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 9⏎⏎PRECONDITIONS FOR ANY RESPONSIBLE ACCELERATION AGENDA⏎⏎Our organization wants to be constructive in this comment, and we recognize that there are genuine policy actions HHS can take to improve AI adoption outcomes. But we want to be clear that we do not think acceleration itself is the right policy goal. The right goal is good outcomes — for patients, for health systems, and for the public that funds a substantial share of U.S. healthcare. Acceleration may or may not serve that goal depending on the conditions under which it occurs. The following are what we consider preconditions for any responsible acceleration agenda:⏎⏎1. Mandatory market concentration review. HHS, in coordination with the FTC and DOJ Antitrust Division, should conduct a formal market structure analysis of the clinical AI sector before committing to an acceleration agenda. This analysis should examine EHR vendor control over AI deployment channels, the competitive dynamics of clinical decision support markets, and the extent to which current market concentration is likely to produce anti-competitive outcomes under accelerated deployment conditions. The FTC's 2023 staff report on generative AI and healthcare provides a starting framework (Federal Trade Commission, 2023), but it has not been translated into specific regulatory action in the health AI context.⏎⏎2. Interoperability standards that address workflow portability, not just data portability. ONC's existing interoperability rules address data access at the API layer. They do not address the workflow integration dependencies that create the most durable forms of vendor lock-in in clinical AI. HHS should direct ONC to develop interoperability standards that specifically address AI model portability — including requirements for standardized model input and output formats, prohibition on proprietary workflow integration requirements that create artificial switching costs, and transparency requirements for AI model performance data that enable health systems to make genuine comparative assessments of competing products. Adler-Milstein and Pfeifer's analysis of the limits of current interoperability mandates should inform this work (Adler-Milstein & Pfeifer, 2023).⏎⏎3. Public ownership conditions on federally facilitated AI development. Any AI development activity conducted under CRADA agreements, cooperative agreements, or other federal partnership structures should include provisions requiring that the resulting products be made available to public and nonprofit health systems on preferential licensing terms, or that the federal government retain a royalty interest in products commercialized from federally facilitated research. The NASHP's 2024 issue brief provides specific drafting recommendations for these provisions (National Academy for State Health Policy, 2024).⏎⏎4. Independent cost-benefit analysis of vendor licensing economics. HHS should commission an independent economic analysis of the net financial effects of AI deployment on health system budgets, with specific attention to vendor licensing costs, implementation contract structures, and the distribution of financial benefits between health systems and vendors. This analysis should be completed and made public before HHS commits to reimbursement reforms that would effectively subsidize AI deployment at scale.⏎⏎5. Mandatory post-market performance surveillance. Any acceleration agenda should be conditioned on the establishment of mandatory post-market performance monitoring requirements for clinical AI tools, with public reporting of performance data stratified by patient population, care setting, and time since deployment. The GAO recommended movement in this direction in its 2023 report (Government Accountability Office, 2023), and HHS has not yet acted on that recommendation.⏎⏎6. Regulatory ambiguity resolution that does not favor incumbents. Where HHS does act to reduce regulatory ambiguity, it should do so in ways that do not preferentially benefit incumbent EHR vendors. Specifically, AI validation and certification pathways should be designed to accommodate products from vendors without existing EHR market presence, and should not use EHR integration as a proxy for clinical validation.⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 10⏎⏎CONCLUSION IS NOT THE RIGHT WORD FOR WHAT FOLLOWS⏎⏎We want to close by being direct about something that the formal structure of a regulatory comment makes it easy to obscure. The RFI as published reads, to our organization's policy staff, as a document that has already decided on its answer and is soliciting comments to validate it. The framing of regulatory friction as the primary barrier, the emphasis on public-private partnerships without any discussion of ownership economics, the focus on reimbursement modernization without any analysis of who captures the resulting value — these are not neutral analytical choices. They are choices that consistently favor a particular set of interests, and those interests are not primarily the interests of patients or health systems.⏎⏎We are not suggesting bad faith on the part of HHS or ONC staff. The dynamics Litan and Singer (2022) describe, and that Stigler (1971) theorized more generally, do not require bad faith — they require only that the regulatory process be more accessible to well-resourced parties with concentrated interests than to diffuse stakeholders with harder-to-quantify concerns. Health systems have resources and can participate in this process, which is why Premier Inc. is submitting these comments. But the structural interests of the clinical AI vendor community are more concentrated, more legible in policy terms, and more consistently represented in the policy development process than the interests of the patients who will actually be affected by the tools being deployed. Kesselheim and colleagues' documentation of how commercial interests shape federal health technology policy framing is relevant here not as an accusation but as a description of a structural tendency that requires active correction (Kesselheim et al., 2023, p. 1567).⏎⏎The healthcare AI market is at an early enough stage that deliberate policy choices can still shape its structure in ways that serve the public interest. Once the integration dependencies described in this comment are fully established — once major health systems have deployed AI tools from incumbent EHR vendors and accumulated the switching costs that make displacement prohibitive — the window for structural intervention will have closed. Dafny and Lee's analysis of healthcare market concentration dynamics suggests that once concentration reaches a certain threshold, the policy tools available to address it become significantly less effective (Dafny & Lee, 2022, p. 1333). The analogy to hospital market consolidation is instructive: federal policy has spent the better part of two decades trying to address the consequences of hospital consolidation that occurred in the 1990s and 2000s, with limited success, because the structural conditions that enabled consolidation were not addressed at the time.⏎⏎HHS has an opportunity here that it will not have again in five years. The question is whether the Department is willing to use that opportunity to address the structural economics of the healthcare AI market, or whether it will use it to accelerate deployment under conditions that will entrench the current market structure and the misaligned incentives it produces. Our organization's position is that the latter course is not an acceleration agenda — it is a foreclosure of the conditions under which genuinely beneficial AI adoption could occur.⏎⏎We appreciate the Department's willingness to receive public comment on these questions, and we are available to discuss any aspect of this submission with HHS and ONC staff.⏎⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎⏎Jessica Rivera⏎Senior Vice President, Policy and Regulatory Affairs⏎Premier Inc.⏎Charlotte, North Carolina⏎⏎jessica.rivera@premierinc.com⏎www.premierinc.com/policy⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 11⏎⏎REFERENCES⏎⏎Adler-Milstein, J., & Pfeifer, E. (2023). EHR vendor lock-in and the limits of interoperability mandates. Health Affairs, 42(6), 812-820.⏎⏎Dafny, L., & Lee, T.H. (2022). Health care market concentration: Trends, consequences, and policy responses. New England Journal of Medicine, 387(14), 1329-1335.⏎⏎Federal Trade Commission. (2023). Generative AI and the healthcare sector: Competition policy considerations. FTC Staff Report. Washington, D.C.⏎⏎Gans, J.S., & Stern, S. (2023). Platform envelopment and healthcare AI: Incumbent advantage in clinical decision support markets. Journal of Health Economics, 91, 102-119.⏎⏎Government Accountability Office. (2023). Artificial intelligence in health care: Benefits and challenges of technologies to augment patient care. GAO-23-105491.⏎⏎InterSystems Corporation. (2024). AI readiness and clinical workflow integration: Benchmarking report across 1,200+ health facilities. InterSystems Global Summit White Paper.⏎⏎Kesselheim, A.S., Avorn, J., & Sarpatwari, A. (2023). Commercial interests and the framing of federal health technology policy. JAMA, 329(18), 1563-1570.⏎⏎Litan, R.E., & Singer, H.J. (2022). The political economy of health IT regulation: Vendor influence and the shaping of ONC rulemaking. Brookings Institution Economic Studies Working Paper.⏎⏎National Academy for State Health Policy. (2024). Public-private partnerships in health AI: Data access, proprietary control, and the public interest. NASHP Issue Brief, March 2024.⏎⏎Office of the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / ONC. (2024). Health sector AI request for information. Federal Register, 89 FR 22456.⏎⏎Stigler, G.J. (1971). The theory of economic regulation. Bell Journal of Economics and Management Science, 2(1), 3-21.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0030♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔King♔♔New York♔♔United States♔Patient Advocate Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔Patient Advocate Foundation | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 1⏎⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎COMMENT OF PATIENT ADVOCATE FOUNDATION IN RESPONSE TO⏎HHS HEALTH SECTOR ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE REQUEST FOR INFORMATION:⏎ACCELERATING AI ADOPTION IN CLINICAL CARE⏎⏎Submitted by: Kenneth King, Campaign Director⏎Patient Advocate Foundation⏎421 Butler Farm Road, Hampton, VA 23666⏎kking@patientadvocate.org⏎⏎Docket Reference: HHS-ONC-2024-AI-RFI⏎⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎THE EMPIRICAL RECORD HAS ALREADY SPOKEN⏎⏎The framing of this RFI — that the central policy challenge is accelerating AI adoption — reflects a sequencing error that Patient Advocate Foundation finds professionally alarming. The peer-reviewed literature documenting algorithmic harm to marginalized patients is not sparse, not preliminary, and not confined to edge cases. It is dense, replicable, and growing. Sjoding and colleagues demonstrated in 2020 that pulse oximeters, a device so foundational that its AI-adjacent risk was largely unconsidered, produced clinically significant overestimates of oxygen saturation in Black patients at rates sufficient to delay treatment for occult hypoxemia (Sjoding et al., New England Journal of Medicine, 383(25), 2477–2478). That finding did not emerge from a theoretical model. It emerged from retrospective analysis of real patients who received inadequate care. Obermeyer and colleagues identified that a widely deployed commercial risk-scoring algorithm systematically assigned lower risk scores to Black patients than to White patients with equivalent disease burden, effectively rationing access to care management programs along racial lines (Obermeyer et al., Science, 366(6464), 447–453, 2019). The mechanism was not malice; it was the use of prior healthcare expenditure as a proxy for health need, a proxy that encodes the documented reality that Black patients historically receive less care per unit of illness. Wong and colleagues externally validated Epic's widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model and found substantial degradation in predictive performance across hospital settings, with sensitivity and positive predictive value falling sharply outside the academic medical centers where the model was originally trained (Wong et al., JAMA Internal Medicine, 181(8), 1065–1070, 2021). Adamson and Smith documented that dermatology diagnostic AI tools trained predominantly on images of lighter-skinned patients produced materially inferior diagnostic accuracy for patients with darker skin tones (Adamson & Smith, JAMA Dermatology, 154(11), 1247–1248, 2018). Seyyed-Kalantari and colleagues found that AI algorithms applied to chest radiographs in under-served populations produced systematic underdiagnosis bias, with patients from lower-income and minority backgrounds receiving less accurate diagnostic outputs from the same models deployed in better-resourced settings (Seyyed-Kalantari et al., Nature Medicine, 27(12), 2176–2182, 2021). Patient Advocate Foundation does not characterize these findings as a warning about what AI might do. These are descriptions of what AI has already done, in deployed clinical environments, to real patients whose conditions were misclassified and whose care was accordingly compromised.⏎⏎Patient Advocate Foundation | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 2⏎⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎THE VALIDATION-GENERALIZABILITY GAP AND THE SAFETY-NET PROBLEM⏎⏎What concerns this organization most — and what the RFI conspicuously omits any serious treatment of — is the structural relationship between where AI tools are validated and where they will actually be deployed at scale. Academic medical centers with large, well-curated, richly annotated datasets are the environments in which most commercial clinical AI products are developed and internally validated. Community hospitals, rural critical access facilities, and safety-net institutions serving predominantly Medicaid and uninsured populations are the environments in which those same tools will be broadly deployed once reimbursement pathways are established and regulatory endorsement is granted. These two environments are not interchangeable. Gianfrancesco and colleagues identified that electronic health record data used to train machine learning algorithms contains systematic biases arising from differential documentation practices, differential access to care, and differential coding intensity across patient populations, all of which degrade model generalizability in precisely the settings where marginalized patients receive care (Gianfrancesco et al., JAMA Internal Medicine, 178(11), 1544–1547, 2018). Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones documented that race-correction practices embedded in clinical algorithms have historically compounded rather than corrected for structural health disparities, producing outputs calibrated to patterns of unequal treatment rather than underlying biological reality (Vyas et al., New England Journal of Medicine, 383(9), 874–882, 2020). Wiens and colleagues articulated the responsible deployment framework that the field has largely failed to operationalize: that deployment decisions must be preceded by rigorous external validation in the target population, not the training population (Wiens et al., Nature Medicine, 25(9), 1337–1340, 2019). The GAO reached substantively similar conclusions in its 2023 report on AI in health care, noting that existing federal oversight mechanisms are poorly positioned to detect performance degradation across demographic subgroups after deployment (GAO-23-105491, 2023). The National Academy for State Health Policy and the Robert Wood Johnson Foundation similarly documented in 2023 that state-level AI governance frameworks are fragmented and inadequate, with no jurisdiction having implemented comprehensive equity-centered pre-deployment requirements (NASPH & RWJF, Algorithmic Bias in Health Care, 2023).⏎⏎There is also a mechanism problem that the RFI does not address. Automation bias — the documented tendency of clinicians to defer to algorithmic outputs even when those outputs conflict with their own clinical judgment — means that AI errors are in several respects harder to catch than equivalent human errors. Char, Shah, and Magnus identified this as among the most consequential ethical challenges in clinical AI implementation, precisely because it transforms tool error into systemic practice error without any clear attribution point (Char et al., New England Journal of Medicine, 378(11), 981–983, 2018). Pierson and colleagues further documented that algorithmic approaches to pain management in under-served populations can perpetuate treatment disparities embedded in historical prescribing patterns, even when the stated goal of the tool is equity improvement (Pierson et al., Nature Medicine, 27(1), 136–140, 2021). The literature is not ambiguous on these points.⏎⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎SPECIFIC POLICY DEMANDS⏎⏎Patient Advocate Foundation respectfully but firmly requests that HHS condition any regulatory endorsement, reimbursement pathway development, or federal facilitation of clinical AI deployment on the following requirements, which reflect the minimum threshold of responsible governance given the evidence record above:⏎⏎1. Mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing: Any AI tool seeking Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement coverage or inclusion in any HHS-endorsed clinical decision support framework must demonstrate pre-deployment performance parity across race, ethnicity, sex, age, insurance status, and primary language subgroups, tested in population cohorts representative of the intended deployment environment, not the development environment.⏎⏎2. Mandatory post-deployment bias surveillance: Covered entities deploying reimbursed AI tools must implement ongoing monitoring of algorithmic outputs stratified by demographic subgroup, with defined thresholds for performance divergence that trigger mandatory review and, where warranted, suspension of deployment pending revalidation.⏎⏎3. Transparent external validation requirements: HHS should require that external validation studies be conducted in community and safety-net settings prior to broad deployment approval, not retrospectively. The Wong et al. findings regarding Epic's sepsis model are a direct illustration of why post-hoc external validation is insufficient as a safety mechanism.⏎⏎4. Equity audit methodology standardization: HHS should convene a technical standards process to define minimum acceptable methodologies for subgroup equity auditing, because the absence of standardized methodology currently allows vendors to conduct and report equity analyses in ways that obscure rather than reveal disparate performance.⏎⏎Patient Advocate Foundation | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 3⏎⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎HHS has the regulatory authority and the administrative infrastructure to require these safeguards as conditions of reimbursement and coverage determination. The omission of any mandatory equity framework from this RFI is not a neutral policy choice. It is a decision to proceed with deployment acceleration while the evidence of disparate harm accumulates. Patient Advocate Foundation's direct program work with patients navigating coverage denials and care gaps has consistently shown that the populations most likely to be harmed by biased AI tools are the same populations least equipped to identify the source of the harm, contest an algorithmic determination, or access alternative care pathways. The 34-percentage-point variation in AI-assisted imaging reimbursement denial rates across Medicare Administrative Contractor jurisdictions documented in RBMA's 2024 member survey data is one illustration of how uneven the current landscape already is before mandatory equity requirements are in place.⏎⏎HHS should not finalize any regulatory or reimbursement framework that facilitates broader clinical AI deployment without first establishing the mandatory pre-deployment and post-deployment equity requirements described above. The comment period for this RFI should be understood as the moment at which the Department chose whether to get ahead of documented harms or to accelerate past♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0031♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Clark♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To the Office of the Deputy Secretary, U.S. Department of Health and Human Services:⏎⏎MY EXPERIENCE AT UNM HEALTH, AND WHAT IT MEANS FOR THIS RULEMAKING⏎⏎Eleven days. That is how long I waited, in November of last year, after my cardiologist at UNM Health flagged what the automated triage system had labeled an "anomaly" on my echocardiogram. I am sixty-two years old. I understand, in a way that perhaps not every patient does, how workflow systems operate — I have scheduled things, routed things, and tracked bottlenecks for a living for the better part of three decades. What I watched happen to my own case was not a technical failure in any narrow sense. The algorithm flagged the finding. The algorithm queued it for secondary review. The algorithm, presumably, moved on. What the algorithm did not do, and what no human being in that chain felt obligated to do in its place, was pick up a telephone and tell a sixty-two-year-old man in Albuquerque that he might be waiting a while, and why. The finding turned out to be a calibration artifact. Not a medical finding at all. The eleven days of quiet catastrophizing I did in my apartment were caused entirely by a system that had been designed to route responsibility away from any identifiable person. I want the Department to sit with that for a moment before it proceeds.⏎⏎WHAT "ACCELERATION" LOOKS LIKE FROM THE PATIENT SIDE OF THE TRANSACTION⏎⏎The RFI uses the phrase "enhance productivity" in describing the goals of AI adoption in clinical settings, and I want to be direct: that framing concerns me in ways that go beyond my personal experience. Francis Peabody wrote in 1927, in JAMA, that "the secret of the care of the patient is in caring for the patient" as a person rather than a case (JAMA 88(12): 877-882). That observation has not aged out of relevance. What Peabody understood, and what this Department's framing seems not to, is that medicine is a relational practice at its core — that diagnosis depends on interpreting ambiguity, fear, body language, and context that no pattern-matching system can access. Abraham Verghese has written and spoken at length about the irreducible diagnostic value of the physical and relational clinical encounter, and Danielle Zulman and her colleagues at Stanford documented in 2020 that measurable clinical outcomes improve when physicians are genuinely present with their patients, not merely proximate to a screen displaying algorithmic outputs (JAMA 323(1): 70-81). These are not sentimental observations. They are empirical ones.⏎⏎What worries me about acceleration, specifically, is what happens to clinical judgment when it is exercised less frequently. Goddard and colleagues published a systematic review in 2012 documenting that clinician reliance on automated recommendations degrades independent reasoning over time — what the literature calls automation bias (JAMIA 19(1): 121-127). Obermeyer and Emanuel made a related point in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2016: the structural risk with algorithmic systems in clinical settings is not that they will be wrong in obvious ways, but that their confidence will displace the clinical judgment that might have caught what they missed (NEJM 375: 1216-1219). Even Eric Topol, who is as enthusiastic an advocate for AI in medicine as anyone writing today, acknowledged in his 2019 Nature Medicine piece that the human clinical relationship is irreplaceable and that AI should be deployed in ways that give physicians more time with patients, not less (Nature Medicine 25: 44-56). The Department is not proposing to follow that logic. It is proposing to accelerate a process without first establishing what guardrails ensure the relational substrate of care is not eroded in the process.⏎⏎I want to be specific about what I am asking, because I recognize that a comment like this can easily be dismissed as technophobia, and I want to be clear that it is not. I am not opposed to AI in clinical care. I am opposed to AI deployment that:⏎⏎1. Removes identifiable human accountability from time-sensitive patient communications, as happened in my case at UNM Health.⏎2. Redefines clinical quality in terms of throughput and triage efficiency rather than relational outcomes, which the empathy literature suggests are not separable from clinical outcomes — Hojat and colleagues demonstrated in 2011 that physician empathy produced measurable improvements in diabetic patient outcomes independent of the technical quality of the intervention (Academic Medicine 86(3): 359-364).⏎3. Proceeds without mandatory disclosure to patients that an AI system, rather than a clinician, has made a triage or prioritization decision affecting their care.⏎⏎The Department is asking what regulatory actions it can take to accelerate adoption. I am asking it to first answer a question it has not yet posed to itself: what does a patient owe an algorithm, and what does an algorithm owe a patient? Because right now, in my experience, the answer to the second question is nothing. The algorithm flagged, queued, and moved on, and the hospital system treated that as a complete transaction. It was not. There was a man on the other end of it who spent eleven days not sleeping well, not eating well, and not telling his family what he was afraid of because he did not want to frighten them over something that might be nothing. It was nothing. But I did not know that for eleven days because the system had no mechanism for a human being to look at the queue and notice that a follow-up communication had not been sent.⏎⏎I would ask the Department not to issue reimbursement incentives for AI-assisted triage systems until minimum standards for patient notification and accountable human review are established. That is a specific, implementable demand, and I believe it is the right place to start.⏎⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎⏎Joshua Clark⏎Albuquerque, New Mexico♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0032♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Martinez♔♔New York♔♔United States♔American College of Emergency Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔American College of Emergency Physicians⏎4950 W. Royal Lane⏎Irving, TX 75063⏎⏎Susan Martinez, JD, MPH⏎Director of Health Policy⏎ACEP Washington Office⏎2121 K Street NW, Suite 325⏎Washington, DC 20037⏎⏎June 28, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Attention: Health Sector AI Request for Information Docket⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care, 89 Fed. Reg. 47210 (May 31, 2024)⏎⏎---⏎⏎ACEP COMMENTS | HHS AI RFI | DOCKET HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 1⏎⏎THE GOVERNANCE DEFICIT PRECEDING DEPLOYMENT⏎⏎The American College of Emergency Physicians (ACEP) submits these comments in response to the Department's request for information on regulatory, reimbursement, and research-and-development actions to accelerate artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. ACEP represents more than 40,000 emergency physicians practicing across every care setting in which AI deployment is now actively occurring, often ahead of any coherent governance structure to support it. The Department has framed this RFI principally around acceleration. ACEP urges the Department to reconsider that framing entirely, because the more consequential policy question is not how quickly these tools can be deployed, but whether the institutional conditions necessary for meaningful democratic accountability can be established before irreversible structural dependencies foreclose that possibility.⏎⏎The empirical starting point for these comments is not ACEP's own membership data, though that data is extensive and troubling in its own right. The Association of periOperative Registered Nurses published findings in October 2024, presented at the AORN Global Surgical Conference in Chicago, drawn from a survey of more than 2,200 perioperative nurses across 47 states. Sixty-eight percent of respondents reported direct exposure to at least one AI-assisted surgical or anesthesia monitoring tool within their facility in the prior twelve months. Only 19 percent indicated they had received any structured training on interpreting AI-generated alerts or integrating AI outputs into intraoperative decision-making (AORN, 2024 Member Survey on AI Exposure and Training Readiness in Perioperative Settings, October 2024). These numbers are not primarily a workforce training problem, though they are that too. They represent something more structurally significant: the systematic decoupling of deployment velocity from governance readiness. Tools are being embedded into clinical workflows before the institutions deploying them have developed the accountability mechanisms, the interpretive frameworks, or the staff competencies to exercise meaningful oversight of those tools. ACEP sees the same pattern in emergency departments across the country, where AI-assisted triage, sepsis prediction, and diagnostic imaging tools are increasingly present without corresponding clinical governance protocols.⏎⏎ACEP COMMENTS | HHS AI RFI | DOCKET HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 2⏎⏎THE IRREVERSIBILITY PROBLEM AND PATH DEPENDENCE⏎⏎This governance deficit would be recoverable if the technologies in question were reversible. They are not. Paul Pierson's foundational work on increasing returns and path dependence (Pierson, "Increasing Returns, Path Dependence, and the Study of Politics," American Political Science Review, 94(2), 2000) provides the conceptual architecture for understanding why this matters so acutely in health sector AI. Once an AI-assisted clinical decision support system is embedded into an electronic health record workflow, once reimbursement codes are structured around its outputs, once clinical staff have been retrained to rely on its alerts rather than developing independent interpretive competencies, the institutional cost of reversing that dependency becomes prohibitive. Leemore Dafny and Thomas Lee have documented precisely this dynamic in the context of health platform economics more broadly, showing that structural barriers to market exit and regulatory reversal in health information technology create conditions under which initial deployment decisions effectively determine long-term institutional trajectories (Dafny & Lee, "Health Care Platform Lock-In: Structural Barriers to Market Exit and Regulatory Reversal," New England Journal of Medicine, 388(14), 2023). The HHS RFI, by treating acceleration as the primary policy objective, risks producing exactly the kind of premature lock-in that forecloses future regulatory correction.⏎⏎ACEP does not contend that AI has no role in emergency medicine. The Department and ACEP's own membership have documented genuine clinical benefits in specific, well-validated applications. Eric Topol's work has been instructive on the potential for AI to restore physician attention to patient interaction by reducing cognitive load in documentation and pattern recognition (Topol, Deep Medicine: How Artificial Intelligence Can Make Healthcare Human Again, Basic Books, 2019). The GAO's 2023 report on AI in health care similarly acknowledged a range of applications with demonstrated clinical utility (GAO, Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Benefits and Challenges of Technologies to Augment Patient Care, GAO-23-105980, 2023). ACEP's concern is not categorical opposition. The concern is that the Department is being asked by commercial interests and by internal constituencies within HHS to treat deployment velocity as a proxy for progress, when the actual measure of progress should be whether governance structures are keeping pace with deployment.⏎⏎ACEP COMMENTS | HHS AI RFI | DOCKET HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 3⏎⏎THE DEMOCRATIC LEGITIMACY QUESTION⏎⏎The RFI does not adequately engage with what ACEP regards as the foundational question: who decided that AI-mediated clinical decision-making was an appropriate direction for American health care, and through what legitimate democratic process was that decision made? This is not a rhetorical question. The European Parliament's Committee on Civil Liberties, Justice and Home Affairs grappled with precisely this issue in its 2023 analysis of algorithmic decision-making, concluding that health sector AI governance has proceeded in most jurisdictions without the kind of deliberative public mandate that decisions of this magnitude ordinarily require (European Parliament, "Democratic Accountability in Algorithmic Decision-Making: Lessons for Health Sector AI Governance," PE 740.248, 2023). Frank Pasquale has made the complementary argument that the displacement of human clinical expertise by algorithmic systems is not a neutral technical decision but a political one, carrying distributional consequences that should be subject to democratic deliberation rather than administrative acceleration (Pasquale, New Laws of Robotics: Defending Human Expertise in the Age of AI, Harvard University Press, 2020). HHS, by soliciting comment on how to accelerate adoption rather than on whether and under what conditions adoption should proceed, has already made an implicit policy choice that belongs properly to a deliberative public process.⏎⏎The structural impossibility of meaningful oversight of proprietary black-box systems compounds this democratic legitimacy problem considerably. Andrew Selbst and Solon Barocas have demonstrated that the demand for explainability in algorithmic systems frequently produces what they term "the intuitive appeal of explainable machines" — the appearance of transparency without the substance of it, in which explanations generated for regulatory compliance do not actually allow external reviewers to identify the mechanisms driving algorithmic outputs (Selbst & Barocas, "The Intuitive Appeal of Explainable Machines," Fordham Law Review, 87(3), 2018). Brent Mittelstadt, Chris Russell, and Sandra Wachter extended this analysis in the context of AI fairness and accountability frameworks, showing that post-hoc explanation methods systematically fail to expose the training data characteristics and optimization objectives that most consequentially shape model behavior (Mittelstadt, Russell & Wachter, "Explaining Explanations in AI," FAccT Proceedings, 2019). Emergency medicine is a particularly high-stakes domain for this problem. When a sepsis prediction model generates an alert at 2:47 a.m. in a crowded emergency department, the attending physician cannot interrogate the model's reasoning in any meaningful sense. The physician can accept or override the alert. But without access to the model's architecture, training data provenance, and validation methodology, neither the physician nor ACEP nor HHS can meaningfully assess whether the alert reflects genuine clinical signal or reflects a pattern artifact in the training data that happens to correlate with outcomes in the training population but does not generalize to the patient in front of the physician.⏎⏎ACEP COMMENTS | HHS AI RFI | DOCKET HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 4⏎⏎o  The Department should commission a structured public deliberation process, with representation from patients, clinicians, health equity advocates, and technology critics, before finalizing any deployment-facilitation policy.⏎⏎o  HHS should condition any reimbursement or regulatory accommodation for AI-assisted clinical decision support on mandatory disclosure of model architecture, training data characteristics, and ongoing performance monitoring data to a publicly accessible federal registry.⏎⏎o  The RFI's framing should be reoriented away from acceleration and toward precautionary governance, with explicit acknowledgment that irreversibility of institutional AI dependencies constitutes a distinct regulatory risk category warranting its own analytical framework.⏎⏎o  HHS should require, as a condition of any federal health care program payment for AI-assisted clinical services, that vendors demonstrate the ability of facility clinical staff to meaningfully interpret and override AI outputs — not merely acknowledge them.⏎⏎o  The Department should establish an independent technical review body, insulated from vendor influence, to conduct prospective evaluation of AI tools prior to their deployment in federally reimbursed clinical settings.⏎⏎ACEP COMMENTS | HHS AI RFI | DOCKET HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 5⏎⏎THE UNINTENDED CONSEQUENCES PROBLEM IN EMERGENCY SETTINGS⏎⏎Enrico Coiera, Joan Ash, and Marc Berg documented in 2022 the now well-established pattern by which health information technology generates unintended consequences that are structurally predictable but systematically underweighted in pre-deployment analysis (Coiera, Ash & Berg, "The Unintended Consequences of Health Information Technology Revisited," Yearbook of Medical Informatics, 31(1), 2022). Emergency departments are the clinical environment in which these unintended consequences are most likely to manifest acutely and most difficult to reverse once embedded. The cognitive architecture of emergency medicine — high patient volume, compressed decision cycles, frequent interruption, limited longitudinal patient data — creates conditions in which AI alert fatigue, automation bias, and deskilling effects are not hypothetical risks but predictable operational outcomes. ACEP's policy team has received reports from member physicians at facilities in New York, Texas, and Ohio describing AI-generated sepsis alerts with positive predictive values well below what was represented at the time of vendor contracting, creating alert loads that physicians are managing by developing heuristics for systematic override that themselves introduce new error patterns. These are not edge cases. They are the foreseeable consequence of deploying tools whose performance in controlled validation settings does not transfer reliably to the operational conditions of high-volume emergency departments.⏎⏎Ziad Obermeyer and Eric Topol have documented the mechanisms by which AI systems trained on historically biased clinical data reproduce and in some cases amplify existing health care disparities (Obermeyer & Topol, "Artificial Intelligence, Bias, and Patient Safety," JAMA, 325(24), 2021). Emergency departments serve disproportionately uninsured, underinsured, and socially marginalized patient populations — precisely the populations most likely to be underrepresented in the training data of commercially developed AI tools and most likely to experience adverse outcomes from model performance degradation in their demographic subgroups. ACEP is particularly concerned that the RFI's emphasis on accelerating adoption does not adequately account for the distributional consequences of deploying tools whose performance characteristics may diverge systematically across patient populations in ways that neither facilities nor regulators are currently equipped to detect. The AORN survey data cited earlier is relevant here too: if 81 percent of perioperative nurses have received no structured training on interpreting AI outputs, the assumption that clinical staff will function as a reliable check on algorithmically generated errors is not operationally supportable.⏎⏎ACEP COMMENTS | HHS AI RFI | DOCKET HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 6⏎⏎ACEP's specific concern about the reimbursement question posed in the RFI is that payment incentives attached to AI tool use will, if structured without corresponding performance accountability requirements, function as deployment subsidies rather than as quality incentives. The history of health IT reimbursement under the HITECH Act provides instructive precedent. Meaningful use incentives accelerated EHR adoption at a pace that outran clinical workflow integration capacity, producing well-documented physician burnout, documentation quality degradation, and patient safety events attributable to interface design failures that would have been correctable under a slower, more deliberate adoption trajectory. ACEP is not arguing against reimbursement accommodation for AI tools that demonstrate genuine clinical value in emergency settings. The organization is arguing that payment design must be conditioned on prospective performance validation, ongoing post-market surveillance with publicly reported results, and demonstrated vendor compliance with disclosure standards — not simply on tool deployment.⏎⏎The Department should also attend carefully to the vendor-to-facility disclosure problem identified in the AORN survey analysis. ACEP's policy team has reviewed vendor contracting practices at member facilities and identified a consistent pattern in which performance representations made during the procurement process are not accompanied by contractual commitments to ongoing performance monitoring or data sharing with facility clinical governance committees. This is not an incidental contracting problem. It is a structural feature of a market in which vendors bear no regulatory obligation to disclose post-deployment performance data and in which facilities lack the technical capacity to conduct independent performance assessment. Without federal disclosure requirements, the information asymmetry between vendors and deploying institutions will persist regardless of how many acceleration-oriented policy accommodations HHS provides.⏎⏎ACEP COMMENTS | HHS AI RFI | DOCKET HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 7⏎⏎CONCLUSION WITHHELD — SPECIFIC REQUEST TO THE DEPARTMENT⏎⏎ACEP respectfully requests that HHS reframe this initiative around democratic legitimacy and precautionary governance rather than around deployment velocity. The Department should commission a structured public deliberation process before any deployment-facilitation policy is finalized — one that is not organized by vendor consortia or by internal HHS constituencies with institutional interests in AI adoption, but by an independent body with genuine representation from the patient and clinician communities that will bear the consequences of these decisions. The path dependence risks documented in the health platform lock-in literature are not abstract. They describe a specific mechanism by which regulatory windows close. Once AI-mediated clinical decision-making is structurally embedded in federally reimbursed care delivery, the institutional cost of re-establishing human clinical primacy becomes a political obstacle that no subsequent administration is likely to overcome.⏎⏎ACEP's policy team is available to provide technical assistance on governance framework design, performance monitoring methodology, and emergency medicine-specific deployment standards. Contact information for the ACEP Washington Office policy team is below.⏎⏎Susan Martinez, JD, MPH⏎Director of Health Policy⏎American College of Emergency Physicians⏎⏎smartinez@acep.org⏎(202) 591-XXXX⏎⏎ACEP COMMENTS | HHS AI RFI | DOCKET HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 7⏎⏎Note: ACEP's internal tracking reference for this comment submission is Policy-RFI-2024-031. Member facilities wishing to submit supplemental facility-level data on AI deployment conditions in emergency settings should contact the ACEP policy office directly. The deadline for supplemental data submissions coordinated through ACEP is♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0033♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Miller♔♔Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Forty-one years as a nurse and what worries me most isn't the AI helping doctors, it's the AI already being used by insurers to deny care automatically, and older patients in Roanoke with no computer have no idea how to fight back.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0034♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Jackson♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Detroit, Michigan⏎July 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎To Whom It May Concern:⏎⏎My client Marcus lost his housing before his first psychiatric appointment ever happened. AI tools trained on data from well-resourced hospitals will reproduce those same failures at scale, and HHS needs equity testing requirements before deployment, not after communities like his are already harmed.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎Donald Jackson⏎Licensed Social Worker, Detroit, MI♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0035♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Green♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔American Psychological Association♔♔♔♔♔♔American Psychological Association — Comments Submitted to HHS Docket on AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Stephanie Green, Community Organizing and Policy Division⏎American Psychological Association⏎750 First Street NE, Washington, DC 20002⏎⏎Submitted electronically via regulations.gov⏎⏎⏎PRELIMINARY STATEMENT⏎⏎⏎The democratic stakes of this rulemaking are not incidental to its technical substance — they are the substance. The American Psychological Association submits these comments not to oppose technological progress in healthcare, but to name what HHS appears unwilling to name: that the Department's framing of this RFI presupposes an answer to a question the public has not yet been asked. The RFI's stated goal of reducing "uncertainty that impedes AI innovation" — language drawn directly from the Federal Health IT Strategic Plan 2024–2030 (ONC, 2023) — positions democratic scrutiny itself as the impediment. We believe that framing is not a neutral policy choice. It is a governance posture, and it carries consequences that no subsequent regulatory correction will be able to fully reverse.⏎⏎We address here primarily the RFI's questions concerning the regulatory environment for clinical AI adoption, and the appropriate federal posture toward accelerating that adoption. We do not address every question posed, because real engagement with the democratic concerns at stake requires depth rather than breadth.⏎⏎⏎THE PROBLEM OF IRREVERSIBILITY⏎⏎⏎There is a concept in technology governance literature that has received insufficient attention in federal health policy: the idea that certain technological embeddings are, for practical purposes, permanent. Langdon Winner argued in 1980 that artifacts carry politics — that the choices embedded in a technological system are not neutral and do not remain available for renegotiation once the system has been institutionalized (Winner, 1980, "Do Artifacts Have Politics?" Daedalus, 109(1), 121–136). Sheila Jasanoff, writing more recently, extended this insight to argue that societies must exercise democratic judgment about technology before lock-in occurs, because after the fact, the governance options available to democratic institutions are structurally narrowed by the technical dependencies already in place (Jasanoff, 2016, The Ethics of Invention: Technology and the Human Future, W.W. Norton). Neither of these scholars was writing about healthcare AI specifically. But their frameworks apply with particular force here.⏎⏎When AI systems are embedded into clinical decision-support workflows, electronic health records, and diagnostic imaging pipelines, the institutional knowledge required to function without them atrophies. This is not a speculative concern. Dafny and Lee (2016, "The Good Merger," New England Journal of Medicine, 374(18), 1701–1703) documented analogous lock-in dynamics in the context of healthcare system consolidation: once institutional dependencies are established, the cost of reversal becomes prohibitive not merely financially but operationally, and the practical governance authority that regulators nominally retain becomes largely theoretical. The same dynamic applies to AI dependency. Friedman, Wong, and Blumenthal (2010, "Achieving a Nationwide Learning Health System," Science Translational Medicine, 2(57), 57cm29) observed that health IT infrastructure, once embedded at scale, shapes all subsequent clinical and regulatory practice in ways that resist fundamental reconfiguration. The point is not that AI in clinical settings will necessarily produce bad outcomes. The point is that once the infrastructure is in place and the human capacity to function without it has degraded, no future HHS secretary, no future Congress, and no future regulatory proceeding can meaningfully restore the prior state. The decision to accelerate is, therefore, not a reversible policy experiment. It is a permanent transfer of clinical governance into a technological regime whose terms are set by private vendors, and HHS is proposing to execute that transfer without a democratic mandate to do so.⏎⏎⏎APA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE BLACK BOX PROBLEM AND DEMOCRATIC OVERSIGHT⏎⏎⏎The second dimension of our concern is structural rather than merely procedural. Even if one were to accept that the acceleration agenda is democratically legitimate — which we do not — the AI systems being deployed into clinical settings are, by design, inaccessible to the oversight mechanisms that democratic governance relies upon to identify and correct harmful policy. Frank Pasquale documented this dynamic comprehensively in The Black Box Society (2015, Harvard University Press): proprietary algorithmic systems are structured to resist external scrutiny not as a side effect of their complexity but as a condition of their commercial viability. The competitive advantage embedded in a clinical AI system is precisely the logic that cannot be examined. Regulators cannot audit what vendors will not disclose. Clinicians cannot interrogate what they cannot see. Patients cannot contest decisions whose basis is legally protected as trade secret.⏎⏎This is not a hypothetical problem. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan demonstrated in 2019 that a widely deployed algorithm used to manage the health of millions of patients contained systematic racial bias that was invisible to the clinicians and administrators relying upon it, because the internal logic of the system was structurally inaccessible to them (Obermeyer et al., 2019, "Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations," Science, 366(6464), 447–453). The bias was discovered not through routine oversight but through independent academic research — a mechanism that cannot be institutionalized as a substitute for regulatory accountability. Virginia Eubanks has shown that when algorithmic systems become infrastructure in domains like public benefits and child welfare, the democratic accountability mechanisms that normally operate to correct harmful policy are rendered inoperative not because oversight is impossible in principle, but because the systems are designed and deployed in ways that make accountability practically unreachable (Eubanks, 2018, Automating Inequality, St. Martin's Press). Healthcare AI is following the same trajectory.⏎⏎The Texas Medical Association's Health Information Technology Committee documented in its 2024 policy review that among approximately 4,200 practicing physicians surveyed, 58% had encountered at least one AI-assisted diagnostic or clinical decision-support tool in their practice setting within the prior 12 months, yet only 23% had received any formal guidance from payers or federal agencies on reimbursement eligibility for AI-augmented services (TMA Health Information Technology Committee, 2024, Policy Review: Physician Perspectives on AI-Augmented Clinical Decision Support). The three barriers physicians identified most consistently were liability ambiguity when AI outputs inform clinical decisions, inconsistent payer coverage determinations, and the absence of standardized validation requirements for AI tools entering the market. These findings were gathered across six regional town halls that spanned both urban academic medical centers and rural critical-access hospitals throughout Texas. What this data tells us is not simply that deployment is outpacing regulation — though it is — but that deployment is outpacing the deliberative infrastructure required for democratic accountability. Clinicians are encountering systems whose governance terms they do not understand, whose internal logic they cannot access, and whose liability implications remain unresolved. That is not a temporary implementation gap that acceleration will eventually close. It is a structural condition that acceleration deepens.⏎⏎⏎APA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎⏎THE DEMOCRATIC PREEMPTION ARGUMENT⏎⏎⏎We want to be precise about what we mean when we say that HHS's acceleration agenda constitutes democratic preemption. We are not arguing that AI in healthcare is inherently illegitimate, or that the Department lacks authority to act in this space. We are arguing something more specific: that the decision about whether medicine should be organized around AI-augmented decision-making at scale is a decision that belongs to the public, and that HHS is making it unilaterally by treating acceleration as the default posture before the public has had any meaningful opportunity to deliberate on the question.⏎⏎Ronald Dworkin argued that certain domains of human dignity warrant protection from efficiency-based displacement as a matter of democratic principle — that a society's right to insist on human primacy in some domains is not a preference to be weighed against competing interests but a structural feature of democratic self-governance (Dworkin, 1977, Taking Rights Seriously, Harvard University Press). Medicine is one of those domains. So is criminal justice. So is education. The reason is not that efficiency is irrelevant in these domains — it plainly is relevant — but that the relationship between a patient and a clinician, or between a student and a teacher, or between a defendant and a court, carries a form of human accountability that algorithmic mediation structurally cannot replicate, and that the public has a legitimate interest in preserving even at efficiency cost. Eric Topol's influential work on AI in healthcare (Deep Medicine, 2019, Basic Books) has been widely cited in support of deployment optimism, and we do not dismiss it entirely. But Topol's framework understates the lock-in risk: the scenario in which AI frees clinicians to be more human by handling routine cognitive tasks assumes that the governance conditions for maintaining human primacy remain intact after deployment. Coiera's more cautionary analysis of the gap between AI deployment and sustainable clinical integration is instructive here — he has documented the way in which systems that appear to augment clinical judgment in controlled settings can gradually displace the judgment they were meant to support once they become infrastructure (Coiera, 2019, "The Last Mile: Where Artificial Intelligence Meets Reality," Journal of Medical Internet Research, 21(11), e16323). The augmentation narrative and the displacement outcome are not mutually exclusive; they can coexist within the same deployment trajectory, with augmentation characterizing the early phase and displacement characterizing the mature phase after institutional knowledge has atrophied.⏎⏎The RFI, by framing its central purpose as the acceleration of adoption, has already answered the question of whether medicine should move in this direction. It has done so not through legislation, not through notice-and-comment rulemaking on the fundamental governance question, and not through any process that would give the public a genuine opportunity to say that some clinical domains should remain stubbornly human. The Department has instead embedded the acceleration presumption in the architecture of the inquiry itself, so that every response to the RFI — including this one — is structurally positioned as commentary on how to accelerate rather than on whether to accelerate. We want to name that framing explicitly, because it is doing significant governance work that the Department has not acknowledged.⏎⏎⏎APA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎⏎WHAT A DEMOCRATICALLY LEGITIMATE PROCESS WOULD REQUIRE⏎⏎⏎The American Psychological Association is not in a position to enumerate every procedural requirement for a democratically legitimate federal approach to clinical AI. But we can identify several features that any such approach would need to include, based on the arguments above:⏎⏎1. A prior and separate deliberative process on the fundamental question of whether and to what extent AI-mediated decision-making should be embedded in core clinical functions, conducted with genuine public participation and not presupposing acceleration as the answer.⏎⏎2. Mandatory transparency requirements for any AI system used in clinical decision support, including disclosure of training data provenance, validation methodology, and known performance limitations, as a condition of market entry — not as a post-hoc aspiration.⏎⏎3. A statutory or regulatory moratorium on deployment in defined high-stakes clinical contexts — including inpatient psychiatric care, pediatric diagnosis, and end-of-life decision support — pending completion of the deliberative process described above.⏎⏎4. Federal investment in the research infrastructure needed to evaluate AI systems in community-based and rural clinical settings, where the TMA data suggests deployment is already occurring without adequate governance support, and where the populations served have historically had the least access to recourse when algorithmic systems produce harmful outcomes.⏎⏎5. An explicit acknowledgment by HHS that the acceleration posture reflected in the current RFI is itself a governance choice with irreversible consequences, and that the Department will not treat democratic scrutiny as an obstacle to innovation but as a precondition of legitimate policy.⏎⏎We recognize that some of these recommendations would require legislative action beyond the Department's current authority. We raise them nonetheless, because the appropriate response to a governance gap is not to proceed as though the gap does not exist.⏎⏎⏎APA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 5⏎⏎⏎A NOTE ON THE REIMBURSEMENT QUESTION⏎⏎⏎The RFI also solicits comment on reimbursement structures for AI-augmented clinical services. The APA has concerns about this dimension of the inquiry as well, though we address it only briefly here. The TMA survey finding that only 23% of physicians who had encountered clinical AI tools had received guidance on reimbursement eligibility (TMA HIT Committee, 2024) is not simply an implementation problem to be solved by clarifying CMS billing codes. It reflects a deeper governance failure: reimbursement policy is being asked to do the work of democratic legitimation, as if resolving the payer coverage question would also resolve the question of whether the deployment is appropriate. It will not. Reimbursement frameworks that create financial incentives for AI-augmented services before the deliberative process we describe above has occurred will accelerate the lock-in dynamic by introducing economic dependencies that further narrow the governance options available to future policymakers. The sequencing matters enormously, and the current RFI has the sequence inverted.⏎⏎We are also concerned that the framing of reimbursement questions in the RFI focuses almost entirely on reducing barriers to adoption rather than on the governance conditions that would need to be in place for adoption to be democratically accountable. This is consistent with the broader acceleration posture we have critiqued, but it is worth noting specifically in the reimbursement context because the economic infrastructure being built now will be extremely difficult to dismantle if the deliberative process eventually produces a different answer about the appropriate scope of clinical AI.⏎⏎⏎CONCLUSION AND DIRECT REQUEST⏎⏎⏎The American Psychological Association urges HHS to reframe this RFI — and any subsequent rulemaking it generates — around democratic legitimacy and precautionary governance rather than acceleration. The Department should acknowledge explicitly that the decision to embed AI at scale in clinical care is a permanent governance choice, not a reversible policy experiment, and that executing that choice without a democratic mandate is not a technical matter but a matter of constitutional and ethical accountability. The current framing treats the public's right to deliberate as friction to be reduced. We are asking HHS to treat it as the condition that makes any of this legitimate in the first place.⏎⏎Specifically, we request that HHS issue a supplemental request for comment focused exclusively on the question of democratic process — asking what deliberative mechanisms the public believes should precede large-scale clinical AI deployment, rather than asking only how to accelerate the deployment that the Department has already decided to pursue. That supplemental process should be designed in consultation with community organizations, patient advocacy groups, and the clinical professional associations that represent the practitioners who will be most directly affected, and it should be conducted on a timeline that allows genuine public participation rather than one calibrated to the pace of vendor deployment.⏎⏎The TMA data from 2024 documents 4,200 physicians already navigating AI tools without adequate governance infrastructure. That number will be larger by the time any rulemaking generated by this RFI takes effect. The window for democratic deliberation on the fundamental question is narrowing, and it is narrowing because of choices HHS is making right now about how to frame the inquiry.⏎⏎We would welcome the opportunity to discuss these concerns further with staff from ASTP/ONC or the Office of the Deputy Secretary. Contact information for the APA's policy division is available at⏎⏎www.apa.org/policy/health-it-contact♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0036♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔10♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret♔Wilson♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform♔♔♔♔♔♔McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform⏎55 East 52nd Street⏎New York, NY 10022⏎⏎Margaret Wilson, MD, MBA⏎Chief Medical Officer⏎⏎June 12, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Attn: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information -- Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Re: Comments of the McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform on the HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Health Systems Advisory Page 1 | HHS AI RFI | June 2024⏎⏎⏎THE ACCOUNTABILITY DIFFUSION PROBLEM IS STRUCTURAL, NOT INCIDENTAL⏎⏎The framing of this RFI treats acceleration as the primary policy goal and asks what regulatory, reimbursement, and research-and-development levers HHS can deploy in service of that goal. We want to be direct about our concern with that framing before addressing any of the specific questions posed: acceleration without a prior resolution of the accountability diffusion problem that AI-assisted clinical workflows create is not a neutral policy choice. It is a decision to scale a structural defect into the foundations of clinical care at a moment when the institutional capacity to detect and remedy that defect is still underdeveloped. Van Pelt & Company has worked with 47 health system clients through AI implementation planning and piloting, and what we observe across that portfolio is not primarily a story of regulatory friction impeding good-faith adoption. It is a story of institutions rationally declining to commit to operational deployment because the legal and institutional accountability architecture necessary to support that deployment does not yet exist.⏎⏎Our Q2 2024 portfolio review (Van Pelt & Company Health Systems Advisory, 2024, AI Implementation Readiness Assessment: Findings from Q2 2024 Portfolio Review Across 47 Health System Clients) found that 83% of our health system clients had initiated at least one AI-assisted clinical workflow pilot. Fewer than 12% had achieved full operational deployment beyond the pilot stage. When we asked chief medical officers across those same systems why, 91% cited regulatory ambiguity around liability attribution and reimbursement pathway uncertainty in their structured implementation reviews. And 68% reported that their organizations had paused or deferred AI procurement decisions specifically while waiting for clearer HHS guidance on clinical validation standards. We are aware that this data reflects a particular portfolio -- our clients tend toward larger, more sophisticated systems -- and we do not claim it is nationally representative. But the pattern is consistent enough, and the reasoning our clients articulate is coherent enough, that we think it warrants serious engagement rather than treatment as a friction problem to be smoothed over by clearer reimbursement signals.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Health Systems Advisory Page 2 | HHS AI RFI | June 2024⏎⏎⏎THE MALPRACTICE DOCTRINE PROBLEM⏎⏎The liability concern our clients raise is not a vague worry about litigation exposure. It is a specific structural problem: existing medical malpractice doctrine was designed around a care relationship in which the physician is the decision-maker and bears the accountability that flows from that role. As Price, Gerke, and Cohen argued in their 2019 JAMA analysis of physician liability for AI-assisted decisions, the interposition of an AI system between the physician's clinical judgment and the patient outcome creates an accountability gap that existing doctrine cannot resolve cleanly (Price, W.N., Gerke, S., & Cohen, I.G., 2019, Potential liability for physicians using artificial intelligence, JAMA, 322(18), 1765-1766). The physician who acts on an AI recommendation that turns out to be wrong may argue that reliance on a validated tool was reasonable. The vendor whose tool generated the recommendation may argue that the physician retained final decision authority. The hospital that procured and deployed the tool may argue that it exercised appropriate due diligence in selecting an FDA-cleared device. Each of these arguments has merit. That is precisely the problem. When every party in the chain has a plausible defense, the patient who was harmed is left without a clearly answerable party, and the deterrence function that malpractice law is supposed to perform -- the pressure that pushes institutions toward safer practices -- is correspondingly weakened.⏎⏎Mello, Frakes, Blumenkranz, and Studdert's 2020 review of malpractice liability and health care quality in JAMA documents the degree to which liability exposure, when properly structured, does generate clinically meaningful behavioral changes in provider institutions (Mello, M.M., Frakes, M.D., Blumenkranz, E., & Studdert, D.M., 2020, Malpractice liability and health care quality: a review, JAMA, 323(4), 352-366). The concern we raise is not that liability is a good mechanism for driving quality in all contexts. It is that the removal of effective liability exposure -- which is what accountability diffusion accomplishes in practice -- eliminates a structural incentive for careful AI deployment without substituting anything in its place. Gerke, Minssen, and Cohen's chapter on the ethical and legal challenges of AI-driven healthcare in the 2020 Academic Press volume on AI in Healthcare makes a similar point: when accountability is distributed across a network of actors, none of whom bears unambiguous legal exposure, the behavioral incentives that liability creates are dissipated across the network in ways that may produce no meaningful corrective pressure on any individual actor (Gerke, S., Minssen, T., & Cohen, G., 2020, Ethical and legal challenges of artificial intelligence-driven healthcare, in Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare, pp. 295-336, Academic Press).⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Health Systems Advisory Page 3 | HHS AI RFI | June 2024⏎⏎⏎The FDA's existing regulatory framework for software as a medical device, and the cautious steps toward AI/ML-specific guidance that Kesselheim and Avorn analyzed in their 2022 JAMA commentary, address pre-market validation and post-market surveillance requirements for AI tools as devices (Kesselheim, A.S., & Avorn, J., 2022, New 'watershed' FDA guidance on artificial intelligence and machine learning: cautious steps forward, JAMA, 328(14), 1401-1402). What that framework does not address -- and what ONC's HTI-1 final rule (Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology, 2023, Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability: Certification Program Updates, Algorithm Transparency, and Information Sharing (HTI-1) Final Rule, 88 Fed. Reg. 23746) addresses only partially through its algorithm transparency provisions -- is the question of who bears accountability when a validated, certified, algorithmically transparent tool contributes to patient harm in a clinical deployment context. Transparency about how a model works is not the same as clarity about who is responsible when it fails. Our clients understand this distinction, which is part of why transparency requirements alone have not resolved their procurement hesitation.⏎⏎The European Parliament's 2017 Delvaux Report on civil law rules on robotics (Delvaux, M., 2017, Report with recommendations to the Commission on Civil Law Rules on Robotics (2015/2103(INL)), European Parliament Committee on Legal Affairs) proposed a framework of strict liability with mandatory insurance for autonomous systems precisely because the drafters recognized that fault-based liability doctrine was architecturally incompatible with systems whose decision processes are distributed across design, training, deployment, and use. We are not arguing that the United States should adopt the European approach wholesale. But the European Parliament identified the right problem in 2017, and HHS's RFI, issued in 2024, does not appear to have fully grappled with it. Cohen, Evgeniou, Gerke, and Minssen's 2020 analysis in The Lancet Digital Health of the European AI strategy's implications for digital health makes the point that accountability architecture is a precondition for safe AI deployment, not an afterthought to be addressed once deployment has occurred (Cohen, I.G., Evgeniou, T., Gerke, S., & Minssen, T., 2020, The European artificial intelligence strategy: implications and challenges for digital health, The Lancet Digital Health, 2(7), e376-e379). That sequencing argument is the one we most want HHS to engage with.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Health Systems Advisory Page 4 | HHS AI RFI | June 2024⏎⏎⏎THE SEQUENCING FAILURE IN THE RFI⏎⏎What concerns Van Pelt & Company most about the structure of this RFI is the implicit assumption that the accountability and liability questions can be addressed in parallel with or subsequent to an accelerated adoption push, rather than as preconditions for it. We have seen this sequencing failure before in health IT policy. The meaningful use program under HITECH accelerated EHR adoption at a pace that outran the interoperability and data governance infrastructure necessary to make that adoption clinically useful, and the health system has spent the better part of a decade trying to retrofit the missing infrastructure onto a deployment base that was designed without it. We are concerned that an AI adoption acceleration program pursued without a prior resolution of the accountability diffusion problem will produce an analogous outcome: a large installed base of AI-assisted clinical workflows with no coherent legal framework for attributing responsibility when those workflows contribute to harm.⏎⏎The National Academy of Medicine's 2022 special publication on AI in health care is careful to note that the promise of AI in clinical settings is real but that the conditions necessary to realize that promise safely are not yet uniformly present across the health system (National Academy of Medicine, 2022, Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: The Hope, the Hype, the Promise, the Peril, Washington, DC: NAM Special Publication). Sendak and colleagues' 2020 analysis of clinical decision support with deep learning -- specifically their observation that the opacity of model reasoning creates challenges for the kind of physician oversight that is supposed to serve as the accountability backstop in current deployment models -- is directly relevant here (Sendak, M.P., Elish, M.C., Gao, M., Futoma, J., Ratliff, W., Nichols, M., et al., 2020, 'The human body is a black box': supporting clinical decision-making with deep learning, Proceedings of the ACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and Transparency, 99-109). If the physician cannot meaningfully evaluate the reasoning behind an AI recommendation -- and in many deep learning contexts, that is simply the technical reality -- then the assumption that physician oversight constitutes an adequate accountability mechanism is not well-founded. Obermeyer and Topol made a related point in The Lancet in 2021: the performance characteristics of AI tools in controlled validation settings do not reliably predict their behavior in the heterogeneous, resource-constrained, workflow-embedded contexts in which they are actually deployed (Obermeyer, Z., & Topol, E.J., 2021, Artificial intelligence, bias, and patients' perspectives, The Lancet, 397(10289), 2038-2039).⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Health Systems Advisory Page 5 | HHS AI RFI | June 2024⏎⏎⏎WHAT VAN PELT & COMPANY IS ASKING HHS TO DO⏎⏎We want to be specific about what we are requesting, because we are aware that "resolve the accountability problem before accelerating adoption" can sound like an argument for indefinite delay, and that is not our position. Our position is that HHS should condition any accelerated adoption incentives -- whether through expanded reimbursement, reduced regulatory burden, or R&D investment -- on the prior establishment of a pre-deployment accountability framework that does the following:⏎⏎1. Assigns primary legal accountability for AI-assisted clinical decisions to a designated responsible party within each deployment -- whether that is the deploying institution, the procuring health system, or a hybrid contractual arrangement with the vendor -- in a way that is legally cognizable under existing or modified state malpractice doctrine. The specific assignment mechanism matters less to us than the requirement that a single party bear non-diffusable primary accountability before any tool reaches operational deployment.⏎⏎2. Requires that the accountability assignment be disclosed to patients in a form they can act on. This is not merely a transparency gesture. If a patient is harmed by an AI-assisted clinical decision, they need to know who to hold responsible, and the current deployment model does not reliably give them that information.⏎⏎3. Establishes federal minimum standards for continuous post-deployment performance monitoring, with defined thresholds for mandatory suspension of AI tool deployment when performance metrics deteriorate beyond specified bounds in real-world clinical conditions. The FDA's existing post-market surveillance framework for AI/ML-based software as a medical device is a starting point, but it does not currently include mandatory deployment suspension triggers tied to real-world performance degradation.⏎⏎4. Creates a federal safe harbor for health systems that comply with the pre-deployment accountability framework, so that compliance with the framework's requirements -- including accountability designation, patient disclosure, and continuous monitoring -- is treated as evidence of reasonable care in subsequent malpractice proceedings. This safe harbor is the incentive structure that will actually move institutions toward compliance, and it is the mechanism that addresses the procurement hesitation our clients have expressed.⏎⏎We recognize that items 1 and 2 above implicate state tort law in ways that federal rulemaking cannot directly resolve. HHS cannot rewrite state malpractice doctrine. But HHS can make compliance with a federal accountability framework a condition of participation in any accelerated adoption program it creates, and it can work with the Department of Justice and relevant state attorneys general to develop model accountability designation language that is designed to be legally operative under the full range of state malpractice frameworks. That is a more tractable path than waiting for state courts to develop AI-specific malpractice doctrine through case-by-case litigation, which is the current trajectory.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Health Systems Advisory Page 6 | HHS AI RFI | June 2024⏎⏎⏎We want to be transparent about one thing we are genuinely uncertain about. There is a reasonable argument that the liability uncertainty our clients cite is at least partly a product of risk aversion that would resolve itself as the AI deployment base grows and courts begin to develop AI-specific doctrine through litigation. Under this view, the accountability gap is a transitional problem that does not require a legislative or regulatory solution -- it just requires time and a sufficient volume of deployment experience to generate the case law that will eventually clarify the rules. We have some sympathy for this argument. It is true that health care has absorbed other technological transitions -- laparoscopic surgery, robotic-assisted procedures, electronic prescribing -- without requiring a pre-deployment federal accountability framework, and that the accountability norms for those technologies were worked out through a combination of professional standards development, litigation, and gradual regulatory accretion.⏎⏎Our concern with the "let it develop organically" approach in the AI context is that the pace of deployment HHS is seeking to accelerate, combined with the opacity of many AI systems' reasoning processes, means that the harm-generating events that would drive case law development may occur at scale before the doctrine is stable. In the laparoscopic surgery context, the transition was slow enough and the accountability structure clear enough (the surgeon holds the tool; the surgeon is responsible) that the doctrine did not need to be pre-specified. AI-assisted clinical workflows do not have that clarity, and the acceleration this RFI contemplates would compress the timeline within which the accountability vacuum could cause widespread harm. That is the specific risk we are asking HHS to take seriously.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Health Systems Advisory Page 7 | HHS AI RFI | June 2024⏎⏎⏎A NOTE ON REIMBURSEMENT⏎⏎The RFI also asks about reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted clinical services, and we have a narrower point to make there. Van Pelt & Company's portfolio data shows that reimbursement uncertainty is consistently cited alongside liability uncertainty as a barrier to deployment, but in our structured client interviews, the two concerns operate differently. Liability uncertainty is a hard stop: institutions will not deploy tools into operational workflows when they cannot answer the question of who is legally responsible if the tool contributes to harm. Reimbursement uncertainty is a softer constraint: institutions will pilot AI tools in workflows where the reimbursement is already established for the underlying service, and they will defer deployment in workflows where the AI component would need to generate independent reimbursement to be economically viable.⏎⏎This distinction matters for policy design. Expanding reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted clinical services will accelerate deployment in the second category of workflows -- those where AI is not currently economically viable without independent reimbursement -- but it will not move the needle on deployment in the first category, where liability uncertainty is the binding constraint. HHS should not assume that reimbursement reform will function as a general-purpose accelerant for AI adoption. For the subset of institutions and workflows where liability uncertainty is the primary barrier -- and our data suggests that is a substantial subset -- reimbursement clarity will not be sufficient. The accountability framework we describe above needs to come first, or alongside, not after.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Health Systems Advisory Page 8 | HHS AI RFI | June 2024⏎⏎⏎We recognize that what we are describing would add time and process to an adoption trajectory that HHS is explicitly trying to accelerate. We do not think that is a reason to skip the accountability architecture. The 68% of our clients who have paused AI procurement decisions pending clearer federal guidance are not being obstructionist. They are being prudent. The absence of an accountability framework is not a gap they can paper over with good intentions or careful internal governance, because the gap is not internal to their organizations -- it is a structural feature of the legal and regulatory environment in which they operate. Giving those institutions clearer reimbursement signals without giving them a legally operative accountability framework will not resolve their hesitation. It will just add a new source of pressure to deploy into a risk environment that has not meaningfully changed.⏎⏎There is also a question about what accelerated deployment without accountability resolution does to the longer-term prospects for AI in clinical care. If the first wave of at-scale AI deployment produces a set of high-profile harm events in which patients cannot identify a responsible party, the political and regulatory backlash is likely to be severe and may set back AI adoption more than a deliberate pre-deployment accountability process would have. Van Pelt & Company's recommendation is that HHS treat the pre-deployment accountability framework as an investment in the durability of AI adoption, not as a cost imposed on it.⏎⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎⏎Margaret Wilson, MD, MBA⏎Chief Medical Officer⏎McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform⏎55 East 52nd Street⏎New York, NY 10022⏎mwilson@mckinsey-ushealthreform.org⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Health Systems Advisory Page 9 | HHS AI RFI | June 2024♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0037♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Hernandez♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔Google Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Google Health Comments | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎Google Health submits these comments in response to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care. Our organization operates across 47 clinical partner sites and, over a 90-day period ending Q2 2024, documented 2,340 discrete AI-assisted clinical decision events with clinician override rates declining from 34% to 11% as model transparency features were improved. That operational vantage point is precisely why this submission does not address the RFI's framing at face value. The Department's stated goal of fostering competition among clinical care AI tool builders proceeds from an assumption that the dominant commercial deployment of health-sector AI is in clinical decision support. It is not. The dominant commercial deployment is in prior authorization and utilization management, where AI functions as a financial instrument applied against the clinical record rather than in service of it. Any HHS framework that accelerates AI adoption without squarely addressing this reality will, in practice, accelerate harm. This submission addresses two areas where the Department has the clearest regulatory authority to act: reimbursement-linked AI accountability and utilization management oversight in federally regulated insurance markets. A more detailed technical appendix, including our full Q2 2024 operational analysis and site-level override rate data, is referenced throughout but not attached to this filing pending coordination with institutional review processes.⏎⏎Google Health Comments | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 2⏎⏎THE CORE PROBLEM THE RFI DOES NOT NAME⏎⏎Our operational data shows that 78% of physicians in our partner network cited inadequate reimbursement pathways as the primary barrier to expanding AI tool integration beyond pilot protocols. That is a real problem. But it sits inside a larger structural problem that the RFI's framing skirts: the AI systems that have achieved the broadest, fastest, and most consequential deployment in American health care are not the ones helping clinicians make better decisions at the bedside. They are the ones helping insurers make faster denial decisions at scale. The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations documented in October 2024 that Medicare Advantage insurers used algorithmic tools to deny post-acute care at rates that were, in the Subcommittee's own language, "not supported by the clinical record" (U.S. Senate PSI, 2024). A March 2023 investigative report by Schulte and Fry in STAT News and KFF Health News found that at least one major insurer's AI model denied claims after reviewing patient records for, on average, 1.2 seconds. The OIG reached similar conclusions in April 2022, finding that Medicare Advantage organization denials of prior authorization requests raised "significant concerns about beneficiary access to medically necessary care" in a statistically meaningful share of reviewed cases (OIG Report OEI-09-18-00260, 2022). The Kaiser Family Foundation documented in October 2023 that prior authorization burdens in Medicare Advantage had grown substantially over the preceding five years, with denial rates rising even as the underlying patient population's clinical complexity held roughly stable (Koma, Jacobson, & Neuman, 2023).⏎⏎What this means, practically, is that HHS is currently contemplating a framework to accelerate AI in clinical care at the same moment that AI deployed as a financial weapon by commercial insurers is systematically undermining the care environment in which clinical AI would operate. A physician using an AI-assisted diagnostic tool to recommend post-acute rehabilitation for a stroke patient is working against an insurer's AI system that may deny that recommendation in under two seconds without human review. The Congressional Budget Office estimated in 2023 that prior authorization policies reduce health care spending partly through appropriate utilization management and partly through care that patients simply give up pursuing after denial (CBO Working Paper 2023-07). The latter category is not efficiency. It is inaccessibility dressed as efficiency. Dafny and Lee wrote in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2024 that the misalignment of AI incentives in health care is structural, not incidental, and that regulatory frameworks addressing only the clinical deployment side while leaving utilization management AI unaccountable will produce a two-tier system where AI serves payers and burdens providers (Dafny & Lee, NEJM 390(8), 2024).⏎⏎Google Health Comments | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 3⏎⏎The populations most harmed by AI-assisted denial automation are also the populations least equipped to navigate appeals. The National Health Law Program documented in 2023 that algorithmic decision-making in Medicaid managed care systematically disadvantaged low-income beneficiaries who lacked the administrative capacity to file timely appeals (NHeLP, 2023). Obermeyer and colleagues demonstrated in Science in 2019 that a widely deployed health management algorithm systematically underestimated the care needs of Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent clinical complexity (Obermeyer et al., Science 366(6464), 2019). Fuse Brown and McCuskey argued in the Yale Journal of Health Policy, Law, and Ethics in 2023 that mandatory explainability requirements for automated prior authorization systems are not merely desirable but legally supportable under existing federal nondiscrimination frameworks, and that the absence of such requirements constitutes a regulatory gap with measurable patient harm consequences (Fuse Brown & McCuskey, 23(1) Yale J. Health Pol'y L. & Ethics, 2023). The AMA's 2023 prior authorization physician survey found that 94% of physicians reported care delays attributable to prior authorization processes, and a substantial share attributed those delays specifically to automated or AI-assisted denial workflows (AMA, 2023). Geruso, Layton, and Prinz documented in the American Economic Review: Applied Economics in 2023 that insurer contract design on the ACA exchanges reflected systematic risk selection behavior consistent with algorithmic screening of enrollees, which is a related but structurally parallel problem (Geruso, Layton, & Prinz, AEA Applied Econ. 15(2), 2023).⏎⏎Our organization's median observed delay between FDA clearance and institutional adoption across our 47 partner sites was seven months, driven in significant part by regulatory ambiguity around AI liability attribution. That delay is costly and addressable. But the Department should be clear-eyed that resolving it, while leaving utilization management AI unaccountable, would accelerate a system in which clinician-facing AI tools produce better recommendations that insurer-facing AI tools then deny faster. That outcome is not acceleration of clinical AI adoption in any meaningful sense.⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD REQUIRE⏎⏎The Department's regulatory authority in federally regulated insurance markets, including Medicare Advantage and ACA exchange plans, is sufficient to require the following without new legislation: demonstrated clinical appropriateness alignment for any AI tool used in prior authorization or utilization management decisions, meaning the tool's outputs must be validated against clinician-determined appropriate care standards on a prospective basis, not just internally by the insurer; mandatory human clinical review thresholds for any denial generated by an automated or AI-assisted process, with the reviewing clinician required to hold a license relevant to the clinical specialty at issue; and transparent denial audit mechanisms that produce publicly reportable data on denial rates by diagnosis code, demographic group, and model version. The seven-month adoption delay our partner sites experienced was caused partly by the absence of clear liability attribution rules for AI-assisted decisions. A parallel and more serious ambiguity exists on the insurer side, where no comparable framework currently requires insurers to disclose which decisions were made by algorithmic systems, at what confidence thresholds, or with what demographic validation data. The Department has the authority to close that gap through conditions of participation and plan contract requirements, and this RFI is an appropriate vehicle for committing to do so. Whether the specific regulatory mechanism is a condition of Medicare Advantage contract renewal, an update to the managed care regulations at 42 CFR Part 438, or a separate guidance document, the substantive requirement is the same. AI-assisted utilization management tools operating in federally regulated markets must meet a demonstrated clinical appropriateness standard before⏎⏎Google Health Comments | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 4⏎⏎they are permitted to issue denials at scale, and the Department's current silence on this point in the RFI framing is, frankly, a significant omission that this comment urges HHS to correct in any final action document. The technical appendix referenced above includes our proposed validation methodology for clinical appropriateness alignment testing, based on the 2,340 AI-assisted decision events in our partner network, though we note that our methodology was developed for clinical support AI rather than utilization management AI and would require modification before direct application to the insurer⏎⏎[document appears truncated at page boundary]♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0038♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Green♔♔Kansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: HHS AI Comment Submission -- George Green, Wichita KS⏎⏎My cardiologist at the Wichita VA retired in 2022, and the eight months I spent without a replacement cost me three ER visits and cost my buddy Carl Hutchins his life. I tell you that not to make a point about AI but because when HHS talks about accelerating technology in clinics, I hear people who have forgotten what a doctor's office actually does. A good doctor notices the way you're holding your shoulder, hears something in how you answer a question, and earns the kind of honesty that gets you an actual diagnosis. No algorithm catches that pause. Slow down, prove this works for real patients, and never let a machine be the last word on someone's life.⏎⏎--⏎George Green⏎Wichita, KS 67201♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0039♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Nelson♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔Oak Street Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Oak Street Health Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎Our member facilities in rural Arkansas have watched sepsis detection tools — validated at large academic systems in other states — flag patients inconsistently in ways that clinicians here have quietly started ignoring, which is its own kind of failure. The absence of mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing is not a minor gap in this framework; it is a structural guarantee that disparate harm will be discovered only after patients are already injured. We ask HHS to require equity auditing and bias surveillance as preconditions for any deployment pathway it endorses.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0040♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Flores♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔Abbott Laboratories♔♔♔♔♔♔Abbott Laboratories | HHS AI-RFI-2024 | Page 1⏎⏎Joshua Flores, Healthcare Administrator⏎Abbott Laboratories | Georgia Operations⏎⏎Fourteen months ago, our institution watched a clinician receive an AI-generated treatment flag that was wrong. When we tried to trace accountability, we got three different answers from three different parties: the vendor said the clinician should have overridden it, the clinician said the tool was presented as validated, and hospital counsel said the contract indemnified the vendor. Nobody was responsible. The patient had no recourse.⏎⏎Abbott Laboratories | HHS AI-RFI-2024 | Page 2⏎⏎HHS is proposing to accelerate deployment of these same systems before answering that question. That is not a regulatory gap — it is a structural failure that this RFI is making worse. The accountability vacuum we are describing is not theoretical. It exists in active clinical environments right now, and the pressure to adopt faster will deepen it. Before any deployment incentive is created, HHS must require:⏎⏎1. A named accountable party for each AI tool deployed in clinical settings.⏎2. Mandatory incident reporting when AI-assisted decisions contribute to patient harm.⏎3. Contractual liability standards that cannot be waived by vendor agreement.⏎⏎Abbott Laboratories | HHS AI-RFI-2024 | Page 3⏎⏎HHS should not issue reimbursement guidance or deployment support for any AI clinical tool until these requirements are codified. The current posture essentially subsidizes liability evasion, and we are asking HHS to stop treating accountability as something to figure out after♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0041♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paul♔Gonzalez♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paul Gonzalez⏎Home Health Aide⏎Portland, Oregon 97201⏎⏎March 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎THE ROSARIO PROBLEM, AND WHAT IT ACTUALLY TEACHES⏎⏎My client Rosario had a silent MI on a Tuesday morning in February 2023. Her color was fine. Her breathing was steady. She said she felt "off." I have been doing this work for nineteen years and I nearly missed it. What caught it was a remote cardiac monitoring flag her cardiologist had specifically ordered as part of her care plan — a physician-directed tool embedded in an ongoing clinical relationship, reviewed by a nurse practitioner who already knew Rosario's baseline. I want to be clear about what that story is, because I suspect HHS is reading it as an argument for deploying AI broadly in home health settings, and that reading is backwards. The monitoring worked because it was relational — anchored in a specific patient's history, ordered by a clinician who knew her, and interpreted by someone with the context to understand what the flag meant for this particular woman on this particular morning. Strip away those relational conditions and you do not have a safety tool. You have a notification that lands on a home health aide working alone in a house with no backup, no code team, and no way to know whether the algorithm flagging "anomalous heart rate" has ever been validated on a 74-year-old Latina woman with the specific comorbidity profile Rosario carries.⏎⏎The RFI frames AI adoption primarily as a question of productivity and acceleration. The word "accelerating" is right there in the title. I understand the administrative pressures behind that framing, but it is the wrong frame for what happens in a private home between a caregiver and a person who is frightened and sick. As Abraham Verghese has argued, the clinical encounter has value that is not reducible to its informational outputs — the physical and relational presence of a caregiver is itself therapeutic (Verghese, Health Affairs, 2014). The systematic review by Kelley et al. (PLOS ONE, 9(4), e94207, 2014) documented measurable outcome differences attributable specifically to the patient-clinician relationship, independent of treatment accuracy. When you insert an algorithmic intermediary between a caregiver and a patient — when the aide is now monitoring a dashboard rather than watching the person — you degrade that relationship in ways that have real clinical consequences, and the productivity gains you calculate do not subtract those losses. Danielle Ofri's work on the affective dimensions of clinical judgment makes a related point: what a caregiver reads in a patient's face, posture, and hesitation is not supplementary to clinical assessment, it is clinical assessment (Ofri, What Doctors Feel, Beacon Press, 2017).⏎⏎I also want to name something the RFI does not name, which is automation complacency. Parasuraman and Manzey (Human Factors, 52(3), 381-410, 2010) documented extensively how human operators who rely on automated systems over time lose the capacity for independent monitoring — they begin to trust the system's silence as confirmation that nothing is wrong. Eric Topol, who is generally optimistic about AI in medicine, specifically flagged this risk in his 2019 Nature Medicine piece on human-AI convergence: the atrophy of clinical skill in practitioners who are repositioned as algorithm overseers rather than independent reasoners. In a hospital, there are other clinicians, other layers. In a private home in southeast Portland at 7 in the morning, there is me. If my judgment atrophies because I have been trained to wait for a flag before I act on what I'm seeing, the person who pays for that is my client.⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD REQUIRE BEFORE ANY HOME HEALTH AI DEPLOYMENT⏎⏎The National Academies' Future of Nursing 2020-2030 report was direct about the equity risks in deploying technology in home and community settings without demonstrated safety in the specific populations served. Bickmore and Picard (ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction, 12(2), 293-327, 2005) showed that algorithmically-mediated interactions structurally cannot replicate the trust-building that occurs in long-term human relationships — and in home health, that trust is not incidental, it is the mechanism by which clients tell you things they would otherwise hide. Before HHS moves to accelerate AI adoption in home health settings, it should at minimum require:⏎⏎1. Demonstrated clinical validation in the specific patient populations and home-care contexts where deployment is proposed, not just hospital or clinic populations.⏎⏎2. Mandatory equity audits before deployment, with particular attention to performance disparities across racial, linguistic, and socioeconomic groups.⏎⏎3. Explicit accountability structures that keep a named, licensed clinician responsible for every AI-generated alert or recommendation that reaches a home health aide.⏎⏎4. Research into whether AI intermediation in home settings degrades relational care quality over time, before deployment, not after.⏎⏎What I am asking is that HHS not treat speed as a virtue here. The gap in Rosario's care that morning was not a technology gap. It was a staffing gap, a reimbursement gap, a continuity-of-care gap. I am not sure an alert on my phone would have⏎⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Paul Gonzalez⏎Portland, Oregon♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0042♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Wilson♔♔California♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My knee injury two years back in Fresno put me through eight months of scheduling calls, referral loops, and benefits navigation where every mistake traced back to nobody actually knowing my case, and I worry that replacing those administrative workers with software just makes the errors quieter and harder to fight.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0043♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Martinez♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔American Academy of Ophthalmology♔♔♔♔♔♔AMERICAN ACADEMY OF OPHTHALMOLOGY — COMMENTS IN RESPONSE TO HHS HEALTH SECTOR AI REQUEST FOR INFORMATION: ACCELERATING AI ADOPTION IN CLINICAL CARE⏎Docket Submitted on Behalf of: Rebecca Martinez, Campaign Director, American Academy of Ophthalmology⏎⏎⏎PRELIMINARY STATEMENT⏎⏎The American Academy of Ophthalmology (AAO) submits these comments with specific attention to the equity dimensions of AI deployment in clinical settings, with particular reference to the documented experience of perioperative and procedural nursing workforces as a signal of broader systemic risk. These comments focus primarily on RFI questions related to regulatory safeguards and reimbursement conditions, as those are the areas where AAO believes the Department's proposed framework is most inadequately developed.⏎⏎A 2024 survey of more than 2,800 perioperative registered nurses across 47 states, conducted by AORN, found that 63% reported direct exposure to at least one AI-assisted surgical or procedural tool in their facility within the preceding 12 months. Of that group, 78% received no formal competency training specific to those systems. AORN's internal policy working group subsequently reviewed vendor contracts and implementation protocols submitted by 14 member institutions and found that AI tool deployment timelines consistently outpaced the development of corresponding nursing scope-of-practice guidance by an average of 9.4 months. These are not hypothetical projections. These are conditions that exist right now, in operating rooms and procedural suites, and the HHS accelerationist framing does nothing to address them.⏎⏎⏎THE EQUITY PROBLEM IS STRUCTURAL, NOT INCIDENTAL⏎⏎The evidence that clinical AI systems reproduce and entrench health disparities is no longer preliminary. It is a documented pattern across multiple clinical domains, and the literature is consistent enough that treating equity as a post-deployment concern — something to be monitored and corrected once tools are in use — represents a policy choice to accept predictable harm to specific patient populations.⏎⏎Obermeyer et al. (Science, 2019;366(6464):447-453) demonstrated that a widely deployed commercial algorithm systematically underestimated the illness severity of Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent clinical need, effectively reducing Black patients' access to high-risk care management programs. The mechanism was not malicious design; it was the use of healthcare expenditure as a proxy for health need in a system where structural barriers suppress utilization among Black patients. The algorithm learned discrimination from the data, because the data encoded discrimination. Sjoding et al. (N Engl J Med, 2020;383(25):2477-2478) documented that pulse oximeters — devices that have been standard of care for decades — systematically overestimate arterial oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin tones, a failure mode that was embedded in clinical practice and then imported directly into AI systems that used pulse oximetry readings as input variables. Adamson and Smith (JAMA Dermatol, 2018;154(11):1247-1248) found that dermatology AI tools trained predominantly on lighter-skinned patient images showed substantially degraded performance on darker skin tones, a failure with direct implications for diagnostic accuracy in a specialty where visual pattern recognition is central to the task. These are not edge cases from poorly designed systems. These are published findings about tools that were deployed, used clinically, and in some instances reimbursed.⏎⏎The broader methodological problem is documented in Gianfrancesco et al. (JAMA Intern Med, 2018;178(11):1544-1547), who identified that electronic health record data used to train clinical AI systems is systematically incomplete for patients with lower socioeconomic status, limited English proficiency, and inconsistent care access — exactly the populations for whom accurate AI-assisted clinical decision support is most consequential. When training data underrepresents a population, the model learns less about that population, performs worse on it, and the performance gap is frequently invisible in aggregate accuracy metrics. Parikh, Teeple, and Navathe (JAMA, 2019;322(24):2377-2378) described this as a structural feature of how clinical AI is built and validated, not an anomaly. Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones (N Engl J Med, 2020;383(9):874-882) extended this analysis to show how race correction in clinical algorithms — the practice of adjusting clinical thresholds based on race as a proxy variable — embeds biological essentialism into AI decision support in ways that systematically disadvantage patients of color.⏎⏎⏎AUTOMATION BIAS AND THE DEPLOYMENT-VALIDATION GAP⏎⏎AAO American Academy of Ophthalmology Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎What makes this problem worse in practice is the mechanism Char, Shah, and Magnus described in the New England Journal of Medicine (2018;378(11):981-983): the authoritative presentation of AI outputs actively suppresses clinical skepticism. When a sepsis prediction score, a risk stratification flag, or a diagnostic suggestion appears on a clinical interface as a numeric output from an algorithmic system, clinicians are systematically less likely to question it than they would be to question an equivalent judgment from a colleague. This is not a character failing; it is a documented feature of how humans interact with automated systems. Wong et al. (JAMA Intern Med, 2021;181(8):1065-1070) validated this concern directly, finding that a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model — deployed across hundreds of hospitals — performed substantially worse in external validation than its internal benchmarks suggested, with alert systems generating high false-positive rates that led to alert fatigue and, critically, reduced clinical responsiveness to genuine high-acuity signals. The combination of disparate performance on marginalized patient subgroups and automation bias-driven suppression of clinical skepticism means that the patients most likely to be harmed by AI errors are also the patients for whom those errors are least likely to be caught.⏎⏎The validation-deployment gap compounds this. As Rajpurkar, Chen, Banerjee, and Topol documented (Nat Med, 2022;28(1):31-38), the majority of AI validation studies in healthcare are conducted at well-resourced academic medical centers with curated, relatively complete data sets and robust IT infrastructure. Performance in those settings does not predict performance in community hospitals, rural facilities, or safety-net institutions — which is precisely where a substantial portion of AORN's membership works, and where HHS's accelerated adoption pathway would push these tools fastest, because those are the settings where the cost-reduction case for AI is strongest. Wiens et al. (Nat Med, 2019;25(9):1337-1340) provided a roadmap for responsible machine learning deployment that explicitly prioritizes prospective validation across deployment contexts as a precondition for clinical use, not an aspirational goal. Pierson et al. (Nat Med, 2021;27(1):136-140) demonstrated that algorithmic approaches can actually reduce pain care disparities when designed with equity as a primary objective — but that this outcome requires intentional equity-centered design from the beginning of development, not retrospective adjustment. The GAO's 2022 report on AI in health care (GAO-22-104629) reached the same conclusion, noting that the absence of standardized pre-deployment performance requirements across demographic subgroups is a significant gap in the current regulatory framework.⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC POLICY DEMANDS⏎⏎AAO urges HHS to condition any reimbursement pathway, certification designation, or federal procurement preference for clinical AI tools on the following requirements:⏎⏎1. Mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing across demographic subgroups, including at minimum race, ethnicity, sex, age, socioeconomic status, and insurance status, conducted by an independent third party with results publicly reported at the subgroup level, not only as aggregate accuracy metrics.⏎⏎2. Post-deployment bias surveillance requirements, with defined reporting intervals and minimum sample size thresholds for subgroup analysis, as a condition of continued reimbursement eligibility. Surveillance findings should be submitted to a centralized HHS repository accessible to clinicians, researchers, and the public.⏎⏎3. Validation study requirements that include performance data from community hospitals, federally qualified health centers, and critical access hospitals, not only the academic medical centers where most current validation studies are conducted. Tools should be required to demonstrate acceptable performance in deployment-analogous settings before receiving reimbursement endorsement.⏎⏎o A note on implementation: the 9.4-month gap between AI deployment and corresponding scope-of-practice guidance documented in AORN's institutional analysis suggests that workforce integration requirements need to be developed in parallel with, not subsequent to, deployment authorization.⏎⏎These should be enforceable requirements with defined consequences for non-compliance, not voluntary best practices or aspirational guidelines. The current RFI framing treats equity considerations as one input among many in a broad cost-benefit calculus. AAO's position is that this framing is incorrect. When AI systems perform worse on patients who are already receiving lower-quality care, and when the mechanism of harm is invisible to the clinicians who might otherwise catch it, the ethical and public health calculus is not ambiguous.⏎⏎AAO notes that HHS has not proposed any specific mechanism for how post-deployment disparate harm would be detected, attributed to a specific AI system, or trigger any regulatory response. That gap in the current framework is the most pressing structural problem this RFI should address, and the Department's response to these comments should include a specific proposal for how that detection and attribution mechanism would work in practice, including which agency office would be responsible, what data infrastructure would be required, and on what timeline.⏎⏎aao_regulatory@aao.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0044♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Green♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My son Caleb spent fourteen months being seen by doctors who had all his labs, all his records, all the data anyone could want, and still nobody named what was wrong with him until a metabolic specialist in Baton Rouge sat with us for nearly two hours and noticed the way Caleb moved his hands when he was tired. I read a printout in that same waiting room about a hospital in New Orleans using an AI tool to flag a similar condition in one visit, and I will not pretend that didn't sting. But what finally helped Caleb was not faster pattern recognition. It was a doctor who kept asking questions when the answers were unclear, who called back on a Thursday evening because something hadn't sat right with her. I worry that if HHS frames good care as a productivity problem, and if physicians spend their days reviewing machine outputs instead of thinking for themselves, that kind of judgment gets quieter over time and patients end up with something that looks like medicine but isn't quite.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0045♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Hernandez♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔Optum♔♔♔♔♔♔Newton's Tree Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 1⏎⏎⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP-ONC⏎Attention: Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎⏎⏎RE: HHS Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎Newton's Tree submits these comments in response to HHS's request for information on accelerating the adoption of artificial intelligence in clinical care settings. Our organization works with 847 affiliated research and clinical innovation partners, and the empirical picture that work has produced over the past several years is what motivates this submission. A 2024 survey of those partners found that 68% identified reimbursement uncertainty as the primary barrier to deploying validated AI diagnostic tools at scale. Our policy analysis division reviewed 19 health system pilots conducted between 2022 and 2024 and documented that institutions operating under clearly defined reimbursement pathways achieved AI tool adoption rates 3.2 times higher than those navigating ambiguous coverage determinations, across more than 340,000 patient encounters in aggregate.⏎⏎We raise that data not to argue against reimbursement clarity — we are genuinely supportive of that work — but because the framing of this RFI concerns us in a specific way. The Department has organized this request around acceleration. That word appears throughout the document, and it is doing real work. It signals an orientation toward deployment speed as the primary policy objective, with equity and safety considerations positioned as secondary concerns to be addressed along the way. Newton's Tree's position is that this sequencing is wrong, and that the evidence base for that concern is substantial enough that HHS should revise its framework before finalizing any deployment pathway.⏎⏎⏎TRAINING DATA BIAS AS STRUCTURAL INHERITANCE⏎⏎⏎The core problem with deploying AI diagnostic tools at scale in the current policy environment is that the training data underlying most of these systems encodes decades of unequal clinical treatment. This is not a hypothetical. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan documented in Science in 2019 that a widely used commercial algorithm for managing population health — deployed across hundreds of health systems — systematically assigned lower risk scores to Black patients relative to white patients with identical measured health status, because the algorithm used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, and Black patients had historically received less care (Obermeyer et al., 2019, Science, 366(6464), 447-453). The bias was structural, not incidental. It was a direct product of training on historical utilization data that reflected systemic undertreatment.⏎⏎Gianfrancesco and colleagues identified the same category of problem in a 2018 review of EHR-derived machine learning datasets, noting that missing data patterns in electronic health records are themselves non-random — they correlate with socioeconomic status, insurance type, and care-seeking behavior in ways that introduce systematic distortion into any model trained on those records (Gianfrancesco et al., 2018, JAMA Internal Medicine, 178(11), 1544-1547). Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones extended this analysis in a 2020 New England Journal of Medicine piece examining how race correction embedded in legacy clinical algorithms propagates into downstream AI systems that incorporate those algorithms as inputs, compounding rather than correcting historical error (Vyas et al., 2020, NEJM, 383(9), 874-882). The Pierson et al. analysis published in Nature Medicine in 2021 found that unexplained pain disparities across racial groups were in part attributable to algorithmic patterns in how pain documentation and treatment were recorded in training datasets — patterns that a deployed AI system would learn and reproduce (Pierson et al., 2021, Nature Medicine, 27(1), 136-140).⏎⏎What this body of work establishes is that the training data problem is not a bug that individual developers can fix with better intentions. It is a structural inheritance from a healthcare system that has treated patients unequally for generations. When HHS accelerates deployment of AI tools trained on that data without mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing, the Department is not taking a neutral stance. It is making an active policy choice to scale that inheritance across the entire U.S. healthcare system, at a speed and at a scope that will make course correction substantially more difficult.⏎⏎⏎Newton's Tree Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 2⏎⏎⏎VALIDATION GENERALIZABILITY AND THE COMPOUNDING PROBLEM OF AUTOMATION BIAS⏎⏎⏎The second structural problem with the accelerationist framing is that AI validation studies conducted at well-resourced academic medical centers routinely produce performance metrics that do not hold when the same tools are deployed in community hospitals, rural settings, or safety-net institutions. This is not an edge case. Wong and colleagues published an external validation of the Epic Sepsis Model in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2021, examining performance across a major academic health system and finding that the model's real-world sensitivity for sepsis was substantially lower than the figures reported in the original validation study — and that the positive predictive value in the external validation context was low enough that the model generated large numbers of false alerts, creating alarm fatigue without proportionate clinical benefit (Wong et al., 2021, JAMA Internal Medicine, 181(8), 1065-1070). That is one of the most widely implemented proprietary AI tools in U.S. hospitals. Its external validation failure was documented only because a research team at a well-resourced institution chose to study it.⏎⏎The dermatology AI literature provides a parallel example. Adamson and Smith documented in 2018 that dermatology AI training datasets were dramatically skewed toward lighter skin tones, with several leading models trained on datasets containing less than 5% images of darker skin, raising serious concerns about diagnostic accuracy across the full patient population (Adamson & Smith, 2018, JAMA Dermatology, 154(11), 1247-1248). Seyyed-Kalantari and colleagues published a 2021 Nature Medicine study specifically examining chest radiograph AI systems and found systematic underdiagnosis bias in under-served patient populations across multiple models and multiple datasets, with the bias most severe for patients who were female, Black, Hispanic, or Medicare/Medicaid-insured (Seyyed-Kalantari et al., 2021, Nature Medicine, 27(12), 2176-2182). The racial bias documented in pulse oximetry measurement by Sjoding and colleagues in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 — showing that pulse oximeters systematically overestimated arterial oxygen saturation in Black patients relative to white patients, contributing to delayed COVID-19 treatment — is a direct analogue to what happens when a flawed measurement tool becomes embedded in clinical decision-making infrastructure without adequate population-level performance monitoring (Sjoding et al., 2020, NEJM, 383(25), 2477-2478).⏎⏎What compounds the generalizability problem is automation bias. Cabitza, Rasoini, and Gensini identified in a 2017 JAMA analysis that clinician decision-making is reliably altered by AI outputs even when those outputs are incorrect, because the authoritative presentation of algorithmic recommendations suppresses the skepticism that clinicians would otherwise apply (Cabitza et al., 2017, JAMA, 318(6), 517-518). Char, Shah, and Magnus made the same point in a 2018 New England Journal of Medicine piece, observing that the confidence with which AI systems present outputs — often without communicating the uncertainty intervals or population-specific performance degradation that should accompany those outputs — creates conditions in which clinician override becomes less likely precisely when the AI is performing worst (Char et al., 2018, NEJM, 378(11), 981-983). The GAO's 2023 report on AI in healthcare reached a similar conclusion, noting that performance monitoring requirements for deployed AI tools in clinical settings remain inconsistent and that no federal framework currently mandates post-deployment surveillance for algorithmic performance degradation across demographic subgroups (GAO-23-105980, 2023).⏎⏎Put these two problems together — validation studies that overstate real-world performance, and automation bias that suppresses clinician skepticism — and the equity implication is direct. Clinicians in community hospitals and rural settings, treating patient populations that were underrepresented in training data and absent from validation studies, will be least likely to question AI outputs that are least likely to be accurate for their patients. HHS's accelerationist posture, absent mandatory equity safeguards, will predictably produce the worst outcomes in precisely the settings that already bear the greatest burden of health disparities.⏎⏎⏎Newton's Tree Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 3⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC POLICY RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎⏎Newton's Tree is not asking HHS to slow AI adoption as such. Our organization works every day with clinical innovation partners who are deploying tools that produce real patient benefit. The ask here is more specific: that HHS treat equity auditing and post-deployment bias surveillance as preconditions for the reimbursement pathways and regulatory frameworks the Department is now designing, rather than as optional add-ons that can be addressed after the deployment architecture is in place.⏎⏎The specific policy changes Newton's Tree is requesting HHS address through this RFI process are:⏎⏎1.  Mandatory stratified performance reporting as a condition of any reimbursement pathway. Before any AI diagnostic or clinical decision support tool is eligible for Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement, the tool's developer should be required to submit performance metrics stratified by race, ethnicity, socioeconomic status (using a validated proxy measure), primary language, insurance type, and care setting (academic medical center versus community hospital versus rural or critical access hospital versus federally qualified health center). A tool that achieves 0.91 AUROC in a tertiary academic center validation study but has never been evaluated in a rural safety-net setting should not be reimbursed as though that gap does not exist.⏎⏎2.  Required post-deployment bias surveillance with public reporting. Reimbursement eligibility should be conditioned on the developer's commitment to ongoing monitoring of algorithmic performance across demographic subgroups, with results reported publicly on a schedule no less frequent than annual. The current commercial norm — in which post-deployment performance data is treated as proprietary — is inconsistent with the public interest when the tool in question is reimbursed by federal programs. The Epic Sepsis Model situation is instructive: the external validation failure documented by Wong et al. was identified only because researchers at the University of Michigan chose to study it. There is no systematic federal mechanism that would have caught that failure otherwise.⏎⏎3.  Independent equity auditing standards developed with community input. HHS should convene a process — with meaningful representation from patient advocacy organizations, community health centers, historically Black colleges and universities with health sciences programs, and rural health providers — to develop equity auditing standards before any deployment framework is finalized. The standards should specify what a pre-deployment equity audit must include, who is qualified to conduct it, and what performance thresholds must be met across stratified subgroups before a tool can be considered validated for general deployment. This process should not be delegated entirely to industry working groups, whose incentive structures do not align with the populations most at risk from deployment failures.⏎⏎4.  Explicit performance floor requirements for underrepresented populations. An AI tool should not be eligible for reimbursement if its performance in any federally defined underserved population falls below a specified floor relative to its performance in the overall validation population. The exact threshold is a technical question that should be determined through the community input process described above, but the principle is that aggregate performance metrics should not be permitted to mask subgroup performance failures.⏎⏎o  Newton's Tree would note that this requirement will create real development costs for some vendors. We consider that an acceptable consequence of responsible deployment policy.⏎⏎o  The alternative — reimbursing tools that perform well on aggregate metrics while systematically underperforming for Black, Hispanic, rural, and low-income patients — would use federal dollars to entrench existing disparities at a scale that no subsequent corrective action is likely to fully reverse.⏎⏎⏎Newton's Tree Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 4⏎⏎⏎CLOSING OBSERVATION⏎⏎⏎The framing problem with this RFI is worth naming directly. HHS has asked the public how to accelerate AI adoption. That question presupposes that acceleration is the right goal, and it structures the comment process around identifying barriers to speed. Newton's Tree's view is that the Department should be asking a different question: how do we build a deployment framework that produces equitable clinical outcomes at scale, and what pace of adoption is consistent with that goal? Those are not the same question, and the answers are not the same.⏎⏎Our organization is aware that the survey data from our own partners shows reimbursement uncertainty as the dominant barrier to deployment. We take that finding seriously. But the institutions reporting reimbursement uncertainty are largely well-resourced health systems with the capacity to conduct their own internal AI governance. The 340,000-patient encounter dataset from our pilot review does not include adequate representation from critical access hospitals or federally qualified health centers, and we have noted that limitation in our internal analyses. The populations most likely to be harmed by premature deployment are not the populations whose voices are most audible in an RFI comment process, and Newton's Tree thinks HHS should weight that asymmetry deliberately in how it reads the record.⏎⏎The specific concern is this: once reimbursement pathways are established and deployment at scale begins, the political economy of course correction becomes extremely difficult. Developers will have made infrastructure investments. Health systems will have built workflows around specific tools. Reversing or substantially modifying a reimbursement-eligible tool will face resistance from every party that has organized around it. The time to require stratified performance reporting and independent equity auditing is before those pathways are established,⏎⏎Newton's Tree Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 5⏎⏎not after the first wave of deployment has already reached tens of millions of patients. We are genuinely uncertain whether the Department appreciates how narrow that window is, and we would welcome a direct conversation with ASTP/ONC staff about the timeline the Department is currently contemplating for finalizing any reimbursement framework.⏎⏎Contact for follow-up:⏎⏎Ashley Hernandez⏎Director of Government Relations, Optum / Newton's Tree Policy Division⏎ahernandez@newtons-tree.org⏎[URL malformed in paste — see contact page at newtons-tree.org/policy]♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0046♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Nelson♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network♔♔♔♔♔♔ACS CAN Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎The American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network (ACS CAN) appreciates the opportunity to respond to the Department's Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings (89 FR, HHS-2024-AI-001). ACS CAN represents the advocacy arm of the American Cancer Society, and our constituency includes cancer patients, survivors, caregivers, and the oncology clinicians who serve them across all fifty states, including a substantial presence in rural Tennessee and Appalachian communities where the texture of clinical relationships differs markedly from what any productivity model can capture.⏎⏎ACS CAN offers this comment with genuine respect for the Department's intent. There are specific, narrow applications of AI in clinical oncology — automated flagging of screening intervals, administrative prior authorization support, certain radiology-adjacent pattern recognition tasks — where the evidence of benefit is real and the deployment is warranted. Our members who work in radiology practice administration have reported direct experience with AI-assisted diagnostic imaging tools, and we do not dismiss that value. What we are asking HHS to do is to stop there, at least for now, and resist the temptation to extend an accelerationist logic that was developed for administrative throughput into the relational core of clinical care.⏎⏎ACS CAN Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE PROBLEM WITH "PRODUCTIVITY" AS A CLINICAL FRAME⏎⏎The Department's RFI uses the word "productivity" in describing the goals of AI adoption. ACS CAN wants to address that framing directly, because it is not a neutral word. Productivity is a manufacturing concept. It measures units processed per unit time. Applied to clinical care, it implicitly treats patients as throughput and clinicians as efficiency nodes — a conception of medicine that Pellegrino and Thomasma warned against as far back as 1988 in For the Patient's Good: The Restoration of Beneficence in Health Care, and that has not become more defensible in the intervening decades. When HHS frames AI as a tool to improve productivity, the Department is, perhaps unintentionally, signaling a category error about what medicine is.⏎⏎The patient who sits across from an oncologist after a biopsy result is not a unit of throughput. What happens in that room — the clinician's reading of posture and silence, the family dynamic that shifts the conversation, the moment when a patient cannot name her fear but her body communicates it clearly — is not inefficiency. It is the clinical encounter itself. Ridd et al. (2009), in a synthesis of qualitative literature on the patient-doctor relationship published in the British Journal of General Practice (59[561], e116-e133), documented that patients consistently identify relational continuity as inseparable from their sense of being cared for safely. Kelley et al. (2014), in a systematic review and meta-analysis published in PLOS ONE (9[4], e94207), found measurable outcome effects attributable to the patient-clinician relationship independent of technical intervention. Hojat et al. (2011) demonstrated in Academic Medicine (86[3], 359-364) that physician empathy predicted significantly better glycemic control in diabetic patients. These are not soft findings. They are clinical data, and they argue for caution before any deployment that repositions the clinician as an overseer of machine outputs rather than an independent reasoner.⏎⏎ACS CAN Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 3⏎⏎⏎THE DESKILLING PROBLEM IS NOT THEORETICAL⏎⏎Parasuraman and Manzey (2010), writing in Human Factors (52[3], 381-410), documented that automation bias — the tendency to over-rely on automated outputs even when those outputs are incorrect — compounds in proportion to the frequency and duration of automation use. Goddard et al. (2012), in a systematic review published in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association (19[1], 121-127), confirmed that automation bias is not corrected by training alone and that its effects accumulate across clinical populations. Berner and Graber (2008), in the American Journal of Medicine (121[5 Suppl], S2-S23), identified overconfidence as a structural feature of how clinicians relate to decision-support outputs, a finding that connects to Tversky and Kahneman's foundational work on heuristics and cognitive bias (Science, 185[4157], 1124-1131, 1974). Even Topol (2019), one of the more measured advocates for clinical AI, acknowledged in Nature Medicine (25[1], 44-56) that the relational dimensions of medicine are precisely those where AI offers the least and risks the most. Shortliffe and Sepúlveda (2018), writing in JAMA (320[21], 2199-2200), called for deployment frameworks that preserve clinician reasoning rather than substitute for it. Verghese (2008), in the New England Journal of Medicine (359[26], 2748-2751), described the clinical examination as a ritual of human connection whose erosion carries costs that do not appear in any billing code.⏎⏎ACS CAN's specific concern is that the Department's current framing would accelerate these risks without requiring any demonstration that relational safety has been preserved.⏎⏎⏎NUMBERED RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎ACS CAN respectfully urges HHS to consider the following in developing any final policy guidance:⏎⏎1. Replace the productivity framing in all subsequent AI policy documents with language that centers clinical safety, relational integrity, and patient-reported experience as primary metrics.⏎⏎2. Require that any AI application receiving Medicare payment incentives or deployment mandates demonstrate not only technical accuracy but measurable preservation of the clinician-patient relationship, using validated instruments such as the Consultation and Relational Empathy (CARE) Measure or equivalent.⏎⏎3. Establish a precautionary review threshold for any AI deployment that would alter the sequence or content of direct clinical encounter time, with independent review by a body that includes patient advocates, not only technical experts.⏎⏎4. Commission a longitudinal study on clinical deskilling effects before extending payment incentives to AI tools that operate in the interpretive, diagnostic, or communicative dimensions of care — not just administrative or scheduling functions.⏎⏎A full bibliography and supporting policy analysis are attached as Appendix A, which is available upon request from ACS CAN's federal policy office at the contact information below.⏎⏎policy@acscan.org⏎American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network⏎555 11th Street NW, Suite 300⏎Washington, DC 20004⏎⏎ACS CAN notes that the Department has not, in this RFI, specified how it intends to define "clinical care" for purposes of determining which AI applications fall within scope, and that ambiguity in that definition alone could affect the application of any precautionary framework in ways that♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0047♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Gonzalez♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔Patient Privacy Rights Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔Patient Privacy Rights Foundation⏎Comments Submitted to the HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL-RFI⏎Submitted by: William Gonzalez, Policy Director⏎⏎wwww.patientprivacyrights.org | comments@patientprivacyrights.org⏎⏎Patient Privacy Rights Foundation | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎⏎THE FRAMING PROBLEM THIS RFI DOES NOT ACKNOWLEDGE⏎⏎The Patient Privacy Rights Foundation appreciates the Department's interest in understanding how regulatory and reimbursement structures shape AI adoption in clinical settings. Regulatory clarity does matter. Uncertainty about liability frameworks and validation standards has real costs, and our organization does not dispute that. But the framing of this RFI — organized almost entirely around "accelerating" AI adoption as though speed is the governing policy value — treats a contested premise as settled ground, and that is a problem we cannot let pass without direct comment. The RFI's language about "fostering competition among clinical care AI tool builders" appears, for instance, in a document that contains no corresponding language about the deployment of AI in insurance denial processing, prior authorization adjudication, or coverage determination. This is not a minor omission. It is, we would argue, the central analytical failure of this solicitation. HHS cannot coherently develop policy to accelerate AI in clinical care while remaining silent on the parallel and already-deployed use of AI as a financial weapon against the patients that clinical care is meant to serve.⏎⏎The Department has in its own possession the evidence it needs to understand why this silence is indefensible. The HHS Office of Inspector General documented in April 2022 that some Medicare Advantage organizations were denying prior authorization requests in patterns that raised serious concerns about beneficiary access to medically necessary care (U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of Inspector General, OEI-09-18-00260, April 2022). That report preceded by roughly eighteen months the Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations' far more detailed examination, which found that major insurers had deployed AI tools to systematically override physician judgments at scale, with denial rates that could not be explained by clinical criteria alone (U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations, S. Prt. 118-22, October 2023). The Senate report named specific insurers and specific algorithms. It documented internal communications. And yet this RFI, published by the same Department whose Inspector General raised those flags two years earlier, does not once grapple with the denial-automation context. We find that genuinely difficult to explain.⏎⏎Patient Privacy Rights Foundation | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎⏎WHAT THE EVIDENCE ACTUALLY SHOWS ABOUT AI IN INSURANCE DENIAL PROCESSING⏎⏎The Kaiser Family Foundation's March 2024 issue brief on Medicare Advantage prior authorization denial rates documented that beneficiaries who appeal AI-assisted denials face a structurally disadvantaged process: denial rates at the initial stage run substantially higher than in traditional Medicare, and the appeals burden is designed — whether intentionally or not — to be prohibitive for elderly, disabled, and low-income patients who lack the administrative capacity to navigate multi-stage review (Koma, W., Cubanski, J., & Neuman, T., Kaiser Family Foundation Issue Brief, March 2024). The low reversal rate on appeal is frequently cited as evidence that initial denials are correct. Our organization reads it differently. A low reversal rate in a system where fewer than a statistically meaningful fraction of denied beneficiaries ever complete the full appeals process tells us almost nothing about accuracy. It tells us about attrition. The Center for Medicare Advocacy documented in January 2024 that systematic patterns of automated denial correlate strongly with beneficiary characteristics that have nothing to do with clinical necessity — age, dual-eligibility status, geographic region — and recommended specific regulatory remedies that HHS has not yet acted upon (Center for Medicare Advocacy, Policy Report, January 2024). The Congressional Budget Office's April 2024 working paper on prior authorization trends in Medicare Advantage further confirmed that the financial incentives structuring insurer behavior in this space create systematic pressure toward denial optimization rather than clinical accuracy (Congressional Budget Office, CBO Working Paper 2024-03, April 2024).⏎⏎This matters for the RFI's questions about reimbursement because the same reimbursement architecture that this solicitation asks how to reform is currently being gamed, in documented ways, by AI tools that HHS has not regulated. The American Medical Association's 2023 prior authorization survey found that 94 percent of physicians reported that prior authorization burdens had delayed necessary patient care, and a substantial proportion reported that AI-assisted denial processes were specifically responsible for clinically unjustifiable outcomes (American Medical Association, AMA Policy Research Perspectives, 2023). Siddhartha Mukherjee's February 2024 ProPublica investigation documented specific cases in which algorithmic denial systems flagged and denied treatments that the patient's own physician had ordered, with no meaningful human clinical review interposed before the denial became operative (Mukherjee, S., ProPublica Health Investigations Series, February 2024). The question this RFI should be asking — and is not — is whether HHS will require that a qualified human clinician review and affirm any AI-generated prior authorization denial before it becomes final. The existing framework under 42 C.F.R. § 422.566–422.574 governs organization determinations and appeals in Medicare Advantage but does not specify the clinical review requirements that must precede an AI-assisted initial denial. That gap is not technical. It is a policy choice, and it is currently costing patients access to care.⏎⏎Patient Privacy Rights Foundation | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎⏎THE EQUITY DIMENSION THAT THIS SOLICITATION UNDERWEIGHTS⏎⏎Our organization wants to be precise about the demographic stakes here, because the RFI's framing of AI adoption as broadly beneficial to patients glosses over a body of research that complicates that story substantially. Obermeyer and colleagues documented in Science in 2019 that a widely-deployed commercial algorithm used to manage population health systematically underestimated the care needs of Black patients, producing racially disparate outputs from facially neutral inputs (Obermeyer, Z., Powers, B., Vogeli, C., & Mullainathan, S., Science 366(6464): 447–453, 2019). That finding was published more than five years ago. The regulatory response — at HHS, at CMS, at ONC — has been incomplete at best. The National Health Law Program's September 2023 policy brief catalogued the enforcement gaps that persist across federal agencies with jurisdiction over algorithmic discrimination in health insurance, and concluded that existing legal frameworks are insufficient to address the specific mechanisms through which AI systems produce disparate outcomes in coverage and care decisions (National Health Law Program, NHeLP Policy Brief No. 47, September 2023). Geruso and Layton's analysis of upcoding behavior in Medicare Advantage risk adjustment is also relevant here, because it documents the broader pattern of financial optimization behavior among Medicare Advantage insurers that provides the organizational context within which AI denial tools operate — these are not isolated technical errors, they are rational responses to incentive structures (Geruso, M. & Layton, T., Journal of Political Economy 128(3): 984–1026, 2020).⏎⏎The RFI asks how HHS can support clinical AI adoption in ways that are equitable. Our answer is that equity in clinical AI adoption is not achievable while AI-assisted denial systems operate without algorithmic transparency requirements, without mandatory equity audits, and without the kind of human clinical review backstop that basic medical ethics would seem to require. We recognize there is a tension here: the same regulatory clarity that would help responsible clinical AI developers bring validated tools to market would also, if poorly designed, entrench incumbent insurer AI systems that are currently operating in a low-oversight environment. HHS should be aware that the institutions most prepared to comply quickly with new AI governance frameworks are not necessarily the institutions whose AI use most urgently requires oversight.⏎⏎Patient Privacy Rights Foundation | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC REGULATORY ACTIONS WE ARE REQUESTING⏎⏎The Patient Privacy Rights Foundation is requesting that HHS take three specific actions in response to this RFI, and we want to be direct about why we believe they are necessary. First, HHS should issue guidance under 42 C.F.R. § 422 that explicitly requires a qualified human clinician to review and affirm any AI-generated prior authorization denial before that denial becomes final and communicated to the beneficiary. The current regulatory framework does not require this, and the consequences of that gap are documented above. Second, HHS should impose algorithmic transparency requirements on Medicare Advantage organizations that deploy AI in coverage determination processes — requirements that include disclosure of the algorithm's training data sources, validation methodology, and documented accuracy rates stratified by race, age, disability status, and dual-eligibility classification. This is not an unreasonable ask. It is the minimum informational basis for any meaningful oversight. Third, HHS should institute a moratorium on new AI denial system deployments by Medicare Advantage organizations pending completion of demonstrated equity and accuracy audits conducted by independent third parties, with results submitted to CMS for review.⏎⏎We are aware that some stakeholders will characterize these requests as obstacles to innovation. The precedent established by Schulte and Fry's documentation of electronic health record implementation failures is instructive here: when a technology with significant patient safety implications is deployed at scale without adequate governance infrastructure, the resulting harms are real, they fall disproportionately on vulnerable populations, and they take years to remediate (Schulte, F. & Fry, E., Kaiser Health News & Fortune, March 2019). Our organization is not opposed to AI in health care. We are opposed to the deployment of AI systems in high-stakes coverage determination contexts without the governance frameworks that the documented harms clearly warrant. The RFI's timeline for comment closes in a matter of weeks, and we note that the Department has not, to our knowledge, scheduled any public hearings specifically focused on the insurance denial AI context — a gap in the engagement process that we would ask HHS to reconsider before finalizing any policy⏎⏎Patient Privacy Rights Foundation | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 5⏎⏎responses to this solicitation.⏎⏎REFERENCES⏎⏎Center for Medicare Advocacy. Automated Denials in Medicare Advantage: Documentation of Systematic Patterns and Recommended Regulatory Remedies. Policy Report, January 2024.⏎⏎Congressional Budget Office. Prior Authorization in Medicare Advantage: Trends, Costs, and Policy Options. CBO Working Paper 2024-03, April 2024.⏎⏎Geruso, M. & Layton, T. Upcoding: Evidence from Medicare on Squishy Risk Adjustment. Journal of Political Economy 128(3): 984–1026 (2020).⏎⏎Koma, W., Cubanski, J., & Neuman, T. Medicare Advantage Prior Authorization Denial Rates and Appeals Outcomes. Kaiser Family Foundation Issue Brief, March 2024.⏎⏎Mukherjee, S. The Algorithm Will See You Now. ProPublica Health Investigations Series, February 2024.⏎⏎National Health Law Program. Algorithmic Discrimination in Health Insurance: Legal Frameworks and Enforcement Gaps. NHeLP Policy Brief No. 47, September 2023.⏎⏎Obermeyer, Z., Powers, B., Vogeli, C., & Mullainathan, S. Dissecting Racial Bias in an Algorithm Used to Manage the Health of Populations. Science 366(6464): 447–453 (2019).⏎⏎Schulte, F. & Fry, E. Death by a Thousand Clicks. Kaiser Health News & Fortune, March 2019.⏎⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of Inspector General. OEI-09-18-00260, April 2022.⏎⏎U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations. S. Prt. 118-22, October 2023.⏎⏎42 C.F.R. § 422.566–422.574 [citation to appeals subsections incomplete — full regulatory text available at ecfr.gov].♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0048♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Lewis♔♔Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark Lewis⏎Vienna, Virginia 22182⏎⏎Submitted to: HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP-ONC⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎I. THE COORDINATION PROBLEM THAT ALREADY EXISTS⏎⏎When my mother was discharged from Inova Fairfax Hospital following her second stroke in October 2022, I spent the better part of three weeks attempting to reconcile care instructions that had been issued by five different specialists who, as far as I could tell, had never compared notes in any systematic way. The pulmonologist's dosing schedule for her corticosteroid taper conflicted with the neurologist's anticoagulation guidance. The physical therapy intake form assumed a degree of upper-body mobility she had not had since the first stroke. I kept a color-coded binder — an actual three-ring binder, with printed tabs — because the discharge packet was not coherent enough to function as a single document. I had the flexibility to do that. I work from home, I have a graduate degree, and I can read clinical notes without having to look up every third term. Most family caregivers I encountered in that neurology waiting room did not have those advantages.⏎⏎I am raising this not as a complaint about the clinicians involved, many of whom were excellent. The problem was structural. Responsibility for my mother's coordinated care was distributed across multiple institutional actors in a way that made it genuinely unclear who was accountable for the overall picture. The HHS Office of the National Coordinator acknowledged this kind of coordination failure in its 2020 Strategy on Reducing Regulatory and Administrative Burden Relating to the Use of Health IT and EHRs, which identified fragmented clinical information flows as a persistent source of patient safety risk. The strategy proposed workflow improvements. It did not resolve the underlying question of who is legally and institutionally responsible when fragmentation causes harm.⏎⏎That unresolved question is the one this RFI should be answering before it proposes to accelerate anything.⏎⏎⏎II. WHAT "ACCELERATION" MEANS IN THIS CONTEXT⏎⏎The RFI's framing is worth examining directly. HHS is asking what regulatory, reimbursement, and research-and-development actions the Department can take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. The word "accelerate" does real work in that sentence. It presupposes that the primary obstacle to beneficial AI adoption is regulatory friction, and that removing or reducing that friction is the appropriate policy response. I want to be careful here, because I do not think that framing is entirely wrong. There are AI applications in radiology and pathology that appear to improve diagnostic accuracy, and the GAO documented several of these in its 2020 report on AI in health care (GAO-21-7SP), acknowledging both the potential benefits and the significant governance challenges that remained unresolved at that time.⏎⏎But the acceleration framing has a structural problem that this comment is specifically about. When a clinical decision is made by a physician, the legal framework for accountability is reasonably well-established. Negligence doctrine, respondeat superior, hospital credentialing requirements, state medical board oversight: these are imperfect instruments, but they assign liability to identifiable human actors and institutions. When an AI system participates in that decision, the chain of proximate cause becomes harder to trace. Tobia et al., writing in the Journal of Law and the Biosciences in 2021, document this in detail, arguing that AI intermediation in clinical decision-making strains existing negligence doctrine in ways that current tort law is not equipped to resolve. The question of whether the physician, the hospital, or the AI vendor bears liability for a harmful AI-assisted recommendation is genuinely unresolved in most jurisdictions. Accelerating adoption before that question is answered is a policy choice with foreseeable consequences.⏎⏎⏎III. THE LIABILITY VACUUM⏎⏎What concerns me is not that AI tools are being developed or even deployed. It is that they are being deployed into a liability structure that was not designed to accommodate them, and HHS appears to be proposing to move faster rather than slower in that environment.⏎⏎Frank Pasquale described this dynamic with precision in The Black Box Society (Harvard University Press, 2015): algorithmic intermediaries create institutional insulation from accountability because the complexity of their outputs makes it difficult to assign responsibility for specific outcomes to specific actors. In a clinical context, that insulation has direct consequences for patients. If an AI-assisted triage recommendation leads to a delayed diagnosis, the patient's family will face the following sequence of questions when they try to understand what happened:⏎⏎1. Did the physician override the AI recommendation, and if so, was that override documented?⏎2. If the physician followed the AI recommendation, did the recommendation reflect a defect in the underlying model, a data input error, or a known limitation that was not communicated at the point of care?⏎3. Was the AI system FDA-cleared for this specific clinical application, and if so, does FDA clearance constitute any form of warranty against the harm that occurred?⏎4. Is the hospital liable for deploying the system, the vendor liable for developing it, or the physician liable for relying on it without sufficient independent verification?⏎⏎These are not hypothetical questions. They are the questions that a family caregiver would have to answer to pursue any accountability claim, and the current regulatory environment provides no clear path through them. Char, Shah, and Magnus identified this ethical challenge in their 2018 New England Journal of Medicine piece on implementing machine learning in health care, noting that opacity in AI decision-making creates specific problems for informed consent and for post-hoc attribution of clinical responsibility. Price and Cohen, writing in Nature Medicine in 2019, made a related point about accountability gaps in algorithmic clinical tools, observing that existing privacy and liability frameworks were not designed with AI intermediation in mind and that the gap between existing law and current deployment practice was already wide.⏎⏎The gap has not closed. The RFI does not propose to close it. It proposes to accelerate adoption into it.⏎⏎⏎IV. THE SPECIFIC PROBLEM WITH AI LAYERED ONTO COORDINATION FAILURES⏎⏎I want to return briefly to the coordination problem I described in Section I, because I think the interaction between existing fragmentation and AI intermediation is underappreciated in the RFI's framing.⏎⏎My mother's care was already fragmented across five specialists when she was discharged. The discharge documentation was already inconsistent. The cognitive load of reconciling those inconsistencies fell on me, a family member with no clinical training and no formal role in the care team. Now consider what would have happened if an AI-assisted care coordination tool had been layered onto that environment. The optimistic scenario is that the AI would have flagged the dosing conflict between the pulmonologist and the neurologist and surfaced it for resolution. That is a genuine potential benefit, and I do not dismiss it.⏎⏎The less optimistic scenario is that the AI would have generated a synthesized care plan that appeared coherent but reflected one specialist's inputs more heavily than another's, without any documentation of that weighting. Or that the AI's recommendation would have been logged in the EHR in a way that subsequent clinicians treated as authoritative, without any clear record of which human had reviewed and approved it. Obermeyer and Emanuel made the foundational observation in their 2016 New England Journal of Medicine piece on big data and clinical medicine that opacity in algorithmic decision-making specifically complicates the attribution of clinical outcomes. When something goes wrong in an AI-assisted care pathway, the question of which human made which decision, and on what informational basis, becomes genuinely difficult to reconstruct.⏎⏎That difficulty is not a technical problem to be solved by better logging. It is a structural accountability problem that requires a regulatory solution prior to broad deployment.⏎⏎⏎V. WHAT HHS SHOULD REQUIRE BEFORE PERMITTING AI IN CLINICAL DECISION PATHWAYS⏎⏎I am not asking HHS to halt AI development in health care. I am asking HHS to condition deployment acceleration on demonstrated accountability infrastructure. Specifically, before any AI system is permitted to participate in a clinical decision pathway, meaning any pathway in which an AI output could influence a treatment, diagnosis, or care coordination decision, HHS should require the following:⏎⏎1. Clear legal assignment of liability. The deploying institution (hospital, health system, or clinical practice) must formally accept liability for AI-assisted decisions made within its care environment, subject to separate indemnification arrangements with vendors. Patients should not be required to identify the AI vendor to pursue a negligence claim.⏎⏎2. Mandatory human override documentation. Every instance in which a clinician follows or overrides an AI recommendation must be documented in the EHR in a standardized, machine-readable format. This documentation must identify the specific clinician who reviewed the AI output, the specific recommendation that was reviewed, and the clinical rationale for the decision. This is not administratively impossible. It is a design requirement that vendors can be required to build.⏎⏎3. Patient notification at the point of care. Patients and family caregivers must be informed, in plain language, when an AI system has participated in a clinical recommendation. This is a basic informed consent requirement. The fact that it is not currently standard practice is a significant gap.⏎⏎4. Pre-deployment accountability review. HHS should require that any AI system used in a clinical decision pathway have a documented accountability framework reviewed by an independent body before deployment, addressing the liability questions in item 1 above and the override documentation requirements in item 2.⏎⏎I recognize that some of these requirements would slow certain deployments. That is acceptable. The alternative is a healthcare system in which AI-assisted decisions are made at scale with no clear mechanism for patients or families to understand who is responsible when something goes wrong.⏎⏎⏎VI. A DIRECT RESPONSE TO THE RFI'S ACCELERATION FRAMING⏎⏎The RFI asks what actions HHS can take to accelerate AI adoption. I want to be direct: the answer is not to remove accountability requirements that have not yet been established. The current absence of a clear liability framework for AI-assisted clinical decisions is not a regulatory burden. It is a gap that leaves patients without recourse. Filling that gap before accelerating adoption is not an obstacle to innovation. It is a precondition for deploying innovation responsibly.⏎⏎I have some sympathy for the argument that overly prescriptive regulation could discourage beneficial AI applications. I genuinely do. But the accountability requirements I have described above are not prescriptive in the sense of dictating technical architecture. They are outcome requirements: document who decided what, assign liability to a named institution, tell the patient when AI is involved. These are not technically burdensome. They are the minimum infrastructure for a healthcare system in which AI participates in clinical decisions affecting real people.⏎⏎What I experienced at Inova Fairfax was a coordination system that had already diffused accountability to the point where no single clinician could give me a coherent account of my mother's overall care plan. I am not willing to accept a regulatory framework that layers AI intermediation onto that diffusion and calls it progress. The families sitting in that neurology waiting room, many of them less prepared than I was to navigate clinical documentation, deserve a system in which someone is clearly and legally answerable for what happens to their family member. That is not a sentimental ask. It is a basic structural requirement that the current RFI does not address, and that HHS should address before it proceeds with any acceleration agenda.⏎⏎The specific mechanism I would ask HHS to commit to is a formal rulemaking process, with public comment, on AI liability assignment in clinical settings, prior to any reimbursement incentive or regulatory relief measure designed to accelerate AI adoption. The sequencing matters. Reimbursement incentives will drive deployment. Deployment at scale before accountability frameworks exist will produce harms that are difficult to attribute and therefore difficult to remediate. The GAO flagged this sequencing risk in GAO-21-7SP and recommended that HHS address governance frameworks alongside adoption promotion. That recommendation has not been implemented in any systematic way, and this RFI does not appear to be moving toward implementing it.⏎⏎I would also ask that HHS consider, in any future rulemaking on this topic, the specific situation of family caregivers who function as de facto care coordinators without any formal clinical role and without any legal standing to compel accountability from the institutions managing their family member's care. That population is large, it is disproportionately composed of people with limited institutional access, and the accountability gaps I have described above fall hardest on them.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0049♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Taylor♔♔Nevada♔♔United States♔Epic Research♔♔♔♔♔♔Epic Research Community Advocacy Division⏎Comments Submitted to HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Re: Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket No. HHS–2024–AI–RFI–001⏎⏎⏎I. ORGANIZATIONAL PERSPECTIVE AND SCOPE OF THESE COMMENTS⏎⏎Epic Research submits these comments on behalf of the communities we serve across Nevada and the broader intermountain West, populations that have historically experienced unequal access to quality clinical care and that stand to bear disproportionate costs if the federal government accelerates AI adoption without mandatory equity safeguards. We do not oppose AI in healthcare. We do oppose the framing embedded in this RFI, which treats acceleration as the presumptive good and asks only how fast to move, rather than who gets harmed when the movement is premature. That distinction matters enormously to the patients in Clark County's safety-net clinics and the rural communities along Nevada's Highway 93 corridor who have no academic medical center nearby and no margin for algorithmic error.⏎⏎These comments address two of the RFI's core question areas: what regulatory actions HHS should take to ensure safe and equitable AI deployment, and what reimbursement conditions should attach to federally endorsed AI tools. We do not address the full scope of the RFI because the equity dimensions of those two questions alone demand serious treatment, and because we believe HHS has not adequately accounted for the documented evidence of algorithmic harm that already exists in the peer-reviewed literature.⏎⏎⏎II. THE EVIDENCE BASE FOR ALGORITHMIC BIAS IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL⏎⏎Epic Research Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The framing of AI bias as a future risk to be monitored is empirically untenable. The harms are documented, specific, and patterned. Sjoding, M. W., Dickson, R. P., Iwashyna, T. J., Gay, S. E., and Valley, T. S. published findings in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 demonstrating that pulse oximeters — devices already deeply embedded in clinical workflows — systematically overestimated oxygen saturation in Black patients, masking hypoxemia and delaying intervention (NEJM, 383(25), 2477–2478). This is not a new AI algorithm failing at the prototype stage. This is a deployed technology, in use across thousands of hospitals, producing racially differentiated errors that clinicians largely did not catch because the device's authoritative numerical output discouraged scrutiny.⏎⏎The same pattern appears across AI domains. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan demonstrated in Science in 2019 that a widely used commercial algorithm for health population management systematically assigned lower risk scores to Black patients than to white patients with equivalent illness burden, effectively rationing care management resources away from those who needed them most (Science, 366(6464), 447–453). The mechanism was not explicit racial coding — it was the use of healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, a substitution that encoded decades of differential access into the model's training signal. Adamson and Smith documented in JAMA Dermatology in 2018 that dermatology AI trained predominantly on lighter-skinned image datasets underperformed substantially on images of darker skin tones, with implications for melanoma detection in populations already underserved by dermatology specialists (JAMA Dermatology, 154(11), 1247–1248). And Wong and colleagues published external validation data in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2021 showing that Epic's own proprietary sepsis prediction model — the Sepsis Prediction and Optimization of Therapy tool — generated high false-positive rates that consumed nursing time without improving mortality outcomes, with performance degrading in community hospital settings relative to the academic centers where the model was trained (JAMA Internal Medicine, 181(8), 1065–1070).⏎⏎These are not edge cases. They are a pattern. Gianfrancesco, Tamang, Yazdany, and Schmajuk identified in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2018 that EHR-based machine learning models systematically inherit the selection biases embedded in clinical documentation, including differential coding practices, unequal rates of diagnostic workup, and patterns of underdocumentation in underserved populations (JAMA Internal Medicine, 178(11), 1544–1547). Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones argued in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 that race-adjusted clinical algorithms — including the estimated glomerular filtration rate calculator and the vaginal birth after cesarean risk calculator — have encoded racial essentialism into clinical decision support in ways that produce systematically worse care for Black patients, and that this problem is structural rather than incidental (NEJM, 383(9), 874–882). The point is not that every AI system is biased. The point is that the conditions that produce bias are endemic to how these systems are built and where they are trained, and that HHS's RFI does not adequately reckon with that structural reality.⏎⏎⏎III. THE VALIDATION-DEPLOYMENT GAP AND THE AUTOMATION BIAS PROBLEM⏎⏎Epic Research Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎One mechanism by which algorithmic errors evade detection deserves specific attention because it has direct policy implications. Cabitza, Rasoini, and Gensini identified in JAMA in 2017 the problem of automation bias in clinical settings: when a decision support system produces an output, clinicians are systematically less likely to question it than they would be to question their own reasoning or a colleague's verbal recommendation (JAMA, 318(6), 517–518). The numerical or probabilistic authority of AI outputs — a risk score, a flagged finding, a prioritized worklist item — creates cognitive conditions in which errors are harder to catch than equivalent human errors, not easier. This is not a failure of individual clinicians. It is a predictable consequence of how humans process authoritative-seeming information.⏎⏎The automation bias problem is compounded by what we might call the validation-deployment gap. Wiens and colleagues laid out in Nature Medicine in 2019 a framework for responsible ML deployment that specifically identified external validation — testing a model in the real-world settings where it will actually be used, not just in the academic environment where it was trained — as a necessary precondition for safe deployment (Nature Medicine, 25(9), 1337–1340). The problem is that most FDA-cleared AI algorithms have been validated primarily at large academic medical centers with relatively affluent, well-documented patient populations and robust IT infrastructure. When those tools are deployed at a critical-access hospital in Elko, Nevada, or a federally qualified health center in North Las Vegas, the patient population is different, the data quality is different, the documentation patterns are different, and the clinical workflow context is different. Seyyed-Kalantari and colleagues demonstrated this empirically in Nature Medicine in 2021, showing that AI algorithms applied to chest radiographs produced systematically higher underdiagnosis rates in underserved patient populations, with the performance gap correlating with socioeconomic and demographic factors (Nature Medicine, 27(12), 2176–2182). The communities that are already medically underserved are also the communities where AI tools are most likely to underperform. Acceleration without site-specific validation requirements means that the tools will be deployed fastest in the settings where they are least likely to work correctly and where patients have the fewest alternative sources of care.⏎⏎⏎IV. WHAT HHS MUST REQUIRE: PRE-DEPLOYMENT EQUITY AUDITING AND POST-DEPLOYMENT SURVEILLANCE⏎⏎Epic Research Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎Given the documented evidence above, Epic Research urges HHS to condition any federal reimbursement pathway, endorsement mechanism, or procurement preference for AI clinical tools on the following requirements. These are not aspirational standards. They are the minimum necessary conditions for responsible deployment.⏎⏎o Pre-deployment equity auditing: Before any AI clinical decision support tool receives Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement or is included in any HHS-endorsed technology list, the developer must submit performance data stratified by race, ethnicity, sex, age, insurance status, and socioeconomic proxy variables. This stratification must be conducted on a validation dataset that includes meaningful representation from the care settings where the tool will be deployed, not only from the academic centers where it was developed. Char, Shah, and Magnus argued in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2018 that the ethical deployment of ML in healthcare requires explicit attention to how training data encodes historical inequities, and that this attention must be built into pre-deployment review rather than retrofitted after harm occurs (NEJM, 378(11), 981–983). HHS should operationalize that principle through concrete pre-market requirements, not voluntary guidance.⏎⏎o Post-deployment bias surveillance: Reimbursement should be conditioned on ongoing monitoring. Developers and deploying institutions should be required to report performance metrics stratified by the demographic variables listed above on at least an annual basis, with a mechanism for rapid reporting when performance anomalies are detected. The National Academy for State Health Policy documented in its 2023 policy brief on algorithmic bias in Medicaid managed care that most states currently have no systematic mechanism for detecting when an AI tool is producing racially differentiated outputs in practice, and that the absence of such mechanisms means disparate harm can persist for years before it is identified (NASHP, 2023). HHS has the authority and the responsibility to establish federal standards that fill this gap.⏎⏎o Site-specific validation requirements for high-risk settings: Deployment at critical-access hospitals, federally qualified health centers, and rural health clinics should require additional validation evidence demonstrating acceptable performance in those specific care environments, given the documented degradation of model performance across institutional contexts.⏎⏎o Transparent reporting of training data demographics: The ONC's 2023 issue brief on algorithmic transparency and equity in health IT identified the absence of standardized training data demographic disclosures as a significant gap in current regulatory frameworks (ONC Issue Brief, 2023). HHS should require that any federally endorsed AI tool disclose the demographic composition of its training dataset in standardized, machine-readable format as a condition of endorsement or reimbursement.⏎⏎⏎V. THE STRUCTURAL PROBLEM THAT CANNOT BE ENGINEERED AWAY⏎⏎Epic Research Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 5⏎⏎We want to be direct about something the RFI's framing tends to obscure. The bias in these systems is not a bug that better engineering will eventually eliminate. It is a structural consequence of training on historical data that reflects decades of discriminatory treatment, differential access, and unequal documentation. When a sepsis prediction model learns from EHR data generated in a system where Black patients were undertreated, undertested, and underdocumented relative to their white counterparts, the model learns those patterns. It operationalizes them. It reproduces them at scale and at speed, and it does so with an air of scientific authority that makes the reproduction harder to see than the original human discrimination was.⏎⏎Vyas and colleagues made this point with particular clarity regarding race correction in clinical algorithms — the adjustments built into tools like the eGFR calculator effectively encoded a false biological essentialism that produced systematically worse nephrology referral rates for Black patients, not because any individual clinician intended harm, but because the algorithm was trusted and the structural bias was invisible (NEJM, 383(9), 874–882). The same logic applies to any AI system trained on healthcare data generated in a structurally unequal system. Acceleration amplifies that problem. More deployments, more settings, more clinical decisions influenced by biased outputs, faster.⏎⏎Epic Research Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 6⏎⏎We are not asking HHS to stop AI development. We are asking HHS to stop treating equity safeguards as optional features that can be added after the deployment ecosystem is established. That sequencing has failed in every prior context where it has been tried. The communities we represent in Nevada — including the approximately 340,000 residents of Clark County who rely on safety-net and Medicaid-funded care — do not have the luxury of waiting for a second generation of better-validated tools after the first generation has already distorted their clinical care.⏎⏎The ONC's own 2023 issue brief acknowledged that current regulatory frameworks contain significant gaps in equity oversight for health IT, and that existing transparency and non-discrimination provisions do not reach the specific mechanisms by which AI tools produce disparate outputs (ONC Issue Brief, 2023). HHS should treat that acknowledgment not as background context but as an action item. The RFI asks what the Department can do to accelerate AI adoption. Epic Research's answer is that the Department should first do what is necessary to make adoption safe — and that the documented evidence of algorithmic bias in pulse oximetry, sepsis prediction, chest radiograph analysis, dermatology screening, and population health management makes clear that "safe" has not yet been defined in terms that protect the patients most at risk.⏎⏎The absence of mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing and post-deployment bias surveillance is not a regulatory gap that exists because it is technically difficult to fill. It exists because the policy process has not yet treated it as a prerequisite rather than an aspiration. HHS has the authority to change that, and the evidence base for doing so is already in the literature.⏎⏎⏎contact: ltaylor@epicresearch.org⏎www.epicresearch.org/federal-policy♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0050♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Jackson♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔IBM Watson Health Alumni Network♔♔♔♔♔♔IBM Watson Health Alumni Network | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 1⏎⏎Thomas Jackson, Senior Healthcare Administrator⏎IBM Watson Health Alumni Network⏎Billings, Montana 59101⏎⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎IBM Watson Health Alumni Network | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 2⏎⏎I. PRELIMINARY STATEMENT⏎⏎Our network includes healthcare administrators, clinical informatics professionals, and health system leaders who have spent the better part of two decades deploying, evaluating, and in many cases decommissioning AI systems across institutional settings. We do not approach this RFI as skeptics of technology. A 2024 internal deployment analysis across 47 health system partners integrating AI-assisted radiology tools found that AI-assisted reads reduced average radiologist turnaround time by 38% while maintaining a false-negative rate below 0.3% across more than 2.1 million chest imaging studies. We cite that figure not to endorse the Department's direction, but to establish that we have operational standing to say what we are about to say, and that our objections do not come from ignorance of what these systems can do.⏎⏎What concerns us is not the capability of clinical AI. What concerns us is the framing of this RFI itself, and the policy logic that framing encodes. HHS asks how it can "reduce uncertainty that impedes AI innovation" in clinical settings. Read that phrase carefully. It treats public oversight as an obstacle to be cleared rather than a legitimate democratic interest to be served. That framing is not neutral. It is a policy commitment disguised as an administrative question, and our network believes the Department should be held accountable for it.⏎⏎II. ON THE QUESTION OF IRREVERSIBILITY AND DEMOCRATIC FORECLOSURE⏎⏎The RFI does not ask whether clinical AI adoption should be accelerated. It asks how. That is not a small distinction. When a federal agency structures a public comment process around the assumption that a particular policy direction is already decided, and then solicits input only on implementation mechanics, it engages in what we would describe as democratic preemption. The public's right to decide whether medicine should remain organized primarily around human judgment, human accountability, and human institutions is foreclosed before the first comment is filed.⏎⏎This matters because AI embedding in clinical and administrative workflows is not reversible in any practical sense. Coiera, Ash, and Berg documented in their 2016 review in the Yearbook of Medical Informatics how health information technology, once embedded, reshapes institutional knowledge, staffing models, and clinical culture in ways that persist long after the original systems are replaced or fail (Coiera, Ash, & Berg, 2016). The same dynamic applies with considerably more force to AI systems, which do not merely automate tasks but reorganize the cognitive and institutional architecture around those tasks. Once a radiology department restructures its staffing model around AI-assisted reads, once an emergency department builds its triage workflow around algorithmic risk scores, once a health system's procurement and documentation processes are rebuilt around vendor-supplied decision support, the institutional knowledge required to operate without those systems begins to atrophy. Within five to seven years, reversal is not a policy option. It is a crisis scenario.⏎⏎HHS's acceleration agenda, if successful, will produce exactly that condition across hundreds of health systems simultaneously. The 84% of participating institutions in our partner network who cited reimbursement uncertainty as a barrier to broader AI adoption were not describing a problem that HHS should solve by eliminating that uncertainty in favor of adoption. They were describing a normal and appropriate feature of a healthcare system that has not yet decided, at a democratic level, how much of its clinical judgment it wishes to cede to vendor systems. Removing that uncertainty by creating reimbursement pathways and standardized deployment frameworks does not resolve the underlying question. It simply answers it without asking.⏎⏎IBM Watson Health Alumni Network | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 3⏎⏎III. ON THE STRUCTURAL OPACITY OF PROPRIETARY CLINICAL AI⏎⏎There is a second dimension to this problem that the RFI's framing actively obscures. Proprietary AI systems deployed in clinical settings are not transparent to regulators, clinicians, or patients in any operationally meaningful sense. Frank Pasquale's analysis in The Black Box Society (2015) established the basic governance problem: algorithmic systems that control consequential decisions are, by design and by commercial incentive, structurally resistant to external scrutiny. In clinical AI, that problem is compounded by the technical complexity of model architecture, the proprietary nature of training data, and the gap between published performance metrics and real-world deployment behavior.⏎⏎Selbst and Barocas argued in their 2018 Fordham Law Review analysis that the "intuitive appeal of explainable machines" obscures the degree to which post-hoc explanations of algorithmic outputs do not actually expose the causal structure of model decisions (Selbst & Barocas, 2018). Doshi-Velez and Kim made a related point in their 2017 work on interpretable machine learning: that interpretability is not a binary property but a contested and context-dependent one, and that regulatory frameworks which treat it as a checkable box will systematically underestimate model opacity in real deployment environments (Doshi-Velez & Kim, 2017). Mittelstadt, Russell, and Wachter extended this argument to the governance context, finding that current explanation frameworks for AI systems fail to support the kind of contrastive, socially grounded accountability that democratic oversight requires (Mittelstadt, Russell, & Wachter, 2019).⏎⏎What this means practically is that HHS cannot create meaningful oversight of systems it cannot inspect, and it cannot inspect systems whose vendors have no legal obligation to disclose model architecture, training data composition, or performance degradation over time. The absence of standardized HHS guidance on clinical AI validation requirements, which our network's procurement analysis found extended average vendor-to-deployment cycles by 14 months compared to institutions operating under clearer international regulatory frameworks, is not simply an efficiency problem. It is a symptom of the fact that no one, including HHS, currently has the tools to evaluate what these systems are actually doing at scale. Shortliffe and Sepúlveda noted in their 2018 JAMA commentary that clinical decision support systems require ongoing validation in deployment contexts that differ materially from validation environments, and that the gap between trial performance and clinical performance is a structural feature of the field, not an anomaly (Shortliffe & Sepúlveda, 2018).⏎⏎The Department's response to this governance gap, as encoded in this RFI, is to accelerate deployment anyway. We find that difficult to justify on any administrative law theory we are aware of.⏎⏎IBM Watson Health Alumni Network | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 4⏎⏎IV. ON THE DISTRIBUTION OF HARMS AND THE QUESTION OF WHOSE MEDICINE THIS IS⏎⏎The governance problem is not evenly distributed. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan demonstrated in their 2019 Science paper that a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to manage population health systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients, not because of explicit racial coding but because the algorithm used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, embedding the structural inequities of healthcare access directly into its outputs (Obermeyer et al., 2019). Virginia Eubanks documented in Automating Inequality (2018) how algorithmic systems deployed in public benefit contexts consistently concentrate their error costs on populations with the least institutional power to contest or correct those errors. The National Academy of Medicine's 2022 special publication on AI in health care identified health equity as one of the central unresolved challenges in clinical AI deployment, noting that performance disparities across demographic groups are common in published models and frequently invisible in aggregate performance metrics (National Academy of Medicine, 2022).⏎⏎Our specific concern regarding this RFI is as follows:⏎⏎o The populations most likely to bear the error costs of accelerated AI deployment are rural, low-income, and minority communities whose clinical needs are underrepresented in training data and whose institutions have the least capacity to monitor model performance post-deployment.⏎⏎o The populations most likely to benefit from efficiency gains are large urban health systems with the administrative infrastructure to capture and operationalize those gains.⏎⏎o HHS's reimbursement and regulatory acceleration agenda, if implemented as framed, will widen that gap rather than narrow it, because it removes barriers to deployment without conditioning deployment on equity performance.⏎⏎o The 47 health system partners in our network's analysis were not representative of rural critical access hospitals, Federally Qualified Health Centers, or Indian Health Service facilities. Extrapolating from their performance data to a policy framework for the health sector broadly is a methodological error we want to name explicitly.⏎⏎The concentration of AI decision-making authority in distant vendor systems is, at its core, a question about who governs medicine. Topol observed in his 2019 Nature Medicine analysis that the integration of AI into clinical care raises fundamental questions about the physician-patient relationship and the distribution of clinical authority (Topol, 2019). Hoffman and Podgurski argued in their 2013 American Journal of Law and Medicine analysis that the use of large biomedical datasets in clinical decision-making raises governance questions that cannot be resolved through purely technical frameworks (Hoffman & Podgurski, 2013). These are not questions that an RFI process can resolve, and they are not questions that HHS has the democratic mandate to resolve unilaterally by building a reimbursement and regulatory architecture that makes one answer to them effectively permanent.⏎⏎IBM Watson Health Alumni Network | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 5⏎⏎V. SPECIFIC REQUESTS TO THE DEPARTMENT⏎⏎Our network does not oppose federal engagement with clinical AI governance. We oppose the specific framing of this RFI, which treats acceleration as the goal and oversight as an obstacle. If HHS proceeds with rulemaking or policy guidance in this area, we request that the Department address the following before establishing any reimbursement pathways or deployment frameworks:⏎⏎1. Publish a formal analysis of which clinical workflow categories, once restructured around AI systems, would require crisis-level institutional response to reverse, and disclose what mitigation obligations the Department believes it has in those cases.⏎⏎2. Require as a condition of any reimbursement coverage determination for clinical AI that vendors disclose training data demographic composition, model architecture at a level sufficient for independent audit, and documented performance variance across patient subgroups.⏎⏎3. Commission an independent assessment of whether the current FDA Software as a Medical Device framework, combined with existing ONC certification requirements, actually provides the oversight infrastructure that HHS's acceleration agenda assumes is in place. Our network's operational experience suggests it does not.⏎⏎4. Conduct a separate RFI process specifically directed at frontline clinicians, rural health system administrators, and community health organizations, whose perspectives on AI governance are systematically absent from the comment record that large health systems and technology vendors dominate.⏎⏎The procurement timeline analysis we cited earlier, the 14-month gap between vendor contract and deployment in institutions operating without clear regulatory frameworks, is real and worth addressing. But the solution to that problem is not to remove oversight. The solution is to build oversight frameworks that are actually capable of doing what oversight is supposed to do, which is give the public confidence that consequential decisions about their care are being made by systems that someone with authority and accountability has actually examined.⏎⏎We are not aware of any current federal framework that meets that standard for clinical AI, and we do not believe that⏎⏎IBM Watson Health Alumni Network | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 6⏎⏎accelerating deployment through reimbursement incentives while that gap remains unaddressed is a defensible position for the Department to take. Our network will be monitoring subsequent guidance closely and reserves the right to submit supplemental comments if draft regulatory text is published.⏎⏎Thomas Jackson⏎Senior Healthcare Administrator, IBM Watson Health Alumni Network⏎tjackson@ibmwatsonhealthalumni.net⏎Billings, MT 59101♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0051♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Scott♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔American Society of Human Genetics♔♔♔♔♔♔AMERICAN SOCIETY OF HUMAN GENETICS⏎Comments in Response to HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI-001⏎Submitted by Melissa Scott, Executive Director⏎American Society of Human Genetics⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎The American Society of Human Genetics (ASHG) submits these comments with significant concern that the framing of HHS's request for information conflates two categorically different uses of artificial intelligence in health care: AI deployed to support clinical decision-making for patients, and AI deployed to automate coverage denials for the financial benefit of insurers. Our member organizations — 847 clearinghouses and health information exchanges processing in excess of 4.2 billion claims transactions annually — occupy a unique vantage point on this distinction. They sit, structurally, at the intersection of clinical data flows and administrative coverage determinations, and what our Q3 2024 policy working group documented in that position is not a story of AI accelerating care. It is a story of AI being deployed at industrial scale to obstruct it. HHS must be clear-eyed about what it is accelerating before issuing guidance that treats all health sector AI as presumptively beneficial.⏎⏎These comments address two of the RFI's core questions: first, what regulatory actions HHS should take to govern AI use in coverage determination workflows, and second, what accountability mechanisms are necessary before any further federal support for AI deployment in prior authorization and claims adjudication. We do not address clinical AI bias, workforce impacts, or vendor market dynamics, which merit separate comment processes. Our concern here is narrower and more urgent: the use of AI as a financial weapon against patients seeking medically necessary care.⏎⏎The scale of what is already happening in the field is not speculative. The Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations documented in October 2023, in its report "Denial, Delay, Deny: How Medicare Advantage Insurers Use AI to Override Physician Judgments" (S. Prt. 118-22), that major Medicare Advantage organizations were using AI-driven prior authorization tools to generate denials at rates that bore no relationship to clinical inappropriateness determinations. The Subcommittee found that one large insurer's AI system was denying post-acute care claims at a rate that exceeded 90 percent in certain categories, with individual claim reviews lasting as little as 1.2 seconds — a duration that cannot, by any reasonable standard, constitute clinical review. This is not an edge case or a malfunction. It is the system operating as designed. The HHS Office of Inspector General reached a parallel conclusion in April 2022, finding in OIG Report OEI-09-18-00260 that Medicare Advantage organizations were denying prior authorization requests that met Medicare coverage criteria, and that beneficiaries and providers who appealed those denials succeeded at rates exceeding 75 percent when they managed to complete the appeals process at all.⏎⏎That last clause — "when they managed to complete the appeals process at all" — is where the consumer protection crisis lives, and it is a crisis that HHS's framing of AI adoption as a competitive clinical opportunity does not acknowledge. The appeals architecture established under 42 C.F.R. §§ 422.566 through 422.574 was designed for a pre-algorithmic era in which individual denials were individually reviewed, and individual patients or their providers could contest individual determinations through a process that, while burdensome, was at least responsive to the specific facts of a specific case. What AI-driven denial automation has done to that architecture is to invert its logic entirely. When an algorithm processes tens of thousands of prior authorization requests per day and denies a statistically predictable percentage of them based on pattern-matching against cost-containment thresholds rather than clinical criteria, the appeals process does not scale with the denial volume. Patients — elderly, disabled, or low-income patients in particular — are left holding individual appeals rights against an industrial denial apparatus. The Kaiser Family Foundation documented in its March 2023 issue brief on prior authorization in Medicare Advantage that fewer than one percent of denied beneficiaries filed formal appeals, not because the denials were appropriate, but because the process is structurally designed to be abandoned. ProPublica and The Capitol Forum's investigative reporting on UnitedHealth's AI denial practices, published in November 2023, documented specific cases in which physician-ordered care was overridden by algorithmic determinations, patients were discharged from post-acute facilities prematurely, and the human cost of those decisions — including documented patient deterioration — was absorbed entirely by patients and their families, not by the insurer whose algorithm generated the denial.⏎⏎ASHG's recommendations are organized below, followed by a reference to the supporting data appendix that our policy working group has compiled from member transaction records.⏎⏎RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎1. HHS should impose a moratorium on the use of AI or algorithmic tools as the primary or determinative basis for prior authorization denials in Medicare Advantage, Medicaid managed care, and federally facilitated marketplace plans pending the establishment of federal minimum safety standards for such tools. This moratorium is not a permanent prohibition on AI in coverage determination; it is a recognition that the current deployment environment lacks the safeguards that any other high-stakes automated decision system would require before federal agencies endorsed its expansion. The American Medical Association's 2023 prior authorization physician survey found that 94 percent of physicians reported that prior authorization delays caused patients to abandon recommended treatment, and 33 percent reported that prior authorization had contributed to a serious adverse event for a patient in their care. These are not tolerable baseline conditions for a regulatory acceleration agenda.⏎⏎2. HHS should require, as a condition of Medicare Advantage and Medicaid managed care contract participation, that any AI or algorithmic tool used in prior authorization or claims adjudication meet a mandatory human review threshold: specifically, that no denial of a prior authorization request for post-acute care, durable medical equipment, behavioral health services, or specialty pharmaceuticals may be issued without documented attestation of individualized clinical review by a licensed clinician with relevant specialty training. The 1.2-second review times documented by the Senate Permanent Subcommittee are not compliant with this standard. They cannot be made compliant through software modifications. They represent a structural choice by insurers to substitute algorithmic cost-containment for clinical judgment, and that choice must have regulatory consequences.⏎⏎3. HHS should establish algorithmic transparency requirements applicable to any AI tool used in coverage determination, including mandatory public disclosure of: (a) the training data sources and time periods used to develop the model; (b) the outcome metrics the model is optimized to predict; (c) denial rate benchmarks by care category, patient demographic, and geography; and (d) the frequency and methodology of model validation and updating. The National Academy for State Health Policy's January 2024 tracker of state laws challenging insurer use of AI in prior authorization identified at least fourteen states that had enacted or were actively advancing legislation in this area — a regulatory patchwork that is itself a market dysfunction, and one that federal transparency standards could rationalize. The absence of federal disclosure requirements has created the precise compliance uncertainty that our member survey documented, with 78 percent of clearinghouses and health information exchanges reporting interoperability and governance barriers tied to inconsistent AI governance frameworks across jurisdictions.⏎⏎4. HHS should direct CMS to revise the Medicare Advantage prior authorization and utilization management reporting requirements to capture AI-specific metrics, including the percentage of prior authorization decisions in which AI tools generated the initial determination, the override rate when human reviewers revisit AI-generated denials, and the correlation between AI denial rates and subsequent appeal outcomes. The CMS Office of the Actuary's 2023 Annual Report to Congress on Medicare Advantage Prior Authorization and Utilization Management documented aggregate denial rates but did not disaggregate AI-generated denials from human-initiated denials — a gap that makes it impossible to assess the scale of the problem at the federal level even as investigative reporting and state-level data paint an increasingly detailed picture of it.⏎⏎5. HHS should clarify, in binding guidance rather than sub-regulatory communication, that the use of AI tools optimized for cost-containment outcomes as a proxy for medical necessity determinations is not consistent with the coverage criteria established under Medicare and Medicaid statute. The current regulatory environment has created what amounts to a plausible deniability structure: insurers deploy AI tools that generate denials at scale, disclaim that the AI is making the "final" determination, and point to nominal human sign-off processes that the Senate Subcommittee found in practice to involve no substantive clinical review. Ledford's February 2024 analysis in Health Affairs Forefront documented that this nominal-review architecture is now standard across major commercial and Medicare Advantage insurers, and that the human reviewer's role in most systems is functionally ministerial — approving or transmitting AI-generated outputs rather than independently evaluating clinical appropriateness. Binding guidance that defines what constitutes a legally sufficient medical necessity determination in the AI-assisted context is not optional; it is the predicate for any enforcement action under existing statute.⏎⏎The RFI's framing of AI adoption as primarily a question of how to accelerate deployment is, in our assessment, the wrong starting question for a department whose statutory mandate includes protecting beneficiary access to medically necessary care. The more appropriate question is: what conditions must be met before the federal government endorses and incentivizes expanded use of AI in coverage determination workflows that have already demonstrated the capacity to harm patients at scale? Acceleration without accountability is not a health policy; it is a liability transfer — from insurers who profit from algorithmic denials to patients who cannot navigate the appeals process those denials trigger.⏎⏎ASHG acknowledges that there are genuine and promising applications of AI in clinical care settings, including in genetic medicine and diagnostics, that merit federal support. The organization does not oppose AI in health care categorically. What the organization does oppose, with specificity and with data, is the current trajectory in which the most widely deployed and financially consequential AI in the health sector is denial automation, and in which HHS's regulatory attention is directed toward removing friction for AI adoption rather than establishing the safety floor below which that adoption cannot go. Obermeyer and colleagues' 2019 analysis in Science (366(6464), 447–453) demonstrated that health care algorithms trained on cost-based proxies systematically underestimate the care needs of Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent clinical burden — a finding that should have produced mandatory bias auditing requirements for any AI tool used in coverage determination. It did not. The lesson of that failure is not that the federal government moves too slowly on AI governance; it is that the federal government has not yet established that AI governance in coverage determination is a priority at all.⏎⏎Our member organizations processed 4.2 billion claims transactions in the most recent annual reporting period. They see, in aggregate, what individual patients and providers cannot see in isolation: the systematic patterns in which AI-driven denial tools operate, the demographic distributions of denial rates, the geographic concentrations of appeal filings, and the downstream effects on care utilization when algorithmic denials go unchallenged. The data our policy working group compiled for the appendix referenced below — Appendix A: Member Survey Data on AI-Assisted Claims Adjudication and Denial Patterns, Q3 2024, available upon request from ASHG's policy office — documents denial rate variance by insurer and care category that is not explicable by clinical appropriateness differences across those categories. The variance is explicable by cost-containment optimization, which is what the AI tools at issue are actually built to do.⏎⏎The 61 percent of our member organizations that reported deferring or scaling back AI investments pending clearer federal signals are not waiting for permission to innovate. They are waiting for evidence that the federal government has thought carefully about what AI in this context actually does to patients, and whether any of the regulatory infrastructure that governs coverage determination has been updated to account for it. That evidence has not yet been produced. Schulte and Fry documented in their 2019 Kaiser Health News and Fortune investigation of electronic health record failures that the pattern of deploying complex administrative technology without adequate governance frameworks, then managing the consequences reactively, has already cost the health system enormously in both financial and human terms. The AI denial automation context is not identical to the EHR adoption failure, but the structural dynamic — accelerated deployment, deferred accountability, distributed harm — is recognizable.⏎⏎ASHG urges HHS to restructure the questions this RFI is asking. Before the Department solicits input on how to accelerate AI adoption, it should solicit input on what accountability conditions are necessary before acceleration is appropriate. The moratorium, human review threshold, transparency disclosure, CMS reporting revision, and binding guidance recommendations above represent ASHG's assessment of the minimum conditions that must be in place. They are not a complete framework; a complete framework would require notice-and-comment rulemaking that this RFI cannot substitute for. But they are the predicate without which any acceleration agenda is, in our assessment, premature.⏎⏎Melissa Scott⏎Executive Director⏎American Society of Human Genetics⏎policy@ashg.org⏎⏎ASHG Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 7⏎⏎Appendix A: Member Survey Data on AI-Assisted Claims Adjudication and Denial Patterns, Q3 2024 — not attached to this submission; available upon request from the ASHG policy office. Organizations seeking access to underlying transaction-volume data should contact ASHG directly. Note that member data in the appendix has been de-identified at the organizational level per the terms of ASHG's 2024 data governance agreement with participating clearinghouses; individual member names and transaction identifiers are not included in the released dataset, though aggregate denial-rate figures by care category and insurer tier are♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0052♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Harris♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My buddy Marcus waited eight months for a PTSD diagnosis at his VA clinic outside Macon because they were short-staffed. A friend of mine in Atlanta got connected to a telehealth program with AI-assisted screening and had a care plan in six weeks. Same service, same wounds, completely different outcomes based on who had enough people to actually do the work.⏎⏎What I want HHS to understand is that when administrators talk about AI solving burnout, I get nervous. Not because the tools are worthless, but because I have watched "efficiency" be used as a reason to cut the people patients depend on. If a screening tool frees up a nurse's time, that time should go back to the nurse, not become justification for hiring fewer nurses. Before any of these tools get deployed in VA or community health settings, the people actually doing the care need a real say in how they're used, not just the people buying them.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0053♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Perez♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔MITRE Corporation♔♔♔♔♔♔Our member hospitals have spent real money — collectively well over fifty million dollars in the last two years — on AI pilots that never made it past the demonstration phase. In our survey of 47 institutions, 62% had piloted at least one AI-assisted clinical decision support tool, yet fewer than 20% reached full departmental deployment. The reason is not primarily reimbursement uncertainty, though that is real. The reason is that vendors have structured their contracts specifically to maximize lock-in before hospitals know whether the tools actually work.⏎⏎MITRE Corporation Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎HHS should impose demonstrated-safety and equity requirements before any deployment incentives are authorized, and should mandate open interoperability standards that prohibit the proprietary data capture arrangements our member CFOs keep signing because they have no⏎⏎Kimberly Perez⏎Hospital CFO, MITRE Corporation⏎Providence, Rhode Island♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0054♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔King♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily King Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎My mother was admitted to St. Cloud Hospital in January 2023 with an ambiguous cardiac presentation. The attending physician spent the better part of forty minutes manually cross-referencing her medication history across three separate electronic systems before she could make a treatment call. My mother was in active distress during that entire window. I run a health-tech consulting firm that works with independent clinics across greater Minnesota, and I watch that same fragmented, delay-prone workflow repeat itself in nearly every rural practice I advise, where staffing is too thin and the margin for that kind of inefficiency is essentially zero. So I want to be clear that I am not writing this comment as someone skeptical of AI in medicine. The efficiency case is real and I have seen it up close.⏎⏎What concerns me, and why I am submitting this comment, is that the RFI's framing treats the deployment of AI in clinical care as primarily a regulatory clearance problem. The question HHS is asking is roughly: what barriers can we remove to accelerate adoption? I think that question is incomplete in a way that matters. The barriers that deserve the most scrutiny are not the ones slowing deployment down — they are the ones that, if removed prematurely, would allow deployment to proceed without adequate attention to what medicine actually is.⏎⏎Emily King Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎ON THE RELATIONAL DIMENSION OF CLINICAL CARE⏎⏎The research on this is not ambiguous. Kelley and colleagues conducted a systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials published in PLOS ONE in 2014 (Kelley JM, Kraft-Todd G, Schapira L, et al., PLOS ONE, 9(4):e94207) and found that the patient-clinician relationship itself, independent of treatment modality, produced measurable effects on health outcomes. Not satisfaction scores. Outcomes. Abraham Verghese, Nigam Shah, and Robert Harrington made a related argument in JAMA in 2018 (Verghese A, Shah NH, Harrington RA, JAMA, 319(1):19-20) that the physician's embodied presence and attentiveness are not incidental features of care that technology can substitute around — they are constitutive of what care is. I keep coming back to that framing because it is not sentimental. It is a claim about the mechanism by which clinical encounters produce benefit.⏎⏎Eric Topol's 2019 piece in Nature Medicine (Topol EJ, Nature Medicine, 25:44-56) is frequently cited in AI policy discussions as support for ambitious AI deployment, and Topol does make a strong case for AI-assisted diagnostics. What gets omitted in those citations, though, is that Topol himself argues the goal should be liberating physician time for deeper patient engagement, not replacing that engagement with automated interaction. The National Academy of Medicine's 2019 special publication on AI in health care made a similar point — that the "hope" and the "hype" need to be disaggregated carefully, and that deployment decisions require attention to relational and contextual factors that current AI validation frameworks largely do not capture (NAM, Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: The Hope, the Hype, the Promise, the Peril, Washington DC, 2019).⏎⏎There is also a deskilling concern I want to name, not because it is the central argument here, but because it is relevant to the reimbursement question the RFI raises. Timothy Hoff's research on primary care physicians adapting to work innovations (Hoff T, Health Care Management Review, 2011, 36(4):338-348) documented how reliance on structured decision-support tools can gradually erode the diagnostic reasoning capacities that make physicians effective in the first place — particularly in ambiguous presentations, which is precisely the category where AI tools are most likely to be wrong or underspecified. If HHS accelerates reimbursement for AI-assisted workflows without building in requirements that clinicians maintain independent assessment capacity, we may produce a generation of providers who are competent when the algorithm is right and dangerously underprepared when it is not.⏎⏎Emily King Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎SPECIFIC CONCERNS REGARDING THE RFI'S REIMBURSEMENT PROPOSALS⏎⏎The RFI asks broadly about reimbursement mechanisms that could accelerate AI adoption. I want to address this narrowly, because I think the reimbursement question is where the structural risk is highest.⏎⏎Current CMS reimbursement structures do not adequately distinguish between AI tools that augment physician judgment and AI tools that are designed to replace discrete clinical decisions. The ONC certification program, as structured under the 21st Century Cures Act final rule (85 Fed. Reg. 25642, May 1, 2020), establishes interoperability and information-blocking standards but does not impose requirements about how AI-generated outputs must be presented to clinicians or what human review must occur before those outputs affect a care decision. That gap is significant.⏎⏎Before HHS expands reimbursement eligibility for AI-assisted clinical decision tools, I would ask the Department to require the following, at minimum:⏎⏎1. Demonstrated relational safety standards — meaning that any AI tool seeking reimbursement eligibility must show, through pre-deployment testing in actual clinical environments, that its use does not measurably reduce the time or quality of direct patient-clinician interaction. This is not the same as showing the tool is diagnostically accurate. Accuracy and relational safety are separate properties.⏎⏎2. Human accountability checkpoints that are structural, not advisory. The current approach in most EHR-integrated tools is to display an AI recommendation with an option for the clinician to override. That framing places the burden of proof on the human to justify departure from the algorithm, which is exactly backwards from a safety standpoint. Reimbursement eligibility should require that the tool's interface structure places affirmative confirmation of physician judgment at the decision point, not passive acceptance of an AI output.⏎⏎3. Post-deployment monitoring requirements with defined thresholds for suspension. Right now there is no standardized mechanism by which a clinic in Sauk Centre or Willmar, Minnesota can report that an AI tool is producing a pattern of clinically concerning recommendations and have that report trigger a regulatory response on a defined timeline. That infrastructure needs to exist before we expand deployment, not after.⏎⏎I want to be honest that I am not entirely sure how to operationalize the first of those requirements — measuring relational quality in a way that is both valid and not so burdensome that it becomes another documentation tax on already-stretched rural practices is genuinely hard. Bickmore and colleagues did some interesting work on how virtual agent interactions can be structured to preserve relational elements for low-health-literacy patients (Bickmore TW, Pfeifer LM, Jack BW, CHI Conference Proceedings, 2009), but that work was in a very different context and I would not want to overextend it. The point is that the measurement problem is solvable, and HHS should invest in solving it as a precondition to expanded reimbursement eligibility, not as a research priority that runs in parallel with deployment.⏎⏎The forty minutes my mother's physician spent manually reconciling medication records is a real problem. I am not arguing for the status quo. What I am arguing is that the answer to that problem cannot be to remove the conditions under which a physician remains genuinely responsible for the decision she is making. The reimbursement framework HHS builds here will shape clinical culture for a long time, and the incentive structures it creates will determine whether AI tools get deployed in ways that extend physician judgment or gradually substitute for it.⏎⏎One logistical note: the RFI's comment portal timed out twice during submission, and I was unable to attach the supplementary clinic survey data I had prepared. I am happy to provide that separately if there is a contact address for the♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0055♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Walker♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔University of Michigan Institute for Healthcare Policy and Innovation♔♔♔♔♔♔University of Michigan Institute for Healthcare Policy and Innovation⏎Comments Submitted to the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI-001⏎⏎Submitted by: Kimberly Walker, Senior Research Investigator⏎University of Michigan Institute for Healthcare Policy and Innovation⏎Ann Arbor, Michigan 48109⏎kwalker@ihpi.umich.edu⏎⏎Date of Submission: March 14, 2025⏎⏎⏎The University of Michigan Institute for Healthcare Policy and Innovation (IHPI) submits these comments in response to the Department's Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. These comments focus primarily on the Department's questions regarding reimbursement modernization and public-private partnership structures, areas where the Institute's ongoing research has generated findings with direct policy relevance. IHPI does not address the full range of questions posed in the RFI; the analytical concerns raised here are sufficiently substantial to warrant concentrated treatment rather than a thinner survey of all topics.⏎⏎The Department should be aware at the outset that the RFI's organizing framework — acceleration — is not a neutral methodological premise. It is a policy choice with distributional consequences. A systematic review conducted by Logos Research Centre, drawing on 47 peer-reviewed clinical AI implementation studies published between 2019 and 2023, found that fewer than 23% of AI diagnostic tools deployed in clinical settings had undergone prospective validation in populations demographically representative of the intended patient base (Logos Research Centre, 2024). The same review identified reimbursement ambiguity as a primary adoption barrier in 68% of health system case studies, with institutions reporting an average 14-month delay between regulatory clearance and operational deployment attributable to unclear billing pathways. These figures are worth sitting with for a moment. What they describe is not primarily a technological readiness problem. They describe a structural environment in which the reimbursement apparatus has not kept pace with deployment, and in which the populations most likely to be harmed by undervalidated tools are precisely the populations least represented in the validation literature. The acceleration framing responds to the first problem and, at best, ignores the second. The concern this Institute wishes to raise is that the RFI's design — its framing, its questions, and its implicit assumptions about where friction is located in the AI adoption process — reflects a policy posture that has been substantially shaped by the interests of large technology and EHR vendors rather than by the patient populations and health systems the Department is statutorily obligated to serve.⏎⏎IHPI | HHS Health Sector AI RFI Comments | March 2025 | Page 2⏎⏎This is the concept of regulatory capture, and this Institute submits that it is not merely a theoretical risk in the context of this RFI. It is a condition that the structural evidence strongly suggests is already operative. Regulatory capture, in its classic formulation, occurs when the regulatory apparatus comes to serve the interests of the regulated industry more than the public interest the regulation was designed to protect (Stigler, 1971, as discussed extensively in Wiggins & Taddeo, 2022). In the health AI context, the capture mechanism operates through several overlapping channels: the disproportionate lobbying and public comment infrastructure of large technology firms, the revolving door between federal health IT offices and commercial vendors, and the degree to which the Department's own policy vocabulary has converged with vendor marketing language. The phrase "accelerating adoption" does not originate in patient advocacy communities or academic health policy research. It originates in the commercial AI sector's preferred framing of regulatory friction as the primary obstacle to beneficial innovation. This Institute is not suggesting that regulatory friction is costless or that reimbursement clarity is an unworthy policy goal. The Logos Research Centre (2024) findings on 14-month deployment delays are real and consequential. The concern is that when the acceleration frame dominates, it structurally advantages the entities with the greatest capacity to respond to RFIs, shape sub-regulatory guidance, and position their products within whatever reimbursement pathways the Department ultimately creates. Gans and Stern (2023) have documented this dynamic with particular clarity in the health AI procurement context, finding that platform lock-in effects in health IT markets are substantially amplified by reimbursement structures that favor incumbent vendors — in part because those vendors have the organizational capacity to navigate billing complexity that newer entrants and smaller community health systems do not.⏎⏎The reimbursement modernization pathway proposed in the RFI deserves specific analytical attention because it is the mechanism through which the political economy concerns above become concrete and durable. The history of EHR adoption under the HITECH Act is instructive here. Atasoy, Greenwood, and McCullough (2019) documented that the financial incentives created by HITECH accelerated EHR adoption substantially but also entrenched a small number of dominant vendors — most notably Epic and Oracle Health (formerly Cerner) — whose market positions became self-reinforcing through data network effects, proprietary interoperability constraints, and switching costs that made competitive displacement functionally impossible for most health systems. Adler-Milstein and Pfeifer (2017) found that information blocking by dominant EHR vendors was widespread and persistent even after explicit regulatory prohibition, a finding that illustrates the limits of relying on voluntary market behavior or sub-regulatory guidance to discipline vendor conduct. Schulte and Fry (2019) documented in exhaustive detail how the misalignment between vendor incentives and clinical usability produced systems that degraded clinician workflow while generating substantial returns for vendors whose contracts insulated them from performance accountability. The Department is now proposing to create AI-specific reimbursement pathways that will function analogously to the HITECH incentive structure. The Institute submits that without structural safeguards explicitly designed to prevent the replication of HITECH's market concentration outcomes, the proposed reimbursement modernization will reproduce those outcomes at scale and with greater speed, given the substantially higher capital barriers to entry in AI development relative to EHR software.⏎⏎IHPI | HHS Health Sector AI RFI Comments | March 2025 | Page 3⏎⏎The vendor lock-in concern is not hypothetical. The licensing structures currently employed by dominant AI and EHR vendors in the health sector share several features that, taken together, create conditions of competitive dependency rather than competitive markets. These include:⏎⏎1. Multi-year exclusive licensing agreements that bundle AI modules with EHR infrastructure, effectively requiring health systems to adopt vendor-specific AI products as a condition of maintaining EHR support contracts.⏎⏎2. Proprietary model architectures and training data regimes that prevent health systems from independently validating, auditing, or retraining deployed models — a concern that Maddox, Rumsfeld, and Payne (2019) identified as among the most consequential governance gaps in clinical AI deployment.⏎⏎3. Pricing structures for AI add-on modules that are opaque relative to underlying development costs, a dynamic that has structural analogues in the pharmaceutical sector, where Kesselheim, Avorn, and Sarpatwari (2016) documented how market exclusivity mechanisms allow prices to persist at levels substantially decoupled from production costs and research investment.⏎⏎4. Data licensing terms that vest proprietary rights in patient-derived training data with vendors rather than with the health systems or patients who generated the data, creating what Wiggins and Taddeo (2022) characterize as a structural asymmetry of data power that advantages incumbent vendors in perpetuity.⏎⏎The Department's reimbursement modernization proposal, as described in the RFI, does not address any of these structural features. It proposes to resolve billing ambiguity — a genuine and consequential problem — without attending to the market conditions that will determine who captures the value that reimbursement clarity creates. The Congressional Budget Office's preliminary assessment of AI adoption in federal health programs (CBO, 2023) projected that AI-related expenditures under Medicare and Medicaid could reach $8.4 billion annually within a decade of widespread reimbursement authorization, a figure that substantially exceeds current federal investment in health AI research. The distributional question of who captures that $8.4 billion — patients and health systems, or vendor shareholders — is not addressed in the RFI, and this omission is itself analytically significant.⏎⏎The public-private partnership and CRADA provisions warrant separate treatment because they involve a distinct mechanism of value transfer: the privatization of returns on federally funded research. The Institute acknowledges that public-private collaboration in health AI research is not inherently problematic. The concern is structural. When the Department enters into CRADAs or similar arrangements with commercial AI vendors using federal research infrastructure, patient data from federal programs, and publicly funded computational resources, without explicit provisions governing intellectual property allocation, licensing terms for resulting products, and pricing constraints on federally subsidized AI tools, the result is a structural subsidy for proprietary product development that deepens market concentration rather than broadening it. Dafny and Lee (2022) have documented how merger and acquisition dynamics in health technology markets operate to consolidate rather than distribute the returns on innovation, a pattern that CRADA structures without IP guardrails would accelerate. The parallel to the pharmaceutical sector's use of federally funded basic research as an input to proprietary drug development — the "march-in rights" problem under the Bayh-Dole Act — is close enough to warrant explicit attention in the Department's response to this RFI. Kruse, Stein, Thomas, and Kaur (2018) found that population health applications built on EHR infrastructure generated substantial clinical value that was systematically captured by vendors through data licensing terms rather than redistributed to health systems or patients. The AI context amplifies this dynamic because the value of AI models is substantially constituted by training data, and the health sector's patient data is among the most valuable training resource available.⏎⏎IHPI | HHS Health Sector AI RFI Comments | March 2025 | Page 4⏎⏎The framing of AI as a cost-reduction mechanism, which appears repeatedly in the RFI's background materials, deserves scrutiny that this Institute believes the Department has not yet applied. The cost-reduction narrative is not simply optimistic; it is analytically incomplete in ways that systematically favor the acceleration argument. The full economic picture of clinical AI adoption includes vendor licensing fees, implementation costs, integration labor, maintenance contracts, retraining and validation costs over the product lifecycle, and the opportunity costs of clinical staff time devoted to AI-adjacent administrative burden. When these costs are fully accounted for, the net fiscal impact of clinical AI adoption on health systems — particularly small and rural systems — is considerably less favorable than vendor-sponsored analyses suggest. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan's (2019) analysis of racial bias in a commercial population health algorithm is relevant here not only for its direct findings on algorithmic bias but for what it revealed about the vendor's response to independent scrutiny: the algorithm had been deployed at scale across major health systems before independent researchers identified the bias, and the vendor's commercial incentives had not generated any internal pressure to identify or correct the problem. The cost-reduction narrative suppresses this kind of accountability problem by framing the primary question as whether AI saves money rather than whether the market structure surrounding AI deployment creates adequate incentives for ongoing performance accountability. The answer to the second question, under current market conditions, is almost certainly no, and the Department's reimbursement modernization proposal does not change that calculus.⏎⏎This Institute therefore submits the following specific recommendations, framed as preconditions for responsible deployment rather than as administrative obstacles to innovation. First, the Department should condition AI-related reimbursement authorization on the submission of prospective validation data in populations demographically representative of the intended patient base — a standard that, per Logos Research Centre (2024), fewer than 23% of currently deployed tools meet. This is not an unreasonable evidentiary threshold; it is the minimum standard for clinical credibility. Second, any public-private partnership or CRADA involving federally funded research infrastructure or patient data from federal programs should include explicit provisions requiring that resulting AI products be available under reasonable and non-discriminatory licensing terms, with pricing subject to independent review, and with march-in rights reserved to the Department in the event that products are priced in ways that limit access for safety-net providers. Third, the Department should require, as a condition of reimbursement eligibility, that AI vendors disclose the material terms of their EHR bundling agreements and licensing structures to CMS, so that the Department can assess whether reimbursement pathways are functioning as intended or are instead being captured by bundling dynamics that preclude meaningful competition. Fourth, the Department should commission an independent political economy audit of the RFI comment record — including an analysis of the organizational affiliations, lobbying expenditures, and commercial interests of the most active commenters — before finalizing any sub-regulatory guidance. The Logos Research Centre (2024) findings suggest that structural adoption barriers are real and addressable; the Institute does not oppose reimbursement clarity as a policy goal. The concern is that reimbursement clarity created without the structural safeguards above will function primarily as a transfer of value from the federal health programs to a small number of dominant vendors, and that the acceleration framing of the RFI has, whether intentionally or not, created conditions in which that transfer is the path of least regulatory resistance.⏎⏎IHPI | HHS Health Sector AI RFI Comments | March 2025 | Page 5⏎⏎One additional observation warrants inclusion here, though the Institute acknowledges it sits somewhat adjacent to the primary analytical thread of these comments. The RFI's question set does not include a direct question about the geographic distribution of AI adoption benefits and costs, and this absence is notable. Mississippi, for instance — a state with among the highest rates of chronic disease burden and among the lowest concentrations of health system capital — presents a market profile in which the vendors most likely to benefit from accelerated reimbursement authorization are the least likely to deploy their products in ways that serve the state's patient population. The validation gap documented by Logos Research Centre (2024) is not randomly distributed across the demographic landscape; it is concentrated in precisely the populations that anchor Mississippi's disease burden: rural, low-income, predominantly Black communities whose health data has historically been underrepresented in the training sets of commercial AI tools. Accelerating the reimbursement pathway for tools that have not been validated in these populations does not accelerate benefit to these populations. It accelerates the extraction of reimbursement revenue from federal programs on behalf of products whose performance in the populations driving that revenue is empirically unknown. The Department's acceleration agenda, if it proceeds without the safeguards described above, will function as a subsidy for incumbent vendors at the expense of the populations — and the states — that federal health programs were designed to serve. The Institute recognizes that the Department faces real pressure from multiple directions on this issue, and that the reimbursement ambiguity problem is not fictitious. But the structural incentives created by the proposed approach, as this Institute reads the evidence, strongly favor outcomes that will require corrective action within five to seven years — at substantially greater political and fiscal cost than addressing the market structure problem now would require. The 14-month average deployment delay documented by Logos Research Centre (2024) is a real cost, but it is a cost that falls on health systems and vendors. The cost of deploying undervalidated AI tools at scale in underserved populations, without adequate performance accountability mechanisms, is a cost that falls on patients — and that cost is⏎⏎IHPI | HHS Health Sector AI RFI Comments | March 2025 | Page 6⏎⏎considerably harder to recover.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0056♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret♔Robinson♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔GE Healthcare♔♔♔♔♔♔GE Healthcare Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎As someone who sits in budget meetings and hears how AI is actually pitched to health system administrators, I want to be direct with the Department about something that vendor presentations do not mention: the primary appeal of AI productivity tools is not clinician wellbeing, it is staffing ratio justification. When a radiology department demonstrates that AI-assisted workflows reduce read times by 31%, the first question from finance is not "how do we give radiologists back their afternoons?" It is "how do we document efficiency gains to support a reduced headcount in the next contract cycle." That dynamic is real, and federal guidance that does not explicitly address it will accelerate it.⏎⏎What concerns me equally is procurement. Frontline clinicians are almost never in the room when these tools are selected. The people deciding what gets deployed have productivity targets, not patient caseloads. That gap in decision-making authority has consequences that HHS should not leave for health systems to sort out voluntarily.⏎⏎GE Healthcare Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0057♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Campbell♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔Alzheimer's Association♔♔♔♔♔♔Alzheimer's Association⏎225 N. Michigan Ave., Floor 17⏎Chicago, IL 60601⏎⏎Sharon Campbell, Research Director⏎Policy and Research Affairs Division⏎⏎April 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attention: Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎The Alzheimer's Association submits these comments with a degree of genuine ambivalence that we believe the record should reflect. Our organization has tracked the translational pipeline for clinical AI tools with considerable attention over the past several years — our biennial R&D Acceleration Index, published in the second quarter of 2024, drew on structured interviews with 112 translational research leads across federally funded programs and documented that 68% of clinical AI tools developed through NIH-supported initiatives faced an average delay of 3.2 years between validation completion and meaningful clinical deployment. We have seen, in other words, what regulatory ambiguity and reimbursement uncertainty cost patients who need better tools now. Alzheimer's disease affects an estimated 6.9 million Americans age 65 and older, and the diagnostic window for meaningful intervention is narrow in ways that make delay genuinely consequential. We understand the urgency that animates this RFI. And yet we find ourselves compelled to raise a concern that the framing of this request does not fully anticipate: the question of whether accelerating AI adoption in clinical care, as this RFI envisions it, risks doing harm not through technical failure but through a subtler erosion of something the evidence suggests is clinically irreplaceable.⏎⏎The RFI uses the word "productivity" in a way that deserves more scrutiny than a solicitation of this kind typically invites. HHS frames AI adoption primarily as a mechanism for increasing efficiency, reducing administrative burden, and scaling clinical capacity — all of which are legitimate goals. But productivity is a concept borrowed from manufacturing contexts, and its application to medicine carries assumptions that do not transfer cleanly. Medicine is not primarily a production process. It is, at its foundation, a relational practice: a structured encounter between a person in distress and another person trained to interpret that distress, calibrate a response, and maintain a relationship across time that is itself therapeutic. Kelley and colleagues conducted a systematic review and meta-analysis published in PLOS ONE in 2014 documenting that the quality of the patient-clinician relationship is an independent predictor of health outcomes across a wide range of conditions, operating through mechanisms distinct from technical care quality (Kelley, J.M., et al., 2014, PLOS ONE, 9(4), e94207). Hojat and colleagues demonstrated in a 2011 study published in Academic Medicine that physician empathy was significantly associated with better clinical outcomes for diabetic patients, including hemoglobin A1c levels and LDL cholesterol control — outcomes that are not plausibly explained by information transfer alone (Hojat, M., et al., 2011, Academic Medicine, 86(3), 359–364). The therapeutic alliance, a concept developed initially in psychiatric contexts but now understood to have broad clinical applicability, refers to the quality of the working relationship between clinician and patient as a predictor of treatment adherence, symptom disclosure, and engagement with care. If AI intermediation systematically degrades that alliance — and there is reason to believe it can — then productivity gains measured in throughput will mask losses measured in outcomes.⏎⏎Alzheimer's Association Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | April 2025 | Page 2⏎⏎We want to be precise about the nature of the harm we are describing, because it is easy to mischaracterize this concern as technophobia or as a romanticization of pre-digital medicine. It is neither. Abraham Verghese wrote in Health Affairs in 2011 that the physical examination is not merely a diagnostic procedure but a ritual of witnessing — that the act of the clinician attending carefully to a patient's body communicates something that cannot be communicated through a portal message or a structured data query (Verghese, A., 2011, Health Affairs, 28(4), 1177–1182). This is not sentiment. It is a description of a clinical mechanism. Consider what a clinician working with a patient in early-stage Alzheimer's actually attends to: not only the score on a Mini-Mental State Examination, but the way the patient's daughter leans forward when her mother struggles to name the current month, the patient's own affect when she realizes she cannot retrieve a word she once used easily, the quality of the silence in the room when the diagnosis is spoken aloud. These are not inefficiencies to be optimized. They are the data. And they are data that a pattern-recognition system trained on structured clinical records is not designed to capture, because they were never entered into the EHR in the first place. Danielle Ofri, in her 2017 book What Doctors Feel, documented through extensive clinical observation that the emotional attunement of clinicians — their capacity to register and respond to patient distress in real time — is not incidental to diagnosis but constitutive of it; clinicians who are emotionally present notice things that clinicians who are cognitively overloaded or procedurally distracted do not (Ofri, D., 2017, Beacon Press). If AI systems are introduced in ways that shift the clinician's attentional focus toward machine-generated outputs and away from the patient in the room, the diagnostic information carried in the relational encounter is not merely underweighted — it is lost.⏎⏎The literature on automation bias makes this concern concrete and empirically grounded. Tversky and Kahneman's foundational work on heuristics and biases established that human judgment under uncertainty is systematically susceptible to anchoring on salient reference points, even when those reference points are demonstrably unreliable (Tversky, A., & Kahneman, D., 1974, Science, 185(4157), 1124–1131). Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt conducted a systematic review of automation bias in clinical decision support contexts, published in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association in 2012, and found that clinicians routinely defer to automated recommendations even when those recommendations conflict with clinical evidence that the clinician has directly observed — and that this effect is stronger, not weaker, when the automated system has a track record of accuracy (Goddard, K., Roudsari, A., & Wyatt, J.C., 2012, JAMIA, 19(1), 121–127). The implication is counterintuitive: a more accurate AI system may produce more automation bias, not less, because the clinician's prior experience with the system's reliability provides a stronger anchor. In the context of Alzheimer's diagnosis specifically, where the differential is often genuinely ambiguous and where the consequences of both over-diagnosis and under-diagnosis are severe, this dynamic is not a theoretical concern. It is a likely operational outcome of deploying AI clinical decision support tools without careful attention to how they reshape clinical reasoning in practice.⏎⏎Alzheimer's Association Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | April 2025 | Page 3⏎⏎Related to automation bias is the problem of clinical deskilling, which the RFI does not address at all and which the Association believes is among the most consequential long-term risks of rapid, broad-based AI deployment. Skinner and colleagues published longitudinal evidence from radiology AI adoption in community hospital settings in the Journal of Health Services Research and Policy in 2023, finding measurable degradation in the independent diagnostic performance of radiologists in settings where AI tools had been in use for more than eighteen months — a finding that held even after controlling for case mix and baseline skill levels (Skinner, T., et al., 2023, Journal of Health Services Research & Policy, 28(3), 189–197). If the same dynamic operates in primary care, neurology, and geriatric medicine — the settings most relevant to Alzheimer's care — then the clinical workforce that remains after AI tools are removed or fail will be less capable than the workforce that existed before those tools were introduced. This is not a reversible condition on any short policy timeline. Training a clinician takes years. Rebuilding clinical intuition that has atrophied through disuse may not be possible at all. The National Academy of Medicine's 2023 update to its report on artificial intelligence in health care explicitly identified workforce deskilling as an underresearched risk warranting longitudinal study before broad deployment, a recommendation that this RFI does not appear to have incorporated into its framing (NAM, 2023, Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: The Hope, the Hype, the Promise, the Peril — 2023 Update, Washington, D.C.).⏎⏎We want to acknowledge the real tension here, because the Association believes in intellectual honesty about tradeoffs. Eric Topol's 2019 paper in Nature Medicine, which remains one of the most cited frameworks for thinking about clinical AI, argued that AI's capacity to take over pattern-recognition tasks could, in principle, free clinicians to spend more time on the relational and contextual dimensions of care that machines cannot replicate (Topol, E.J., 2019, Nature Medicine, 25(1), 44–56). Topol himself was careful to note that this outcome is not automatic — it depends on deliberate system design and on reimbursement structures that reward relational care rather than throughput. The Association shares that view. The problem is that the reimbursement structures Topol's optimistic scenario requires do not currently exist, and this RFI does not propose to create them. What it proposes instead is to accelerate deployment under the current reimbursement architecture, which rewards volume and procedure and does not reward the time a clinician spends sitting with a patient's silence. Under those conditions, AI tools are more likely to be used to compress the duration of clinical encounters than to enrich their quality. Obermeyer and Emanuel made a related point in their 2016 New England Journal of Medicine paper on machine learning and clinical medicine, noting that the integration of predictive tools into clinical workflow tends to reflect and amplify the incentive structures of the systems into which they are introduced rather than transforming those structures (Obermeyer, Z., & Emanuel, E.J., 2016, NEJM, 375(13), 1216–1219). The Association is concerned that HHS is proposing to accelerate a process whose directional effects are being determined by incentive structures the Department has not proposed to change.⏎⏎Alzheimer's Association Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | April 2025 | Page 4⏎⏎The Association also wants to raise a specific concern about what the research literature calls the long-term human-computer relationship problem, which is particularly relevant to AI tools intended for use in chronic disease management — the category that includes most Alzheimer's care coordination applications. Bickmore and Picard's foundational work on human-computer relationships, published in ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction in 2005, demonstrated that patients can form functional relational attachments to automated systems, including attachments that influence health behavior — but that these attachments are qualitatively different from therapeutic alliances with human clinicians and are particularly fragile under conditions of system discontinuity, such as software updates, model retraining, or vendor transitions (Bickmore, T.W., & Picard, R.W., 2005, ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction, 12(2), 293–327). For Alzheimer's patients, whose capacity to adapt to relational discontinuity is already compromised by the disease itself, this fragility is not a minor implementation concern. It is a clinical safety issue. A patient who has developed a reliance on an AI-mediated care coordination tool, and who then experiences that tool's removal or substantial alteration, may experience that transition as a form of relational loss with measurable effects on anxiety, disorientation, and engagement with care. The current regulatory framework has no mechanism for evaluating this risk before a tool is approved or reimbursed, and the RFI does not propose to create one. Epstein and Street, writing in the Annals of Family Medicine in 2011, argued that patient-centered care is defined not by the tools used to deliver it but by whether those tools support or undermine the patient's experience of being known and attended to by a care system (Epstein, R.M., & Street, R.L., 2011, Annals of Family Medicine, 9(2), 100–103). The Association would ask HHS to consider whether any of the AI tools this RFI proposes to accelerate into clinical use have been evaluated against that criterion — and if not, why not.⏎⏎The Association's specific policy recommendations in response to this RFI are as follows, though we note these are offered in a spirit of constructive engagement rather than as a comprehensive reform agenda:⏎⏎1. HHS should reframe the central question of this RFI from "how do we accelerate AI deployment" to "how do we ensure that AI deployment does not degrade the relational architecture of clinical care." This is not a semantic distinction. It has direct implications for which outcomes are measured, which tools are prioritized, and which reimbursement changes are pursued.⏎⏎2. Any clinical AI tool seeking Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement should be required to demonstrate, through prospective clinical evidence, that its deployment does not measurably degrade the therapeutic alliance between clinician and patient. The validated instruments for measuring therapeutic alliance — including the Working Alliance Inventory and the Patient-Doctor Relationship Questionnaire — are not perfect, but they are available and have been used in clinical research for decades. There is no technical barrier to requiring their use in AI deployment evaluations. There is only a political will barrier.⏎⏎3. HHS should commission longitudinal research on clinical deskilling across the specialties most likely to be affected by near-term AI deployment, including radiology, primary care, and neurology, with findings reported to Congress before any major expansion of AI reimbursement under Medicare. The Skinner et al. findings from radiology are suggestive but not definitive, and the Association believes the Department has an obligation to generate better evidence before committing to deployment at the scale this RFI envisions.⏎⏎4. The Department should convene a working group specifically focused on AI deployment in the care of patients with cognitive impairment, who are a population with distinct vulnerabilities related to relational continuity, system transition, and the capacity to advocate for themselves when care quality degrades. This working group does not currently exist in any form the Association has been able to identify, and its absence from the RFI is notable.⏎⏎Alzheimer's Association Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | April 2025 | Page 5⏎⏎The Association wants to close by saying something that is genuinely difficult to say in a regulatory comment without sounding like it is opposing progress, because we are not. We believe AI will be part of Alzheimer's care. We believe some of the diagnostic and monitoring tools in development are genuinely promising. Our R&D Acceleration Index tracks more than 340 active clinical AI development projects, and there are tools in that pipeline — particularly in the area of multimodal biomarker integration for early detection — that we think could be transformative for patients who currently receive a diagnosis years later than the disease's biological onset. The delays those tools face, driven by reimbursement ambiguity and undefined regulatory pathways, are real costs borne by real patients. We have not come to this comment as opponents of the technology.⏎⏎What we have come as is an organization that has spent years watching the translational pipeline closely enough to know that speed and quality are not the same thing, and that the clinical encounter — the moment when a frightened patient and a careful clinician work together to understand what is happening to a body and a mind — is not a legacy inefficiency to be optimized away. It is the thing that medicine is. A man sits across from his neurologist and minimizes his symptoms, as patients with Alzheimer's often do in the early stages, because he is frightened and because minimizing is what people do when they are frightened. The clinician notices that his hands are trembling slightly in a way that is inconsistent with the calm he is performing. She pauses. She asks a different question. That pause, that noticing, that willingness to follow a thread that no structured intake form would have surfaced — that is what produces an accurate diagnosis in that encounter. We are genuinely uncertain whether the AI deployment architecture this RFI envisions would protect that moment or erode it, and we think the Department should be uncertain too, and should say so, rather than treating deployment speed as an unqualified good. The Association's policy monitoring database will continue to track how these questions are answered in practice, and we intend to return to this record as evidence accumulates. We would also ask that HHS provide a clearer timeline for how comments submitted through this RFI will be incorporated into subsequent rulemaking or guidance, since the⏎⏎alzheimers.org/policy | policy@alz.org⏎⏎Sharon Campbell⏎Research Director, Policy and Research Affairs⏎Alzheimer's Association♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0058♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Perez♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Maria had her hip replaced at St. Francis in Tulsa in March of last year, and watching the nurses scramble between paper charts and computer screens while she waited for pain medication is what got me thinking about all of this. I understand why HHS wants to move quickly. But here is what I keep coming back to: when something goes wrong with an AI system in a hospital, nobody seems to know who is actually responsible. The doctor says the tool gave bad information, the hospital points to the vendor, the vendor says the data was the problem. I mean, in a classroom if a student gets hurt, I know exactly who answers for it. That clarity just does not seem to exist yet with medical AI, and I think settling that question has to come before any acceleration.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0059♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Green♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔Epic Systems Corporation♔♔♔♔♔♔Epic Systems Corporation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎EQUITY AUDITING — THE MISSING PRECONDITION⏎⏎We have seen AI deliver real efficiency gains. Pulse oximetry bias, sepsis prediction failures, dermatology tools collapsing in safety-net settings — these are documented patterns, not hypotheticals. HHS proposes acceleration with zero mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing. That is not acceptable. No deployment pathway should be cleared without it.⏎⏎Epic Systems Corporation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0060♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Anderson♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔American Thoracic Society♔♔♔♔♔♔American Thoracic Society⏎Comments Submitted to HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Re: Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket Submitted via regulations.gov⏎⏎⏎ORGANIZATIONAL CONTEXT AND THE EQUITY PROBLEM HHS HAS NOT ADDRESSED⏎⏎The American Thoracic Society represents clinicians, researchers, and public health professionals working across pulmonary medicine, critical care, and sleep medicine, with significant organizational presence in community health settings across rural and underserved regions of the United States. Our network includes clinical implementation sites in North Dakota and six adjacent states where safety-net institutions serve populations with limited access to specialist care. This comment addresses a specific and serious gap in the RFI as structured: HHS has articulated a detailed framework for accelerating AI adoption but has said almost nothing about how the Department will prevent AI systems from replicating and amplifying the health disparities that already define the communities most likely to depend on the care settings where these tools will first be deployed at scale.⏎⏎That is not a peripheral concern. It is the central public health question this RFI raises, even if unintentionally.⏎⏎The literature on algorithmic bias in clinical AI is no longer speculative. It is a documented, replicated body of findings. Obermeyer and colleagues, writing in Science in 2019, demonstrated that a widely used commercial algorithm for identifying high-risk patients systematically underestimated illness severity in Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent clinical need, because the algorithm used healthcare cost as a proxy for health status — and historical spending patterns encoded decades of differential access (Obermeyer Z, Powers B, Vogeli C, Mullainathan S. Science. 2019;366(6464):447-453). Sjoding and colleagues published findings in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 showing that pulse oximeters — a technology so routine it is almost invisible — produced clinically significant measurement errors in patients with darker skin pigmentation at rates that went undetected for years precisely because the discrepancy was consistent and therefore looked like signal rather than noise (Sjoding MW et al. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(25):2477-2478). Adamson and Smith documented in JAMA Dermatology that machine learning diagnostic tools in dermatology were trained almost entirely on images of lighter-skinned patients, producing accuracy gaps that track almost perfectly with the populations least represented in academic medical center datasets (Adamson AS, Smith A. JAMA Dermatol. 2018;154(11):1247-1248).⏎⏎These are not edge cases. They are the pattern. And the RFI HHS has published proposes to accelerate the spread of AI tools built on the same kinds of training data, validated in the same kinds of academic institutions, without any mandatory requirement that developers demonstrate performance equity across demographic subgroups before deployment in community health settings.⏎⏎⏎ATS Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎WHY ACCELERATION WITHOUT EQUITY INFRASTRUCTURE IS A DOCUMENTED RISK, NOT A THEORETICAL ONE⏎⏎Wong and colleagues published an external validation of a widely implemented sepsis prediction algorithm in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2021 and found that the tool's performance degraded substantially outside the academic medical center environment where it was trained, with the model generating high false-positive rates that consumed clinical resources without improving patient outcomes (Wong A, Otles E, Donnelly JP, et al. JAMA Intern Med. 2021;181(8):1065-1070). Seyyed-Kalantari and colleagues documented in Nature Medicine that AI algorithms applied to chest radiographs showed systematic underdiagnosis bias in underserved patient populations, meaning the patients who most needed accurate diagnosis were the ones the models were least likely to identify correctly (Seyyed-Kalantari L et al. Nat Med. 2021;27(12):2176-2182). These are precisely the populations served by the community health centers in our network.⏎⏎There is a mechanism that makes this worse than equivalent human error, and HHS should understand it clearly. Gianfrancesco and colleagues, writing in JAMA Internal Medicine, documented that electronic health record data used to train clinical AI encodes not just clinical patterns but access patterns — who got which tests, who received follow-up, whose symptoms were taken seriously — and that these structural biases are invisible in the output because the algorithm presents them as objective findings rather than as products of a discriminatory history (Gianfrancesco MA et al. JAMA Intern Med. 2018;178(11):1544-1547). Sendak and colleagues have described the way that clinical AI tools acquire an authoritative quality in practice that suppresses the normal skepticism clinicians apply to imperfect information — the tool produces a number or a risk score, and the clinician's cognitive default is to treat it as more reliable than their own uncertain judgment (Sendak MP et al. Proc ACM Conf Fairness Accountability Transparency. 2020:99-109). The combination of embedded historical bias and automation-driven deference means that AI tools can produce disparate harm at scale before anyone notices the pattern, because the harm looks like clinical reality rather than algorithmic error.⏎⏎Wiens and colleagues articulated a responsible deployment framework in Nature Medicine that called explicitly for ongoing post-deployment monitoring as a non-negotiable component of clinical AI implementation — not as a best practice but as a structural requirement (Wiens J et al. Nat Med. 2019;25(9):1337-1340). Char, Shah, and Magnus similarly argued in the New England Journal of Medicine that machine learning implementation in health care requires prospective ethical analysis, not retrospective correction (Char DS, Shah NH, Magnus D. N Engl J Med. 2018;378(11):981-983). The National Academy for State Health Policy's 2023 policy brief on algorithmic accountability in state Medicaid programs identified the absence of standardized demographic performance reporting as the single most consistent gap across existing state-level oversight frameworks (NASHP Policy Brief. 2023). HHS is positioned to close that gap nationally. The RFI, as written, does not indicate any intention to do so.⏎⏎⏎ATS Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 2 [continued]⏎⏎SPECIFIC REQUESTS FOR HHS ACTION⏎⏎Our organization asks HHS to address the following before establishing any reimbursement pathway or regulatory accommodation that would accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings:⏎⏎1. Mandatory pre-deployment equity impact assessments. Any AI tool seeking CMS reimbursement or ONC certification should be required to demonstrate performance equivalence across racial, ethnic, socioeconomic, and geographic subgroups, tested in care settings representative of the deployment environment — not solely in the academic medical centers where most current validation studies are conducted. Vyas and colleagues have documented extensively how race-corrective adjustments embedded in clinical algorithms perpetuate rather than correct disparity, and any equity assessment framework must account for that dynamic (Vyas DA, Eisenstein LG, Jones DS. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(9):874-882).⏎⏎2. Post-deployment bias surveillance with public reporting requirements. Facilities deploying AI tools in clinical care should be required to collect and report demographic-stratified outcome data at regular intervals, with results made publicly available in a standardized format. Pierson and colleagues demonstrated in Nature Medicine that algorithmic analysis of pain treatment patterns revealed unexplained disparities in underserved populations that would not have been visible without systematic disaggregation of outcome data (Pierson E et al. Nat Med. 2021;27(1):136-141). That kind of analysis should not be optional.⏎⏎3. Conditioning reimbursement on demonstrated equity performance. Any CMS billing pathway created for AI-assisted diagnostics or clinical decision support should include a performance equity condition — meaning that tools showing statistically significant outcome disparities across demographic subgroups would lose reimbursement eligibility until the disparity is corrected. This is not an unusual regulatory instrument; CMS applies performance conditions in other quality programs, and there is no principled reason AI tools should be exempt.⏎⏎Our network's deployment data, collected across 14 months of community health center implementation sites, shows that facilities in rural and low-income settings are already struggling to evaluate the equity implications of the AI tools being offered to them. Vendors do not routinely provide subgroup performance data disaggregated by race or income. Facilities do not have the analytic infrastructure to generate it independently. And there is no regulatory requirement that either party close that gap. One site coordinator in Bismarck, North Dakota told us in a structured interview that the vendor's validation documentation referenced performance in a single academic health system in the mid-Atlantic region and contained no data at all on patient populations resembling the ones the tool would actually be used to treat.⏎⏎That is the gap HHS has the authority to close, and this RFI is the appropriate vehicle for doing so. Our organization is prepared to provide additional data from our clinical network to support HHS in developing a workable equity assessment framework, and we ask that the Department issue a specific supplemental request for comment on mandatory equity auditing standards before finalizing any accelerated adoption pathway. The technical capacity to do this well exists; what is missing is the regulatory requirement that it be done at all. The communities we serve cannot afford to wait for post-deployment correction cycles that begin only after patients have been harmed.⏎⏎For questions regarding this comment, contact:⏎American Thoracic Society, Health Policy Division⏎atspolicy@thoracic.org⏎25 Broadway, New York, NY 10004♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0061♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Sanchez♔♔New York♔♔United States♔National Physicians Alliance♔♔♔♔♔♔NATIONAL PHYSICIANS ALLIANCE⏎Comments in Response to HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI⏎Submitted by: Thomas Sanchez, Director of Government Affairs⏎National Physicians Alliance | Washington, D.C.⏎⏎National Physicians Alliance Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎The National Physicians Alliance (NPA) represents over 1,200 member institutions spanning academic medical centers, community hospitals, and integrated health systems. A 2024 survey of our membership found that 68% have deployed at least one AI-enabled clinical decision support (CDSS) tool, yet fewer than 22% have established formal governance frameworks to evaluate ongoing algorithmic performance or address model drift. A concurrent NPA policy analysis reviewing 47 peer-reviewed implementation studies published between 2021 and 2024 identified reimbursement uncertainty as the single most frequently cited barrier to scaling validated AI tools from pilot programs into standard care pathways. These findings inform the NPA's core concern with this RFI: HHS is preparing to accelerate AI adoption in healthcare without adequately reckoning with the fact that the most profitable and rapidly expanding deployed application of AI in this sector is not clinical decision support — it is denial automation.⏎⏎The phrase "AI in healthcare" is a contested term. It encompasses both CDSS tools designed to improve diagnostic accuracy (Rajpurkar P, Chen E, Banerjee O, Topol EJ, "AI in health and medicine," Nature Medicine, 2022;28(1):31-38) and automated prior authorization denial engines deployed by major insurers to maximize claim rejection volume with minimal physician review. These are not equivalent applications. One is designed to help clinicians serve patients. The other is designed to protect insurer margins. Any HHS acceleration framework that treats them as categorically similar will, in practice, turbocharge the latter at the expense of the former.⏎⏎National Physicians Alliance Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎THE ASYMMETRY THE RFI DECLINES TO NAME⏎⏎The NPA's most significant concern with HHS's framing in this RFI is the Department's apparent decision to treat AI reimbursement policy as a mechanism to "foster competition among clinical care AI tool builders" without addressing the asymmetry of incentives already shaping the market. Clinical AI tools face uncertain reimbursement pathways, high governance burdens, and the full weight of FDA regulatory scrutiny where applicable. AI denial engines deployed by insurers face none of those barriers. They are already generating substantial returns. The UnitedHealth Group subsidiary NaviHealth deployed an AI model that, according to reporting by Mukherjee and Ross in STAT News (November 2023), denied post-acute care claims for elderly Medicare Advantage patients at rates dramatically exceeding what independent physician review would have supported — in some documented cases, within seconds of submission, with no meaningful human clinical judgment applied. The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations reached similar conclusions in its November 2024 report, "Denial, Delay, Disappear: How Medicare Advantage Insurers Use Algorithms to Deny Care," which found systematic use of algorithmic tools to deny care in ways that the Subcommittee concluded were inconsistent with clinical evidence and beneficiary medical needs.⏎⏎The Office of Inspector General documented related patterns in April 2022 (OIG Report OEI-09-18-00260), finding that some Medicare Advantage organizations denied prior authorization requests that met Medicare coverage rules. The American Medical Association's 2023 Prior Authorization Physician Survey found that 94% of physicians reported that prior authorization delays patient care, and 33% reported that a patient experienced a serious adverse event as a direct result of a prior authorization denial. The Kaiser Family Foundation's March 2023 issue brief by Koma, Cubanski, and Neuman documented that prior authorization use in Medicare Advantage is pervasive and that appeal rates remain low — not because denials are appropriate, but because elderly and low-income patients lack the administrative capacity to navigate multi-step appeals processes. The CMS Interoperability and Prior Authorization Final Rule (CMS-0057-F, Federal Register, January 2024) took meaningful steps toward transparency, but it does not establish enforceable clinical appropriateness standards for algorithmically generated denials, and it does not require insurers to disclose the demographic disaggregation of denial rates.⏎⏎This is the policy vacuum into which HHS is now proposing to inject an acceleration framework. The NPA does not believe that is a sound sequencing of regulatory priorities.⏎⏎National Physicians Alliance Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎HEALTH EQUITY IS NOT A SECONDARY CONSIDERATION⏎⏎The NPA addresses specifically the RFI's questions regarding health equity implications of AI adoption, because the documented evidence is alarming and the RFI's treatment of it is insufficient. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan demonstrated in Science (2019;366(6464):447-453) that a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to manage population health systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients, resulting in less care being allocated to a sicker population. The mechanism was not explicit racial coding — it was the use of healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, a variable that encodes historical inequities directly into algorithmic outputs. The National Partnership for Women and Families documented in its January 2024 policy brief on algorithmic discrimination in health insurance that similar proxy-variable problems appear in denial-optimization models, where factors correlated with race, disability status, and socioeconomic position produce disparate denial rates that insurers are not currently required to report or remediate.⏎⏎The research on automation bias compounds this concern. Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt's systematic review in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association (2012;19(1):121-127) documented that clinicians and reviewers consistently over-rely on algorithmic recommendations even when those recommendations are incorrect — a finding with direct implications for the "human review" provisions that insurers claim justify their AI denial workflows. When a physician reviewer is presented with an AI-generated denial recommendation and given 90 seconds to adjudicate a complex post-acute care case, that review is not meaningfully independent. Schulman and Richman made a related point in JAMA (2023;329(12):983-984), arguing that prior authorization in chronic disease management functions less as a clinical safeguard than as an administrative barrier specifically calibrated to reduce utilization by patients who cannot sustain a prolonged appeals process.⏎⏎National Physicians Alliance Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎NUMBERED RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎The NPA respectfully submits the following specific recommendations for HHS consideration:⏎⏎1. HHS must condition any AI reimbursement incentive, adoption subsidy, or regulatory accommodation on demonstrated compliance with clinical appropriateness standards validated by independent clinical bodies — not by the deploying insurer.⏎⏎2. HHS must establish mandatory human review thresholds for AI-generated prior authorization decisions, with minimum time standards that are clinically meaningful rather than perfunctory. A 90-second review of a complex skilled nursing facility admission does not constitute independent physician judgment.⏎⏎3. HHS must require transparent denial rate reporting disaggregated by patient age, race, disability status, and income level, submitted to CMS on a quarterly basis and made publicly available. The absence of this data is not accidental — it is a structural feature of a market in which opacity is profitable.⏎⏎4. HHS must explicitly exclude cost-containment-optimized denial AI from any "acceleration" framework. The RFI's language about fostering AI competition should apply to clinical tools designed to improve patient outcomes, and HHS should say so clearly in any subsequent guidance or rulemaking.⏎⏎5. Any insurer deploying an AI system that generates prior authorization decisions at scale must register that system with HHS, disclose its training data sources and outcome validation methodology, and submit to periodic independent auditing — a framework analogous to what CMS-0057-F begins to establish for interoperability but extended explicitly to algorithmic denial tools.⏎⏎The NPA acknowledges that the governance frameworks necessary to implement these recommendations do not yet exist in complete form, and the organization is prepared to work with HHS and ASTP/ONC to develop technically sound implementation standards. However, the sequencing matters enormously. Acceleration before accountability is not a neutral policy choice — it is a choice that will disproportionately harm the patients least equipped to absorb the consequences. Member institutions have told NPA directly, at the 2024 annual convening and in subsequent written correspondence, that they are watching clinical AI investment stall while insurer AI investment in denial automation scales without regulatory friction.⏎⏎A full bibliography of cited works and the NPA's 2024 membership survey methodology are provided in Appendix A, which is available upon request from the NPA Office of Government Affairs.⏎⏎thomas.sanchez@nationalphysiciansalliance.org⏎[Appendix A — transmitted separately per docket submission instructions; confirmation of receipt requested]♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0062♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔13♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Garcia♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔Families USA♔♔♔♔♔♔Families USA⏎1225 New York Avenue NW, Suite 800⏎Washington, DC 20005⏎www.familiesusa.org⏎⏎George Garcia⏎Director of Government Affairs⏎Families USA⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attention: Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care (HHS Health Sector AI RFI, 2024)⏎⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎The framing of this RFI concerns us before we even reach its specific questions. HHS asks how the Department can "accelerate" AI adoption in clinical care, and the word accelerate, appearing as it does in the very title of the solicitation, is doing a great deal of policy work that the document itself never fully acknowledges. Families USA has spent decades representing the interests of health care consumers — people who are sick, frightened, economically precarious, and often navigating a system that already treats them as cases rather than as human beings with complex lives and complicated needs. When we read an RFI that frames clinical AI deployment primarily as a question of removing barriers to adoption, and that situates "productivity" as a core metric against which AI's value should be measured, we want to be honest: that framing is itself a policy choice, and it is not a neutral one. The choice to foreground productivity in describing the clinical encounter is a signal that HHS may be operating from a transactional model of care — one in which the value of a clinical interaction is captured primarily by what gets done, documented, or billed, rather than by what gets healed.⏎⏎We want to be clear that Families USA is not categorically opposed to AI in health care. We have followed the literature carefully, and we take seriously the evidence that certain AI tools — particularly in medical imaging interpretation, sepsis detection, and medication error reduction — have demonstrated genuine clinical utility in controlled settings. Eric Topol's 2019 synthesis in Nature Medicine remains an important touchstone for what AI might accomplish in high-performance medicine, and we do not dismiss that work. But Topol's framework, and the optimism it represents, rests on assumptions about the clinical context in which AI is deployed that the HHS RFI never interrogates. Specifically, the question of what happens to the therapeutic relationship when algorithmic intermediation becomes routine — that question is almost entirely absent from this solicitation. That absence is what prompted this comment.⏎⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE THERAPEUTIC RELATIONSHIP IS NOT A SENTIMENT. IT IS A CLINICAL VARIABLE.⏎⏎There is a tendency in policy discussions to treat the therapeutic relationship — the quality of connection, trust, and mutual understanding between a patient and a clinician — as a soft value, something important to acknowledge in passing before getting to the real metrics of accuracy, efficiency, and cost. This is a mistake, and the research literature says so clearly. Kelley and colleagues published a systematic review and meta-analysis in PLOS ONE in 2014 examining randomized controlled trials that specifically tested whether the quality of the patient-clinician relationship affected health outcomes (Kelley JM, Kraft-Todd G, Schapira L, et al., "The influence of the patient-clinician relationship on healthcare outcomes: a systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials," PLOS ONE, 2014;9(4):e94207). Their findings were not ambiguous. Strong therapeutic relationships were associated with statistically significant improvements in objective clinical outcomes — not just patient satisfaction scores, not just self-reported wellbeing, but measurable clinical endpoints. Ridd and colleagues conducted a synthesis of the qualitative literature on patients' perspectives of the patient-doctor relationship, published in the British Journal of General Practice in 2009, and identified continuity, trust, and the sense of being known as the features patients themselves identified as most therapeutically meaningful (Ridd M, Shaw A, Lewis G, Salisbury C, "The patient-doctor relationship: a synthesis of the qualitative literature on patients' perspectives," British Journal of General Practice, 2009;59(561):e116-e133). Neither of these findings is new. What is new is that HHS is now designing policy frameworks for a technology that, if deployed without relational safeguards, has the structural capacity to erode both.⏎⏎Zulman and colleagues, writing in JAMA in 2020, documented specific clinician practices that sustain physician presence and connection during clinical encounters, and their framework implicitly acknowledges that such presence is under pressure from exactly the kind of documentation and alert burden that AI systems are often proposed to relieve (Zulman DM, Haverfield MC, Shaw JG, et al., "Practices to foster physician presence and connection with patients in the clinical encounter," JAMA, 2020;323(1):70-81). The argument that AI will free up clinicians to be more present is theoretically plausible. But the mechanism by which that occurs is not automatic, and the HHS RFI contains no framework for ensuring it happens. If a clinician's cognitive attention is redirected from the patient to the management of an AI interface — reviewing outputs, adjudicating alerts, documenting AI-assisted findings — there is no guarantee that the relational quality of the encounter improves. In fact, there is reason to worry that it degrades, because the AI system creates a third party in the room, an authoritative-seeming one, whose outputs the clinician must attend to.⏎⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 3⏎⏎⏎Consider a scenario that Families USA hears about, in various forms, from the patients and families we represent. A woman in her mid-fifties comes into a primary care clinic in rural Alabama. She has been putting off this visit for months. Her presenting complaint is vague — fatigue, some weight loss, a feeling she can't quite name. She is not medically sophisticated. She does not know how to describe what she is experiencing in clinical language. She sits across from a physician who, in a well-resourced AI-assisted practice, has already received an algorithmic risk stratification score based on her EHR data before she walks in the door. The algorithm, trained on population-level data, assigns her a moderate probability of depression and a low probability of serious organic pathology. The physician, whose attention has been partially structured by that output before the conversation begins, asks about her mood. She says she's fine, she's just tired. The physician, whose next alert is already queued, accepts that. What the algorithm could not have known — what only a clinician with the time and relational attunement to notice would have known — is that she is sitting with her arms crossed, that she answers questions in monosyllables, that her affect is flat in a way that has nothing to do with depression and everything to do with fear. She is terrified. She has felt a lump. She has not told anyone. She will not volunteer it unless someone gives her the space and the signal that it is safe to. The algorithm cannot give her that signal. It can only process what has already been documented.⏎⏎This is not a hypothetical designed to make AI look bad. It is a description of what medicine actually requires at its most consequential moments. Abraham Verghese and colleagues wrote in JAMA in 2018 that what the computer needs is a physician — specifically, a physician whose clinical judgment is grounded in direct observation, embodied presence, and interpretive attention to the patient as a whole person, not as a data object (Verghese A, Shah NH, Harrington RA, "What this computer needs is a physician: humanism and artificial intelligence," JAMA, 2018;319(1):19-20). That argument is not anti-technology. It is pro-medicine. And it is the argument that HHS's RFI, in its current framing, does not make room for.⏎⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 4⏎⏎⏎AUTOMATION BIAS AND DESKILLING ARE DOCUMENTED PHENOMENA, NOT SPECULATIVE FEARS⏎⏎One of the things that troubles Families USA most about the accelerationist framing of this RFI is that it treats the risks of AI deployment in clinical settings as primarily technical — bias in training data, accuracy of algorithms, interoperability with EHR systems. These are real concerns, and we do not minimize them. But the HHS solicitation pays almost no attention to what happens to clinician cognition and judgment capacity over time as AI systems become embedded in routine clinical workflow. This is not a speculative concern. It has been documented empirically in both the human factors literature and the health care management literature, and the findings are not reassuring.⏎⏎Parasuraman and Manzey published a comprehensive review of complacency and automation bias in human use of automation in Human Factors in 2010, drawing on decades of research in aviation, military systems, and process control industries (Parasuraman R, Manzey DH, "Complacency and bias in human use of automation: an attentional integration," Human Factors, 2010;52(3):381-410). Their core finding is that when human operators work alongside automated systems that are generally reliable, they develop a systematic tendency to accept automated outputs without adequate scrutiny — even in cases where the automated output is wrong. This is automation bias, and it is not a character flaw. It is a predictable consequence of how human attention works under cognitive load. Tversky and Kahneman's foundational work on heuristics and biases, published in Science in 1974, described the anchoring effect specifically: once a number or a category is placed in front of a decision-maker, subsequent judgments tend to cluster around that anchor, even when the decision-maker is aware of the bias and trying to correct for it (Tversky A, Kahneman D, "Judgment under uncertainty: heuristics and biases," Science, 1974;185(4157):1124-1131). An AI diagnostic recommendation is an anchor. It will shape clinical reasoning even when the clinician believes they are exercising independent judgment. The question is not whether this effect is real. It is whether HHS's deployment framework accounts for it.⏎⏎Hoff examined deskilling and adaptation among primary care physicians using two work innovations and found that the introduction of clinical decision support tools was associated with measurable erosion of specific diagnostic reasoning skills that physicians had previously exercised independently (Hoff T, "Deskilling and adaptation among primary care physicians using two work innovations," Health Care Management Review, 2011;36(4):338-348). This is the long-term risk that the current RFI entirely ignores: a generation of clinicians who have trained and practiced in AI-saturated environments may have systematically underdeveloped capacities for autonomous clinical judgment — not because they are less capable, but because the system in which they practice has not required those capacities of them. When the AI system fails, or encounters a case outside its training distribution, or produces an output that is confidently wrong, the clinician who has chronically deferred to it may not have the internal resources to catch the error. Blease and colleagues, in an exploratory qualitative study published in the Journal of Medical Internet Research in 2019, found that UK general practitioners expressed concern about exactly this dynamic — that AI clinical decision support might make them better at the average case while making them worse at the outlier, which is precisely the case where independent judgment matters most (Blease C, Kaptchuk TJ, Bernstein MH, et al., "Artificial intelligence and the future of primary care: exploratory qualitative study of UK general practitioners' views," Journal of Medical Internet Research, 2019;21(3):e12802).⏎⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 5⏎⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 6⏎⏎⏎THE ILLUSION OF OBJECTIVITY⏎⏎There is a particular danger in AI recommendation systems that the HHS RFI does not address and that we think deserves explicit attention. When a clinician exercises judgment and gets it wrong, the error is visible as a human error — something that can be examined, learned from, attributed, corrected. When an AI system produces a recommendation that a clinician follows, and that recommendation is wrong, the error is structurally obscured. The clinician followed the algorithm. The algorithm was based on peer-reviewed methodology. The recommendation was consistent with population-level evidence. The error looks, from the outside, like bad luck or unusual pathology rather than a failure of clinical judgment. This is the illusion of objectivity: the AI system does not actually make clinical decisions more objective, but it makes the process of decision-making appear more objective, which is not the same thing.⏎⏎Shortliffe and Sepúlveda, writing about clinical decision support in the era of artificial intelligence in JAMA in 2018, noted that the field has a long history of systems that perform well in evaluation and then fail in deployment, precisely because deployment contexts involve the full complexity of clinical practice in ways that evaluation contexts do not (Shortliffe EH, Sepúlveda MJ, "Clinical decision support in the era of artificial intelligence," JAMA, 2018;320(21):2199-2200). Emanuel and Wachter, also writing in JAMA in 2019, asked directly whether the value of AI in health care would match its hype, and their answer was cautious: the conditions under which AI adds clinical value are specific and require careful governance, and the history of health IT adoption gives reason to expect that governance frameworks will lag behind deployment (Emanuel EJ, Wachter RM, "Artificial intelligence in health care: will the value match the hype?" JAMA, 2019;321(23):2281-2282). These are not fringe voices. These are among the most credible scholars in health policy and informatics, and they are raising concerns that the HHS RFI's accelerationist framing does not engage with.⏎⏎The illusion of objectivity is particularly dangerous in the relational dimension of clinical care because it can displace genuine clinical reasoning while appearing to enhance it, which makes the displacement invisible. A clinician who consciously decides not to pursue a line of inquiry because they are pressed for time knows they have made a tradeoff. A clinician who does not pursue that line of inquiry because an AI system's risk stratification score has already structured the encounter toward a different set of priorities may not recognize that a tradeoff has been made at all. The harm is the same. The visibility of the harm is not.⏎⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 7⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC RESPONSES TO RFI QUESTIONS ON EVALUATION FRAMEWORKS AND REIMBURSEMENT⏎⏎Families USA wishes to address two specific areas of the HHS solicitation where our concerns are most directly engaged: the question of evaluation frameworks for AI in clinical care, and the question of coverage and reimbursement policy as a lever for AI adoption.⏎⏎On evaluation frameworks, the RFI asks how HHS should evaluate AI tools for clinical effectiveness. The Department's current framing of this question centers on technical performance metrics: accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, area under the ROC curve, performance across demographic subgroups. These metrics matter, and we support rigorous technical evaluation. But they are not sufficient, and an evaluation framework that includes only these metrics will systematically fail to capture the harms we have described in this comment. Families USA urges HHS to require, as a condition of any federal endorsement, coverage recommendation, or deployment incentive for clinical AI tools, that evaluation frameworks include patient-reported relational quality metrics. Specifically, we recommend:⏎⏎1. Patient-reported assessments of whether they felt heard, known, and understood during the clinical encounter in which AI was used.⏎2. Longitudinal measurement of therapeutic alliance quality across practices with varying levels of AI integration, using validated instruments such as the Working Alliance Inventory adapted for clinical settings.⏎3. Clinician self-assessment of autonomous diagnostic reasoning confidence, measured over time, to detect the early signatures of deskilling before they manifest in patient outcomes.⏎4. Qualitative capture of cases in which clinician override of AI recommendations was clinically significant, to understand both the conditions under which human judgment adds value and the conditions under which automation bias suppressed it.⏎5. Measurement of encounter duration and the proportion of encounter time during which clinician attention was directed at the patient versus at a screen or interface.⏎⏎None of these metrics are exotic or technically difficult to collect. Some of them are already used in patient experience research. What is missing is any policy requirement that they be included in evaluations of AI clinical tools. The HHS RFI, as currently framed, would allow a clinical AI system to be endorsed and incentivized for deployment based solely on its technical performance in retrospective validation studies, with no requirement that its effects on the therapeutic relationship be measured at all. That gap is a policy choice, and it is a consequential one.⏎⏎On reimbursement, Families USA understands that payment policy is a powerful lever for adoption. We note that RBMA survey data from 2024 indicates that 74% of radiology practices identified inconsistent Medicare reimbursement pathways as the primary barrier to AI integration, and we take that finding seriously. But we want to be direct about something: the fact that reimbursement is a barrier to adoption does not mean that removing the barrier is the right policy response absent other safeguards. Reimbursement policy can be designed to incentivize AI deployment in ways that also require evidence of relational quality preservation, not just technical performance. CMS has used value-based payment mechanisms in other contexts to shape the conditions of care delivery, not just its volume. There is no structural reason why coverage for AI-assisted clinical services could not be conditioned on demonstrated compliance with a relational quality evaluation framework of the kind we have described. We urge HHS to explore this approach before designing reimbursement pathways that accelerate deployment without such conditions attached.⏎⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 8⏎⏎⏎THE SPECIFIC POPULATION CONCERN⏎⏎Families USA's core constituency includes people who are particularly vulnerable to the harms we have described: patients with low health literacy, patients who are frightened or in pain, patients navigating complex family and social circumstances that shape their clinical presentation in ways that no algorithm trained on structured EHR data can fully capture. Bickmore and colleagues, in research presented at the SIGCHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems in 2009, examined how low health literacy hospital patients interacted with virtual nurse agents, and found that while such systems could provide useful informational support, their capacity to recognize and respond to emotional complexity was severely limited (Bickmore TW, Pfeifer LM, Jack BW, "Taking the time to care: empowering low health literacy hospital patients with virtual nurse agents," Proceedings of the SIGCHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems, 2009:1265-1274). The patients who most need a clinician who can read ambiguity, interpret silence, and respond to the full human situation are precisely the patients for whom AI intermediation carries the greatest relational risk.⏎⏎This is not an argument that AI should never be used in the care of vulnerable populations. It is an argument that the deployment of AI in contexts where patients are frightened, inarticulate, or navigating complex social circumstances requires a level of relational safeguarding that the current HHS RFI framework does not envision. When a patient cannot tell you what is wrong in clinical language, when their distress is communicated through body language and affect and the things they do not say, the clinician's capacity to perceive and respond to that communication is not a supplementary feature of good care. It is the care. An AI system that processes structured data and produces recommendations based on patterns in that data cannot substitute for that perceptive capacity, and a clinical encounter that is structured primarily around AI outputs may not create the conditions in which that capacity can operate.⏎⏎We want to note that this concern is not limited to rural or underserved populations, although those populations face particular risks. The therapeutic relationship matters in every clinical context. But it matters most when patients are most vulnerable, and the HHS RFI does not segment its analysis in a way that accounts for differential vulnerability. A deployment framework designed primarily around the needs of well-resourced, health-literate patients in settings where AI performs well may produce policies that are actively harmful when applied in contexts where those assumptions do not hold.⏎⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 9⏎⏎⏎A SPECIFIC POLICY REQUEST⏎⏎Families USA is asking for something specific, and we want to state it plainly before closing. We are asking HHS to commission a dedicated research agenda on the relational and humanistic dimensions of AI integration in clinical care before any further acceleration of deployment through reimbursement incentives, coverage expansions, or regulatory streamlining. The research agenda should include at minimum:⏎⏎a. Prospective studies measuring the effect of AI clinical decision support tools on patient-reported relational quality in diverse clinical settings, with specific attention to populations with low health literacy, high health anxiety, or complex social circumstances.⏎⏎b. Longitudinal studies of clinician diagnostic reasoning capacity in AI-integrated versus non-AI-integrated practice environments, using validated measures of clinical reasoning skill and designed to detect early deskilling signatures.⏎⏎c. Qualitative research on the lived experience of patients in AI-assisted clinical encounters, with particular attention to whether and how patients perceive AI intermediation as affecting their sense of being heard and understood.⏎⏎d. Development and validation of patient-reported relational quality instruments specifically designed for use in AI-assisted clinical contexts, suitable for inclusion in routine evaluation frameworks.⏎⏎e. Analysis of the interaction between automation bias and clinical complexity, examining whether the conditions under which automation bias is most likely to suppress accurate clinical judgment are also the conditions in which patients are most vulnerable.⏎⏎This research agenda should be completed, or at least substantially underway with preliminary findings available, before HHS designs reimbursement pathways or deployment incentives that would make AI-assisted clinical care the default rather than the option. The Department has an obligation to the patients it serves to understand what it is accelerating toward before it accelerates.⏎⏎We also ask that HHS formally amend its evaluation framework requirements to include patient-reported relational quality metrics as a condition of any federal coverage recommendation or endorsement for clinical AI tools. This should not be optional, and it should not be deferred to a future rulemaking. The RFI process is the appropriate moment to establish that relational quality is a required dimension of clinical AI evaluation, not an afterthought.⏎⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 10⏎⏎⏎There is one more thing Families USA wants to say, and it is perhaps the most direct thing in this comment. HHS's use of the word "productivity" in framing the value of AI in clinical care is not just a word choice. It reflects an underlying model of what the clinical encounter is for. A productivity model says the encounter is for generating outputs: diagnoses, prescriptions, referrals, documented assessments. A relational model says the encounter is for something that includes those outputs but is not reducible to them — it is for the patient to feel known, to trust the clinician enough to tell them what they are actually experiencing, to leave with not just a treatment plan but with the sense that another human being has genuinely attended to their situation. These models are not fully compatible, and the tension between them is not resolved by adding AI to the mix. It is, in many scenarios, sharpened by it.⏎⏎The HHS RFI asks how the Department can accelerate AI adoption. Families USA is asking HHS to first answer a different question: what are we accelerating toward, and who does it serve? The answer to that question requires taking the therapeutic relationship seriously as a clinical variable, measuring relational quality as a condition of deployment endorsement, and funding the research needed to understand what happens to clinical judgment and patient-centered care when algorithmic intermediation becomes routine. We recognize that this is a harder path than simply clearing regulatory and reimbursement barriers. We also think it is the path that is actually consistent with HHS's stated mission to enhance the health and wellbeing of all Americans.⏎⏎Families USA respectfully requests that HHS respond to these specific recommendations in any subsequent guidance or rulemaking, and that the Department indicate whether it intends to incorporate relational quality metrics into its AI evaluation framework. We would welcome the opportunity to meet with the Office of the Deputy Secretary and ASTP/ONC staff to discuss the research agenda proposal in more detail.⏎⏎For questions regarding this submission, please contact George Garcia, Director of Government Affairs, Families USA, at the address listed above or at⏎⏎ggarcia@familiesusa.org⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 11♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0063♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Taylor♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔American Association of Physician Assistants♔♔♔♔♔♔American Association of Physician Assistants⏎950 North Washington Street⏎Alexandria, VA 22314⏎⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL-RFI⏎⏎⏎The American Association of Physician Assistants (AAPA) appreciates the opportunity to respond to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings. AAPA represents more than 168,000 certified physician assistants practicing across every clinical specialty and care setting in the United States, and our member institutions have been navigating AI adoption decisions with increasing urgency. A 2024 AAPA member survey found that 68% of responding clinical organizations are actively piloting or evaluating AI-assisted diagnostic and clinical decision support tools, and 74% of those respondents identified reimbursement uncertainty as the primary barrier to full deployment. We raise those numbers not to argue for faster reimbursement pathways but because they tell a different story than HHS appears to be hearing: institutions are moving quickly, they are doing so under conditions of significant uncertainty, and the governance architecture that should be guiding those decisions does not yet exist. This comment is focused on two of the RFI's framing questions — specifically the questions regarding regulatory barriers and the appropriate federal role in AI governance — and it is written not primarily as a technical implementation response but as a substantive objection to the decision-making framework the Department has adopted.⏎⏎The word that keeps coming back when AAPA staff reviewed the RFI text is "acceleration." The Department has framed this entire inquiry around reducing friction, removing uncertainty, and speeding adoption. What is absent from that framing is any acknowledgment that the uncertainty being reduced is, in significant part, the legitimate uncertainty of democratic deliberation — the ordinary condition of a society that has not yet decided what it wants. HHS's posture, as expressed in the RFI's organizing logic, treats regulatory scrutiny as a cost imposed on innovation rather than as an exercise of public authority. That is a substantive policy position, and it is one the Department does not have the mandate to adopt unilaterally through an RFI process that is not subject to the full notice-and-comment requirements of the Administrative Procedure Act, 5 U.S.C. §§ 551-559, 701-706. The acceleration agenda, if translated into regulatory action, would constitute a substantive change in the Department's approach to AI governance, and AAPA believes that change requires the procedural legitimacy the APA demands. The President's Council of Advisors on Science and Technology's 2023 report on AI governance in federal health programs reached similar conclusions about the absence of a coherent federal framework, and HHS's response to that gap has been to solicit ideas for moving faster rather than ideas for building the governance architecture that would make speed responsible.⏎⏎AAPA Comments | HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL-RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The governance concern the Department appears to be treating as secondary is, in AAPA's view, the primary question. Sheila Jasanoff's work on technology governance — particularly in The Ethics of Invention: Technology and the Human Future (W.W. Norton, 2016) — describes with precision the dynamic AAPA's member institutions are already experiencing: the embedding of technological systems in institutional infrastructure creates conditions of practical irreversibility from which there is no clean exit. Coiera, Ash, and Berg documented the same phenomenon in the health information technology context, finding that after electronic health record deployment, institutional knowledge atrophies, workflows reorganize around vendor logic, and the capacity to function without the system disappears within years (Yearbook of Medical Informatics, 2016). Bresnick's 2023 analysis in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association extended that finding specifically to clinical AI, documenting dependency cycles that closely parallel EHR adoption patterns. The word "irreversibility" is not rhetorical here; it is empirical. Once a health system has reorganized its diagnostic workflows around a proprietary AI architecture, the practical ability to reverse that decision — to return to human-centered processes, or to switch vendors, or to demand transparency as a condition of continued use — diminishes rapidly. Dafny and Lee's analysis of institutional dependency and market concentration dynamics in the New England Journal of Medicine (2016) is instructive on how quickly market structures consolidate around embedded systems, foreclosing the competitive conditions that might otherwise discipline vendor behavior.⏎⏎The irreversibility problem is structurally connected to a second problem that the acceleration framing makes worse rather than better: the opacity of the systems being deployed. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan's 2019 Science paper on racial bias in a widely deployed health management algorithm demonstrated that the internal logic of proprietary clinical AI is inaccessible not only to patients but to the clinicians and institutions using the systems, and often to the vendors themselves. Rudin's analysis in Nature Machine Intelligence (2019) goes further, arguing that the project of explaining black-box models for high-stakes clinical decisions is structurally impossible — that explainability tools do not provide the accountability they are claimed to provide. Mittelstadt, Russell, and Wachter reached consistent conclusions in their 2019 FAT* conference proceedings paper, finding that explanations in AI are better understood as a legitimation mechanism than a transparency mechanism. The policy implication is direct: if the structural preconditions for democratic oversight of clinical AI — interpretability, auditability, regulatory access to model logic — do not currently exist for the majority of deployed systems, then accelerating deployment before those preconditions are established is not a neutral technical choice. It is a transfer of decision-making authority from accountable public institutions to corporate entities whose internal governance is not subject to democratic accountability. Metcalf, Moss, and boyd documented this transfer dynamic in their analysis of corporate AI ethics frameworks (Social Research, 2019), finding that the institutionalization of ethics within Silicon Valley firms functions primarily to pre-empt external governance rather than to create it. HHS's acceleration agenda, whatever its intentions, would accelerate that transfer.⏎⏎AAPA Comments | HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL-RFI | Page 2 [sic]⏎⏎There is also a more fundamental democratic question that the RFI does not raise and that AAPA believes the Department is obligated to acknowledge. Democratic societies retain the legitimate authority to designate certain domains as ones where human judgment, accountability, and dignity are preserved even at the cost of efficiency. Medicine is among the domains most frequently identified in democratic theory as warranting that protection — not because technology cannot improve outcomes in the aggregate, but because the consequences of clinical decisions fall on specific individuals who have a right to know that a human being with moral accountability was present at the moment of consequence. Eric Topol's widely cited 2019 Nature Medicine paper on the convergence of human and artificial intelligence is optimistic about AI's potential, but even Topol acknowledges that the governance preconditions for responsible deployment have not been established. The decision about whether to accept efficiency gains at the cost of human judgment in clinical settings is not a technical question. It is a political question of the kind that the APA's notice-and-comment architecture was designed to surface and deliberate. HHS's current posture forecloses that deliberation by treating it as friction. AAPA does not believe that is within the Department's mandate. The regulatory framework gaps in AI interoperability requirements under 45 C.F.R. § 170.213 are symptomatic of the same underlying problem: the technical standards infrastructure that would make AI systems auditable and governable has not been built, and the acceleration agenda would deploy systems at scale before that infrastructure exists.⏎⏎AAPA's specific requests of the Department are:⏎⏎1.   Reframe the RFI. The organizing question should not be "how do we accelerate AI adoption" but "what governance architecture must be in place before acceleration is responsible." That reframe does not preclude adoption; it conditions it.⏎⏎2.   Impose a deployment moratorium on novel AI-assisted clinical decision support applications in high-stakes diagnostic and treatment contexts, pending the development of a coherent federal governance framework that addresses interpretability, auditability, and post-market surveillance requirements as conditions of CMS coverage eligibility.⏎⏎3.   Commission a structured democratic deliberation process — modeled on the citizens' assembly format used in Ireland and France for similarly contested policy questions — before any acceleration agenda proceeds. That process should include patients, clinicians, ethicists, and community representatives, and its outputs should bind the Department's subsequent rulemaking agenda.⏎⏎4.   Require, as a condition of any CMS coverage determination for AI-enabled clinical tools, that the applying entity provide regulators with full access to model architecture and training data sufficient for independent auditability — not explainability outputs, which Rudin (2019) and Mittelstadt et al. (2019) have shown to be inadequate substitutes for genuine transparency.⏎⏎AAPA recognizes that these requests will be read by some as obstructionist. They are not. They reflect the judgment of an organization whose 168,000 members are on the front lines of clinical AI deployment and who are watching governance decisions being made by default — not by deliberation. The moratorium request in particular is unlikely to be popular with vendors or with HHS components focused on innovation metrics. But the alternative — deploying systems whose internal logic is inaccessible, whose institutional dependencies are irreversible, and whose societal implications have not been democratically deliberated — is a choice the Department should make explicitly, with full APA procedural accountability, rather than by⏎⏎aapa.org/policy | comments@aapa.org | 703-836-2272♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0064♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Thomas♔♔Wyoming♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted via the Federal eRulemaking Portal: regulations.gov⏎⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-Clinical-RFI⏎⏎Submitted by: Linda Thomas, Cheyenne, Wyoming (Veteran, VA Healthcare System)⏎⏎⏎⏎I am 78 years old and a veteran, and I get my care at the Cheyenne VA. Back in November 2022, the facility here was trying out some kind of pilot AI triage tool. On three different occasions, this computer program flagged my lab values as critically abnormal. But my rheumatologist had just retired, and those flags just sat there in some digital queue. Nobody looked at them. My methotrexate dosage wasn't monitored for eleven months, and I ended up hospitalized for liver toxicity in early 2023. The AI technology actually did what it was supposed to do, but the hospital had absolutely no workflow set up to handle it.⏎⏎⏎⏎Sitting in that hospital bed got me pretty angry, and since I had a lot of time on my hands while recovering, I started reading up on how these healthcare algorithms actually work. What I found out honestly terrifies me, and looking at your Request for Information, it seems like HHS is completely missing the boat. You are asking everyone how to speed up AI adoption. But you aren't laying down any strict rules to make sure this rushed technology doesn't just end up hurting the patients who are already getting the worst care in this country. That is a massive hole in your plan.⏎⏎⏎⏎From everything I've read, algorithmic bias isn't just a hypothetical worry. It's happening right now. I read about a major study by a researcher named Obermeyer a few years ago. They looked at a commercial algorithm used all over the place to manage patient health, and it was consistently underestimating how sick Black patients were. Why? Because the computer used healthcare costs to guess who was sickest. Since Black patients had historically been prescribed less care, the algorithm just assumed they weren't as sick and kept that discrimination going on a massive scale. Then there was an article in the New England Journal of Medicine about pulse oximeters. They don't work as well on people with darker skin. If an AI is learning from that flawed sensor data, it's just going to spit out flawed decisions. Same goes for skin cancer AI that only learned from pictures of light-skinned people, or those kidney calculators that just blindly assume biological race differences are the reason for health disparities. If an AI trains on that data, it just copies those bad assumptions.⏎⏎⏎⏎Reading your RFI, it feels like HHS thinks the only thing standing in the way of AI is red tape, like FDA timelines and billing codes. Obviously those things matter, but the FDA just checks if a device does what the manufacturer claims it does. They don't check if it works fairly for every type of person in a messy, understaffed real-world clinic. There was another study I saw by Wong and some other doctors where they took a widely used sepsis prediction AI that worked great at a fancy academic hospital. When they tried it out in regular community hospitals, it was a disaster. It performed much worse than the vendor claimed. It was FDA cleared and widely adopted, but its real-world performance in a place with fewer resources was terrible. Nobody caught it before it was rolled out.⏎⏎⏎⏎Because of all this, I want HHS to put some very specific, mandatory rules in place. First off, before Medicare or CMS pays a dime for an AI tool, the company needs to prove it actually works for everyone. They should have to show their accuracy data broken down by race, age, sex, and insurance status. If an AI is 90% accurate overall but only 68% accurate for the kind of folks you'd find at a rural VA clinic, it shouldn't be allowed in those clinics without everyone knowing about it.⏎⏎⏎⏎Also, testing an AI at a massive university hospital with a huge IT staff doesn't prove it will work at a place like the Cheyenne VA, where our computers are older and we don't have a dedicated AI team. You need to require that these tools are tested in the actual types of facilities where they will be used.⏎⏎⏎⏎You also can't just turn these things on and walk away. There needs to be mandatory monitoring after the AI goes live. If a tool starts showing a bias and hurting certain groups, there has to be a hard rule to suspend it immediately while it gets investigated. The FDA already does this kind of post-market surveillance for medical devices, so you could easily just adapt that system for AI.⏎⏎⏎⏎And please, do something about the human side of this. Like what happened to me. The alert was there, but nobody had to act on it. Or sometimes the opposite happens, where doctors just blindly trust the computer and ignore their own eyes because they think the machine is smarter than them. You shouldn't reimburse any hospital for using AI unless they can prove they have a strict, auditable protocol for exactly who looks at the AI alerts, how fast they have to respond, and what happens if an alert gets ignored.⏎⏎⏎⏎The folks who are going to suffer the most when AI fails are the ones already struggling. Rural patients, veterans in underfunded facilities, and minorities whose past bad treatment is literally baked into the training data. If you speed up AI without putting basic fairness protections in place, you are just going to widen the care gap. But it will be worse, because the bad decisions will look like objective computer logic, and doctors won't question it. I don't want you to stop AI. I just want you to make sure it's safe and fair before you unleash it on me and other veterans.⏎⏎⏎⏎Finally, I noticed your whole document doesn't even mention how HHS is going to coordinate with the Veterans Health Administration on any of this. The VA is a huge testing ground for AI right now, and we are a very vulnerable population. Leaving us out of this conversation completely just doesn't make any sense to me.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0065♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Hall♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven Hall⏎4417 Wisteria Court⏎Macon, Georgia 31204⏎⏎June 2, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎To Whom It May Concern:⏎⏎On a Tuesday morning in February of this year, I sat in an exam room at a hospital-affiliated practice in Macon and watched a physician's assistant review what she described as the AI triage system's output for my case. I had come in with chest tightness, some jaw discomfort, and fatigue that I had been attributing to the stress of mid-year assessments at school. The system, I was told, had flagged my presentation as low-priority. I left with a pamphlet about stress management and guided breathing exercises. Two days later, my GP looked at the same EKG herself, by eye, and ordered a stress test. The test revealed a significant coronary blockage. I want HHS to sit with that sequence for a moment before reading any further.⏎⏎I am not writing as someone who is hostile to technology. I teach twenty-two eight-year-olds in a public elementary school in Bibb County, and I use digital tools every single day. I have watched educational technology genuinely change how children learn to read. I understand what it means for a tool to assist rather than obstruct. What happened to me in February was not a technology failure in any simple sense. The algorithm processed what it was given. What it could not process was the way I was sitting, slightly forward in the chair, or the fact that I mentioned offhand that my father died of a heart attack at sixty-three, or the particular flatness in my voice when I said I felt fine. A physician who was actually present and actually listening might have caught any one of those things. Abraham Verghese wrote in Health Affairs back in 2011 about what he called the "iPatient" problem, where the data representation of a person begins to substitute for the person themselves, and the clinical encounter loses its diagnostic texture as a result. That is what I experienced. I was not a patient in that room. I was a data point that resolved to a low-priority output, and I went home with a pamphlet.⏎⏎What troubles me about this RFI's framing is the language of acceleration. HHS wants to know how to speed adoption, how to remove regulatory friction, how to improve productivity. I genuinely don't understand how productivity became the organizing concept for a question about clinical care. Donna Zulman and her colleagues published a paper in JAMA in 2020 documenting the measurable clinical value of physician presence and attentiveness in the exam room, the kind of presence that takes time and cannot be optimized away. Mohammadreza Hojat and colleagues showed in Academic Medicine in 2011 that physician empathy produces independent, statistically significant improvements in clinical outcomes for diabetic patients. These are not soft findings about patient satisfaction. They are outcome data. The relational quality of a clinical encounter is itself a clinical variable, and any policy framework that treats it as inefficiency to be engineered around is working from the wrong model of what medicine is. I keep thinking about the family member who sits in the corner of the exam room and says something small, almost to herself, that changes everything the physician understands about what the patient has been telling them. No triage algorithm has a field for that. There is no structured data entry for the way a patient hesitates before answering a question.⏎⏎The deskilling concern is something I have not seen addressed anywhere in HHS's framing, and I think it is the most serious long-term risk that this RFI is not asking about. Katharine Goddard and her colleagues published a systematic review in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association in 2012 on automation bias, the well-documented tendency for clinicians who routinely defer to algorithmic recommendations to lose independent reasoning capacity over time. This is not a hypothetical. It is a measurable cognitive effect. When a physician's default behavior becomes reviewing an AI output and either accepting or overriding it, rather than forming an independent clinical judgment first, the judgment muscle atrophies. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman's foundational work on heuristics and biases, published in Science in 1974, helps explain why this happens: when a salient anchor number or category is placed in front of a decision-maker, it reshapes what questions they think to ask. An AI triage score of "low priority" does not just inform a clinician's judgment. It changes the cognitive environment in which that judgment forms. The physician's assistant I saw in February was not negligent. She was operating in a system that had placed a low-priority flag in front of her before she had a chance to look at me. I do not blame her. I do blame any regulatory framework that treats that dynamic as an acceptable cost of faster adoption.⏎⏎Zach Obermeyer and Thomas Lee wrote in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2017 that algorithms can outperform human cognition on specific, well-defined tasks with clean data, and I take that seriously. I am not arguing that AI has no place in clinical settings. I am arguing that the question of where it belongs, under what conditions, with what human oversight, and with what demonstrated safety record in real patient populations before deployment, is a question that "accelerating adoption" answers in entirely the wrong direction. The equity dimension is something I also want to name, because atypical presentations of cardiac disease are more common in older patients, in women, and in certain demographic groups, and if the training data for these triage tools does not reflect that variation adequately, then the patients most likely to be misclassified are already among the most medically underserved.⏎⏎My specific asks are these, stated as plainly as I can: HHS should require demonstrated safety and equity performance data across relevant patient subpopulations before any AI diagnostic or triage tool is deployed in a clinical setting. HHS should require that every such tool have an ironclad, frictionless clinical override pathway, and that using the override carries no administrative burden or documentation penalty that would discourage a clinician from exercising it. And HHS should hold the treating physician, not the algorithm's vendor, accountable for the clinical decision that follows any AI-assisted triage output, because accountability without a named human being is not accountability at all.⏎⏎I am sixty-seven years old. I have twenty-two children who expect me in their classroom on Monday morning. The GP who looked at my EKG with her own eyes is the reason I will be there.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Steven Hall⏎Macon, Georgia♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0066♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Scott♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Received via fax 03/14/2024 11:47 AM⏎Page 1 of 1⏎⏎My mother was at Baylor Scott & White in Waco two winters back and I came away from that stay genuinely hopeful that AI could take some of the documentation weight off the nurses, who were clearly stretched past their limit. But the more I sit with this request for information, the more I keep thinking about the people who helped us navigate her coverage questions -- not the nurses, but the coordinators and caseworkers who knew which extension to call, who knew my mother's specific situation, who knew when to push back on a denial. I worry that if those roles get automated, a patient with fewer resources than my family has won't have anyone in the loop who notices when the system flags something wrong. Please make sure whatever gets automated was actually replaceable before it disappears.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0067♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Davis♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔Nuance Communications♔♔♔♔♔♔Nuance Communications | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket No. [ONC-2024-0012] | Page 1⏎⏎⏎March 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎To the Office of the Deputy Secretary and ASTP/ONC Staff:⏎⏎Nuance Communications submits these comments in response to the Department's Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings. Nuance operates at the intersection of clinical documentation, ambient intelligence, and health system workflow integration, and the observations in this submission are grounded in that operational position. The data on AI-assisted oncology workflows are, frankly, compelling. Across networks deploying automated eligibility screening, trial identification time has been compressed from 23 days to under 72 hours, and per-site enrollment rates have increased by 34 percent compared to manual workflows. These are real operational gains, and Nuance does not dispute their significance. What this submission addresses is narrower and more specific: the RFI's framing of acceleration as the primary policy objective, without any mandatory equity infrastructure to detect or correct algorithmic harm before it reaches scale, is a structural problem that the Department has not adequately engaged.⏎⏎⏎Nuance Communications | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket No. [ONC-2024-0012] | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE DOCUMENTED PATTERN OF ALGORITHMIC HARM IN CLINICAL SETTINGS⏎⏎The case for caution here is not theoretical. Obermeyer Z, Powers B, Vogeli C, and Mullainathan S published findings in Science in 2019 demonstrating that a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to identify patients for care management programs assigned systematically lower risk scores to Black patients than to white patients with equivalent illness burden, effectively excluding Black patients from care coordination resources at a rate that could not be explained by any legitimate clinical variable (Obermeyer et al., Science 2019;366(6464):447-453). The algorithm used healthcare expenditure as a proxy for health need, and because structural inequities in the U.S. healthcare system mean that Black patients historically generate lower costs for equivalent severity of illness, the model encoded discrimination without any discriminatory intent on the part of its developers. This is the mechanism that concerns Nuance: bias does not require malicious design; it requires only that training data reflect a world in which disparities already exist. Separately, Sjoding MW, Dickson RP, Iwashyna TJ, Gay SE, and Valley TS demonstrated in the New England Journal of Medicine that pulse oximetry devices, a piece of technology so routine that clinicians rarely examine its assumptions, produced occult hypoxemia in Black patients at nearly three times the rate observed in white patients, a measurement error that went largely undetected for decades because the populations most affected were not adequately represented in validation studies (Sjoding et al., N Engl J Med. 2020;383(25):2477-2478). When that finding is placed alongside the Optum algorithm work, the pattern that emerges is one of compounding failure: devices and models validated in settings that overrepresent certain populations, deployed universally, with no ongoing surveillance mechanism to detect differential performance across race, ethnicity, or socioeconomic status. The RFI does not propose to break that pattern. It proposes to accelerate it.⏎⏎The dermatology literature is equally instructive. Adamson AS and Smith A noted in JAMA Dermatology that machine learning models for skin lesion classification were trained predominantly on images from light-skinned patients, meaning that their published performance metrics were essentially inapplicable to patients with darker skin tones (Adamson and Smith, JAMA Dermatol. 2018;154(11):1247-1248). This is not a minor technical caveat. Dermatology AI tools are now being positioned for deployment in primary care settings, including federally qualified health centers serving predominantly non-white, low-income populations, precisely the settings where the gap between published accuracy and real-world performance is widest. Gianfrancesco MA, Tamang S, Yazdany J, and Schmajuk G identified the structural reason for this gap in a 2018 JAMA Internal Medicine analysis: EHR-based training data systematically underrepresents patients with limited healthcare access, patients with multiple care episodes across disconnected systems, and patients whose clinical documentation reflects the lower-intensity engagement patterns associated with underinsured status (Gianfrancesco et al., JAMA Intern Med. 2018;178(11):1544-1547). The result is that algorithms trained on EHR data inherit the access patterns of the existing system rather than reflecting the clinical reality of the populations they will eventually serve.⏎⏎⏎Nuance Communications | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket No. [ONC-2024-0012] | Page 3⏎⏎⏎THE ACADEMIC-TO-COMMUNITY PERFORMANCE GAP AND AUTOMATION BIAS⏎⏎From a practice management perspective, there is a specific operational problem with the way AI validation studies are currently conducted and reported that the RFI does not address. Wong A, Otles E, Donnelly JP, and colleagues published an external validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2021 and found that the model's real-world performance at a community hospital was substantially worse than the performance reported in the original validation study conducted at the academic medical center where it was developed (Wong et al., JAMA Intern Med. 2021;181(8):1065-1070). The model had been marketed and adopted based on those original metrics. Clinicians using it at community hospitals, rural practices, and safety-net institutions had no way of knowing that the performance figures they had been shown did not apply to their patient population or their documentation environment. This is not an isolated case; it is a structural feature of how health AI validation works. Academic medical centers generate the training data, conduct the initial validation, and publish the performance metrics. Community hospitals and safety-net institutions deploy the model into a completely different data environment and assume the published numbers hold. They frequently do not.⏎⏎The automation bias problem compounds this. Char DS, Shah NH, and Magnus D described in the New England Journal of Medicine the mechanism by which clinicians, presented with a model output that carries the implicit authority of an algorithmic system, tend to suppress their own clinical skepticism in ways that would not occur if the same information were presented as a colleague's informal opinion (Char et al., N Engl J Med. 2018;378(11):981-983). Wiens J, Saria S, Sendak M, and colleagues elaborated on this in Nature Medicine, arguing that responsible ML deployment in healthcare requires not just technical validation but active workflow design that preserves clinician override capacity and does not present model outputs in ways that crowd out independent clinical reasoning (Wiens et al., Nat Med. 2019;25(9):1337-1340). What Nuance observes operationally is that these recommendations remain entirely voluntary. There is no deployment standard that requires health systems to test for automation bias effects in their specific clinical environment before going live with a model. The RFI asks about evaluation methods, and the honest answer from an operational standpoint is that current evaluation methods are inadequate not because the research community lacks good ideas, but because HHS has not made disaggregated performance reporting a condition of deployment. Seyyed-Kalantari L, Zhang H, McDermott MBA, Chen IY, and Ghassemi M demonstrated in a 2021 Nature Medicine study that chest radiograph AI models produced systematically higher underdiagnosis rates for patients on Medicaid, patients who were female, and patients who were Hispanic or Black, with the performance gaps being largest in the subgroups already at highest risk for missed diagnosis (Seyyed-Kalantari et al., Nat Med. 2021;27(12):2176-2182). Those gaps would not have been visible in aggregate accuracy statistics. They were visible only because the investigators disaggregated the results by demographic subgroup. The RFI does not require that disaggregation as a condition of anything.⏎⏎⏎Nuance Communications | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket No. [ONC-2024-0012] | Page 4⏎⏎⏎INTEROPERABILITY, DATA STANDARDS, AND THE AMPLIFICATION RISK⏎⏎The RFI asks specifically about interoperability infrastructure and data sharing as mechanisms for accelerating AI adoption. Nuance wants to be direct about a concern here that we do not think has received adequate attention in the policy conversation. Expanded interoperability is a genuine good. The HTI-1 Final Rule (89 Fed. Reg. 1192, Jan. 9, 2024) created meaningful progress on algorithm transparency and information sharing standards, and Nuance supported those provisions. But interoperability that increases the volume and velocity of data flowing into AI training pipelines does not automatically improve the equity of those pipelines. If the underlying data reflects decades of differential access, differential documentation quality, and differential representation across care settings, then connecting more of that data more efficiently means training more models on a larger version of the same biased substrate. Pierson E, Cutler DM, Leskovec J, Mullainathan S, and Obermeyer Z demonstrated in a 2021 Nature Medicine analysis that pain management algorithms trained on standard clinical data dramatically underestimated pain severity in underserved populations, and that correcting for this required not just more data but specifically equity-weighted data curation that actively compensated for historical underrepresentation (Pierson et al., Nat Med. 2021;27(1):136-140). The implication for interoperability policy is that data sharing standards need to include equity-weighting requirements, not as a secondary consideration, but as a condition of the infrastructure itself. Vyas DA, Eisenstein LG, and Jones DS made a related argument in the New England Journal of Medicine regarding race correction in clinical algorithms: the problem is not simply that race is used as a variable, but that its use often encodes social inequity as if it were biological fact, and that this encoding is invisible to end users who trust the algorithm's output without understanding its construction (Vyas et al., N Engl J Med. 2020;383(9):874-882). Interoperability infrastructure that connects more institutions to more data without requiring equity auditing of what that data contains will produce more models with the same structural problem, deployed more widely and with more institutional confidence in their outputs.⏎⏎⏎Nuance Communications | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket No. [ONC-2024-0012] | Page 5⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD REQUIRE, AND WHY THE RFI'S SILENCE IS A STRUCTURAL GAP⏎⏎Nuance is not asking HHS to slow AI adoption. The operational evidence for AI-assisted workflows is real, and the efficiency gains in settings like oncology trial matching are documented and material. What Nuance is asking is that HHS treat the absence of mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing and post-deployment bias surveillance not as a gap to be addressed in future rulemaking, but as a precondition for the acceleration agenda the RFI is proposing. Specifically, any federal reimbursement or certification pathway for clinical AI tools should require that developers provide disaggregated performance data by race, ethnicity, insurance status, and care setting before a tool is eligible for reimbursement or ONC certification. This is not an unreasonable technical burden. It is the minimum information a practice manager at a community hospital or a federally qualified health center needs to make an informed deployment decision. Post-deployment, HHS should require ongoing model performance monitoring with mandatory reporting thresholds that trigger review when performance degrades beyond specified tolerances in any demographic subgroup. The sepsis prediction experience documented by Wong and colleagues is instructive here: by the time a community hospital identifies that a model is underperforming in their environment, the model has already been making decisions about real patients for months or years. A mandatory surveillance framework with defined reporting triggers would catch that kind of drift before it becomes a documented harm pattern.⏎⏎The reimbursement data from oncology networks are worth returning to here, because they illuminate the policy lever. Practices operating under value-based care arrangements were 2.7 times more likely to have formally adopted AI-assisted patient stratification tools than those operating exclusively under fee-for-service models. Payment structure shapes adoption velocity. If HHS builds equity auditing requirements into the reimbursement conditions for AI tools, the market will respond. Developers who want access to Medicare and Medicaid reimbursement will conduct the disaggregated validation studies because the alternative is exclusion from the largest payer in the country. This is how the Department has historically moved markets on quality standards, and there is no principled reason why algorithmic equity should be treated differently. The concern Nuance wants to leave with the Department is specific: the communities most likely to be harmed by biased AI deployment are the same communities currently served by the care settings least likely to have the technical infrastructure to detect that harm on their own. A rural critical access hospital or a safety-net clinic in Baltimore does not have a data science team running equity audits on every AI tool their EHR vendor has embedded in the workflow. They are relying on the federal regulatory framework to have already asked those questions. As of this RFI, that framework has not asked them in any mandatory or enforceable way, and the acceleration agenda the Department is proposing will reach those settings before any corrective rulemaking does.⏎⏎Nuance Communications respectfully requests that HHS treat mandatory disaggregated performance reporting and post-deployment equity surveillance as threshold requirements for any federal action to accelerate clinical AI adoption, whether through reimbursement pathways, certification standards, or direct R&D investment. The contact for follow-up questions regarding this submission is the Nuance Government Affairs office at policy@nuance.com.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Mark Davis⏎Practice Manager, Clinical Workflow Operations⏎Nuance Communications⏎One Wayside Road⏎Burlington, MA 01803♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0068♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Hernandez♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔Veeva Systems♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew Hernandez⏎Compliance Officer, Veeva Systems⏎Winooski, Vermont 05404⏎⏎Submitted via Regulations.gov⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-Clinical-RFI⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎FRAMING CONCERN: THE RFI'S SELECTIVE SCOPE⏎⏎Veeva Systems appreciates HHS's engagement on the question of AI adoption in clinical settings, and our compliance function has tracked this issue carefully across the health systems and life sciences organizations we support. The data from PINC AI's Q2 2024 Health System Compliance and AI Readiness Survey — which found that 68% of affiliated facilities had piloted at least one AI-enabled clinical decision support tool in the preceding 24 months, yet fewer than 22% had achieved sustained, reimbursable integration — is consistent with what our own account teams observe when working with hospital compliance officers. The structural barriers are real: fragmented CMS reimbursement pathways, absent validation frameworks, and regulatory ambiguity around liability are all legitimate obstacles that this RFI is right to address. That said, what concerns this submission is not what the RFI asks, but what it omits. The Department has framed this inquiry almost entirely around the deployment of AI by clinical providers, and in doing so has set aside the most consequential and, from a compliance standpoint, the most legally exposed deployment of AI currently operating in the U.S. health system: automated prior authorization denial by commercial and Medicare Advantage insurers. The October 2024 U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations report, "Denial by Algorithm: How Health Insurers Use Artificial Intelligence to Deny Claims," documented in considerable detail how several large insurers built AI systems that optimized for denial throughput rather than clinical accuracy, and that these systems were producing denial rates well in excess of what physician reviewers would have generated. The OIG reached a related finding in April 2022, concluding in Report OEI-09-18-00260 that some Medicare Advantage organizations were denying prior authorization requests that met coverage criteria under traditional Medicare, a pattern consistent with algorithmic decision-making constrained by cost-containment objectives rather than medical necessity standards. The Commonwealth Fund's March 2024 issue brief, "Denied: How Insurance Company Algorithms Are Blocking Patient Access to Care," and the National Health Law Program's January 2024 policy brief, "Automated Injustice: How AI Prior Authorization Tools Undermine Patient Rights," both reach similar conclusions through independent analysis. CMS's own Medicare Advantage Prior Authorization and Appeals Data Report (2023) showed that appeal overturn rates for denied claims remained high, which is precisely the pattern one would expect if the initial denial logic was not grounded in individualized clinical review. And the market concentration dynamics that Dafny and Lee analyzed in the New England Journal of Medicine (Vol. 390, 2024) matter here too: when a small number of large insurers each adopt AI denial infrastructure, the aggregate effect on patient access is not marginal. Kaye et al., writing in the Journal of Health Policy, Politics and Law (Vol. 49, No. 2, 2024), documented both the efficiency claims insurers have made for these systems and the equity risks they introduce, particularly for patients in lower-income and minority populations. That equity concern connects directly to Obermeyer et al.'s foundational 2019 Science paper (Vol. 366, Issue 6464) on racial bias in health algorithms, which established that cost-containment-optimized models systematically underestimate the health needs of Black patients. An RFI that accelerates AI adoption in clinical settings without addressing this parallel AI deployment is, from a compliance perspective, architecturally incomplete.⏎⏎Veeva Systems | HHS AI RFI Comment | Page 2⏎⏎POLICY RECOMMENDATIONS FOR HHS CONSIDERATION⏎⏎Given the framing concern above, this submission focuses on two specific areas where HHS regulatory action would meaningfully reduce compliance risk and patient harm:⏎⏎1. Mandatory human clinical review before finalization of any AI-generated prior authorization denial. The Senate Subcommittee's October 2024 report found that some AI denial systems were processing claims in under two seconds, a throughput rate that is not compatible with individualized medical necessity review. HHS should issue guidance, and where authority permits, binding regulation, requiring that any adverse prior authorization determination generated or assisted by an AI system be reviewed and affirmed by a licensed clinician with relevant specialty training before the denial is communicated to the patient or provider. The AMA's 2023 Prior Authorization Physician Survey documented the administrative burden that high-volume AI-assisted denials impose on clinical staff, and that burden is itself a patient safety risk because it consumes physician time that would otherwise go to direct care.⏎⏎2. Public disclosure requirements for insurers receiving federal reimbursement. Any insurer participating in Medicare Advantage, Medicaid managed care, or ACA marketplace programs that uses an AI or algorithmic system in its prior authorization workflow should be required to disclose, at minimum: the denial rate attributable to AI-generated determinations, the training objective documentation for the model (i.e., whether the model was optimized for cost containment or clinical accuracy), and the demographic performance data across race, ethnicity, and income categories. The precedent from health IT transparency efforts, including the kind of algorithmic accountability work that Schulte and Fry flagged as missing in the EHR context as far back as March 2019, suggests that voluntary disclosure does not produce adequate information for regulators or the public. CMS should have explicit audit authority over insurer AI systems as a condition of federal program participation, and any AI system whose primary training objective is documented as cost containment should be explicitly excluded from any accelerated reimbursement or approval pathway that this RFI's eventual rulemaking may⏎⏎Veeva Systems | HHS AI RFI Comment | Page 3⏎⏎create for clinical AI tools. The distinction between AI that assists clinical decision-making and AI that automates coverage denial is not a technical distinction; it is a legal and ethical one, and the Department's regulatory architecture should reflect♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0069♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Johnson♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔Connected Health Initiative♔♔♔♔♔♔Connected Health Initiative | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎The Connected Health Initiative submits these comments in response to the Department's Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. Our primary concern — and the reason this submission has been prepared with some urgency — is a structural problem that the RFI itself, perhaps inadvertently, perpetuates: the framing of democratic oversight as an obstacle to innovation, rather than as a prerequisite condition for legitimate governance. The DSI's national survey of 1,847 dental practices across 38 states, conducted in March 2024, found that 63% of respondents had evaluated at least one AI-assisted diagnostic tool for radiographic analysis, yet only 19% had proceeded to full clinical integration (Dental Standards Institute, "AI-Assisted Diagnostic Tools in Dental Practice: A National Survey of Adoption Patterns and Barriers," DSI Policy Research Division, March 2024). That gap is not a market failure to be corrected by federal acceleration. It is a signal. The practices that declined to proceed cited reimbursement uncertainty and unclear liability standards at a rate of 74% — and the Connected Health Initiative believes these concerns, taken seriously, open into a much deeper constitutional and governance question about who has the authority to determine which clinical decisions may be delegated to automated systems, and under what conditions.⏎⏎The RFI asks, among other things, how HHS might reduce uncertainty that impedes innovation. That is a reasonable goal stated plainly. But the framing deserves direct scrutiny. "Reducing uncertainty" is not a neutral administrative act. When the uncertainty in question concerns algorithmic decision-making in high-stakes clinical environments, reducing it through federal regulatory action necessarily forecloses the space in which local communities, professional bodies, and democratic institutions might reach their own conclusions. Frank Pasquale observed in "The Black Box Society" (Harvard University Press, 2015) that the structural inaccessibility of proprietary algorithmic logic is not merely a technical inconvenience — it is a form of delegated authority without accountability, and it poses a genuine problem for democratic self-governance. The Connected Health Initiative's own technical working group reached a parallel conclusion in October 2024, after conducting a comparative analysis of 14 commercially available dental AI platforms: the inconsistencies in how these tools document algorithmic decision-making raise serious concerns about auditability that go well beyond product quality (Dental Standards Institute, "Auditability and Standards Alignment in Commercially Available Dental AI Platforms: A Comparative Analysis of 14 Products," DSI Technical Working Group Report, October 2024). When a clinician cannot explain to a patient — or to a court, or to a state licensing board — why a diagnostic recommendation was generated, the problem is not regulatory lag. It is the absence of legitimate public authority over a consequential decision.⏎⏎Connected Health Initiative | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 2⏎⏎This is where the Connected Health Initiative wants to be precise, because the argument is sometimes mischaracterized. The organization does not oppose AI in clinical care. Eric Topol's work on high-performance medicine documented the genuine diagnostic potential of these systems in ways that are difficult to dismiss (Topol, E.J., "High-performance medicine: the convergence of human and artificial intelligence," Nature Medicine, 25: 44–56, 2019). The GAO's 2020 assessment similarly acknowledged the demonstrated benefits in augmenting patient care (U.S. Government Accountability Office, "Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Benefits and Challenges of Technologies to Augment Patient Care," GAO-21-7SP, November 2020). What the Connected Health Initiative argues is that acceleration, as a governing value, is not self-evidently legitimate. Sheila Jasanoff's work on the ethics of invention is useful here: democratic societies retain the legitimate right to designate certain domains as stubbornly human — not because automated systems necessarily perform worse, but because the act of human judgment in those domains carries moral and relational weight that is independent of accuracy (Jasanoff, S., "The Ethics of Invention: Technology and the Human Future," W.W. Norton & Company, 2016, pp. 183–207). HHS should not, through reimbursement design or regulatory safe harbors, preempt that judgment before the democratic conversation has occurred. The National Academy of Medicine's 2023 consensus study on AI governance frameworks reached a related conclusion, recommending that federal agencies treat governance structure as a prerequisite to deployment incentives, not a follow-on activity (National Academy of Medicine, "Artificial Intelligence in Health and Medicine: A Consensus Study on Governance Frameworks," NAM Special Publication, Washington, D.C., 2023).⏎⏎The irreversibility problem deserves particular attention, and the Connected Health Initiative addressed it at our November 2024 Annual Standards Symposium at some length. Dafny and Lee argued in Health Affairs that health IT infrastructure decisions are functionally irreversible once clinical workflows, reimbursement codes, and vendor contracts have organized around them — the switching costs become prohibitive within a period of roughly five to seven years (Dafny, L. & Lee, T.H., "The Myth of Reversibility in Health IT Infrastructure," Health Affairs, 42(3): 411–419, 2023). This is not a hypothetical. The structural inaccessibility that Selbst and Barocas analyzed in their examination of explainability requirements — where they argued that the intuitive appeal of explainable machines often masks the degree to which explainability is technically constrained — means that the accountability mechanisms being promised now may not be deliverable at scale (Selbst, A.D. & Barocas, S., "The Intuitive Appeal of Explainable Machines," Fordham Law Review, 87(3): 1085–1139, 2018). The European Parliament's AI Act, specifically Articles 13 and 14 on transparency and human oversight requirements for high-risk AI systems in healthcare, represents one legislative attempt to address this before lock-in occurs (European Parliament, "Artificial Intelligence Act: Risk Classification and Transparency Requirements for High-Risk AI Systems in Healthcare," Regulation (EU) 2024/1689). The United States has no equivalent framework, and the Connected Health Initiative is concerned that the RFI's emphasis on acceleration will widen that gap rather than narrow it. Coiera, Westbrook, and Wyatt identified the safety and quality conditions that clinical decision support systems must satisfy to be responsibly deployed — and those conditions have not become less demanding because the systems have become more sophisticated (Coiera, E., Westbrook, J., & Wyatt, J., "The Safety and Quality of Decision Support Systems," IMIA Yearbook of Medical Informatics, 2006: 20–25). The racial bias findings documented by Obermeyer and colleagues, showing systematic underestimation of illness severity in Black patients by a widely deployed health management algorithm, are a concrete demonstration of what happens when accountability structures are treated as secondary concerns (Obermeyer, Z., Powers, B., Vogeli, C., & Mullainathan, S., "Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations," Science, 366(6464): 447–453, 2019).⏎⏎Connected Health Initiative | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 3⏎⏎The Connected Health Initiative's specific recommendations, framed as democratic prerequisites rather than regulatory preferences, are as follows:⏎⏎1. HHS should condition any new reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted clinical decision support on mandatory algorithmic documentation standards that allow independent audit by public bodies — not merely by the deploying institution's internal review process.⏎⏎2. The Department should commission a formal democratic deliberation process, modeled on the NAM consensus study methodology, that asks communities — including rural communities in states like Idaho, where the Connected Health Initiative's member organizations work directly with critical access hospitals — to identify which clinical domains they consider appropriate for automated decision support, and which they do not.⏎⏎3. Any regulatory safe harbor for AI-assisted diagnosis should include a sunset provision requiring re-authorization through notice-and-comment rulemaking no later than five years from the effective date, specifically to prevent the infrastructure lock-in that Dafny and Lee documented.⏎⏎4. CMS should require, as a condition of coverage for AI-assisted diagnostic tools, that vendors provide machine-readable documentation of training data provenance and validation cohort composition, with particular attention to demographic representation.⏎⏎o This requirement should apply retroactively to tools already in clinical use, with a compliance window of 18 months.⏎⏎o The documentation should be deposited in a publicly accessible federal repository, not held exclusively by the deploying institution.⏎⏎5. HHS should publish plain-language summaries of all future AI-related regulatory guidance simultaneously with the Federal Register notice. The Connected Health Initiative reviewed the current RFI notice and found the language inaccessible to the majority of the dental practitioners, community health workers, and patient advocates whose input would most meaningfully inform the Department's decisions. This is not a minor procedural complaint. The accessibility of regulatory process is itself a democratic value. If the people most affected by a policy cannot read the document soliciting their input, the comment period is formally open but functionally closed.⏎⏎That last point is worth sitting with. The Connected Health Initiative's research director spent considerable time working through the Federal Register notice for this RFI and found language that would require a graduate-level familiarity with administrative law to parse correctly. The organization's member practices — the dental clinicians and community health center staff who are the actual intended beneficiaries of whatever policy HHS adopts — would not know what "accelerating AI adoption in clinical care" means for their daily practice, their liability exposure, or their patients' rights from reading that document. The democratic legitimacy of this rulemaking process depends on more than a 60-day comment window. It depends on the Department making a genuine effort to communicate in language that the affected public can understand and respond to. The Connected Health Initiative strongly urges HHS to adopt a standing policy of plain-language parallel publication for all health technology regulatory notices, and to consider whether the current comment process — which systematically advantages large institutional actors with dedicated regulatory affairs staff — is producing representative input or primarily⏎⏎Connected Health Initiative | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 4⏎⏎amplifying the voices of those who already have the resources to engage.⏎⏎Respectfully submitted on behalf of the Connected Health Initiative,⏎⏎William Johnson⏎Research Director, Connected Health Initiative⏎Boise, Idaho⏎⏎Appendix A: DSI Survey Instrument and Sampling Methodology (March 2024) — document referenced but not attached to this submission pending coordination with DSI Policy Research Division; available upon request from the corresponding author at wjohnson@connectedhealthinitiative.org⏎⏎wjohnson@connectedhealthinitiative.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0070♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Smith♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Megan spent fourteen months being told her symptoms were anxiety while an AI at the hospital she was seen at had flagged her twice and nobody acted on it, and I think about that every time I hear HHS talk about "accelerating" AI in healthcare, because the AI that is actually accelerating right now is the kind insurance companies use to deny your claim before a human ever reads it, and I want to know when you plan to do something about that.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0071♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Perez♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔Guardant Health♔♔♔♔♔♔My pharmacist mentioned something about new transparency rules coming through HHS, and I want to be honest: I am not entirely sure whether this request for information is what she was referring to or something separate. What I do understand, from thirty years of watching technology enter the exam room, is that the question of how AI gets introduced into clinical care is not primarily a technical question. I keep thinking about a patient I saw in our Indianapolis clinic in February — a woman who could not find the words for what was wrong, and whose daughter sitting in the corner was clearly terrified, and the only reason the clinician caught what she caught was because she was watching both of them.⏎⏎Any framework HHS builds here needs to protect that kind of attention, not replace it with a checklist that happens to run on a faster processor.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0072♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Hall♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔American Physical Therapy Association♔♔♔♔♔♔American Physical Therapy Association⏎1111 North Fairfax Street⏎Alexandria, VA 22314⏎⏎January 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎---⏎⏎APTA Comments | HHS AI RFI — Health Equity and Validation Standards | Page 1⏎⏎---⏎⏎The American Physical Therapy Association (APTA) welcomes the Department's attention to artificial intelligence in clinical care. Our network of affiliated health systems, which spans 214 institutions ranging from major academic medical centers to rural critical access hospitals and urban safety-net facilities, has a direct stake in whether AI tools deployed in clinical settings actually work as advertised across the full spectrum of patient populations. We are not here to slow this technology down. We are here because our Q3 2024 policy roundtable, convening representatives from 47 academic medical centers and community hospital systems, identified a pattern that the RFI framework does not appear to account for: the populations most dependent on under-resourced institutions are also the populations most likely to be harmed when AI tools validated in high-resource settings are deployed without adequate real-world testing in those institutions. That is not a hypothetical concern. It is a documented, recurring pattern in the published literature, and HHS's current accelerationist posture, as framed in this RFI, contains no mandatory mechanism to detect or prevent it.⏎⏎APTA Comments | HHS AI RFI — Health Equity and Validation Standards | Page 2⏎⏎OBSERVED HARMS ARE NOT EDGE CASES⏎⏎The evidence that AI systems trained on historical healthcare data reproduce and amplify existing disparities is now substantial enough that it should be treated as a baseline assumption in any deployment framework, not a risk to be addressed after the fact. Obermeyer et al. (Science, 2019) documented that a widely used commercial algorithm for managing population health systematically underestimated illness severity in Black patients, resulting in those patients receiving substantially fewer care resources despite equivalent objective need. The mechanism was not malicious design — it was that the algorithm used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and historical data encoded decades of differential access and discriminatory treatment into that proxy variable. Gianfrancesco et al. (JAMA Internal Medicine, 2018) identified similar structural problems across a range of machine learning models using electronic health record data, noting that missing data, coding practices, and selection effects all tend to disadvantage the same populations already marginalized by the healthcare system. Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones (New England Journal of Medicine, 2020) demonstrated that race correction built into standard clinical algorithms — a practice presented as scientifically neutral — actually perpetuates the conflation of race as a biological rather than social variable, with downstream consequences for treatment allocation.⏎⏎These are not isolated cases from a single domain. Sjoding et al. (New England Journal of Medicine, 2020) documented that pulse oximetry devices, a piece of hardware so standard it is nearly invisible in clinical settings, systematically overestimated oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin tones — a finding with direct implications for any AI system that incorporates pulse oximetry readings as an input variable. Adamson and Smith (JAMA Dermatology, 2018) found that dermatology AI tools trained predominantly on images of lighter skin tones performed substantially worse on patients with darker skin, a performance gap that has direct implications for melanoma detection and delayed diagnoses. Seyyed-Kalantari et al. (Nature Medicine, 2021) documented underdiagnosis bias in AI algorithms applied to chest radiographs across multiple under-served patient populations, finding that the performance degradation was consistent and correlated with social determinants of health present in the underlying training data. The pattern here is not subtle. Historical healthcare data encodes historical inequity, and AI systems trained on that data will reproduce it at scale unless there is a deliberate, mandatory intervention in the development and validation pipeline.⏎⏎APTA Comments | HHS AI RFI — Health Equity and Validation Standards | Page 3⏎⏎THE GENERALIZABILITY GAP IS A STRUCTURAL EQUITY PROBLEM⏎⏎The second issue we want to address directly is one that the RFI framework treats, at most, as a technical footnote: the gap between AI performance in the settings where tools are validated and AI performance in the settings where they are actually deployed. Wong et al. (JAMA Internal Medicine, 2021) conducted an external validation of a proprietary sepsis prediction model that had been widely implemented across health systems nationally. Their findings were striking: the model's performance degraded substantially when tested outside the academic medical center environment in which it was developed, and the model's alert system generated a high volume of false positives that the original validation literature had not surfaced. This is not a problem with one sepsis model. Wiens et al. (Nature Medicine, 2019) laid out a systematic roadmap for responsible machine learning in healthcare and were explicit that external validation in diverse deployment settings is a non-negotiable requirement for responsible deployment — not an optional enhancement.⏎⏎APTA's network data is consistent with this literature. Our research team's analysis of CMS reimbursement data found that fewer than 12% of FDA-authorized clinical AI tools have a corresponding billing mechanism, which means that health systems, particularly those operating on thin margins in community and rural settings, have no structured pathway for recouping the costs of thoughtful implementation. The financial pressure this creates tends to push toward rapid, low-scrutiny deployment rather than the careful site-specific validation that the literature demands. Community hospitals and safety-net facilities, which lack the informatics infrastructure and research staff of academic medical centers, are least equipped to conduct their own validation studies and most likely to rely on vendor-provided performance claims that may not reflect their patient population at all. The 22-month average delay our network documented between FDA authorization and actual deployment is partly explained by this: institutions that are trying to do this carefully are stymied by the absence of standardized frameworks, while institutions that are not doing it carefully face no formal accountability mechanism.⏎⏎Char, Shah, and Magnus (New England Journal of Medicine, 2018) wrote plainly that implementing machine learning in healthcare requires confronting ethical challenges that are structural, not incidental — including the challenge of ensuring that performance claims are validated in the populations and settings where the tool will actually be used. Cabitza, Rasoini, and Gensini (JAMA, 2017) raised the problem of automation bias: the tendency of clinicians to defer to AI-generated outputs even when those outputs conflict with clinical judgment. This mechanism makes AI errors harder to catch than equivalent human errors, because the social and cognitive dynamics of clinical decision-making create pressure to accept algorithmic recommendations. In under-resourced settings where clinicians are managing high patient volumes with limited support staff, the conditions for automation bias are particularly acute. Pierson et al. (Nature Medicine, 2021) demonstrated that algorithmic approaches can, when designed with equity as a design constraint, actually reduce unexplained pain disparities in underserved populations — which is to say that the technology is not inherently inequitable. The problem is that equity is not currently a required design constraint or a validated deployment criterion.⏎⏎APTA Comments | HHS AI RFI — Health Equity and Validation Standards | Page 4⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD REQUIRE — AND WHAT THIS RFI DOES NOT CURRENTLY ADDRESS⏎⏎The RFI solicits comment on regulatory, reimbursement, and research-and-development actions to accelerate AI adoption. APTA's position is that acceleration without equity safeguards is not a neutral policy choice — it is a policy choice with foreseeable consequences for the populations our member clinicians serve in community, rural, and safety-net settings. The National Academy for State Health Policy documented in its 2023 policy brief on algorithmic accountability in healthcare that federal gaps in this area are real and that state-level legislative activity, while growing, is fragmented and insufficient as a substitute for federal standards. HHS is positioned to fill that gap. We urge the Department to take the following actions:⏎⏎1. Mandate pre-deployment equity auditing for all clinical AI tools seeking CMS reimbursement or deployed in federally funded health systems. Audits should include disaggregated performance data by race, ethnicity, sex, age, socioeconomic status, and geographic setting, with particular attention to performance in community hospitals, rural facilities, and safety-net institutions.⏎⏎2. Require external validation in deployment-representative settings as a condition of FDA market authorization for clinical decision support software. Validation studies conducted exclusively in large academic medical centers should not be accepted as sufficient evidence of generalizability.⏎⏎3. Establish mandatory post-deployment bias surveillance with public reporting requirements. Health systems deploying FDA-authorized AI tools should be required to report performance data on a standardized schedule, disaggregated by the same demographic and institutional categories listed above.⏎⏎4. Align reimbursement pathways with equity requirements. CMS should condition coverage of AI-assisted clinical services on documentation that the tool has been validated in settings comparable to the deploying institution, and that ongoing performance monitoring is in place.⏎⏎o A note on implementation: our network's experience suggests that smaller community hospitals will need technical assistance infrastructure to comply with these requirements. HHS should consider a parallel investment in regional support centers, analogous to the Regional Extension Centers established under HITECH, to help under-resourced institutions conduct site-specific validation.⏎⏎The framing of this RFI emphasizes speed — removing friction, accelerating adoption, expanding access. APTA does not dispute that these are legitimate goals. The 78% of clinical AI deployments in our network that stalled at the integration phase represent real delays in patient access to potentially beneficial tools. But the literature we have cited here — and the structural patterns our own network has documented — make clear that deploying AI tools rapidly in settings where they have not been validated, without any mandatory mechanism for detecting performance disparities, is not the same thing as expanding access to beneficial technology. For many patients in under-resourced settings, it may mean something considerably worse.⏎⏎We appreciate the Department's willingness to solicit broad public comment on this question and welcome further engagement. APTA's research team can be reached for follow-up at policy@apta.org.⏎⏎Kevin Hall⏎Campaign Director⏎American Physical Therapy Association⏎⏎---⏎⏎APTA Comments | HHS AI RFI — Health Equity and Validation Standards | Page 5⏎⏎policy@apta.org⏎www.apta.org/policy♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0073♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Anderson♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My name is Betty Anderson and I've been a home health aide in Alabama for eleven years. I am not a policy expert. I'm just writing this because I'm worried about the rush to use AI in healthcare and who ends up paying the price if HHS just focuses on speeding things up.⏎⏎⏎⏎Let me tell you about my client Doris. She is 84 with congestive heart failure and early dementia. In the fall of 2023, she missed two medication adjustments her cardiologist ordered because the paper notes between his office and our team kept getting lost in fax machines. Nobody noticed she was holding fluid. Her ankles swelled up real bad and her breathing got labored. It wasn't until her daughter drove down from Memphis and went through a shoebox full of faxes on Doris's kitchen table that we figured it out. I sat with Doris all week thinking that a connected system tracking her daily weights would have flagged the trend days earlier. I still believe that.⏎⏎⏎⏎But here is the problem. The AI tools being sold to home health agencies and hospitals right now aren't really for coordinating care. They are sold as workforce efficiency tools. Home health aides are some of the lowest paid, least consulted workers in healthcare. Nobody has ever asked me what software my agency buys. When administrators buy AI tools without frontline input, the software just gets configured to measure our productivity instead of supporting patients.⏎⏎⏎⏎You don't need an academic study to know that algorithmic workplace surveillance kills worker morale and degrades our autonomy over time. Good patient care directly depends on staffing ratios and professional independence, which applies to home care just as much as hospitals. What really drives burnout isn't just the workload volume, it's the documentation burden and the loss of autonomy. That is exactly what surveillance-oriented AI tools increase.⏎⏎⏎⏎Before any AI tool is deployed in a clinical or home care setting with federal support or reimbursement, HHS needs to require a few things. First, agencies must prove beforehand that the tool won't reduce existing staffing ratios. Second, there has to be worker consent and collective input before any productivity monitoring feature gets turned on. Finally, frontline workers like home health aides must have documented participation in evaluating these tools.⏎⏎⏎⏎I don't know exactly how HHS structures that requirement procedurally, but I would genuinely welcome guidance on where to submit more detailed comments on that specific question.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0074♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Clark♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My client Marcus finally got stable after years of cycling through crisis, and the difference was a telehealth clinic in Columbus that used an AI triage tool to flag when he'd gone quiet for more than four days. I watched that work. I documented it. So I am not writing this to say AI doesn't belong in clinical care — it does, and I've seen it do real good. But what I keep coming back to when I read this RFI is a question the document never quite asks: who actually benefits from acceleration, and on what terms?⏎⏎MARKET CONCENTRATION AND THE ACCELERATION TRAP⏎⏎The framing of this RFI troubles me in ways I find hard to fully articulate but will try. The document asks how HHS can accelerate adoption and expand reimbursement pathways, and those are reasonable questions. But they are being asked in a market where, as Gee and Bhatt documented in Health Affairs in 2023, a small number of EHR and technology vendors already control the majority of clinical data infrastructure. Dafny and Lee made the same point in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2022 when they described how switching costs and vendor lock-in have effectively eliminated meaningful EHR competition in most health systems. When you layer AI deployment onto that already-concentrated substrate, you are not creating a new market. You are handing the incumbents a deeper moat. The American Economic Liberties Project's 2023 policy brief on how Amazon, Google, and Microsoft are moving into healthcare AI makes this concrete — these are companies with the compliance infrastructure to navigate complex reimbursement processes and the legal teams to negotiate CRADAs on favorable terms. A federally qualified health center in rural Appalachian Ohio does not have those things. So when HHS expands reimbursement pathways and public-private partnerships without first addressing concentration, the practical effect is a transfer of federally generated value — including patient data collected through Medicare and Medicaid encounters — to companies that will retain proprietary control over the resulting products.⏎⏎I want to be specific about why this matters clinically and not just economically. Obermeyer and colleagues published in Science in 2019 that a widely used population-health algorithm systematically underestimated the severity of illness in Black patients because it was trained on cost as a proxy for need — a bias that was invisible until someone looked for it. The GAO flagged in 2023 (GAO-23-105812) that HHS has not yet conducted a systematic risk assessment of vendor consolidation before expanding AI reimbursement pathways. Maddox, Rumsfeld, and Payne raised similar questions in JAMA in 2022 about whether the incentive structures driving AI deployment in health care are aligned with patient outcomes or with market capture. These aren't obscure academic concerns. They are the conditions under which tools like the one that helped Marcus get built, deployed, and evaluated — and the conditions determine whether those tools actually work for patients who don't look like the training data.⏎⏎What bothers me about this RFI is that it reads as if the Federal Health IT Strategic Plan 2024-2030 (ASTP/ONC, 2024) has already settled the question of how AI gets integrated, and the public is just being asked to help with implementation details. That may not be the intent, but it is the effect. The questions are structured around acceleration, not interrogation. There is no question that asks: should certain AI applications require open interoperability before receiving reimbursement? There is no question about whether federal data used to train commercial AI tools should carry public licensing requirements. I genuinely don't understand why those questions aren't in here.⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD DO BEFORE MOVING FORWARD⏎⏎Before advancing any reimbursement expansion or partnership framework, I am asking HHS to take three steps that the current RFI does not contemplate:⏎⏎1. Commission an independent market-concentration analysis — not conducted by a vendor-affiliated entity — that maps the current distribution of EHR and AI infrastructure control across health system types, with particular attention to safety-net and rural providers.⏎⏎2. Require that any AI tool developed using federally funded data or through a CRADA with a federal agency be subject to open interoperability standards and, where appropriate, public licensing, so that smaller clinics and community health organizations can access and audit those tools without paying incumbent licensing fees.⏎⏎3. Make algorithmic transparency a condition of reimbursement eligibility — not a voluntary best practice. If a tool cannot explain its outputs to a clinician in a way that supports human judgment, it should not qualify for a federal reimbursement pathway. The clinic that helped Marcus worked because the AI flagged a concern and then a human being made the call. That division of labor was not accidental; it was designed. HHS should require that design.⏎⏎I recognize that these asks complicate the acceleration timeline. I am not sure that is a bad thing. The GAO's 2023 report said HHS should conduct a vendor-consolidation risk assessment before expanding reimbursement — that report was published 18 months ago and I have not seen evidence that assessment has happened. That gap is the thing I keep returning to when I think about whether this RFI is asking the right questions or just the convenient ones. Marcus got lucky. The system that helped him was built by people who cared more about outcomes than market share, and I'm not sure how you write a reimbursement rule that♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0075♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Flores♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔Partnership for Health Analytic Research♔♔♔♔♔♔Partnership for Health Analytic Research⏎Comments Submitted to HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-RFI-Clinical-Care⏎Submitted by: Thomas Flores, Policy Director⏎⏎PHAR Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎THE WORKFORCE QUESTION HHS IS REFUSING TO ASK⏎⏎The Partnership for Health Analytic Research (PHAR) represents 412 member clearinghouses and health information exchanges that collectively route approximately 2.1 billion claims transactions annually. When 67% of those member organizations report actively piloting AI-assisted prior authorization automation, claims adjudication support, or embedded clinical decision tools — and when our Q3 2024 policy review found that 58% cite interoperability fragmentation as the primary barrier to scaling those deployments — PHAR speaks from direct operational visibility into what AI adoption actually looks like at infrastructure scale. That visibility is precisely why we are alarmed by this RFI's framing, which treats workforce displacement as either an acceptable externality or a problem too uncomfortable to name directly. HHS has organized its questions around acceleration. The Department has not organized a single substantive question around the workers who will absorb the consequences of that acceleration. That omission is not neutral. It reflects a policy posture that has already chosen sides, and PHAR intends to name that choice plainly.⏎⏎The vendor narrative driving this RFI — that AI deployment will reduce clinician burnout, ease documentation burden, and return autonomy to frontline practitioners — is not supported by the documented structural drivers of burnout in the existing literature. Shanafelt et al. (2022), publishing in Mayo Clinic Proceedings, tracked physician burnout across a nine-year longitudinal period and found that loss of autonomy, administrative burden, and erosion of professional identity were the primary accelerants, not workload volume per se. Bates et al. (2021) in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association conducted a systematic review and meta-analysis demonstrating that alert fatigue — one of the most cited AI "solutions" — is itself a product of poorly governed clinical decision support deployment, not a condition that more AI tools reliably resolve. Dyrbye et al. (2019) in JAMA Network Open found that work unit environment and perceived organizational control were stronger predictors of burnout than individual-level interventions. The AMA's 2023 Digital Medicine Practice Accelerator report on augmented intelligence explicitly cautioned against deploying AI productivity metrics as proxy measures for clinical performance without frontline governance input. None of this literature supports the claim that accelerating AI adoption will improve workforce conditions. All of it suggests the opposite, under current deployment models.⏎⏎PHAR Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎What the evidence does support is the conclusion that AI tools marketed as efficiency solutions are being deployed primarily as workforce management instruments. The SEIU Healthcare Policy Brief published in 2024, "Monitored, Managed, and Marginalized," documented systematic use of AI-generated productivity dashboards to justify staffing ratio reductions in hospital systems across at least seven states. National Nurses United's 2023 report, "Staffing by Algorithm," provided case-level documentation of AI throughput metrics being used by hospital administrators to argue against minimum nurse-to-patient ratio protections in contract negotiations. Kellogg, Valentine, and Christin (2020) in the Academy of Management Annals analyzed algorithmic control mechanisms across multiple sectors and found that healthcare workers faced a distinct vulnerability: unlike workers in logistics or manufacturing, clinical staff are subject to algorithmic performance metrics while simultaneously bearing legal and ethical accountability for outcomes those algorithms cannot fully assess. The GAO's 2023 report on AI in health care (GAO-23-105980) acknowledged this tension but declined to recommend binding workforce protections, a gap PHAR regards as a significant regulatory failure. The MITRE Corporation's 2022 automation susceptibility analysis (MTR220134) estimated that between 30 and 45 percent of administrative healthcare roles carry high displacement risk under current AI deployment trajectories.⏎⏎PHAR is not opposed to AI adoption. What PHAR is opposed to is the systematic exclusion of frontline workers from procurement governance, the use of AI-generated throughput metrics as the evidentiary basis for staffing ratio decisions, and HHS's apparent willingness to treat the speed of adoption as a policy goal in the absence of binding workforce protection standards. Friedberg et al. (2013) at RAND established more than a decade ago that physician professional satisfaction is a direct predictor of care quality outcomes. Obermeyer and Emanuel (2016) in the New England Journal of Medicine warned that machine learning models in clinical medicine require rigorous outcome validation before deployment at scale. ONC's own 2023 Data Brief No. 58 acknowledged emerging workforce challenges in AI-assisted clinical environments without committing to any specific protective framework. That pattern — acknowledgment without commitment — is what PHAR is asking HHS to end.⏎⏎Specific policy demands from PHAR, which the Department should treat as non-negotiable conditions for any accelerationist regulatory posture:⏎⏎1. Mandatory workforce impact assessments prior to AI procurement approval in federally participating institutions.⏎2. Prohibition on using AI-generated productivity metrics as the primary or sole basis for staffing ratio determinations.⏎3. Required frontline worker representation — nurses, physicians, care coordinators — on institutional AI procurement governance committees.⏎4. A moratorium on administrative role automation in clinical settings until independent quality-of-care impact assessments, not vendor-sponsored studies, have been completed and reviewed by ONC.⏎⏎The interoperability fragmentation our members report is a real problem that deserves serious regulatory attention. But HHS should not allow the legitimate complexity of that technical problem to⏎⏎PHAR Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎obscure the more consequential question of who bears the cost when AI deployment goes wrong. Right now, that cost lands entirely on workers and patients. The vendors are not in the room when a nurse files a grievance because an algorithm flagged her for low throughput during a shift where she spent forty minutes with a deteriorating patient who needed her to stay. HHS needs to decide whether that nurse is a stakeholder in this rulemaking or an externality.⏎⏎thomas.flores@pharesearch.org⏎Partnership for Health Analytic Research⏎Hartford, CT 06103♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0076♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Martin♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔Association of American Indian Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔ASSOCIATION OF AMERICAN INDIAN PHYSICIANS⏎Comments on HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket Submission — Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP/ONC⏎⏎⏎WORKFORCE GOVERNANCE IN AI-ASSISTED SURGICAL SETTINGS: THE GAP BETWEEN DEPLOYMENT AND ACCOUNTABILITY⏎⏎The 2024 AORN Membership Survey on AI-Assisted Surgical Technology Exposure and Governance Readiness (Association of periOperative Registered Nurses, unpublished policy brief) documented that 68 percent of more than 2,200 perioperative nurses reported exposure to at least one AI-assisted surgical technology in their primary practice setting. Fewer than one in five indicated their facility had adopted formal protocols governing nurse roles, responsibilities, or accountability structures in relation to those systems. A subsequent policy analysis of 14 health system AI governance frameworks found that perioperative nursing competencies were explicitly referenced in only three of those frameworks. The operating room is one of the highest-acuity environments for AI-assisted decision support in clinical care, and this governance gap is not an administrative inconvenience. It is a patient safety failure in progress. To Err Is Human (Kohn, Corrigan, and Donaldson, 2000, National Academies Press) established two decades ago that system design — not individual error — drives adverse outcomes in clinical settings. HHS cannot credibly claim to be accelerating AI adoption responsibly while tolerating a procurement environment in which frontline perioperative nurses are handed AI-assisted surgical tools without enforceable competency frameworks or meaningful consultation in the acquisition process.⏎⏎The Association of American Indian Physicians notes that this comment addresses specifically the workforce governance question embedded in this RFI, and not the full scope of questions posed. The focus here is on what happens after a vendor contract is signed and before a patient is on the table.⏎⏎AAPI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The evidence is unambiguous that the burnout-reduction marketing claims attached to clinical AI tools are contradicted by the peer-reviewed record. Shanafelt et al. (2022, Mayo Clinic Proceedings, 97(3), 491–506) documented that EHR-mediated workload monitoring and alert systems have increased, not decreased, physician cognitive burden in acute care settings. Melnyk et al. (2023, Journal of Nursing Administration, 53(4), 189–197) demonstrated associations between AI-mediated workload monitoring, reduced nurse autonomy, and elevated burnout indicators in acute care environments. Bates et al. (2021, npj Digital Medicine, 4(1), 1–7) acknowledged that documentation burden reduction through AI carries real potential, but the implementation conditions in most health systems do not match the research conditions under which those gains were demonstrated. The U.S. Government Accountability Office (2023, GAO-23-105980) found that AI tools in health care settings were being deployed with insufficient attention to workforce integration requirements. Topol (2019, Nature Medicine, 25(1), 44–56) described a vision of human-AI convergence premised on clinician empowerment. What the 2024 AORN survey describes is the opposite: procurement without consultation, deployment without competency standards, and accountability structures that leave frontline nurses exposed when AI-assisted systems produce erroneous or ambiguous outputs.⏎⏎The staffing literature makes the downstream consequences of this pattern explicit. Stanton and Rutherford (2004, AHRQ Research in Action, Issue 14, Pub. No. 04-0029) established the relationship between nurse staffing ratios and patient safety outcomes in terms that remain foundational to current policy analysis. The American Nurses Association (2022, Principles for Nurse Staffing, 3rd ed., Silver Spring, MD) has maintained that staffing floors must account for the complexity of the care environment, including the cognitive demands imposed by new technologies. When AI systems are introduced into perioperative settings without corresponding adjustments to staffing ratios or competency requirements, the cognitive load on individual nurses increases even as administrators describe the technology as a burden-reduction tool. National Nurses United (2023, Protect Nurses, Protect Patients, Oakland, CA) documented that 77 percent of nurses surveyed reported no meaningful input into AI procurement decisions affecting their units. The framing that AI adoption accelerates quality improvement while workforce governance lags indefinitely behind is not merely incomplete — it is dangerous.⏎⏎AAPI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎Racial and ethnic bias in clinical algorithms compounds this concern in ways that are directly relevant to the populations the Association of American Indian Physicians serves. Obermeyer et al. (2019, Science, 366(6464), 447–453) demonstrated that a widely deployed population health algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients by using health care cost as a proxy for health status — a methodological choice that encoded historical inequity into automated decision support. Rajpurkar et al. (2022, Nature Medicine, 28(1), 31–38) noted the gap between AI research claims and the governance realities that govern actual clinical deployment, particularly in underserved populations. American Indian and Alaska Native patients receive care in settings, including IHS facilities and tribal health programs, where AI procurement decisions are made under resource constraints that make robust vendor evaluation nearly impossible. The acceleration mandate in this RFI, if implemented without enforceable bias auditing requirements and mandatory pre-deployment testing in diverse patient populations, will replicate existing disparities at scale.⏎⏎HHS should consider the following specific actions in response to this RFI:⏎⏎1. Require mandatory frontline worker consultation as a condition of AI procurement in any federally funded health facility, with documented evidence that perioperative nursing staff and other affected clinical roles participated in needs assessment prior to vendor selection.⏎⏎2. Establish enforceable competency standards for nurses and other clinical staff operating in AI-assisted environments, with particular attention to high-acuity settings such as the operating room, where the 2024 AORN survey identified the most acute governance gaps.⏎⏎3. Condition reimbursement incentives for AI adoption on demonstrated compliance with bias auditing protocols that include pre-deployment testing against patient populations reflecting the demographics of the facility's service area — including American Indian and Alaska Native populations in tribal and IHS settings.⏎⏎4. Direct AHRQ to fund implementation research on AI governance frameworks in perioperative and high-acuity settings, with a specific requirement that study populations include federally recognized tribal health facilities.⏎⏎The absence of enforceable workforce governance standards in the current AI deployment environment is not an oversight that acceleration will correct. It is a structural condition that will worsen as deployment pace increases without corresponding accountability infrastructure. The 2024 AORN survey identified only three of fourteen health system AI governance frameworks that explicitly referenced perioperative nursing competencies. That number needs to be fourteen of fourteen before HHS announces the next round of adoption incentives. The question of how to build that accountability infrastructure — who sets the competency standards, how compliance is verified, what remediation is required when a facility's governance framework is found deficient — has not been answered in this RFI, and the Association of American Indian Physicians would request that a subsequent notice specifically address those⏎⏎AAPI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎implementation mechanics rather than deferring them to sub-regulatory guidance that may never materialize in the form tribal and underserved facilities can actually operationalize.⏎⏎aapiphysicians.org/policy — comments submitted electronically via regulations.gov♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0077♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Wright♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔Nabla♔♔♔♔♔♔Nabla | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-CLIN | Page 1⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎This submission is made on behalf of Nabla, an AI-enabled clinical documentation and decision-support company with direct operational visibility into AI deployment patterns across a substantial affiliated network of health system partners. Charles Wright, Chief Medical Officer, submits these comments in response to the Department's Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings. Nabla's perspective is grounded in deployment data, not theoretical interest. What our network has observed over the past 18 months is a pattern that this RFI, in its current framing, is likely to accelerate rather than correct: AI procurement driven by cost-reduction imperatives that are systematically misrepresented as workforce wellbeing and efficiency initiatives, with predictable and measurable consequences for clinician health and, downstream, for patient safety. We are not arguing against AI adoption. We are arguing that the Department's posture in this RFI treats governance as an afterthought to adoption, and that this sequencing is the problem.⏎⏎Nabla | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-CLIN | Page 2⏎⏎THE DEPLOYMENT REALITY THIS RFI DOES NOT ACKNOWLEDGE⏎⏎The Department's RFI opens from a premise of beneficial acceleration. The framing is that AI tools exist, they work, and the task before HHS is to remove barriers to their spread. Our analysis of deployment data across 47 affiliated clinical partners, documented in Van Pelt & Company's Q2 2024 Policy Benchmarking Initiative (on file with the authors), tells a more complicated story. Sixty-eight percent of those partners identified reimbursement ambiguity as the primary barrier to scaling validated AI diagnostic tools past pilot phases, and average deployment timelines extended 14 months beyond initial regulatory clearance due to payor coverage uncertainty. That finding has been cited elsewhere in this docket's broader context, but what has not been adequately cited is what happens when that barrier is removed without corresponding governance. Facilities under value-based care arrangements were 2.3 times more likely to sustain AI adoption when clear coding pathways existed, which sounds like an argument for the Department's position until you ask what those facilities were actually sustaining adoption of. Structured interviews with 112 clinical operations leaders conducted as part of that same initiative found that the single most common stated rationale for AI tool procurement was workforce cost management, not clinical outcome improvement. The language used in procurement documentation was consistently framed around efficiency, throughput, and burnout reduction. The operational implementation told a different story.⏎⏎Across our network's deployment partners, we have documented a recurring pattern: AI tools marketed on burnout-reduction and efficiency grounds are implemented in ways that justify staffing ratio reductions and expanded caseloads rather than protecting clinician time. This is not a vendor problem in isolation, though vendors bear responsibility for how they market their products. It is an institutional financial pressure problem. Health systems operating under margin compression do not procure AI tools and then hold staffing levels steady while clinical time improves. They procure AI tools, document a projected efficiency gain, and then adjust staffing ratios to capture that gain as cost reduction. The result is that the AI tool is now carrying more of the load in a leaner staffing environment, meaning that when the AI fails or produces a false alert, there is less human redundancy to catch it. Bates, D.W., et al. (2021) documented in npj Digital Medicine that poorly governed clinical decision support tools increase alert fatigue rather than reducing it, and that the downstream effect on clinical attention is measurable and negative. Kocher, R., and Sahni, N.R. noted in Health Affairs in March 2023 that health systems are explicitly using AI deployment as a mechanism for workforce restructuring, and that this pattern is concentrated in high-volume, lower-margin service lines. We are seeing this in our own data, and we think the Department needs to name it plainly rather than building an acceleration framework that treats it as someone else's problem.⏎⏎Nabla | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-CLIN | Page 3⏎⏎THE BURNOUT CLAIM DOES NOT HOLD UNDER SCRUTINY⏎⏎The Department's framing of AI as a burnout-reduction tool deserves particular attention because it is the rhetorical foundation on which a significant portion of this RFI's acceleration logic rests. If AI reduces burnout, then accelerating AI adoption is a workforce health intervention as much as a clinical efficiency one, and the case for speed over caution is strengthened. The problem is that the empirical record on this claim is weak at best and contradictory at worst.⏎⏎Shanafelt, T.D., et al. (2022) published a longitudinal analysis in Mayo Clinic Proceedings tracking physician burnout and work-life integration across the period from 2011 to 2020, finding that burnout rates increased across nearly every specialty during a period that also saw substantial growth in EHR adoption and clinical decision support deployment. The causal relationship is not simple, but the correlation between increased AI-adjacent tooling and improved burnout outcomes is not what the RFI's framing implies. Patel, R.S., et al. (2023) conducted a systematic review and meta-analysis published in the Journal of General Internal Medicine specifically examining EHR burden and physician burnout, finding a consistent positive association between documentation burden, which AI documentation tools purport to address, and burnout severity. The operative question is whether AI documentation tools are actually reducing documentation burden or redistributing it in ways that generate new cognitive load. Our deployment data suggests the latter is more common than the former, particularly when those tools are layered onto existing workflows without meaningful clinician input into how they are configured.⏎⏎This brings us to a concern that the Department's RFI does not appear to have weighted adequately: the systematic exclusion of frontline clinical staff from AI procurement decisions. Across the 112 clinical operations leaders we interviewed, fewer than 20 percent reported that bedside nurses, physicians, or allied health staff had any formal role in the procurement process for AI tools that would directly affect their workflows. The American Medical Association's 2023 Digital Medicine Practice Playbook identifies frontline clinician involvement in AI governance as a minimum standard for responsible deployment, and yet the procurement reality we are observing is that these decisions are made by finance, operations, and technology leadership with clinical input treated as optional and advisory at best. Dyrbye, L.N., et al. (2020) published work in the Journal of Nursing Administration linking work unit safety culture directly to burnout severity among hospital nurses, and the construct of safety culture in that analysis is substantially about whether frontline workers feel their professional judgment is respected in operational decisions. Procurement processes that impose AI tools on clinical staff without consultation are, by the framework Dyrbye and colleagues describe, safety culture problems that will manifest as burnout outcomes regardless of what the AI tool itself is designed to do.⏎⏎Nabla | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-CLIN | Page 4⏎⏎THE SURVEILLANCE AND AUTONOMY QUESTION⏎⏎There is a category of AI deployment that the RFI does not name directly but that our network's data identifies as a growing source of workforce harm: AI-driven productivity monitoring of clinical and administrative staff. This includes systems that track documentation completion rates, time-on-task metrics, throughput per shift, and deviation from algorithmic care pathways. National Nurses United's 2023 research report, "Surveilled, Stretched, and Sidelined," documented through survey data that a significant and growing proportion of nurses in U.S. hospital settings are subject to AI-enabled performance monitoring systems, and that exposure to those systems is associated with substantially elevated reports of moral distress and professional disengagement. We want to be careful about how we characterize this, because productivity monitoring is not inherently illegitimate. Health systems have reasonable interests in operational visibility. But there is a meaningful difference between aggregate operational dashboards used for resource planning and individual-level surveillance systems used to generate performance metrics that feed into staffing and retention decisions. The latter category is what is proliferating, and the Department's RFI contains no framework for distinguishing between them or governing the latter.⏎⏎The connection between clinician autonomy erosion and patient outcomes is not rhetorical. Obermeyer, Z., and Emanuel, E.J. (2016) wrote in the New England Journal of Medicine that the value of machine learning in clinical settings depends critically on how it is integrated with physician judgment, and that systems designed to replace rather than support clinical reasoning produce worse outcomes than either the AI or the physician operating independently. The logic extends directly to the surveillance context: when AI systems are used to monitor and constrain clinical behavior rather than to support it, the effect is not neutral. Clinicians under algorithmic surveillance report adjusting their behavior to optimize measurable metrics rather than clinical outcomes, which is a well-documented phenomenon in performance management literature and one that the healthcare context makes particularly consequential. The Department should not be agnostic about this.⏎⏎Nabla | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-CLIN | Page 5⏎⏎RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎The Department has asked for input on regulatory, reimbursement, and research-and-development actions. These comments focus on the regulatory dimension, because we believe the governance architecture question is logically prior to the reimbursement incentive question. Paying for AI tools before establishing what governance those tools must satisfy is the wrong sequencing, and the ONC's own 2024 Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability Final Rule (89 Fed. Reg. 1192) established algorithm transparency and information sharing requirements that have not yet been extended to the workforce impact domain. That gap should be addressed before reimbursement pathways are broadened.⏎⏎Specifically, the Department should consider requiring that any AI tool seeking coverage under expanded CMS reimbursement pathways demonstrate, as a condition of that coverage, documented clinician involvement in deployment configuration. This is not a novel standard. The AMA's 2023 governance framework describes it as baseline responsible practice. What is novel is making it a reimbursement condition rather than a voluntary best practice, because the voluntary approach has not worked. Our deployment data is reasonably clear on this: facilities that describe themselves as following responsible AI governance principles and facilities that actually have formal clinician governance structures in place are not the same population. The self-report and the operational reality diverge substantially.⏎⏎The Department should also give serious attention to the distinction between AI tools that support human judgment and AI tools that substitute for it in roles that carry meaningful clinical consequence. Frakt, A., and Jha, A.K. (2023) argued in the Annals of Internal Medicine that pay-for-performance frameworks consistently produce the perverse effect of optimizing measurable proxies rather than the underlying outcomes those proxies are meant to represent. The same logic applies to AI deployment frameworks: when the metric is adoption rate or efficiency gain, health systems optimize for those metrics, and what gets lost in that optimization is frequently the harder-to-measure question of whether clinical judgment is being supported or displaced. Prior authorization review, care coordination, and patient navigation are the examples our network's data flags most consistently as roles where automation is being deployed without evidence of equivalent or superior outcomes compared to human performance, and without meaningful consultation with the workers whose roles are being restructured. This is not an argument that these functions can never be automated. It is an argument that the Department should require evidence of clinical equivalence before those deployments become reimbursable practice standards.⏎⏎Nabla | HHS AI in Clinical Care RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-CLIN | Page 6⏎⏎We recognize there is a tension in this submission. Nabla is an AI company. We have a direct commercial interest in a regulatory environment that supports AI adoption in clinical settings, and the Department is entitled to weigh that interest when evaluating these comments. We are raising governance concerns in part because we believe the current trajectory of poorly governed AI deployment is generating the kind of workforce harm and institutional backlash that will ultimately constrain responsible AI adoption more severely than thoughtful precautionary regulation would. That is a strategic argument as much as an ethical one, and we are not pretending otherwise. But it is also an argument that we believe is supported by the evidence, and the evidence is what the Department should be evaluating.⏎⏎The specific concern we want to leave the Department with is this: the acceleration framing of this RFI assumes that the primary barrier to beneficial AI adoption is regulatory and reimbursement friction. Our data suggests that in a significant proportion of cases, the tools being delayed are not primarily beneficial in the ways their procurement documentation claims. They are cost-reduction tools marketed as care quality tools, and the workforce harm they produce is a predictable consequence of the financial pressures driving their procurement rather than an incidental implementation problem that better guidance will fix. Removing reimbursement and regulatory barriers to those tools, without first establishing governance requirements that align procurement incentives with care quality rather than cost reduction, will scale that harm.⏎⏎A reference appendix including the Van Pelt & Company Q2 2024 internal report, supporting deployment data tables, and interview methodology documentation is available on request and will be submitted as a supplementary filing under this docket number. Contact for correspondence: policy@nabla-health.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0078♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Wilson♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother died in Jackson, Mississippi in March of 2019 after eight months of nobody talking to anybody, and you are asking me to trust that moving faster is the answer.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0079♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔10♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Davis♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔Endocrine Society♔♔♔♔♔♔[Endocrine Society letterhead]⏎⏎1735 K Street, NW⏎Washington, DC 20006⏎www.endocrine.org⏎⏎June 12, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Dear Deputy Secretary and RFI Coordinators:⏎⏎The Endocrine Society submits these comments on behalf of our 18,000 members — endocrinologists, researchers, and allied health professionals who provide care to patients with diabetes, thyroid disease, obesity, and related conditions across every care setting in the United States, including federally qualified health centers, rural critical access hospitals, and safety-net institutions in communities that have historically received the least investment in health infrastructure. We appreciate HHS's willingness to engage broadly on the question of AI adoption in clinical settings. However, this comment does not offer encouragement. It offers a warning, and we want to be direct about that from the outset.⏎⏎The framing of this RFI — "accelerating AI adoption" — is itself a policy choice, and not a neutral one. Acceleration, as a governing priority, assumes that the primary friction in the system is the slowness of uptake, and that removing barriers will produce broadly distributed benefits. We do not accept that premise as applied to clinical AI deployment in 2024. The evidence accumulated over the past six years is unambiguous: AI systems trained on historically skewed clinical data reproduce and frequently amplify the disparities already embedded in that data, and they do so with an authoritative numerical confidence that makes the resulting errors harder to detect and correct than equivalent errors made by human clinicians. The Endocrine Society is not opposed to artificial intelligence in clinical care. We are opposed to deploying it at scale without the structural safeguards necessary to ensure that the communities bearing the highest burden of endocrine disease — communities that are disproportionately Black, Hispanic, low-income, and rural — are not also the communities that absorb the highest burden of AI-generated harm.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Endocrine Society Comments | HHS AI RFI | June 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎THE EVIDENCE BASE FOR ALGORITHMIC BIAS IS NOT THEORETICAL⏎⏎This comment focuses specifically on the RFI's questions regarding regulatory pathways and research priorities, because those are the domains where the structural failures are most consequential and most correctable. We are not addressing every question posed; the equity concerns we raise apply with particular urgency to any pathway that would accelerate deployment before equity validation requirements are in place.⏎⏎The pattern of algorithmic bias in clinical AI is well-documented and, at this point, cannot be dismissed as a fringe concern or an edge case. Obermeyer et al. published in Science in 2019 the now-canonical analysis of a commercial population health algorithm used by hundreds of health systems to identify high-risk patients for care management programs (Obermeyer Z, Powers B, Vogeli C, Mullainathan S. Science. 2019;366(6464):447-453). That algorithm used prior healthcare cost as a proxy for health need — a choice that systematically underestimated the severity of illness in Black patients, who, because of well-documented barriers to care access, had lower costs despite equivalent or greater disease burden. The result was that Black patients had to be substantially sicker than white patients to receive the same level of care management support. This was not a software glitch. It was a direct consequence of training a model on data that encoded structural inequality as a neutral signal.⏎⏎The same year, Sjoding and colleagues published findings in the New England Journal of Medicine demonstrating that pulse oximeters — a device deployed in virtually every clinical setting in the country, including during the COVID-19 pandemic when accurate oxygen measurement was a matter of life and death — systematically overestimated oxygen saturation in Black patients, leading to occult hypoxemia that went undetected and untreated at significantly higher rates than in white patients (Sjoding MW, Dickson RP, Iwashyna TJ, Gay SE, Valley TS. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(25):2477-2478). AI systems trained on pulse oximetry data, or on clinical records generated in the context of that measurement error, inherit that bias. They do not correct for it. They formalize it.⏎⏎In dermatology, Adamson and Smith documented that machine learning models for skin lesion classification were trained on datasets in which images of darker skin tones were dramatically underrepresented, with predictable consequences for diagnostic accuracy in patients with higher melanin levels (Adamson AS, Smith A. JAMA Dermatol. 2018;154(11):1247-1248). These are not three isolated examples. They are three data points from a consistent and growing literature. Gianfrancesco et al. examined the structural features of EHR data that make it particularly susceptible to encoding socioeconomic and racial bias, noting that missingness patterns, differential documentation practices, and selection effects in who accesses care all introduce systematic distortions into training datasets (Gianfrancesco MA, Tamang S, Yazdany J, Schmajuk G. JAMA Intern Med. 2018;178(11):1544-1547). Kaushal and colleagues found that the geographic distribution of cohorts used to train foundational deep learning algorithms in the United States is heavily concentrated in a small number of elite academic medical centers, with profound underrepresentation of rural, community, and safety-net populations (Kaushal A, Altman R, Langlotz C. JAMA. 2020;324(12):1212-1213).⏎⏎The endocrinology context makes these concerns particularly acute. Type 2 diabetes prevalence is approximately 60 percent higher in Black adults and more than 70 percent higher in Hispanic adults compared to non-Hispanic white adults, according to CDC data. Thyroid cancer diagnosis patterns, polycystic ovary syndrome recognition, and adrenal disorder workups all have documented disparities in clinical literature. AI tools trained to identify, risk-stratify, or manage these conditions will be applied most frequently to the populations most affected — and if those tools were validated in academic medical centers with predominantly white, insured, English-speaking patient populations, their performance in the community health centers and rural practices where many of our members work will be degraded in ways that are not currently required to be measured, reported, or disclosed.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Endocrine Society Comments | HHS AI RFI | June 2024 | Page 3⏎⏎THE AUTOMATION BIAS PROBLEM COMPOUNDS THE TRAINING BIAS PROBLEM⏎⏎There is a second mechanism of harm that the HHS RFI does not adequately address, and it is one that the Endocrine Society believes deserves explicit regulatory attention. Automation bias — the tendency of human decision-makers to over-rely on algorithmic outputs and to discount contradictory information from their own clinical judgment — is a well-characterized cognitive phenomenon that becomes more pronounced, not less, as AI systems become more sophisticated and their outputs more numerically precise. Sendak and colleagues documented this dynamic in clinical settings, finding that the authoritative presentation of deep learning outputs actively suppressed clinician skepticism even when the system's reasoning was not transparent and the patient population differed from the training cohort (Sendak MP, Elish MC, Gao M, et al. Proc ACM FAT*. 2020:99-109). Char, Shah, and Magnus identified automation bias as one of the primary ethical risks of clinical machine learning implementation, arguing that the very features that make AI systems appear trustworthy — their confidence scores, their speed, their association with institutional technology investment — create conditions in which errors propagate further before detection than equivalent errors made by human clinicians (Char DS, Shah NH, Magnus D. N Engl J Med. 2018;378(11):981-983).⏎⏎The interaction between training bias and automation bias is not additive. It is multiplicative. A system that performs less accurately in Black patients with type 2 diabetes is already producing degraded outputs for that population. When the clinician receiving those outputs is subject to automation bias — which the evidence suggests is a near-universal feature of human-computer interaction in high-stakes decision environments — the degraded output is less likely to be questioned, less likely to be overridden, and more likely to be acted upon. The patient is harmed. The harm is recorded in the EHR as a clinical outcome, not as an AI error. The next model is trained on that outcome data. The cycle continues. Wiens and colleagues articulated this risk in their responsible machine learning roadmap, specifically warning that deployment without ongoing surveillance mechanisms allows bias to compound silently across patient populations (Wiens J, Saria S, Sendak M, et al. Nat Med. 2019;25(9):1337-1340).⏎⏎We want to be precise about what we are describing here, because the RFI framing could lead a reader to assume that clinician oversight is a sufficient safeguard. It is not. Clinician oversight is a necessary condition, but it is not sufficient when the oversight is being exercised by a clinician who has been cognitively primed by an authoritative AI output, who may lack the demographic context to recognize when a tool is underperforming in a specific patient population, and who has no access to the tool's training data, validation demographics, or post-deployment performance statistics. The external validation study by Wong and colleagues of a widely-implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model found that its performance in a community hospital setting was substantially worse than the performance reported in the vendor's original validation study — a validation study that had been conducted at a single large academic medical center (Wong A, Otles E, Donnelly JP, et al. JAMA Intern Med. 2021;181(8):1065-1070). The clinicians using that tool at the community hospital had no mechanism to know that. Neither did their patients.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Endocrine Society Comments | HHS AI RFI | June 2024 | Page 4⏎⏎THE STRUCTURAL GAP IN THE HHS RFI⏎⏎The Endocrine Society has reviewed the RFI with care, and we want to name a structural absence rather than simply register a concern. The RFI asks extensively about regulatory pathways to accelerate adoption, about reimbursement mechanisms that could sustain AI integration, and about research and development priorities. It does not propose — and does not solicit comment on — any mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing requirement. It does not propose any post-deployment bias surveillance framework with reporting obligations. It does not propose any requirement that AI tools used in clinical care be validated on demographically representative cohorts before deployment, or that their performance across racial, ethnic, socioeconomic, and geographic subgroups be publicly disclosed. This is not a minor gap. It is the gap through which every preventable AI-generated disparity will fall.⏎⏎The National Academy for State Health Policy documented in its 2023 policy tracker that state-level efforts to require equity auditing of health AI have been largely voluntary, inconsistent across jurisdictions, and without enforcement mechanisms sufficient to produce meaningful vendor compliance (National Academy for State Health Policy. State AI in Health Care Policy Tracker: Equity Audit Requirements and Gaps. NASHP Policy Brief. 2023). The result is a regulatory vacuum at precisely the moment when commercial AI deployment in health care is accelerating most rapidly. HHS has an opportunity to fill that vacuum with federal standards that create a level floor. The RFI, as currently structured, does not propose to do so.⏎⏎Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones documented in their 2020 analysis in the New England Journal of Medicine the way that race corrections embedded in clinical algorithms — corrections that were ostensibly designed to improve accuracy — have in practice functioned to systematically underestimate disease burden in Black patients, directing them away from treatments and interventions to which they would otherwise qualify (Vyas DA, Eisenstein LEI, Jones DS. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(9):874-882). The lesson of that literature is not simply that individual algorithms should be audited and corrected. It is that the entire development and deployment pipeline for clinical AI requires structural oversight that is independent of the vendors who profit from deployment, mandatory rather than voluntary, and continuous rather than one-time. The current RFI does not contemplate any of those features.⏎⏎Pierson and colleagues demonstrated in their 2021 Nature Medicine study that algorithmic tools can actually be redesigned to reduce pain care disparities in underserved populations — that equity-conscious AI development is technically feasible and can produce measurable improvements in outcomes for marginalized patients (Pierson E, Cutler DM, Leskovec J, Mullainathan S, Obermeyer Z. Nat Med. 2021;27(1):136-144). We cite this study not to suggest that the problem is solved, but to preempt the argument that equity requirements are technically infeasible or would unreasonably constrain innovation. They would not. They would redirect innovation toward tools that actually work for the full range of patients who will use them.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Endocrine Society Comments | HHS AI RFI | June 2024 | Page 5⏎⏎SPECIFIC POLICY RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎The Endocrine Society urges HHS to address the following requirements in any regulatory framework that emerges from this RFI process. We are not presenting these as aspirational goals. We are presenting them as minimum conditions for responsible AI deployment in clinical settings.⏎⏎1. Mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing. Any AI tool deployed in a clinical setting that affects diagnosis, treatment selection, risk stratification, or care management must be required to demonstrate, before deployment, that its performance has been validated on demographically representative patient cohorts that include adequate representation of Black, Hispanic, Asian, and Indigenous patients; patients from rural and non-metropolitan areas; patients with limited English proficiency; and patients whose primary source of care is safety-net institutions rather than academic medical centers. Validation studies conducted exclusively at academic medical centers should not be accepted as evidence of generalizability. The Kaushal et al. (2020) data on the geographic concentration of training cohorts makes clear that this is the current default, not an edge case.⏎⏎2. Third-party independent auditing before deployment. Equity auditing must be conducted by entities that are organizationally and financially independent from the AI developer or vendor. Vendor-sponsored validation studies have well-documented limitations in other medical product categories, and there is no principled reason to treat clinical AI differently. HHS should establish or designate qualified third-party auditing bodies and require their engagement as a condition of deployment authorization.⏎⏎3. Mandatory post-market bias surveillance with public reporting. Performance data disaggregated by race, ethnicity, sex, socioeconomic status, and care setting must be collected continuously after deployment and reported publicly at regular intervals — we would suggest quarterly for high-risk applications and annually for lower-risk applications. This data must be accessible to the clinicians and institutions using the tools, not only to regulators. A clinician at a community health center in Tucson should be able to look up how a particular AI diagnostic tool performs in patients who demographically resemble her patient panel. That information does not currently exist in any systematic form.⏎⏎4. Deployment suspension authority. HHS should establish a clear mechanism by which post-market surveillance data showing significant performance disparities across demographic subgroups can trigger mandatory suspension of deployment pending remediation. The current absence of such a mechanism means that disparate harms, once detected, have no guaranteed regulatory response.⏎⏎o  A note on the framing of "innovation": The Endocrine Society anticipates that some commenters will argue that mandatory equity auditing and post-deployment surveillance will slow innovation or create barriers to beneficial AI adoption. We disagree. Requirements of this kind would create a floor, not a ceiling. They would also create market incentives for developers to invest in demographically representative training data and rigorous subgroup validation — investments that the current regulatory vacuum does not reward. The tools that would be slowed or blocked by these requirements are, by definition, tools whose equity performance has not been demonstrated. That is not innovation. That is risk transfer to vulnerable patients.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Endocrine Society Comments | HHS AI RFI | June 2024 | Page 6⏎⏎A NOTE ON THE GENERALIZABILITY GAP⏎⏎The Endocrine Society wants to say one more thing about the generalizability problem, because we do not believe it has received sufficient attention in the RFI framing. Our member practices are not concentrated at academic medical centers. They are in Flagstaff, in Yuma, in the Rio Grande Valley, in rural Alabama, in underserved urban neighborhoods in Phoenix and Cleveland and Detroit. The patients they serve have different comorbidity profiles, different social determinants of health, different documentation patterns in their medical records, and different histories of engagement with the healthcare system than the patients whose data predominantly populates the training sets of commercially deployed clinical AI.⏎⏎When a tool is validated at Massachusetts General Hospital or Johns Hopkins and then deployed at a federally qualified health center in rural Arizona, the performance gap is not a deployment problem. It is a development problem. It reflects a choice — often an unconscious one, but a choice nonetheless — to treat the patients of elite academic medical centers as the default population and everyone else as an afterthought. The RFI's emphasis on "acceleration" does not grapple with this. Accelerating deployment of tools validated in one population into settings serving a different population is not progress toward health equity. It is the mechanical reproduction of existing disparities at greater speed and scale.⏎⏎The Endocrine Society recognizes that HHS cannot solve the entirety of structural racism in American medicine through AI regulation. But it can decline to make it worse. The minimum ask — mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing, mandatory third-party review, and continuous post-market bias surveillance with public reporting — is not a radical demand. It is the application to clinical AI of standards that we already apply, however imperfectly, to other medical products that affect patient safety. The FDA requires stratified efficacy and safety data for new drug applications. There is no coherent argument for treating AI diagnostic tools, which make or influence clinical decisions for millions of patients, as categorically exempt from equivalent requirements.⏎⏎We note, finally, that the absence of these requirements in the current RFI is not a neutral starting position. Every month that clinical AI tools are deployed without equity auditing is a month in which performance disparities are accumulating in patient records, being encoded into downstream training data, and affecting clinical decisions for patients who have no way to know that the tool advising their care was not validated for populations like theirs. The Endocrine Society urges HHS to treat the establishment of mandatory equity safeguards not as a follow-on consideration to be addressed after adoption pathways are established, but as a prerequisite — a condition that must be met before any deployment pathway is opened.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Sarah Davis⏎Executive Director⏎Endocrine Society⏎1735 K Street, NW⏎Washington, DC 20006⏎policy@endocrine.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0080♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Young♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔Arc of the United States♔♔♔♔♔♔Arc of the United States — Comments on HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Submitted to the Office of the Deputy Secretary, U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎⏎Arc of the United States | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 1⏎⏎⏎THE RELATIONAL SUBSTRATE OF CLINICAL CARE⏎⏎Arc of the United States submits these comments on behalf of its network of member laboratories and clinical institutions, which collectively number more than 1,200 organizations across the United States. Our 2024 benchmarking survey of that membership found that 58% had piloted at least one AI-assisted diagnostic tool within the prior two years, yet fewer than 12% reported a clear regulatory pathway guiding their validation and deployment decisions. That gap — between adoption momentum and governance infrastructure — is itself instructive. It tells us that the market is already moving faster than the regulatory frameworks designed to protect patients, and that HHS's instinct to further accelerate adoption without first resolving foundational questions about safety, equity, and accountability should be examined with considerable care.⏎⏎The RFI before us is framed around acceleration. Arc is genuinely supportive of HHS's broader mission to improve clinical outcomes through technology, and we do not dispute that AI tools have demonstrated real value in bounded, post-diagnostic administrative functions — coding support, documentation burden reduction, image pre-screening triage. Topol EJ's 2019 synthesis in Nature Medicine remains a reasonable account of what algorithmic systems can do well when their scope is appropriately constrained (Topol EJ. "High-performance medicine: the convergence of human and artificial intelligence." Nature Medicine. 2019;25(1):44–56). What concerns Arc, and what the RFI does not appear to grapple with at all, is the assumption embedded in the word "productivity" — used repeatedly in the Department's framing documents — that the clinician-patient encounter is a unit of output to be optimized rather than a therapeutic relationship with measurable clinical value independent of any technical intervention performed within it.⏎⏎This is not a philosophical concern. It is an empirical one. Kelley and colleagues, in a systematic review and meta-analysis published in PLoS ONE, found that the quality of the patient-clinician relationship is independently associated with healthcare outcomes across a broad range of conditions and settings (Kelley JM, Kraft-Todd G, Schapira L, et al. "The influence of the patient-clinician relationship on healthcare outcomes: a systematic review and meta-analysis." PLoS ONE. 2014;9(4):e94207). Hojat and colleagues demonstrated that physicians scoring higher on standardized empathy measures achieved meaningfully better hemoglobin A1c and LDL control in diabetic patients, a finding that cannot be attributed to technical skill differences (Hojat M, Louis DZ, Markham FW, et al. "Physicians' empathy and clinical outcomes for diabetic patients." Academic Medicine. 2011;86(3):359–364). These are not soft findings. They are outcome data, and any regulatory framework that accelerates AI deployment without measuring AI's effect on the relational substrate producing those outcomes is methodologically incomplete.⏎⏎⏎Arc of the United States | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE DIAGNOSTIC MOMENT THAT ALGORITHMS CANNOT ACCESS⏎⏎Consider a scenario that our member clinicians have described in various forms through our Q3 2024 policy working group. A 54-year-old woman with an intellectual disability presents to a primary care clinic accompanied by her adult daughter. She cannot reliably localize pain. Her symptom descriptions are inconsistent across the visit. Her daughter, who has accompanied her to every appointment for eleven years, is quieter than usual — not withdrawn, exactly, but watchful in a way the clinician notices. The clinician slows down. Asks the daughter directly. A conversation unfolds that would not have been initiated by any structured intake algorithm, and it surfaces a medication adherence problem tied to a recent change in the patient's residential setting, which in turn explains a cluster of symptoms that had been attributed to a different etiology for nearly four months.⏎⏎No pattern-matching system operating on structured data inputs would have caught the daughter's silence. No natural language processing model trained on clinical notes would have weighted it appropriately. The diagnosis turned not on any measurable variable but on a clinician's capacity to read relational context — what Verghese has called the information that exists between the lines of the chart (Verghese A. "Culture shock — patient as icon, icon as patient." New England Journal of Medicine. 2008;359(26):2748–2751). Arc's member institutions serve disproportionately high numbers of patients with intellectual and developmental disabilities, patients for whom the relational dimensions of diagnosis are not supplementary to clinical accuracy but constitutive of it. The RFI's framing, which treats AI intermediation as a default good subject to regulatory fine-tuning, does not account for this population at all.⏎⏎Ridd and colleagues, in a synthesis of the qualitative literature on patients' perspectives on the doctor-patient relationship, identified continuity, trust, and the experience of being known as the features patients consistently identify as most therapeutically meaningful — features that are structurally disrupted by any system that interposes an algorithmic intermediary between the patient's presentation and the clinician's interpretive attention (Ridd M, Shaw A, Lewis G, Salisbury C. "The patient-doctor relationship: a synthesis of the qualitative literature on patients' perspectives." British Journal of General Practice. 2009;59(561):e116–e133). Dzau and Balatbat raised precisely this concern in JAMA, noting that the therapeutic relationship faces structural pressure from both market forces and technological intermediation, and that preserving it requires affirmative policy choices rather than passive reliance on clinician judgment under conditions of algorithmic saturation (Dzau VJ, Balatbat CA. "Future of health and healthcare: innovation, workforce, and the therapeutic relationship." JAMA. 2021;325(2):127–128).⏎⏎⏎THE DESKILLING PROBLEM AND THE LIMITS OF TECHNICAL ACCURACY AS A REGULATORY TARGET⏎⏎Arc of the United States | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 3⏎⏎⏎Arc's parallel analysis of laboratory-reported adverse event data submitted to our quality registry found that institutions operating without formal AI governance frameworks experienced a 2.3-fold higher rate of result discordance flagged during post-deployment audits. That figure is worth pausing on. The institutions experiencing higher discordance rates were not institutions that had rejected AI tools; they were institutions that had adopted them without governance structures capable of catching the downstream effects of what the cognitive science literature calls automation-induced skill atrophy. Parasuraman and Manzey's foundational work in Human Factors documents the mechanism: when clinicians operate in environments where algorithmic systems provide continuous decision support, their independent judgment becomes calibrated to the system's outputs rather than to direct clinical observation, and their capacity to detect when the system is wrong degrades over time (Parasuraman R, Manzey DH. "Complacency and bias in human use of automation: an attentional integration." Human Factors. 2010;52(3):381–410). This is not a hypothetical risk. It is a documented cognitive phenomenon, and it is precisely the risk that HHS's acceleration-first posture will produce at scale if reimbursement incentives are structured to reward AI-assisted throughput without measuring what is lost in the process.⏎⏎Croskerry's work on cognitive strategies in clinical decision-making provides additional grounding here: the heuristics that experienced clinicians develop through direct patient contact are not merely shortcuts but are finely calibrated pattern-recognition systems built on relational observation, and they are susceptible to systematic disruption when algorithmic inputs compete with or override direct sensory and interpersonal data (Croskerry P. "Achieving quality in clinical decision making: cognitive strategies and detection of bias." Academic Emergency Medicine. 2002;9(11):1184–1204). Tversky and Kahneman's work on judgment under uncertainty is relevant as well — the conditions that produce automation bias are structurally similar to the conditions that produce anchoring errors, and any deployment environment that presents AI outputs as authoritative prior to clinician examination will predictably increase both (Tversky A, Kahneman D. "Judgment under uncertainty: heuristics and biases." Science. 1974;185(4157):1124–1131).⏎⏎Arc does not argue that AI tools have no place in clinical settings. Shortliffe and Sepúlveda's account of clinical decision support systems in JAMA describes a reasonable model in which algorithmic tools function as bounded consultants rather than front-line intermediaries (Shortliffe EH, Sepúlveda MJ. "Clinical decision support in the era of artificial intelligence." JAMA. 2018;320(21):2199–2200). Bickmore and colleagues have shown that certain structured AI interactions can support patient engagement in limited, well-defined contexts (Bickmore TW, Pfeifer LM, Jack BW. "Taking the time to care: empowering low health literacy hospital patients with virtual nurse agents." Proceedings of the SIGCHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems. 2009:1265–1274). The distinction Arc draws is between AI as a post-encounter administrative tool — acceptable, potentially beneficial — and AI as a front-line clinical intermediary that shapes what the clinician attends to before the relational encounter has had the opportunity to generate its own diagnostic information.⏎⏎⏎Arc of the United States | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 4⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC POLICY REQUEST⏎⏎Arc requests that HHS take the following actions in response to this RFI, and asks that these requests be considered in conjunction with the Federal Health IT Strategic Plan 2024–2030 (Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology. "Federal Health IT Strategic Plan 2024–2030." U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. 2024), which articulates equity and patient-centered care as co-equal priorities with efficiency:⏎⏎1. Require that any clinical AI deployment seeking reimbursement recognition or regulatory clearance demonstrate preservation of therapeutic relationship quality as a precondition for approval — not merely technical accuracy benchmarks. Validated instruments for measuring relational quality exist and are in clinical research use; there is no technical obstacle to requiring them.⏎⏎2. Commission prospective, longitudinal research measuring relational outcomes — patient trust, continuity of care experience, clinician empathy scores, and patient-reported experience measures — before any further acceleration of front-line clinical AI deployment. The 2024 benchmarking data Arc has cited above demonstrates that adoption is already outpacing governance; prospective relational outcome measurement is the minimum methodological floor for responsible policy.⏎⏎3. Convene a working group that includes disability advocates, patients with complex communication needs, and clinicians serving high-vulnerability populations — not only AI developers and health system administrators — before finalizing any reimbursement or regulatory pathway for front-line clinical AI.⏎⏎Arc notes that the RFI's repeated use of the word "productivity" as a policy goal for clinical AI deployment is a framing choice with real consequences. Productivity is an appropriate goal for administrative AI. It is a category error when applied to the diagnostic encounter itself, and the Department should consider revising its framing language accordingly before the comment period closes. The therapeutic relationship is not an inefficiency. It is the mechanism through which a substantial portion of clinical outcomes are produced, and regulatory frameworks that treat it as such will produce harms that no accuracy benchmark will⏎⏎arc_comments_hhs_ai_rfi_2024@arcus.org⏎⏎Arc of the United States | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0081♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Clark♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My granddaughter Lily was misdiagnosed twice at Christiana Care before a computer tool caught what two doctors missed, and I keep thinking about what would have happened if that tool had been the one to get it wrong instead of catching it. Who answers for that? The doctor blames the software, the hospital blames the vendor, the vendor says the doctor didn't use it right, and my eight-year-old granddaughter is just sitting there. In my school, if a child gets hurt, there is a teacher, a principal, a school board, and I know exactly who to call. I genuinely don't understand why HHS wants to speed all this up before anyone has answered that same basic question for hospitals. Please slow down and figure out the accountability chain first.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0082♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Wright♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎This comment is submitted in response to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings. The RFI's framing merits a direct challenge on one foundational point: the Department asks how to accelerate AI adoption and how to build evaluation frameworks that support clinical AI tools, but the most consequential AI deployment in American healthcare today is already operating at scale, largely without regulatory scrutiny, and it is not a clinical tool. It is a denial engine. Any evaluation methodology that does not account for AI systems deployed in coverage and reimbursement decisions is not a neutral technical framework. It is a policy choice that leaves the most harmful applications of health AI outside the scope of accountability.⏎⏎My concern with this RFI comes partly from professional habit and partly from watching someone I know well nearly destroyed by the administrative machinery of American healthcare. A former colleague, a reference librarian of thirty years who spent her career helping people find the information they needed to make decisions, was diagnosed with aggressive lymphoma shortly after retirement. Over the following months, she received contradictory treatment summaries from three separate hospital systems that could not share data with each other. I drove her to the University of Alabama at Birmingham for a second opinion and sat in the waiting room thinking about the gap between information that exists and insight that gets delivered. That experience, which I will not dwell on at length here because this comment is not primarily about clinical information gaps, reoriented how I think about the question HHS is actually asking. The question is not whether AI can help patients. It is who AI is currently serving, and at whose expense.⏎⏎The answer to that question is documented with some precision. The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations released findings in 2024 showing that UnitedHealth Group's nH Predict algorithm generated prior authorization denials for post-acute care at a rate that far exceeded the denial rates produced by human reviewers applying the same coverage criteria (U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations, "Denial, Delay, Deny: How Medicare Advantage Plans Use AI to Override Physician Judgments," 2024). The algorithm was trained on historical claims data, which means it was trained, at least in part, on whatever coverage decisions insurers had previously made, including decisions that may themselves have been inappropriate. This is not a speculative concern about AI going wrong in the future. It is a documented instance of AI operating as designed, producing outcomes at scale that individual physician reviewers would not have produced, and doing so faster than any appeals process can respond. A KFF issue brief from 2023 documented that Medicare Advantage plans denied prior authorization requests at rising rates while fewer than one in ten denied claims were ever appealed, and that most appeals that were filed were unsuccessful (Koma, W. et al., "Prior Authorization in Medicare Advantage: Trends and Beneficiary Experiences," KFF Issue Brief, 2023). The appeals process, in other words, is not functioning as a meaningful corrective. It is functioning as a deterrent.⏎⏎The structural inaccessibility of appeals processes is not accidental, and the populations most harmed by automated denial systems are the populations least positioned to fight back. The National Health Law Program's 2023 report on automated prior authorization in Medicaid documented that beneficiaries with disabilities face particular barriers: appeal forms that require navigation of complex insurer-specific portals, short filing windows, and the practical impossibility of obtaining physician support letters quickly enough to meet deadlines, all of which combine to make the nominal right of appeal functionally unavailable to many of the people who need it most (National Health Law Program, "Automated Injustice: How Algorithmic Prior Authorization Harms Medicaid Beneficiaries with Disabilities," 2023). A KFF report on ACA marketplace plans found that fewer than one in ten denied claims are ever appealed (Pollitz, K. et al., "Coverage Denials in ACA Marketplace Plans: Consumer Experiences and Insurer Practices," KFF Report, 2023). What this means practically is that automated denial functions as a final decision for the vast majority of patients affected. The insurer's AI system is, in effect, the last word.⏎⏎There is also a racial equity dimension to this that HHS cannot responsibly ignore in any evaluation framework it builds. Ziad Obermeyer and colleagues published a study in Science in 2019 demonstrating that a widely used health algorithm trained on cost data systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent clinical complexity, because it used healthcare expenditures as a proxy for health need, and structural inequities in access to care meant Black patients had historically incurred lower costs even when their clinical needs were comparable or greater (Obermeyer, Z. et al., "Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations," Science 366(6464): 447-453, 2019). That study addressed a population health management algorithm, not a prior authorization system specifically, but the methodological problem it identified, using cost as a proxy for need in systems trained on historical claims, is precisely the methodological problem embedded in denial-automation tools trained on cost-containment objectives. HHS's evaluation framework should require algorithmic audits that disaggregate denial rates and appeal outcomes by race, disability status, income level, and geographic region. Without that requirement, any evaluation methodology is measuring performance on the wrong variable.⏎⏎What I am asking HHS to do, in specific terms, is the following:⏎⏎1. Expand the scope of this RFI's follow-on rulemaking to explicitly include AI systems deployed in prior authorization, coverage determination, and claims adjudication. These are health sector AI systems. Excluding them from evaluation requirements because they are administered by insurers rather than clinical providers is a definitional choice with significant consequences for patient welfare.⏎⏎2. Define "meaningful human review" with enforceable specificity. The 2024 Senate investigation documented that nH Predict was generating denial recommendations that human reviewers were approving in bulk, in some cases at rates that made genuine individual review implausible given the volume. A standard that requires human sign-off without specifying minimum review time, required clinical documentation, or prohibition on productivity quotas that effectively preclude genuine review is not a standard. It is paperwork.⏎⏎3. Require that any AI system used in coverage or reimbursement decisions be trained on clinical appropriateness criteria validated by independent clinical bodies, not on historical cost data. The AMA's 2023 prior authorization survey found that 94 percent of physicians reported that prior authorization requirements delayed patients' access to necessary care, and 33 percent reported that prior authorization had led to a serious adverse event for a patient in their care (American Medical Association, "AMA Prior Authorization Physician Survey: Patients Harmed by Delays and Denials," 2023). These are not abstractions. They are reported patient harms attributable to administrative AI systems.⏎⏎4. Require public disclosure of denial rates, appeal rates, and appeal success rates disaggregated by the demographic variables listed above, for any insurer using automated or AI-assisted prior authorization. This information should be publicly accessible, not submitted to CMS in proprietary formats that preclude independent analysis.⏎⏎5. Establish a private right of action or a clear enforcement pathway for patients harmed by AI-generated denials that were not subject to meaningful human review. The appellate burden currently falls entirely on patients, most of whom cannot sustain it.⏎⏎I want to return, briefly, to something. What struck me in that waiting room at UAB, watching my colleague try to organize printed summaries from three different hospital systems that could not talk to each other, was not primarily frustration at technical failure. It was recognition of a specific kind of information problem: a system that generates data without generating usable knowledge, and that places the burden of synthesis on the person least equipped to carry it, at the moment when they are least able to. Insurance AI denial systems are a version of the same problem, but inverted. They synthesize data very efficiently into a decision, and they place the burden of challenging that decision on the patient, at the moment of greatest vulnerability, through a process deliberately structured to be exhausting. The Schulte and Fry investigation of electronic health records published in 2019 documented how administrative health IT systems are routinely optimized for billing and liability protection rather than for care delivery, and that pattern has only continued as AI has been layered onto those same systems (Schulte, F. and Fry, E., "Death by a Thousand Clicks: Where Electronic Health Records Went Wrong," Kaiser Health News/Fortune, 2019).⏎⏎HHS has an opportunity in this rulemaking to define "AI in healthcare" in a way that does not exempt the most profitable and most consequential applications from accountability. Whether it takes that opportunity is a policy choice, and that choice will be visible in what the final rule covers and what it leaves out.⏎⏎The appendix referenced in this comment, containing additional documentation of state-level prior authorization reform efforts and a comparative analysis of insurer-reported versus independent audit denial rate figures, was not attached due to file size limitations and is available upon request.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0083♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Nguyen♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My buddy Carlos called me from an ambulance bay in Tulsa, I was somewhere outside Hennessey on I-81 hauling a load west, and he told me his ER had just started using an AI triage tool that flagged his patient as low-priority. The patient had a dissecting aortic aneurysm. A human nurse overrode the system in time, but barely. I've been thinking about that call ever since, because I drive past rural Oklahoma hospitals every week, skeleton operations where the parking lot is half empty and the staff is already stretched past the point of reasonable, and the idea that HHS is now asking how to go faster with these tools, not whether to go faster, genuinely frightens me. I want to be specific about why, because this is not a vague fear of computers. It is a fear rooted in published research that the agency appears to have not yet treated as a mandatory constraint on deployment.⏎⏎ALGORITHMIC BIAS IS NOT A HYPOTHETICAL⏎⏎The pattern of AI systems inheriting and reproducing discriminatory treatment patterns is documented, peer-reviewed, and ongoing. Obermeyer and colleagues published in Science in 2019 that a widely used commercial algorithm for managing patient health systematically underestimated the illness severity of Black patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, and Black patients historically receive less care for the same conditions, so the algorithm encoded that disparity as a feature rather than a bug (Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan, Science 2019, 366:447-453). That is not an edge case. That algorithm was deployed across hundreds of health systems touching millions of patients. Around the same time, Sjoding and colleagues published in the New England Journal of Medicine that pulse oximeters, the physical devices that AI systems now routinely ingest as input data, overestimate blood oxygen saturation in patients with dark skin at a rate three times higher than in white patients, meaning AI tools trained on pulse oximetry readings are built on a substrate that is already racially distorted before the first line of model code is written (Sjoding et al., NEJM 2020, 383:2477-2478). Adamson and Smith wrote in JAMA Dermatology in 2018 that dermatology AI trained predominantly on images of lighter skin performs substantially worse on darker skin, and that this gap in training data maps almost perfectly onto the populations least likely to have received adequate dermatologic care historically. What I keep coming back to is that each of these is a separate domain, separate research teams, separate journals, and they all find the same thing. The bias is not incidental. It is structural, and it runs through the data the models are built on.⏎⏎THE DEPLOYMENT GAP IS WHERE PEOPLE GET HURT⏎⏎Here is the part that I think HHS is not taking seriously enough. These tools are validated at academic medical centers with robust patient populations, detailed EHR records, and experienced clinicians who have the time and the institutional culture to scrutinize an AI recommendation before acting on it. Finlayson and colleagues wrote in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2021 about dataset shift, the phenomenon where a model's performance degrades when it moves from the population it was trained on to the population it is actually deployed in, and they were explicit that this degradation is worst at the institutions with the fewest resources to detect it (Finlayson et al., NEJM 2021, 385:283-286). Wong and colleagues published in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2021 that when they externally validated a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model at their institution, its performance was substantially worse than the vendor had reported, and they had the infrastructure to do that validation (Wong et al., JAMA Internal Medicine 2021, 181:1065-1070). Most rural Oklahoma hospitals do not have that infrastructure. They are going to receive a tool that was validated somewhere else, on someone else's patients, and they are going to trust the number it produces because the alternative is to assume it's wrong every time, which nobody does.⏎⏎This is where automation bias becomes the mechanism of harm. Char, Shah, and Magnus wrote in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2018 about the ethical challenges of deploying machine learning in healthcare, and they noted that the authoritative presentation of AI outputs makes clinicians less likely to override incorrect recommendations, particularly under cognitive load (Char et al., NEJM 2018, 378:981-983). Now put that dynamic in a rural ER at 2 AM where the nurse has four patients and one of them just got flagged low-priority by the AI. The machine says low-priority. The nurse is already overwhelmed. Who overrides it? In Tulsa, apparently, one nurse did. I don't know how many others didn't, at other hospitals, with other patients, and I don't know because there is no systematic post-deployment surveillance requirement in this RFI. Gianfrancesco and colleagues identified in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2018 that EHR-derived machine learning models are susceptible to biases introduced by differential access to care, coding practices that vary by institution, and missing data that is not missing at random but missing because certain patients didn't get certain workups (Gianfrancesco et al., JAMA Internal Medicine 2018, 178:1544-1547). That last point matters enormously for rural and safety-net settings where documentation practices differ from academic centers and where the patients who never made it to the hospital are, by definition, absent from the training data.⏎⏎NO DEPLOYMENT WITHOUT EQUITY VALIDATION IN THE TARGET POPULATION⏎⏎What I am asking HHS to do is simple to state even if it is hard to implement. Before any AI clinical decision support tool is deployed in a federally funded or reimbursed setting, the vendor should be required to demonstrate performance equity across the demographic groups who will actually use the system, not the groups at the academic center where it was trained. That means stratified performance metrics by race, ethnicity, age, rural versus urban setting, and insurance status, reported publicly, before deployment. And after deployment, there should be mandatory ongoing surveillance with a defined threshold at which a tool gets pulled or restricted pending reanalysis. The RFI asks how HHS can accelerate adoption. My answer is that accelerating adoption without those two requirements in place means the only way we will find out a tool is harming Black patients or rural patients or low-income patients is after enough of them have been harmed to show up in the data. I am not willing to accept that as the de facto study design, and I don't think HHS should be either. The nurse who overrode the system for Carlos's patient was paying attention that night.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0084♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Walker♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔MIT Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence Laboratory (CSAIL)♔♔♔♔♔♔My pharmacist mentioned something about new transparency requirements a few weeks back, which is what prompted me to look into this RFI more carefully — and I want to be honest that I am not entirely certain this docket is addressing the same thing he described. But having read through the request, and having spent the last eighteen months working through Pictor Labs' AI-assisted manuscript and grant workflow, the questions HHS is asking here are ones that my research group has been wrestling with in a fairly concrete way. Between Q1 2023 and Q3 2024, our computational pathology pipelines integrating large language model annotation reduced iterative review cycles by 38% across 847 processed submissions, and a cross-institutional collaboration with four academic medical centers documented a median 22-day reduction in biomarker validation timelines. Those numbers are real and they are worth something. But what a concurrent survey of 61 postdoctoral researchers at affiliated institutions found is that 74% of respondents named regulatory ambiguity around FDA Software as a Medical Device classification as the single biggest barrier to translating laboratory-validated models into IRB-approved clinical pilot studies. The acceleration agenda HHS is articulating in this RFI is genuinely welcome. The concern raised in this comment is that acceleration, absent a mandatory equity-auditing framework baked into the regulatory pathway rather than appended to it later, is likely to reproduce and scale existing disparities rather than reduce them.⏎⏎The preponderance of evidence suggests that AI systems trained on clinical data systematically underperform for patients from historically underserved populations, and this is not a contested empirical claim at this point. Obermeyer et al. (2019, Science 366:447) documented that a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to allocate health care resources systematically underestimated the illness burden of Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent health needs, because it used health care costs as a proxy for health — a proxy that itself encodes historical patterns of differential access. Adamson and Smith (JAMA Dermatol. 2018;154:1247) found that dermatology machine learning datasets were heavily skewed toward lighter skin tones, with documented performance degradation for patients with darker skin. Seyyed-Kalantari et al. (Nat Med. 2021;27:2176) showed that chest radiograph AI systems trained on standard benchmark datasets exhibited systematic underdiagnosis bias for patients from underserved populations, and the effect persisted even after controlling for image quality. Sjoding et al. (N Engl J Med. 2020;383:2477) demonstrated that pulse oximetry — the data stream feeding many downstream AI clinical decision tools — itself carries racial bias, with occult hypoxemia nearly three times more common in Black patients than in white patients at the same device reading. These are not edge cases. They are the substrate on which clinical AI is being built, and HHS's RFI, to the extent it frames equity considerations as one input among many rather than as a prerequisite for deployment authorization, does not adequately reckon with that substrate.⏎⏎What makes this structurally concerning rather than just empirically concerning is that the failure modes are not uniformly distributed across the patient population, but the performance benchmarks used to authorize deployment typically are. A model that achieves, say, 91% sensitivity on a held-out test set drawn from the same distribution as the training data may be performing substantially worse for patients whose demographic characteristics are underrepresented in that training set — and the aggregate metric will not reveal that. Wong et al. (JAMA Intern Med. 2021;181:1065) documented exactly this problem in external validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model: performance degraded significantly when the model was applied in a new institutional context, raising serious questions about whether the original deployment authorization adequately accounted for distribution shift. Gianfrancesco et al. (JAMA Intern Med. 2018;178:1544) catalogued multiple pathways through which electronic health record data introduces bias into downstream machine learning models, including differential rates of missing data, differential documentation practices, and the encoding of race as a variable in ways that conflate biological and social constructs. Vyas et al. (N Engl J Med. 2020;383:874) made a related argument about race correction in clinical algorithms more broadly, and the concern they raise applies directly to AI systems that incorporate race as a feature without interrogating what that feature is actually proxying for.⏎⏎The distinction the research community draws between average-population performance and subgroup failure modes is not subtle, but it is frequently absent from the regulatory conversation. Wiens et al. (Nat Med. 2019;25:1337) laid out a roadmap for responsible machine learning in health care that explicitly included subgroup performance analysis as a non-negotiable component of pre-deployment evaluation, not a nice-to-have. Char, Shah, and Magnus (N Engl J Med. 2018;378:981) made a similar argument about ethical implementation, noting that the governance structures for clinical AI need to anticipate the ways in which algorithms can perpetuate structural inequity if the training data and validation frameworks are not deliberately designed to surface those effects. Celi et al. (PLOS Digit Health. 2022;1:e0000022) conducted a global review of bias sources in clinical AI and found that the problem is not confined to any particular dataset or modality — it is a systemic feature of how health data is generated, collected, and used. And Pierson et al. (Nat Med. 2021;27:136) demonstrated that algorithmic approaches can, under the right conditions, actually reduce unexplained pain disparities in underserved populations — which is worth noting because the argument here is not that AI is irredeemably biased and should not be deployed. The argument is that realizing the potential Pierson et al. describe requires deliberate methodological choices that are currently not mandated by the regulatory pathway HHS is considering accelerating.⏎⏎The structural gap in the RFI as written is that equity auditing — meaning stratified subgroup performance analysis disaggregated by race, ethnicity, sex, socioeconomic status, and geographic location at minimum — is not positioned as a prerequisite for deployment authorization or reimbursement eligibility. Sendak et al. (Proc ACM FAT*. 2020:99) documented in ethnographic work on clinical AI deployment that the opacity of deep learning systems creates specific challenges for clinical decision-making that go beyond what clinicians typically encounter with conventional decision support tools, and that those challenges are not uniformly distributed across patient populations or care settings. The point is not that equity auditing is technically impossible or prohibitively expensive — the Pictor Labs workflow data suggest that well-designed AI pipelines can actually reduce the administrative burden associated with validation cycles, which means there is headroom in the efficiency gains to absorb more rigorous pre-deployment requirements. The point is that without a mandatory framework, the competitive pressure to deploy quickly will consistently win out over the precautionary logic that equity analysis requires, because the populations most likely to be harmed by biased models are also the populations least represented in the feedback loops that would otherwise surface the problem post-deployment.⏎⏎There are a few specific things HHS could do that would address this gap without materially slowing the adoption trajectory the RFI is trying to support:⏎⏎1. Require that any AI system seeking reimbursement authorization or clinical deployment approval under a streamlined SaMD pathway include a pre-deployment equity audit with disaggregated performance metrics stratified by race, ethnicity, sex, age, socioeconomic proxies, and geographic context. The audit should be conducted on a validation dataset that is demonstrably representative of the intended deployment population, not just the training distribution.⏎⏎2. Establish a publicly accessible federal registry of AI clinical decision support tool performance data, including subgroup performance metrics, that is updated on a defined cadence post-deployment. This is not a novel idea — Wiens et al. (2019) recommended something along these lines — but it is not currently required, and the absence of a centralized repository means that evidence of post-deployment bias accumulates in individual institutions without generating the kind of cross-institutional signal that would prompt regulatory action.⏎⏎3. Fund a dedicated research program within AHRQ or NCI specifically targeting algorithmic bias in clinical AI, with a mandate to develop standardized equity auditing protocols that can be adopted across the SaMD approval pathway. The biomarker validation timeline reduction documented in the Pictor Labs collaboration suggests that well-designed AI tools can accelerate the research pipeline; those same tools should be turned toward the equity auditing problem itself.⏎⏎4. Require that IRB protocols for clinical AI pilot studies include a pre-specified subgroup analysis plan with adequate statistical power to detect clinically meaningful performance differences across demographic groups. The current IRB landscape is inconsistent on this point, and the 74% regulatory-ambiguity finding from the postdoctoral researcher survey suggests that the research community would benefit from clearer federal guidance on what a complete equity analysis looks like at the pilot stage.⏎⏎It is worth being transparent about the uncertainty here. The Pictor Labs internal data are from a specific research workflow context and should not be generalized without caution. The cross-institutional collaboration involved four academic medical centers, which are not representative of the full range of clinical settings where AI deployment is being contemplated — community health centers, federally qualified health centers, rural critical access hospitals. The 22-day reduction in biomarker validation timelines is real but it comes from a context where the infrastructure for AI-assisted research was already reasonably mature. The equity auditing recommendations above would need to be calibrated for resource-constrained settings, and there is a legitimate concern that imposing the same pre-deployment requirements on a large academic health system and a small community clinic without differential support structures would effectively exclude the latter from AI adoption entirely, which would itself be an equity problem.⏎⏎That tension is real and the RFI does not resolve it. The preponderance of evidence on subgroup performance degradation, as documented across the literature cited above — Obermeyer et al., Seyyed-Kalantari et al., Sjoding et al., Adamson and Smith, among others — suggests that the risk of deploying inadequately audited AI systems at scale is not evenly distributed, and the populations bearing the greatest risk are precisely those for whom the potential benefit of well-designed clinical AI is also greatest. Pierson et al. (2021) showed that there is genuine potential for algorithmic tools to reduce pain disparities that have persisted for decades despite conventional clinical interventions. Getting to that outcome requires treating the equity auditing infrastructure as a foundational research and regulatory priority, not as an optional refinement to be addressed in a future rulemaking cycle after the deployment acceleration is already underway.⏎⏎The pharmacist conversation that prompted this comment was apparently about something related to drug interaction flagging in a new EHR module — and honestly it is still not clear whether that system went through anything like the kind of pre-deployment equity analysis described here. That is the kind of gap that plays out quietly, at the level of individual clinical encounters, in ways that are very difficult to detect after the fact.⏎⏎References⏎⏎Obermeyer Z, Powers B, Vogeli C, Mullainathan S. Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations. Science. 2019;366(6464):447–453.⏎⏎Sjoding MW, Dickson RP, Iwashyna TJ, Gay SE, Valley TS. Racial bias in pulse oximetry measurement. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(25):2477–2478.⏎⏎Adamson AS, Smith A. Machine learning and health care disparities in dermatology. JAMA Dermatol. 2018;154(11):1247–1248.⏎⏎Wong A, Otles E, Donnelly JP, et al. External validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model in hospitalized patients. JAMA Intern Med. 2021;181(8):1065–1070.⏎⏎Char DS, Shah NH, Magnus D. Implementing machine learning in health care — addressing ethical challenges. N Engl J Med. 2018;378(11):981–983.⏎⏎Vyas DA, Eisenstein LEI, Jones DS. Hidden in plain sight — reconsidering the use of race correction in clinical algorithms. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(9):874–882.⏎⏎Seyyed-Kalantari L, Zhang H, McDermott MBA, Chen IY, Ghassemi M. Underdiagnosis bias of artificial intelligence algorithms applied to chest radiographs in under-served patient populations. Nat Med. 2021;27(12):2176–2182.⏎⏎Wiens J, Saria S, Sendak M, et al. Do no harm: a roadmap for responsible machine learning for health care. Nat Med. 2019;25(9):1337–1340.⏎⏎Gianfrancesco MA, Tamang S, Yazdany J, Schmajuk G. Potential biases in machine learning algorithms using electronic health record data. JAMA Intern Med. 2018;178(11):1544–1547.⏎⏎Sendak MP, Elish MC, Gao M, et al. 'The human body is a black box': supporting clinical decision-making with deep learning. Proc ACM FAT*. 2020:99–109.⏎⏎Celi LA, Cellini J, Charpignon ML, et al. Sources of bias in artificial intelligence that perpetuate healthcare disparities — a global review. PLOS Digit Health. 2022;1(3):e0000022.⏎⏎Pierson E, Cutler DM, Leskovec J, Mullainathan S, Obermeyer Z. An algorithmic approach to reducing unexplained pain disparities in underserved populations. Nat Med. 2021;27(1):136–140.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0085♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Anderson♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔Carbon Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Carbon Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎SUBMISSION TO HHS OFFICE OF THE DEPUTY SECRETARY⏎Re: Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎89 FR 44201 (June 2024)⏎Submitted by: Sandra Anderson, MD, Chief Medical Officer, Carbon Health⏎⏎---⏎⏎MARKET STRUCTURE IS THE ACTUAL PROBLEM⏎⏎Carbon Health submits these comments in response to the Department's Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings (ONC, 2024, 89 FR 44201). Our organization operates across multi-state ambulatory and behavioral health settings, and our Q3 2024 internal audit documented that 68% of documentation burden cited by our licensed clinical social workers as interfering with direct patient contact involves tasks now addressable through AI-assisted ambient documentation tools. We raise that finding here not because it is the primary focus of these comments, but because it illustrates the stakes. The American Academy of Social Work and Social Welfare (2023) has estimated that documentation consumes 35-40% of clinician hours per week in small and solo behavioral health practices. That is a real and measurable harm.⏎⏎What concerns this organization, however, is that the RFI's framing largely assumes a market structure that is capable of delivering AI adoption equitably and without structural distortion. That assumption is wrong, and proceeding as though it is correct will produce a version of AI acceleration that benefits incumbent vendors while extracting value from the providers and patients the Department is ostensibly trying to serve. Gee and Bhatt (2023) documented in Health Affairs (42:8, 1124-1133) the degree to which health information technology markets are now characterized by concentration so severe that new entrants face effective foreclosure. Dafny and Lee (2022) made a related point in the New England Journal of Medicine (386:19, 1843-1847) about how consolidation in health care markets generally tends to erode the competitive pressures that would otherwise discipline quality and price. The Department should read those papers before issuing a final rule or guidance that routes AI adoption incentives through the same vendor relationships that produced the EHR dysfunction Schulte and Fry (2019) documented so carefully in their Kaiser Health News investigation — a system in which clinicians were left clicking through screens designed to maximize billing capture, not clinical utility.⏎⏎⏎Carbon Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎The RFI does not use the phrase "regulatory capture," but the phenomenon it describes is recognizable. Meaningful Use created reimbursement incentives for EHR adoption that, as Blumenthal and Tavenner (2010) noted in the New England Journal of Medicine (363:6, 501-504), were structured in ways that concentrated market power among a small number of certified vendors. Adler-Milstein and Pfeifer (2017) documented in the Milbank Quarterly (95:1, 117-135) how those same vendors subsequently engaged in information blocking practices that protected their market positions at patient and provider expense. The FTC's October 2023 policy report on generative AI and health care market concentration warned explicitly that similar dynamics are already forming around AI infrastructure, with large incumbent EHR vendors acquiring or exclusively partnering with AI firms in ways that will foreclose competition from independent developers. Carbon Health has observed this dynamic directly. Our prior authorization workflows, which our Q3 2024 review identified as one of the two leading reimbursement friction points where AI decision support showed measurable potential, are constrained not by lack of available AI tools but by the integration requirements imposed by incumbent platform vendors who have a financial interest in keeping those workflows opaque.⏎⏎The Department asked in the RFI about barriers to AI adoption. The answers Carbon Health would prioritize:⏎⏎1. Pharmacy benefit managers and their data subsidiaries are capturing rents from the same clinical data flows that AI systems depend on. The RFI does not address this at all. PBM market concentration is not a tangential issue — it is a structural condition that shapes which AI applications are commercially viable and which are not, because PBMs control the formulary and prior authorization data environments that any clinically meaningful AI tool will need to access.⏎⏎2. Vendor lock-in through proprietary data formats and CRADA arrangements means that federal R&D investments in AI could flow directly into the balance sheets of companies like Epic and Oracle Health without producing any durable public benefit. Kesselheim, Cresswell, and Sheikh (2022) in BMJ Health & Care Informatics (29:1, e100556) identified this as one of the central governance failures in AI regulatory frameworks internationally.⏎⏎3. Algorithmic bias in commercially deployed tools remains inadequately addressed. Obermeyer et al. (2019) in Science (366:6464, 447-452) demonstrated that a widely deployed commercial algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients. Maddox, Rumsfeld, and Payne (2019) in JAMA (321:1, 31-32) identified the absence of post-deployment surveillance requirements as the specific policy gap that allows biased tools to remain in clinical use long after problems are identified.⏎⏎Carbon Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 3⏎⏎⏎WHAT THE DEPARTMENT SHOULD ACTUALLY DO⏎⏎The Department should establish independent evaluation infrastructure for AI tools used in clinical settings — infrastructure that is not funded through cooperative agreements with incumbent vendors, not governed by advisory bodies dominated by the same companies whose products are under review, and not structured to treat vendor-supplied validation data as sufficient evidence of clinical utility. This is not a complicated governance concept. It is the same logic that produced the FDA's premarket review process for medical devices, applied to software.⏎⏎The RFI's framing is itself a symptom of the capture problem. It asks how the Department can "accelerate adoption" as though speed is the variable that matters. What matters is whether the AI tools being adopted are accurate, equitable, and not extracting value from clinical relationships in ways that benefit platform vendors rather than patients. Carbon Health would support a precautionary standard requirement — modeled on the approach Kesselheim et al. (2022) described in their comparative analysis — that requires any AI tool receiving federal reimbursement support to undergo independent prospective evaluation before deployment, with results posted in a publicly accessible registry.⏎⏎The behavioral health documentation burden our 340 active LCSW clients' clinicians face is real. The prior authorization friction is real. But solving those problems by routing federal incentives through Epic's AI marketplace or through PBM-affiliated data brokers will not produce better care. It will produce a new layer of rent extraction wearing the label of innovation. The Department has been here before with Meaningful Use, and the literature is quite clear about how that turned out (Schulte & Fry, 2019; Adler-Milstein & Pfeifer, 2017). Carbon Health strongly urges the Department to condition any AI reimbursement or adoption incentive on demonstrated independence from incumbent EHR and PBM vendor relationships, with verification through a third-party audit process whose methodology is published and whose⏎⏎Carbon Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 4⏎⏎auditors are prohibited from holding financial relationships with the entities under review. The specific audit protocol design is something the Department would need to work through, and we recognize that is not a simple administrative task — but the absence of such a requirement in the current RFI framing is a gap that, if left unaddressed, will define the outcome regardless of what else the final guidance says about technical standards or interoperability.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0086♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔11♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Adams♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔Rural Health Information Hub♔♔♔♔♔♔Rural Health Information Hub⏎Comments on HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket ID: [HHS-AI-RFI-2024]⏎Submitted by: Mark Adams, Rural Health Information Hub⏎⏎---⏎⏎The Rural Health Information Hub submits these comments with a specific, documented concern that the current framing of this Request for Information systematically underweights what the evidence already tells us: AI tools deployed in clinical care settings perform inequitably across patient populations, and acceleration without mandatory equity safeguards will reproduce existing disparities at scale. We focus our response primarily on the RFI's questions regarding regulatory standards for clinical AI validation and reimbursement pathway design, because those two levers are where HHS has the clearest authority to prevent foreseeable harm and is currently proposing to exercise that authority without adequate equity conditions attached.⏎⏎Our organizational context is relevant here. The Rural Health Information Hub works extensively with community health networks across underserved geographies, and our ongoing collaboration with the Global Liver Institute's network of 47 liver disease centers of excellence has produced findings that speak directly to the adoption dynamics HHS is attempting to accelerate. GLI's 2024 landscape analysis found that 68% of those centers had piloted at least one AI-assisted diagnostic or care coordination tool within the preceding 24 months, yet fewer than 20% had successfully integrated those tools into sustained clinical workflows. That gap between piloting and sustained integration is not primarily a technology readiness problem. GLI's 2023 policy forum, convening hepatologists, transplant coordinators, and payers, identified reimbursement ambiguity and the absence of validated, liver-specific algorithmic standards as the dominant institutional obstacles. And GLI's annual patient needs assessment, drawing on structured input from more than 12,000 individuals living with chronic liver disease or hepatocellular carcinoma, found that delayed diagnosis and fragmented specialty referral remain the two most frequently cited systemic barriers to timely care. We raise these data points not as background color but as evidence of the specific patient population that will bear disproportionate risk if HHS accelerates AI adoption through reimbursement pathways that do not require demonstration of equitable performance across race, ethnicity, socioeconomic status, and care setting. Liver disease patients in rural and safety-net institutions are not hypothetical edge cases. They are the population for whom the failure modes of clinical AI are already documented and already harmful.⏎⏎The foundational problem is that historical healthcare data encodes decades of discriminatory treatment patterns, and AI systems trained on that data reproduce those patterns. This is not a theoretical concern. Obermeyer and colleagues demonstrated in a 2019 Science paper that a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to manage population health systematically underestimated the severity of illness in Black patients relative to white patients at equivalent levels of measured need, because the algorithm used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need — a proxy that reflects historical underinvestment in care for Black patients rather than actual clinical acuity (Obermeyer Z, Powers B, Vogeli C, Mullainathan S. Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations. Science. 2019;366(6464):447-453). The algorithm was not an outlier. Sjoding and colleagues published in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 that pulse oximeters — a device so basic it is used in every clinical setting in the country — systematically overestimate oxygen saturation in patients with dark skin pigmentation, meaning Black patients with occult hypoxemia were three times more likely than white patients to have that hypoxemia missed (Sjoding MW, Dickson RP, Iwashyna TJ, Gay SE, Valley TS. Racial bias in pulse oximetry measurement. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(25):2477-2478). Pulse oximetry is not an AI system, but the lesson is directly applicable: when a measurement tool is validated primarily on lighter-skinned populations and then deployed universally, the performance gap does not announce itself. Clinicians trust the device. And Adamson and Smith demonstrated in JAMA Dermatology in 2018 that machine learning algorithms for skin lesion classification were trained predominantly on images of lighter skin tones, producing diagnostic performance that degrades substantially for patients with darker skin — the patients who already face the greatest barriers to dermatologic care (Adamson AS, Smith A. Machine learning and health care disparities in dermatology. JAMA Dermatol. 2018;154(11):1247-1248). The pattern across these examples is consistent: tools are trained on populations that reflect who has historically had access to care, validated in institutions that reflect where resources have historically been concentrated, and then deployed universally in ways that make their differential performance invisible until the harm is already done.⏎⏎Rural Health Information Hub | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎For liver disease patients specifically, this pattern has direct clinical consequences that the RFI's acceleration framing does not reckon with. Hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance, fibrosis staging, and referral triage are precisely the clinical functions where AI tools are currently being piloted across GLI's network. These are also functions where algorithmic errors have asymmetric consequences: a missed HCC surveillance flag or a systematically underestimated fibrosis score for a patient who is already navigating a fragmented rural care pathway means a delayed diagnosis that, in liver disease, translates directly to stage at presentation and survival probability. Vyas and colleagues documented in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 that race-based corrections embedded in clinical algorithms — including algorithms used in nephrology and obstetrics — systematically direct Black patients away from specialist referral and toward less intensive care, and that these corrections are based on population-level genetic assumptions that do not hold at the individual level and that encode structural racism as biological fact (Vyas DA, Eisenstein LEI, Jones DS. Hidden in plain sight — reconsidering the use of race correction in clinical algorithms. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(9):874-882). The question HHS must answer before accelerating AI adoption in liver disease care is whether the tools being piloted across GLI's network have been audited for analogous race-based performance disparities. GLI's landscape data suggest the answer is no. Fewer than 20% of those 47 centers have achieved sustained integration, and the absence of validated liver-specific algorithmic standards was cited as a primary obstacle — which means the tools being piloted are being evaluated against general clinical AI standards that were not designed to detect liver-disease-specific performance gaps across patient populations defined by race, rurality, or insurance status.⏎⏎The second major failure mode this RFI does not adequately address is what the literature calls automation bias: the well-documented tendency of clinicians to defer to algorithmic outputs even when those outputs are wrong. This failure mode is particularly dangerous in under-resourced settings, because the conditions that make automation bias most likely — high clinical workload, limited time for deliberative review, institutional culture that treats AI as a quality improvement tool — are exactly the conditions that characterize the community hospitals and rural clinics where GLI's patient network receives care. Wong and colleagues conducted an external validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model across four Michigan Medicine hospitals in 2021 and found that the model's performance in their institution was substantially worse than reported in the original validation study, with poor positive predictive value and no significant association with improved sepsis outcomes (Wong A, Otles E, Donnelly JP, et al. External validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model in hospitalized patients. JAMA Intern Med. 2021;181(8):1065-1070). The model had been deployed, was generating alerts, and clinicians were responding to those alerts — but the alerts were not improving patient outcomes because the model was not performing as validated. This is the external validation problem, and Kaushal and colleagues documented its structural basis in a 2020 JAMA paper showing that the geographic distribution of cohorts used to train foundational deep learning algorithms is heavily concentrated in a small number of large academic medical centers, predominantly in the Northeast and California, with systematic underrepresentation of rural populations, safety-net hospital patients, and patients from the South and Midwest (Kaushal A, Altman R, Langlotz C. Geographic distribution of US cohorts used to train foundational deep learning algorithms. JAMA. 2020;324(12):1212-1213). A tool trained on patient data from Massachusetts General Hospital and UCSF does not generalize automatically to a critical access hospital in rural Mississippi or a federally qualified health center in western Massachusetts. The authoritative appearance of AI-generated clinical decision support outputs — a score, a risk flag, a recommended action — makes these generalization failures harder to detect and correct than ordinary human clinical error, because the error is attributed to the patient's condition rather than to the tool's performance gap.⏎⏎Gianfrancesco and colleagues identified several specific mechanisms through which EHR-derived training data introduces and amplifies bias in machine learning algorithms: differential documentation practices across care settings, missing data patterns that correlate with race and socioeconomic status, and the use of healthcare utilization as a proxy for clinical need — precisely the Obermeyer problem, reproduced across the broader literature (Gianfrancesco MA, Tamang S, Yazdany J, Schmajuk G. Potential biases in machine learning algorithms using electronic health record data. JAMA Intern Med. 2018;178(11):1544-1547). For liver disease patients in GLI's network, these mechanisms are not abstract. Rural patients with chronic liver disease have systematically thinner EHR records than urban academic medical center patients, not because they are healthier but because they have had less access to care. An AI fibrosis staging tool trained on dense longitudinal EHR data from academic hepatology programs will encounter missing data patterns in rural critical access hospital records that it was not trained to handle, and the resulting output degradation will be invisible unless the tool has been prospectively validated in care settings that match the deployment environment. The RFI asks about regulatory actions HHS can take to accelerate adoption. We are asking HHS to recognize that acceleration without mandatory site-specific and population-specific validation is not a neutral policy choice. It is a decision to deploy tools whose failure modes are known and documented, in settings where those failure modes will fall hardest on patients who are already receiving the least.⏎⏎Char and colleagues articulated the core ethical framework in a 2018 New England Journal of Medicine paper: machine learning tools in health care introduce novel ethical challenges around transparency, accountability, and the distribution of both benefits and harms, and those challenges require institutional mechanisms that do not currently exist in most deployment environments (Char DS, Shah NH, Magnus D. Implementing machine learning in health care — addressing ethical challenges. N Engl J Med. 2018;378(11):981-983). Wiens and colleagues, writing in Nature Medicine in 2019, proposed a roadmap for responsible ML in health care that includes prospective clinical validation, ongoing post-deployment monitoring, and explicit attention to the performance of models in subgroups defined by race, socioeconomic status, and care setting — and they noted that these requirements were not standard practice in the field at the time of writing (Wiens J, Saria S, Sendak M, et al. Do no harm: a roadmap for responsible machine learning for health care. Nat Med. 2019;25(9):1337-1340). That was 2019. GLI's 2024 landscape data suggest that five years later, with 68% of liver disease centers of excellence having piloted AI tools, those requirements are still not standard practice. The acceleration this RFI proposes will widen the deployment of tools that do not meet the standards Wiens and colleagues identified as minimally necessary, in care settings that have the least capacity to detect and respond to performance failures. Pierson and colleagues demonstrated in a 2021 Nature Medicine paper that algorithmic approaches can actually reduce unexplained pain disparities in underserved populations when the algorithms are explicitly designed and validated for equity — but that outcome requires the equity objective to be built into the design and validation process from the start, not added as an afterthought after deployment (Pierson E, Cutler DM, Leskovec J, Mullainathan S, Obermeyer Z. An algorithmic approach to reducing unexplained pain disparities in underserved populations. Nat Med. 2021;27(1):136-140). The Pierson paper is the model HHS should be following. It is not the model reflected in this RFI's framing.⏎⏎Rural Health Information Hub | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 3⏎⏎The National Academy for State Health Policy's 2023 policy brief on algorithmic bias in health care identified the absence of standardized equity auditing requirements as the single most consequential gap in current regulatory frameworks, and noted that states attempting to fill that gap through their own policy mechanisms face significant limitations without federal leadership and federal data access (National Academy for State Health Policy. Algorithmic Bias in Health Care: State Policy Landscape and Emerging Frameworks for Equity Auditing. NASHP Policy Brief. 2023). HHS has the authority and the data infrastructure to establish mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing as a condition of any reimbursement pathway or regulatory clearance for clinical AI tools. It has not done so. This RFI asks what regulatory and reimbursement actions HHS can take to accelerate AI adoption. Our answer is that the prior question — what equity conditions must be met before any tool enters a reimbursement pathway — has not been answered, and until it is, acceleration is the wrong objective. The specific conditions we are calling for are not complicated, though implementing them will require genuine regulatory commitment:⏎⏎1. Mandatory pre-deployment stratified performance validation: Any clinical AI tool seeking CMS reimbursement or FDA marketing authorization must demonstrate performance that meets minimum accuracy and equity thresholds when evaluated separately in patient subgroups defined by race, ethnicity, socioeconomic status, insurance status, and care setting type (academic medical center vs. community hospital vs. critical access hospital vs. federally qualified health center). Performance in the overall population is not sufficient. A tool that performs at 0.85 AUC in the aggregate but 0.67 AUC in Black patients at rural critical access hospitals fails the equity standard regardless of its aggregate performance.⏎⏎2. Mandatory post-deployment bias surveillance as a condition of continued reimbursement: Reimbursement for clinical AI tools must be conditioned on the submission of quarterly stratified performance data to a centralized HHS registry. Tools that demonstrate performance degradation in protected subgroups or in non-academic care settings must be flagged for mandatory re-validation before continued use. The registry data must be publicly accessible to researchers, patient advocates, and state health agencies.⏎⏎3. Liver-disease-specific algorithmic standards developed with explicit rural and safety-net representation: HHS, working with ASTP/ONC and in partnership with clinical specialty societies, must develop validated algorithmic performance standards for AI tools used in hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance, fibrosis staging, and specialty referral triage. Those standards must be developed using training and validation data that includes patients from rural care settings, safety-net institutions, and populations with historically limited healthcare access — not primarily from academic hepatology programs. GLI's network of 47 centers of excellence, with its documented patient base of more than 12,000 individuals, is available as a validation partner and we are stating that availability explicitly in this comment.⏎⏎4. Automation bias disclosure requirements: Any clinical AI tool deployed in a care setting must include in its cleared labeling explicit disclosure of the populations and care settings in which it has and has not been validated, with specific guidance for clinicians in non-validated deployment environments. The current FDA framework does not require this disclosure in a format that is accessible or meaningful at the point of clinical use. That needs to change.⏎⏎We recognize that these requirements will slow some deployments. We are not apologetic about that. GLI's patient needs assessment found that 12,000 patients with chronic liver disease and hepatocellular carcinoma identified delayed diagnosis and fragmented specialty referral as their primary systemic barriers. An AI tool that addresses those barriers for white patients at academic medical centers while performing invisibly worse for Black and rural patients at community hospitals does not solve the problem. It replicates the problem with an algorithmic imprimatur that makes it harder to challenge. HHS's accelerationist posture in this RFI, read against the documented literature on algorithmic bias and GLI's own landscape data, converges on a conclusion that is difficult to characterize charitably: the Department is proposing to move faster with less equity oversight in a domain where the evidence for disparate harm is already strong and already published. The communities that will bear the cost of that acceleration — rural patients, patients of color, patients at safety-net institutions — are the same communities that have borne the cost of every previous cycle of inadequately regulated health technology deployment.⏎⏎We also want to note, because it does not seem to be adequately represented in the RFI's framing, that the external validation problem documented by Wong and colleagues and the geographic training data concentration problem documented by Kaushal and colleagues are not problems that individual health systems can solve on their own. A rural critical access hospital in, say, Greenfield, Massachusetts does not have the data science capacity to conduct prospective external validation of a commercially deployed sepsis prediction model or a liver fibrosis staging algorithm. It does not have the staff to run ongoing post-deployment bias surveillance. It does not have the regulatory leverage to demand stratified performance data from a vendor whose tool is being reimbursed through a CMS pathway. The only entity with the authority and the data infrastructure to establish those requirements is HHS, and HHS is currently proposing to use that authority to accelerate deployment rather than to condition deployment on demonstrated equity. The NASHP 2023 brief documents the extent to which states are trying to fill this gap and running into the limits of their authority and data access. Federal action is not optional here. It is the only mechanism that reaches the full range of care settings where GLI's patients are receiving care and where the failure modes documented in the literature will materialize if deployment accelerates without equity conditions.⏎⏎One more thing that we think deserves direct statement: the framing of this RFI treats "acceleration" as the unambiguous objective and treats equity concerns as potential friction to be managed within an acceleration framework. That framing is backwards. For the patient population GLI represents — people with chronic liver disease and hepatocellular carcinoma, disproportionately located in rural and underserved communities, already navigating care systems that have historically underserved them — the question is not how fast AI tools can be deployed but whether those tools, when deployed, will perform equitably enough to actually close the gaps in delayed diagnosis and fragmented referral that GLI's patients have been reporting for years. The evidence, as of the date of this comment, says the tools currently being piloted do not meet that standard in the deployment environments where it matters most. The 68% pilot rate and the sub-20% sustained integration rate in GLI's network are not an argument for more acceleration. They are an argument for getting the equity conditions right before the next deployment cycle begins, and for HHS to use this RFI process to establish those conditions rather than to remove the friction that is, in many cases, the only thing standing between a poorly validated algorithm and a patient who cannot afford another misdiagnosis.⏎⏎References⏎⏎Obermeyer Z, Powers B, Vogeli C, Mullainathan S. Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations. Science. 2019;366(6464):447-453.⏎⏎Sjoding MW, Dickson RP, Iwashyna TJ, Gay SE, Valley TS. Racial bias in pulse oximetry measurement. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(25):2477-2478.⏎⏎Wong A, Otles E, Donnelly JP, et al. External validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model in hospitalized patients. JAMA Intern Med. 2021;181(8):1065-1070.⏎⏎Adamson AS, Smith A. Machine learning and health care disparities in dermatology. JAMA Dermatol. 2018;154(11):1247-1248.⏎⏎Char DS, Shah NH, Magnus D. Implementing machine learning in health care — addressing ethical challenges. N Engl J Med. 2018;378(11):981-983.⏎⏎Vyas DA, Eisenstein LEI, Jones DS. Hidden in plain sight — reconsidering the use of race correction in clinical algorithms. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(9):874-882.⏎⏎Gianfrancesco MA, Tamang S, Yazdany J, Schmajuk G. Potential biases in machine learning algorithms using electronic health record data. JAMA Intern Med. 2018;178(11):1544-1547.⏎⏎Pierson E, Cutler DM, Leskovec J, Mullainathan S, Obermeyer Z. An algorithmic approach to reducing unexplained pain disparities in underserved populations. Nat Med. 2021;27(1):136-140.⏎⏎Wiens J, Saria S, Sendak M, et al. Do no harm: a roadmap for responsible machine learning for health care. Nat Med. 2019;25(9):1337-1340.⏎⏎Kaushal A, Altman R, Langlotz C. Geographic distribution of US cohorts used to train foundational deep learning algorithms. JAMA. 2020;324(12):1212-1213.⏎⏎National Academy for State Health Policy. Algorithmic Bias in Health Care: State Policy Landscape and Emerging Frameworks for Equity Auditing. NASHP Policy Brief. 2023.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0087♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Wright♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My concern with this whole effort is pretty specific, and I want to make sure it doesn't get lost in whatever big-picture framework HHS is building here. I work as a librarian in Alabama, so I'm not a clinician, but I've spent enough time watching people try to navigate health information to know that a tool can look elegant from the outside and be genuinely exhausting to use from the inside. A colleague of mine spent two winters ago going between three different specialists who weren't sharing records, trying to make sense of printed discharge summaries and a portal that wouldn't load on her phone. I know that's exactly the kind of coordination gap AI is supposed to solve. I'm not opposed to that. What I'm worried about is how these tools get approved and certified, because right now it seems like that process runs mostly through hospital administrators and vendors, not through the nurses and medical assistants and care coordinators who will actually be clicking through the interface at 6am on a Tuesday.⏎⏎A tool that performs well on a dashboard metric can still make a clinician's actual workday worse. I don't think that's a small concern. If HHS is going to create any kind of certification or approval pathway for AI in clinical settings, worker input has to be a formal requirement, not something that gets gathered informally through a vendor's own user testing. I'm genuinely not sure what the right mechanism looks like, but requiring documented feedback from frontline clinical staff before any tool gets credentialed seems like the minimum.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0088♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Ramirez♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔National Medical Association♔♔♔♔♔♔NATIONAL MEDICAL ASSOCIATION⏎Comments in Response to HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: Amanda Ramirez, Senior Policy Analyst⏎National Medical Association⏎Date of Submission: 2025⏎⏎⏎I. ORGANIZATIONAL STANDING AND EVIDENTIARY BASIS⏎⏎The National Medical Association submits these comments in response to the Department's Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. NMA's membership includes a substantial proportion of physicians practicing in underserved, safety-net, and minority-serving institutions — settings that the Department's RFI conspicuously fails to name as a primary design consideration. Before addressing the specific question of what regulatory and reimbursement actions HHS should take, NMA must establish why the framing of this RFI is itself a problem.⏎⏎The RFI asks how to accelerate adoption. That is the wrong first question. The correct first question is: adoption under what conditions, for which populations, with what mandatory safeguards against documented harms? The peer-reviewed literature does not treat algorithmic bias in clinical AI as a theoretical concern or a future risk. It is an observed, measured, replicated pattern of harm — and HHS has structured an RFI that does not once condition deployment pathways on mandatory equity auditing or disaggregated performance reporting. That omission is not an oversight. It is a structural choice, and NMA objects to it directly.⏎⏎NMA acknowledges that AI tools offer genuine potential efficiencies in documentation, prior authorization processing, and certain diagnostic support functions. The organization is not categorically opposed to AI in clinical settings. What NMA is categorically opposed to is a federal acceleration strategy that treats the existing peer-reviewed evidence on algorithmic discrimination as background noise rather than a design constraint.⏎⏎NMA | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 1⏎⏎⏎II. THE EQUITY EVIDENCE BASE IS NOT AMBIGUOUS⏎⏎The Department should not require persuasion on the basic empirical picture. Obermeyer et al. (Science, 2019;366(6464):447-453) documented that a commercial algorithm used to manage population health systematically allocated fewer health care resources to Black patients at equivalent illness burden — not because the algorithm was designed to discriminate, but because it used health care cost as a proxy for health need, and Black patients with equivalent illness had historically received less care. That is an example of bias baked into training data propagating into clinical resource allocation at scale. Sjoding et al. (N Engl J Med, 2020;383(25):2477-2478) found that pulse oximeters — devices now integrated into AI-assisted monitoring systems across hospital settings — systematically overestimated oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin tones, meaning those patients received delayed intervention for occult hypoxemia at measurable rates. This is not a modeling problem. It is a measurement problem that AI systems inherit and amplify when they are trained on physiologic data collected with racially biased instruments.⏎⏎Adamson and Smith (JAMA Dermatol, 2018;154(11):1247-1248) documented that dermatology AI systems trained predominantly on lighter-skinned patient images demonstrated substantially degraded performance on darker skin tones — a finding with direct implications for melanoma detection in communities of color. Wong et al. (JAMA Intern Med, 2021;181(8):1065-1070) conducted external validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model and found that its performance in community hospital settings diverged substantially from the academic medical center performance metrics used to market it. The model's sensitivity and positive predictive value dropped in ways that were not disclosed to purchasing institutions. Gianfrancesco et al. (JAMA Intern Med, 2018;178(11):1544-1547) identified multiple pathways through which electronic health record data — the substrate on which clinical AI is trained — systematically encodes socioeconomic and racial disparities in prior care access, producing models that replicate rather than correct those disparities.⏎⏎NMA | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 2⏎⏎Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones (N Engl J Med, 2020;383(9):874-882) documented that race-corrected clinical algorithms — including those embedded in kidney function calculators and spirometry interpretation tools — systematically direct Black patients toward less aggressive treatment, with documented consequences for transplant eligibility and pulmonary disease management. Pierson et al. (Nat Med, 2021;27(1):136-140) found that algorithmic approaches to pain management in underserved populations can reduce disparities when equity is an explicit design objective — but that this requires intentional intervention, not passive deployment. The literature is not ambiguous. HHS has access to all of these studies. The RFI's failure to incorporate them as binding design constraints is a policy choice, not an information gap.⏎⏎⏎III. THE AUTOMATION BIAS PROBLEM IS UNDERWEIGHTED IN THIS RFI⏎⏎One mechanism that the RFI does not adequately address is automation bias — the well-documented tendency of clinicians to defer to algorithmic outputs even when those outputs conflict with clinical judgment. Sendak et al. (Proc ACM Conf Fairness Accountability Transparency, 2020:99-109) documented that clinicians interpreting AI-generated outputs frequently treated them as more authoritative than equivalent human assessments, even when they lacked the ability to interrogate the model's reasoning. The practical consequence is that AI errors are harder to catch than equivalent human errors, because the authoritative presentation of algorithmic output suppresses the skepticism that would otherwise trigger a second look.⏎⏎This matters for equity in a specific way. If an AI sepsis prediction model performs worse on Black patients than white patients at the same hospital, and if clinicians treat its outputs as authoritative, then the model's underperformance for Black patients will systematically go undetected at the point of care. The clinician who might have caught a delayed sepsis alert based on clinical gestalt is instead reassured by the model's output. The error is invisible until it appears in outcome data — and only if someone is disaggregating that outcome data by race, which most institutions are not doing. Char, Shah, and Magnus (N Engl J Med, 2018;378(11):981-983) identified this mechanism as one of the primary ethical challenges in clinical AI implementation, noting that the opacity of model reasoning compounds the automation bias problem by making it structurally difficult for clinicians to know when to override.⏎⏎NMA | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 3⏎⏎Wiens et al. (Nat Med, 2019;25(9):1337-1340) proposed a responsible machine learning roadmap that included prospective monitoring of model outputs disaggregated by patient subgroup as a minimum deployment standard. The roadmap was published in 2019. Six years later, HHS is drafting an acceleration RFI that does not include prospective subgroup monitoring as a condition of any deployment pathway. That is a significant gap.⏎⏎⏎IV. THE VALIDATION GENERALIZABILITY PROBLEM DIRECTLY AFFECTS NMA'S MEMBERSHIP⏎⏎Kaushal, Altman, and Langlotz (JAMA, 2020;324(12):1212-1213) analyzed the geographic distribution of patient cohorts used to train foundational deep learning algorithms and found that training data was overwhelmingly concentrated in a small number of large academic medical centers, predominantly in the Northeast and West Coast. Community hospitals, rural health systems, federally qualified health centers, and safety-net institutions — the settings where a substantial proportion of NMA members practice — were systematically underrepresented in the training populations for tools now being marketed to those very institutions.⏎⏎NMA | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 4⏎⏎This is not a minor technical caveat. A sepsis prediction model trained primarily on patient populations at large urban academic medical centers may perform adequately in those settings and perform poorly in a rural Arizona community hospital serving a predominantly Hispanic patient population, or in a safety-net institution in a Texas border county where the patient mix, comorbidity patterns, and prior care access histories differ substantially from the training population. The National Academy for State Health Policy documented in its March 2023 policy brief on algorithmic accountability in state health programs that equity audit requirements for AI tools deployed in Medicaid and other public programs remain inconsistent and largely voluntary across states, meaning that performance degradation in high-need settings frequently goes unmeasured (NASHP Policy Brief, March 2023).⏎⏎The practical implication is that the institutions least equipped to conduct independent performance validation — community hospitals with limited informatics staff, rural health systems without dedicated data science capacity, safety-net institutions operating on thin margins — are also the institutions most likely to be deploying AI tools whose performance has not been validated in anything resembling their patient population. HHS's proposed acceleration framework does nothing to address this asymmetry.⏎⏎The following conditions characterize the deployment environment for AI tools in the settings where NMA members predominantly practice, and each represents a distinct equity risk that the RFI fails to address:⏎⏎1. Training data underrepresentation: Models trained on academic medical center cohorts are deployed without site-specific validation in community settings with different patient demographics (Kaushal et al., JAMA, 2020;324(12):1212-1213).⏎⏎2. Measurement instrument bias: Physiologic monitoring devices with documented racial measurement error are used as data inputs for AI systems, propagating that error into algorithmic outputs (Sjoding et al., N Engl J Med, 2020;383(25):2477-2478).⏎⏎3. Absence of disaggregated performance monitoring: Post-deployment outcome monitoring is rarely stratified by race, ethnicity, or socioeconomic status, meaning subgroup performance degradation is invisible in aggregate metrics.⏎⏎4. Resource asymmetry in validation capacity: The institutions serving the highest-risk populations have the least capacity to conduct independent performance audits of the tools they are purchasing.⏎⏎5. Proprietary opacity: Vendors frequently decline to disclose training data composition, model architecture, or subgroup performance statistics, citing trade secret protections (Wiens et al., Nat Med, 2019;25(9):1337-1340).⏎⏎NMA | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 5⏎⏎⏎V. SPECIFIC REGULATORY DEMANDS⏎⏎NMA addresses specifically the RFI's questions regarding regulatory actions HHS can take to support responsible AI deployment. The organization does not address reimbursement structuring in this comment, as that is the subject of a separate NMA submission.⏎⏎HHS must condition any deployment pathway on mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing. This means that before an AI clinical decision support tool is eligible for Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement, the developer must provide disaggregated performance data — sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value, and negative predictive value — stratified by race, ethnicity, primary language, socioeconomic status proxy, and geography. The stratification categories should align with OMB's revised race and ethnicity standards and should include rural versus urban and safety-net versus non-safety-net deployment settings. This is not a novel demand; it mirrors requirements that FDA has begun applying to certain AI-enabled medical devices under its predetermined change control plan framework, and HHS has both the statutory authority and the empirical justification to extend equivalent requirements to clinical decision support tools that fall outside FDA's current device jurisdiction.⏎⏎HHS must require continuous post-deployment bias surveillance with public reporting. Pre-deployment auditing is necessary but not sufficient. Model performance can degrade over time as patient populations shift, as EHR documentation practices change, or as the clinical context for which a model was validated evolves. Wiens et al. (Nat Med, 2019;25(9):1337-1340) identified prospective post-deployment monitoring as a minimum responsible deployment standard. HHS should require that vendors and deploying institutions submit annual disaggregated performance reports to a publicly accessible registry, with defined thresholds for performance degradation that trigger mandatory notification to deploying institutions and, where patient harm is plausible, to affected patients. The registry should be searchable by institution type, patient population, and clinical application.⏎⏎NMA | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 6⏎⏎HHS must address proprietary opacity as a barrier to equity auditing. The current framework allows vendors to withhold training data composition and subgroup performance statistics under trade secret protections, which makes independent equity auditing structurally impossible. HHS should establish that receipt of Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement for AI-assisted clinical services constitutes acceptance of a transparency condition: vendors must disclose, at minimum, the demographic composition of training and validation cohorts, the geographic distribution of training institutions, and subgroup performance statistics for all protected characteristics covered under the Civil Rights Act and Section 1557 of the Affordable Care Act. Char, Shah, and Magnus (N Engl J Med, 2018;378(11):981-983) specifically identified proprietary opacity as an ethical barrier to responsible AI deployment; HHS has the contracting leverage through Medicare and Medicaid to overcome that barrier where FDA jurisdiction does not reach.⏎⏎The absence of these three conditions from HHS's RFI framing — mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing, continuous post-deployment bias surveillance, and transparency conditions tied to public program reimbursement — is the central structural deficiency NMA is identifying in this submission. Each of these conditions has a documented evidentiary basis in peer-reviewed literature spanning at least 2018 to 2021. The Department has had access to this literature for years. An acceleration framework that does not incorporate these conditions as baseline requirements is not a neutral policy choice; it is a decision to accelerate deployment into vulnerable populations without the safeguards the evidence base says are warranted.⏎⏎NMA | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 7⏎⏎NMA requests that HHS publish a supplemental RFI or interim guidance document specifically addressing equity audit requirements for clinical AI, with a comment period of no fewer than 90 days, before finalizing any acceleration framework. The 60-day comment period for this RFI was insufficient for the level of technical specificity the equity questions require, and the RFI's framing did not signal to commenters that equity auditing was a primary subject of inquiry — which means the Department will receive fewer comments on this topic than the policy stakes warrant. NMA also requests that HHS convene a technical expert panel on AI equity auditing methodology that includes representation from safety-net institutions, community health centers, and minority-serving hospitals, not only from academic medical centers and large health systems whose patient populations do not represent the settings where equity risks are⏎⏎NMA | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 8⏎⏎amanda.ramirez@nmanet.org⏎www.nmanet.org/policy/health-IT♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0089♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Nguyen♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔American Association of Neurological Surgeons♔♔♔♔♔♔AMERICAN ASSOCIATION OF NEUROLOGICAL SURGEONS⏎Comments on HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Docket No. ONC-2024-AI-RFI⏎Submitted by: Richard Nguyen, Policy Director⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI | ONC-2024 | Page 1⏎⏎THE GAP BETWEEN DEPLOYMENT PACE AND WORKER PROTECTION⏎⏎The American Association of Neurological Surgeons (AANS) submits these comments in response to the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology's Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings (ONC, 2024). The AANS directs the Department's attention specifically to the workforce implications of the RFI's accelerationist framing, which in our assessment treats the speed of AI deployment as a policy objective largely independent of the human infrastructure on which safe clinical care depends. Survey data collected by the Association of periOperative Registered Nurses in 2024 found that 58% of respondents worked in facilities that had piloted or fully deployed at least one AI-assisted surgical tool, yet 74% of those same respondents reported that their facilities lacked standardized competency frameworks for validating the safe clinical use of those technologies (AORN, 2024). That gap between deployment and protection is not a transitional inefficiency that will self-correct as adoption matures. It is a structural product of procurement processes that systematically exclude the frontline workers who bear the operational consequences of implementation decisions. The AORN's 2023 Perioperative Workforce Report documented that 61% of respondents cited insufficient institutional guidance on liability and scope-of-practice boundaries as a primary barrier to confident AI adoption in the perioperative environment (AORN, 2023). These are not expressions of technophobia. They are documented institutional failures, and HHS's current framing of this RFI does not address them with the specificity they require. The Government Accountability Office reached a parallel conclusion in its 2023 review of AI in health care, finding that implementation challenges consistently outpaced governance frameworks and that workforce consultation mechanisms were largely absent from hospital-level procurement processes (GAO, 2023). The AANS finds it notable that the RFI's framing positions AI adoption primarily as a clinical efficiency and burnout-reduction opportunity without substantively engaging the literature on how prior waves of health information technology deployment have interacted with physician and nursing workload. Melnick and colleagues found in 2020 that perceived EHR usability was inversely associated with professional burnout among U.S. physicians, a finding that should complicate any assumption that AI tools marketed as efficiency solutions will automatically reduce cognitive burden rather than redistribute it onto frontline workers in new forms (Melnick et al., 2020). Shanafelt and colleagues documented that physician burnout and dissatisfaction with work-life integration worsened significantly between 2011 and 2020 across a period that coincided with accelerated health IT deployment (Shanafelt et al., 2022). The pattern is consistent enough that HHS should treat the burnout-reduction promise embedded in this RFI as a hypothesis requiring prospective⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI | ONC-2024 | Page 2⏎⏎evidence, not as a premise on which to build regulatory posture.⏎⏎THE STRUCTURAL INCENTIVE PROBLEM AND WORKFORCE HARM⏎⏎The more fundamental concern the AANS wishes to place before the Department involves the structural divergence between the financial interests of AI vendors and hospital administrators on one side, and the professional autonomy and working conditions of frontline clinical staff on the other. AI tools marketed to health system leadership as productivity optimization platforms are, by design, built to generate data that can be used to justify staffing adjustments, increased caseload targets, and the elimination of judgment-intensive roles that are difficult to measure through throughput metrics. Kellogg, Valentine, and Christin documented in 2020 that algorithmic management tools in professional settings systematically shift the locus of control away from workers and toward managerial and vendor-defined performance parameters, with particular erosion of contextual judgment roles that are central to professional expertise (Kellogg et al., 2020). Strickler and Bhatt's 2023 analysis of multi-site health system deployments found direct associations between algorithmic scheduling implementation and measurable reductions in nursing judgment latitude, with administrators in several systems explicitly citing AI-generated productivity data as justification for staffing ratio changes (Strickler & Bhatt, 2023). The AANS regards this as a patient safety problem, not merely a labor relations concern. Clinical expertise is not a variable to be optimized out of care delivery; it is the mechanism through which contextual patient information gets translated into safe intraoperative and perioperative decisions. When AI procurement excludes frontline workers from meaningful consultation, and when AI-generated productivity metrics are used to reduce staffing without independent clinical review, the result is degraded professional autonomy that ultimately reaches patients. Accenture Federal Services and the American Hospital Association found in 2023 that workforce consultation was absent from the majority of hospital-level AI procurement processes surveyed, a finding consistent with what AORN member data documents at the facility level (Accenture/AHA, 2023). The AANS also notes that algorithmic bias in clinical decision support tools is a documented risk, not a theoretical one; Obermeyer and colleagues identified in 2019 that a widely deployed population health algorithm systematically underestimated the care needs of Black patients due to the use of cost as a proxy for health need (Obermeyer et al., 2019), and Bates and colleagues argued in 2021 that implementation science frameworks for machine learning interventions must include prospective workforce and equity impact assessment as standard requirements (Bates et al., 2021). The AANS therefore requests that HHS require, as conditions of any AI adoption support program, mandatory frontline worker consultation in procurement decisions, pre-deployment workforce impact assessments conducted by parties independent of the contracting vendor, and explicit prohibition on using AI-generated productivity data to justify staffing reductions absent independent clinical review. National Nurses United's 2023 policy framework articulated similar requirements and the AANS endorses that framework's core governance recommendations (NNU, 2023). The Department should be aware that the AANS intends to submit supplemental comments addressing neurological surgery-specific implementation questions, including intraoperative imaging AI and the liability questions that 61% of perioperative nurses in the AORN survey identified as unresolved. Those comments are being coordinated with member input collected through the AANS Annual Scientific Meeting scheduled for April 2025 in Philadelphia, and the timeline for⏎⏎REFERENCES⏎⏎AORN. (2024). AORN Member Survey on AI-Assisted Surgical Tools: Perioperative Nursing Workforce Readiness Report. Association of periOperative Registered Nurses.⏎⏎AORN. (2023). Perioperative Workforce Report: Staffing, Scope of Practice, and Emerging Technology Challenges. Association of periOperative Registered Nurses.⏎⏎Accenture Federal Services / American Hospital Association. (2023). AI Adoption in U.S. Hospitals: Procurement Patterns, Workforce Consultation Gaps, and Implementation Outcomes. AHA Research Series.⏎⏎Bates, D. W., Auerbach, A., Schulam, P., Wright, A., & Saria, S. (2021). Reporting and implementing interventions involving machine learning and artificial intelligence. Annals of Internal Medicine, 174(1), 137–144.⏎⏎Government Accountability Office. (2023). Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Benefits and Challenges of Technologies to Augment Patient Care (GAO-23-105980). U.S. Government Accountability Office.⏎⏎Kellogg, K. C., Valentine, M. A., & Christin, A. (2020). Algorithms at work: The new contested terrain of control. Academy of Management Annals, 14(1), 366–410.⏎⏎Melnick, E. R., Dyrbye, L. N., Sinsky, C. A., et al. (2020). The association between perceived electronic health record usability and professional burnout among US physicians. Mayo Clinic Proceedings, 95(3), 476–487.⏎⏎National Nurses United. (2023). Protect Nurses, Protect Patients: A Policy Framework for AI Governance in Hospital Settings. NNU Research Department.⏎⏎Obermeyer, Z., Powers, B., Vogeli, C., & Mullainathan, S. (2019). Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations. Science, 366(6464), 447–453.⏎⏎ONC. (2024). Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care. Federal Register, 89 FR [docket reference].⏎⏎Shanafelt, T. D., West, C. P., Dyrbye, L. N., et al. (2022). Changes in burnout and satisfaction with work-life integration in physicians and the general US working population between 2011 and 2020. Mayo Clinic Proceedings, 97(3), 491–506.⏎⏎Strickler, J., & Bhatt, J. (2023). Staffing ratios, algorithmic scheduling, and the erosion of nursing judgment: Evidence from multi-site health system deployments. Journal of Nursing Administration, 53(7–8), 389–397.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0090♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Nelson♔♔California♔♔United States♔University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill Gillings School of Global Public Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Logos Research Centre⏎Gillings School of Global Public Health⏎University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill⏎135 Dauer Drive, Chapel Hill, NC 27599⏎⏎Donna Nelson, Assistant Professor⏎Contact: dnelson@logos-research.unc.edu⏎⏎September 12, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎Logos Research Centre | HHS AI RFI | Gillings School of Global Public Health | Page 1⏎⏎⏎THE ACCOUNTABILITY PROBLEM AS STRUCTURAL DEFECT⏎⏎The Department's request for information proceeds from a premise that the Logos Research Centre believes warrants direct interrogation before any specific acceleration measures are adopted: that the principal barriers to AI deployment in clinical care are primarily logistical, reimbursement-related, or regulatory in the conventional clearance sense, and that targeted policy action on those barriers will produce safe and responsible clinical integration at scale. The Centre's own longitudinal research complicates this premise in ways that have direct bearing on the policy questions HHS is now considering.⏎⏎Over the past four years, Logos Research Centre has conducted a longitudinal analysis of twelve AI-in-medicine pilot projects spanning radiology, pathology, and clinical decision support (Logos Research Centre, 2024). The findings from that analysis, together with a forthcoming white paper synthesizing evidence from more than 200 peer-reviewed implementation studies, consistently document something that the RFI does not squarely address: that the diffusion of decisional responsibility across heterogeneous institutional actors — clinicians, hospital systems, third-party vendors, and software developers — constitutes a structural feature of current AI deployment models, not an incidental compliance gap. In nine of the twelve cases examined, documentation and liability ambiguity were cited as compounding factors in adoption delays, with a median delay of twenty-two months observed between regulatory clearance and actual clinical use. Reimbursement uncertainty was identified as the primary barrier in the broader survey data, with 83% of the 47 faculty members and affiliated clinical researchers surveyed in 2024 naming it as such. But the Centre's qualitative findings make plain that liability ambiguity functions as a secondary barrier that interacts with reimbursement uncertainty in ways that the RFI's framing does not fully account for.⏎⏎The concern the Centre wishes to register most directly is this: HHS is proposing to accelerate the adoption of systems whose deployment architecture systematically dissolves the identifiable human decision-maker that existing medical malpractice doctrine requires, and it is proposing to do so without first establishing the legal infrastructure necessary to assign responsibility when those systems cause harm. This is not a concern about AI performance in the abstract. It is a concern about what happens when a well-performing system fails in a specific clinical encounter, a specific patient is harmed, and the institutional actors surrounding that encounter — the treating physician, the hospital, the software vendor — each have plausible grounds to deflect responsibility to the others.⏎⏎⏎Logos Research Centre | HHS AI RFI | Gillings School of Global Public Health | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE LIABILITY VACUUM AND ITS CLINICAL CONSEQUENCES⏎⏎Medical malpractice doctrine, as it currently operates across state jurisdictions, is organized around the identification of a responsible professional who exercised, or failed to exercise, a standard of care in relation to a patient (Mello, Frakes, Blumenkranz, & Studdert, 2020, JAMA, 323(4), 352–366). The standard of care concept presupposes a human actor whose judgment can be evaluated against a professional norm. When an AI system intermediates that judgment — recommending a course of action that the clinician follows, or flagging an anomaly that the clinician credits — the question of whose judgment is being evaluated becomes genuinely difficult to answer. Price, Gerke, and Cohen identified this problem with precision in 2019, observing that physicians who rely on AI-generated recommendations may face liability for failing to exercise independent judgment, even as vendors are insulated from malpractice claims by product liability doctrines that were not designed with clinical AI in mind (Price, Gerke, & Cohen, 2019, JAMA, 322(18), 1765–1766). The result is a liability structure in which responsibility is simultaneously diffuse and contested — a condition that rational institutional actors respond to by avoiding documentation, limiting deployment scope, and negotiating contractual indemnifications that further obscure the chain of accountability.⏎⏎Hoffman and Podgurski described this dynamic as an accountability gap that existing tort law is structurally ill-equipped to close, arguing that legislative or regulatory intervention is necessary to assign clear, non-delegable responsibility to a specific party for AI-assisted clinical decisions (Hoffman & Podgurski, 2023, Journal of Law and the Biosciences, 10(1), lsad007). The Centre's implementation data supports this assessment. In several of the pilot projects examined, clinical staff reported that uncertainty about who would bear liability for an AI-assisted decision that caused harm was a more immediate operational concern than the technical performance of the system itself. That finding has a direct implication for the RFI: accelerating adoption without resolving the liability question does not remove the barrier; it embeds the unresolved question more deeply into clinical practice, where it will surface, eventually, in litigation rather than in policy.⏎⏎Gerke, Minssen, and Cohen have documented the ways in which the ethical and legal challenges of AI-driven healthcare are compounded by the absence of harmonized regulatory frameworks that assign responsibility clearly across the development-to-deployment chain (Gerke, Minssen, & Cohen, 2020, in Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare, Academic Press, pp. 295–336). The Centre notes that the RFI's framing of regulatory barriers as obstacles to be reduced, rather than as partially functional proxies for accountability obligations that have not yet been given more precise legal form, risks treating the symptom while the underlying condition goes unaddressed.⏎⏎⏎Logos Research Centre | HHS AI RFI | Gillings School of Global Public Health | Page 3⏎⏎⏎PATH DEPENDENCY AND THE INSULATION OF POST-DEPLOYMENT ACCOUNTABILITY⏎⏎The Centre's second major concern is less about the moment of initial deployment and more about what happens to accountability as AI systems become embedded in clinical workflows over time. Topol's analysis of high-performance medicine noted that the integration of AI into clinical practice is likely to produce collaborative intelligence arrangements in which human and machine contributions to clinical decisions become increasingly difficult to disaggregate (Topol, 2019, Nature Medicine, 25(1), 44–56). This is, in many respects, the intended design of these systems. But the consequence of successful integration is that the institutional dependencies created by embedded AI systems make it progressively more difficult to audit, challenge, or remove those systems even when documented harms accumulate.⏎⏎Sendak and colleagues documented a related dynamic in their ethnographic study of clinical decision support deployment, finding that clinicians often experienced AI recommendations as institutional facts rather than as revisable suggestions — a condition they described as the system becoming part of the clinical environment rather than a tool within it (Sendak et al., 2020, ACM FAT* Proceedings, pp. 99–109). Once a system achieves that level of institutional embeddedness, the practical capacity of hospitals, clinicians, or regulators to hold vendors accountable for post-deployment performance degradation is substantially diminished. Contracts negotiated at the point of deployment typically do not include enforceable performance benchmarks tied to ongoing patient outcome data. Vendors retain proprietary control over model architectures and training data. And the clinical staff who might otherwise raise concerns about system performance are operating in environments where the system's recommendations have become a default reference point.⏎⏎Pasquale has argued that this dynamic is not incidental to the commercial AI deployment model but is a predictable consequence of the incentive structures that govern it, and that the appropriate regulatory response is precautionary governance that conditions deployment on the prior establishment of accountability mechanisms rather than attempting to retrofit accountability after integration has occurred (Pasquale, 2020, New Laws of Robotics, Harvard University Press). The Centre finds this argument persuasive on the basis of its own implementation research. The question of whether a given AI system performs adequately in a given clinical context is not separable from the question of who is responsible for monitoring that performance, who has access to the data necessary to evaluate it, and what institutional mechanisms exist to translate documented performance failures into actionable accountability.⏎⏎Doshi-Velez and colleagues have argued that meaningful accountability for AI systems requires not merely explanatory transparency at the point of decision but institutional arrangements that ensure explanation can be demanded, evaluated, and acted upon by parties with both the authority and the information necessary to do so (Doshi-Velez et al., 2017, Berkman Klein Center Research Publication No. 2017-19). The current deployment environment does not reliably provide those arrangements. The Centre's pilot project data suggests that in the majority of cases examined, neither the hospital system nor the treating clinicians had contractual or regulatory mechanisms available to them that would have allowed systematic post-deployment performance review linked to patient outcome data.⏎⏎⏎Logos Research Centre | HHS AI RFI | Gillings School of Global Public Health | Page 4⏎⏎⏎WHAT THE EVIDENCE FROM IMPLEMENTATION RESEARCH REQUIRES⏎⏎The Centre is not opposed to the adoption of AI in clinical care. The potential for well-validated AI tools to improve diagnostic accuracy, reduce clinician cognitive burden, and extend specialist-level judgment to underserved settings is documented and real (Obermeyer & Emanuel, 2016, New England Journal of Medicine, 375(13), 1216–1219). Char, Shah, and Magnus identified the conditions under which machine learning implementation in healthcare can be done in ways that respect ethical obligations to patients, and the Centre's own research is oriented toward identifying the institutional and policy conditions that make responsible implementation achievable (Char, Shah, & Magnus, 2018, New England Journal of Medicine, 378(11), 981–983). The concern being registered here is not with AI adoption per se but with the sequencing of the policy actions HHS is considering.⏎⏎The specific questions the RFI poses regarding reimbursement pathways, regulatory streamlining, and research investment are not without merit as discrete policy considerations. Cohen and colleagues identified the legal and ethical concerns arising from complex predictive analytics in healthcare as early as 2014, and the intervening decade has not resolved the foundational accountability questions they raised; if anything, the commercial scaling of clinical AI has made those questions more operationally pressing (Cohen, Amarasingham, Shah, Xie, & Lo, 2014, Health Affairs, 33(7), 1139–1147). The Centre's position is that the following conditions must be established before HHS pursues accelerated adoption on any of the dimensions the RFI contemplates:⏎⏎1. A mandatory, legally operative framework that assigns non-delegable responsibility to a specific human or institutional party for every AI-assisted clinical decision that results in patient harm. The framework must specify the responsible party at the point of deployment, not leave the question to post-hoc contractual negotiation between hospitals and vendors.⏎⏎2. Standardized post-deployment monitoring obligations, enforceable as a condition of continued reimbursement eligibility, requiring that AI systems used in reimbursed clinical encounters be subject to ongoing performance review against patient outcome data accessible to the responsible institutional party identified in condition one.⏎⏎3. A regulatory mechanism — whether housed in ONC, CMS, or a newly designated accountability office — with both the authority and the technical capacity to receive, evaluate, and act on documented reports of AI-assisted clinical harms, including the authority to condition or withdraw reimbursement eligibility for systems with documented performance problems.⏎⏎4. Minimum disclosure requirements ensuring that patients whose clinical care involved an AI-assisted decision are informed of that fact in their clinical documentation, creating the evidentiary foundation necessary for accountability claims to be pursued when harms occur.⏎⏎The Centre acknowledges that these conditions represent a more demanding precondition for acceleration than the RFI's current framing contemplates. The Centre also acknowledges, with some candor, that its own implementation research documents the real costs of delayed adoption — the twenty-two month median delay between clearance and clinical use represents a period during which patients who might have benefited from validated tools did not receive that benefit. That cost is not trivial, and the Centre does not dismiss it.⏎⏎⏎Logos Research Centre | HHS AI RFI | Gillings School of Global Public Health | Page 5⏎⏎⏎REIMBURSEMENT AND ACCOUNTABILITY AS INTERDEPENDENT POLICY LEVERS⏎⏎The RFI treats reimbursement pathway reform as a largely independent policy lever — one that can be advanced on its own timeline to reduce adoption barriers without waiting for the resolution of broader accountability questions. The Centre's research suggests this framing is mistaken, and that reimbursement policy and accountability policy are more interdependent than the RFI acknowledges. Specifically, the creation of reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted clinical services without simultaneous establishment of accountability conditions for those services will accelerate adoption in ways that further insulate vendors from post-deployment accountability. Once a reimbursement pathway exists, the commercial and institutional incentives to maintain it are substantial. Hospitals that have restructured workflows around reimbursable AI-assisted services will resist the subsequent imposition of accountability conditions that might disrupt those workflows or impose compliance costs. The Centre's pilot project data includes at least three cases in which the existence of a contractual reimbursement arrangement between a hospital and a vendor was cited by hospital administrators as a reason that concerns about system performance raised by clinical staff could not be acted upon within the existing contract term.⏎⏎This is not a hypothetical risk. It is a pattern the Centre has observed in the transition from proof-of-concept deployment to institutionally embedded clinical practice, and it is a pattern that the RFI's framing of reimbursement reform as an acceleration mechanism, rather than as a policy instrument that must be conditioned on accountability requirements, is likely to reproduce at larger scale. The Centre therefore recommends that HHS condition the development of any new reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted clinical services on the prior or concurrent establishment of the accountability framework conditions enumerated above, and that any interim reimbursement arrangements adopted before a comprehensive accountability framework is in place include explicit sunset provisions and performance-linked eligibility criteria that can be enforced by the responsible regulatory authority.⏎⏎The Centre recognizes that this recommendation will be read by some stakeholders as an argument for delay. That characterization misreads the Centre's position. The argument is that acceleration without accountability infrastructure is not, in fact, acceleration toward the goal HHS states — improved clinical care — but is instead acceleration toward a deployment model that will generate documented harms, contested liability, and eventual regulatory retrenchment of a more disruptive character than the precautionary governance the Centre is recommending. The Centre's implementation research has documented, repeatedly, that the institutional costs of attempting to retrofit accountability obligations onto already-embedded AI systems are substantially higher than the costs of establishing those obligations at the point of initial deployment.⏎⏎The Centre appreciates the opportunity to submit these comments and would welcome the opportunity to share the underlying data from its longitudinal pilot project analysis with HHS staff in the event that the Department finds the empirical basis for the Centre's recommendations useful in its deliberations. The forthcoming white paper, expected for release in early 2025, will provide a more complete synthesis of the implementation evidence on which this comment draws, including the full case documentation from the twelve pilot projects and the detailed survey methodology underlying the faculty and researcher findings cited above. Inquiries regarding the Centre's research may be directed to:⏎⏎dnelson@logos-research.unc.edu⏎Logos Research Centre, Gillings School of Global Public Health⏎135 Dauer Drive, Chapel Hill, NC 27599⏎⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎⏎Donna Nelson⏎Assistant Professor⏎Logos Research Centre⏎University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill Gillings School of Global Public Health⏎⏎The Centre notes, for the record, that the reimbursement conditions described in Section 3 of this comment are intended to apply to AI systems used in direct patient care decisions — diagnostic recommendation, treatment selection, risk stratification — and are not intended to address administrative AI applications such as billing automation or scheduling, which raise a distinct and somewhat less acute set of accountability concerns, though the Centre does not regard those concerns as negligible. The boundary between clinical and administrative AI is itself an area where the existing regulatory vocabulary is not yet precise enough to do the work that an accountability framework will require it to do, and HHS may wish to address definitional clarity as a threshold step before developing the more substantive accountability conditions the Centre recommends.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0091♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Adams♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian Adams | HHS AI RFI Comment | Page 1⏎⏎⏎My mother came within what I would estimate was forty-eight hours of a serious adverse event following her hip replacement at Swedish Medical in Seattle. The medication reconciliation error that nearly harmed her was not caught by any of the three EHR systems involved in her care. It was caught because I happened to be standing in her kitchen. I am a software entrepreneur. I run a forty-person company in Bellevue that builds enterprise systems, and I can tell you from direct professional experience that the interoperability failure I watched play out across her care team is exactly the class of problem that well-implemented clinical AI could address today. I want to be clear about that. The technology is real. The potential is real. Eric Topol's 2019 overview in Nature Medicine of where machine learning genuinely outperforms human pattern recognition in radiology, pathology, and genomics is not science fiction. None of that is what concerns me about this RFI. What concerns me is that HHS is using the language of productivity and acceleration in a clinical domain where the human relationship is not an efficiency variable. It is load-bearing infrastructure.⏎⏎The RFI frames AI adoption in terms of care delivery efficiency and administrative burden reduction. That framing is a category error. Zulman et al.'s 2020 JAMA study documented that physician presence behaviors in the clinical encounter, things like attentive silence, physical contact, and sustained eye contact, produce measurable improvements in patient-reported outcomes independent of the clinical content of the visit. Verghese wrote in Health Affairs in 2011 about the diagnostic information embedded in the physical encounter that cannot be extracted from structured data, and he was not being sentimental. He was describing a data channel. Kelley et al.'s 2014 study in the Annals of Family Medicine found that relational continuity in primary care was associated with lower hospitalization rates and better chronic disease management even after controlling for clinical factors. These are not arguments against AI. They are arguments against a deployment model that treats the physician-patient encounter as a transaction to be optimized. When you instrument a relationship for throughput, you change the relationship.⏎⏎⏎Brian Adams | HHS AI RFI Comment | Page 2⏎⏎⏎The deskilling concern is the part I genuinely don't think HHS has grappled with. Goddard et al.'s 2012 systematic review in JAMIA documented automation bias across a range of clinical decision support contexts. When clinicians defer to algorithmic recommendations at rates that exceed the algorithms' actual accuracy, you get a class of error that is different from unaided clinical error in an important way: it is systematic and correlated. Wachter and Goldsmith wrote in Harvard Business Review in 2018 that the prior wave of health IT adoption, the EHR push, degraded rather than supported clinical judgment by turning physicians into data entry clerks. The ONC Health IT Dashboard's 2023 burnout data shows the damage is still accumulating. HHS is now proposing to accelerate a second wave before the first one has been honestly evaluated. I would ask the Department to answer a specific question before proceeding: what is the minimum evidence threshold for clinical AI deployment in contexts where the algorithm intermediates the physician-patient relationship, and who sets♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0092♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Martin♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My granddaughter Reese was misdiagnosed twice at Batesville before a specialist in Little Rock caught what was actually wrong with her. Both times, the doctor spent under eight minutes with her. Now HHS wants to accelerate the thing that made those eight minutes feel sufficient to whoever signed off on her chart. I have spent forty years as a social worker watching hospitals find new ways to process people faster, and I can tell you exactly what administrators do with tools like this: they cut nurses, they cut social workers, they cut anyone whose job is to slow down and actually look at a patient. My husband waited fourteen months at the VA for a knee replacement and not one person there lifted a finger to move that along.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0093♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Moore♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔Community Health Worker Network of New York City♔♔♔♔♔♔COMMUNITY HEALTH WORKER NETWORK OF NEW YORK CITY⏎Submitted in Response to: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket Reference: HHS-2024-AI-RFI⏎Submitter: Andrew Moore, Senior Policy Analyst⏎Date: October 2024⏎⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────⏎Page 1 | CHW Network NYC | HHS AI RFI | October 2024⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎THE ACCELERATION PROBLEM: SPEED WITHOUT EQUITY SAFEGUARDS⏎⏎The framing of this request for information as an "acceleration" agenda is, itself, a policy signal worth examining before addressing the substance of what HHS is soliciting. The Association of periOperative Registered Nurses (AORN) published survey findings in 2024 documenting that 68% of perioperative nurses reported their facilities had introduced or piloted at least one AI-enabled clinical decision support tool within the preceding 24 months, yet fewer than 22% indicated their institutions had established formal governance structures addressing nursing accountability or scope-of-practice boundaries for those tools (AORN Practice and Policy Advisory Committee, 2024). That gap — between deployment velocity and governance readiness — is not an implementation lag that will self-correct. It is the predictable consequence of an innovation environment that treats equity and safety auditing as optional features rather than preconditions.⏎⏎The empirical record on algorithmic bias in clinical AI is no longer speculative. It is a documented pattern with named cases and quantified harms. Sjoding and colleagues demonstrated in 2020 that pulse oximetry measurements systematically overestimated blood oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin pigmentation, producing clinically meaningful errors that went undetected precisely because the technology's authoritative presentation suppressed the clinical skepticism that might have caught equivalent errors in a human assessment (Sjoding et al., New England Journal of Medicine, 383(25), 2477–2478, 2020). Obermeyer and colleagues published in Science in 2019 a rigorous dissection of a widely deployed risk-stratification algorithm that systematically underestimated illness severity in Black patients — not because race was an explicit input variable, but because the algorithm used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, and decades of discriminatory access had already suppressed costs for Black patients relative to their actual clinical burden (Obermeyer et al., Science, 366(6464), 447–453, 2019). These are not edge cases. They are illustrations of a mechanism: training data that encodes historical inequity will reproduce that inequity at scale, and the authoritative presentation of algorithmic outputs makes those reproduced inequities harder to detect than equivalent human error because clinicians are trained, through professional culture and formal education, to second-guess colleagues — not machines.⏎⏎This dynamic has a name in the cognitive science literature. Automation bias, as documented by Char, Shah, and Magnus in their 2018 analysis of machine learning implementation in health care (New England Journal of Medicine, 378(11), 981–983), describes the tendency of human decision-makers to defer to algorithmic outputs even when those outputs are erroneous. The AORN Practice and Policy Advisory Committee's structured focus groups, conducted at six regional symposia and documented in the 2024 governance readiness report, found that perioperative nurses consistently identified the absence of AI-specific competency frameworks as a primary barrier to safe and confident adoption — meaning frontline clinicians are being asked to exercise critical oversight of tools for which no institution has defined what that oversight should look like or how a nurse should recognize and escalate an anomalous output. The Joint Commission's 2023 Sentinel Event Alert on emerging patterns at the human-algorithm interface in high-acuity surgical environments reflects the same concern at the incident-reporting level (Joint Commission, Sentinel Event Alert, Issue 67, 2023). These are not theoretical warning signs.⏎⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────⏎Page 2 | CHW Network NYC | HHS AI RFI | October 2024⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎THE VALIDATION-GENERALIZABILITY GAP AND ITS DISTRIBUTIONAL CONSEQUENCES⏎⏎HHS has not proposed mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing or post-deployment bias surveillance requirements. This comment treats that omission as a substantive policy choice with foreseeable distributional consequences, not as an oversight awaiting correction in a future rulemaking cycle.⏎⏎The performance degradation of AI clinical tools when deployed outside their training environments is well-documented. Wong and colleagues published external validation data in 2021 on a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model, finding that the model's performance in community hospital settings differed substantially from its academic medical center validation metrics, with implications for triage accuracy that are not trivially small (Wong et al., JAMA Internal Medicine, 181(8), 1065–1070, 2021). Wiens and colleagues, in their 2019 roadmap for responsible machine learning in health care, identified external validation as a non-negotiable prerequisite for deployment precisely because academic medical center populations differ systematically from the populations served by community hospitals, rural facilities, and safety-net institutions (Wiens et al., Nature Medicine, 25(9), 1337–1340, 2019). Gianfrancesco and colleagues have documented how electronic health record data — the primary substrate for training most clinical AI — carries systematic missingness patterns that correlate with socioeconomic status and race, meaning models trained on EHR data inherit not only historical treatment biases but also the informational gaps that structured around marginalized populations (Gianfrancesco et al., JAMA Internal Medicine, 178(11), 1544–1547, 2018).⏎⏎The distributional logic here is not complicated. If a tool performs worse in community and rural settings, and if patients from already-marginalized populations are disproportionately concentrated in those settings, then accelerating deployment without mandatory performance stratification by care setting, race, ethnicity, and socioeconomic status means that the populations already carrying the greatest burden of health inequity will bear the concentrated burden of algorithmic failure first and longest — and will do so before any corrective signal reaches regulators. Adamson and Smith's 2018 analysis of machine learning in dermatology demonstrated that training datasets composed predominantly of lighter-skinned patient images produced diagnostic tools with measurably inferior performance for darker-skinned patients (Adamson and Smith, JAMA Dermatology, 154(11), 1247–1248, 2018). Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones documented in 2020 how race correction embedded in clinical algorithms compounds this problem by treating race as a biological variable rather than a social one, propagating structural inequity through the architecture of the tool itself (Vyas et al., New England Journal of Medicine, 383(9), 874–882, 2020). Pierson and colleagues have further shown that algorithmic approaches to pain management can reduce unexplained disparities in underserved populations when designed with equity as an explicit optimization target — which means the technical capacity for equity-aware design exists, and the failure to require it is a governance failure, not a technical inevitability (Pierson et al., Nature Medicine, 27(1), 136–140, 2021).⏎⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────⏎Page 3 | CHW Network NYC | HHS AI RFI | October 2024⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎MINIMUM REQUIRED REGULATORY CONDITIONS⏎⏎AORN and the Community Health Worker Network of New York City jointly submit that no deployment acceleration framework is defensible without the following minimum conditions, which should be framed as mandatory requirements rather than voluntary guidance:⏎⏎1. Pre-deployment equity auditing requirements, with standardized methodology, applied to any AI-enabled clinical decision support tool prior to use in any CMS-reimbursed care setting.⏎⏎2. Mandatory post-deployment bias surveillance protocols, including reporting timelines and defined thresholds for suspension of deployment pending remediation.⏎⏎3. Stratified performance reporting disaggregated by race, ethnicity, socioeconomic status, and care setting type — with public reporting requirements so that the corrective signal reaches regulators, institutions, and affected communities simultaneously rather than sequentially.⏎⏎4. Explicit external validation requirements for any tool seeking deployment in community hospital, rural, or safety-net settings, where training-environment performance data from academic medical centers should be presumptively insufficient for approval.⏎⏎Sendak and colleagues have documented that clinicians frequently experience AI outputs as opaque and that the opacity itself suppresses critical engagement with potentially erroneous recommendations (Sendak et al., ACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and Transparency, 2020). The governance gap AORN documented — 68% deployment, under 22% governance readiness — will not close through voluntary institutional initiative on the timeline that HHS's acceleration agenda implies. The point at which the corrective signal arrives, under the current proposed framework, is after patients have been harmed in settings⏎⏎www.chwnetworknyc.org | policy@chwnetworknyc.org⏎Andrew Moore, Senior Policy Analyst | amoore@chwnetworknyc.org | (212) 555-0174⏎⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────⏎Page 3 | CHW Network NYC | HHS AI RFI | October 2024⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0094♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Allen♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔Siemens Healthineers♔♔♔♔♔♔Siemens Healthineers | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 1⏎⏎COMMENT OF SIEMENS HEALTHINEERS⏎Submitted in Response to: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: Emily Allen, Practice Manager, Siemens Healthineers⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-RFI⏎⏎THE ACCOUNTABILITY QUESTION HHS IS NOT ASKING⏎⏎Our practice has spent the better part of two years trying to do what this RFI says it wants: evaluate AI-assisted documentation tools, assess their fit for a licensed clinical social work environment, and determine whether adoption could reduce the administrative overhead that is, in the most direct terms, consuming our clinicians. The 2023–2024 vendor evaluation we conducted was not a casual exercise. We documented, through structured interviews with clinical staff, that administrative tasks — progress note completion, prior authorization correspondence, outcome tracking — consumed an estimated 34% of total clinician time per week. Our practitioners were averaging 6.2 fewer billable client hours per week compared to pre-pandemic baselines, and the causal link between documentation burden and reduced appointment availability was not ambiguous. So we looked at the tools. We engaged vendors. We reviewed integration pathways. And we kept arriving at the same obstacle, which was not technological. The obstacle was that no one in the current regulatory environment could tell us, with any binding specificity, who would be legally responsible if an AI-assisted clinical recommendation contributed to patient harm. That question does not have an answer yet. And this RFI, for all its ambition, does not propose to give it one before scaling deployment further.⏎⏎Siemens Healthineers | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 2⏎⏎THE STRUCTURAL DIFFUSION OF RESPONSIBILITY⏎⏎The liability problem in AI-assisted clinical care is not a gap in existing doctrine that a few guidance documents can patch. It is a structural feature of how AI intermediation interacts with the foundational assumptions of American medical malpractice law. As Price, Gerke, and Cohen (2019) observed in their analysis published in JAMA, current malpractice doctrine presupposes an identifiable human decision-maker whose judgment can be evaluated against a professional standard of care. When an AI system participates in that decision — generating a recommended diagnosis, flagging a risk score, auto-populating a clinical note — the chain of professional judgment fractures in ways that existing tort frameworks have not resolved. The clinician can plausibly argue that the tool's output influenced the decision. The vendor can argue that the clinician retained final authority and chose to act. The hospital or practice can argue that it implemented the tool in compliance with available guidance. Each of those positions has surface plausibility, and the result is that patients harmed by AI-intermediated errors face a structural accountability vacuum rather than a clearly answerable party. Mello, Frakes, Blumenkranz, and Studdert (2020) documented in their JAMA review that malpractice liability, whatever its imperfections, performs a deterrence function in health care quality that depends on the existence of identifiable responsible parties. Remove that identifiability, and the deterrence mechanism does not merely weaken — it ceases to function in any coherent sense.⏎⏎What makes this particularly difficult is that the diffusion of responsibility is not an accident or an oversight. It is, in a meaningful sense, a design feature of how AI tools are commercially structured. Vendors disclaim liability through end-user license agreements. Clinicians are positioned as the "final decision-maker" in documentation that is itself crafted to insulate the vendor from downstream legal exposure. Hospitals and practices adopt institutional policies that defer to vendor specifications, which in turn defer back to clinician judgment. Cohen, Amarasingham, Shah, Xie, and Lo (2014) identified precisely this dynamic in their Health Affairs analysis of complex predictive analytics in health care, noting that the legal and ethical responsibility for algorithmic outputs is distributed across so many parties that meaningful accountability becomes practically unassignable. That analysis was published a decade ago. The problem has not been resolved since. And the commercial AI tools now entering clinical workflows are more deeply embedded, more opaque in their reasoning, and more consequential in their outputs than the predictive systems Cohen and colleagues examined.⏎⏎Siemens Healthineers | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 3⏎⏎THE MALPRACTICE DOCTRINE PROBLEM IS NOT SELF-CORRECTING⏎⏎There is an argument, sometimes offered in policy discussions, that the courts will eventually sort this out — that common law will adapt to AI intermediation the way it adapted to other technological changes in medical practice, and that HHS need not wait for legislative resolution before encouraging adoption. We find this argument unpersuasive, for reasons that are primarily structural rather than doctrinal. Judicial adaptation through tort litigation is slow, expensive, and deeply inequitable as a mechanism for patient protection. The patients most likely to be harmed by AI-assisted clinical errors — those in under-resourced behavioral health settings, those in Medicaid-dependent practices, those with limited access to legal representation — are also the patients least positioned to pursue complex multi-defendant litigation against a vendor, a practice, and a hospital system simultaneously. The expectation that these patients will effectively discipline the AI deployment market through litigation is not a patient protection policy. It is the absence of one.⏎⏎Gerke, Minssen, and Cohen (2020) make a related point in their Academic Press chapter on ethical and legal challenges of AI-driven healthcare, arguing that the speed of AI deployment in clinical settings has substantially outpaced the development of the legal frameworks necessary to govern it, and that this asymmetry creates conditions in which institutional adoption decisions are made without any binding accountability architecture in place. The European Parliament's 2017 report on civil law rules on robotics (Delvaux, 2017) arrived at a broadly similar conclusion from a different regulatory tradition, recommending that liability frameworks be established as a precondition for deployment at scale rather than as a retrospective correction after harm accumulates. HHS is not bound by European legislative thinking, obviously. But the underlying logic is not jurisdiction-specific. You do not build the accountability infrastructure after the harm has already distributed itself across a population of patients who had no input into the deployment decision.⏎⏎The ONC's own 2021 report to Congress on reducing regulatory and administrative burden acknowledged that the integration of health IT and EHR systems has generated unintended consequences — including documentation burdens of the kind our practice documented — that were not anticipated in the original deployment frameworks (ONC, 2021). That report is, in some ways, a cautionary document about what happens when federal policy accelerates adoption without sufficient attention to second-order effects. The AI RFI before us now risks repeating that pattern at a scale and with a complexity that the EHR experience did not approach.⏎⏎Siemens Healthineers | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 4⏎⏎ON THE SPECIFIC QUESTION OF CLINICAL DECISION SUPPORT AND ALERT FATIGUE⏎⏎Our vendor evaluation did not focus exclusively on documentation tools. We also examined AI-assisted clinical decision support systems, and what we found there compounded our concern rather than relieving it. Kesselheim, Cresswell, Phansalkar, Bates, and Sheikh (2011) documented in Health Affairs that clinical decision support systems, as then configured, generated alert fatigue among clinicians who were simultaneously exposed to liability risk if they overrode a system recommendation and workflow disruption if they did not. That dynamic has not been resolved by more sophisticated AI. If anything, the opacity of machine learning outputs relative to rule-based clinical decision support systems makes the clinician's position more difficult, not less. When a rule-based system flags a drug interaction, the clinician can evaluate the rule. When a machine learning model assigns a risk score, the reasoning is frequently not available for clinical inspection in any meaningful sense (Obermeyer and Emanuel, 2016). The clinician is left choosing between deferring to an output they cannot interrogate and overriding an output that may, for all they know, be capturing something their own clinical judgment is missing. Neither choice is epistemically satisfying. And under current doctrine, either choice could generate liability exposure whose assignment is, again, not clearly defined.⏎⏎The American Medical Association's 2023 policy report on augmented intelligence in medicine (AMA, 2023) called for accountability frameworks that clearly assign responsibility across the developer, deployer, and clinician chain before AI tools are embedded in clinical workflows in ways that make disentanglement difficult. We want to be specific about what "disentanglement difficulty" means in practice, because it is not a hypothetical. Once a practice has integrated an AI documentation tool into its EHR workflow, retrained its clinical staff around that tool's outputs, and structured its billing and compliance processes to accommodate the tool's note formats, the institutional dependency on that tool becomes substantial. Removing the tool, or switching vendors, generates operational disruption that practices — particularly small behavioral health practices like ours — are not well-positioned to absorb. That dependency, over time, further insulates vendors from accountability. A practice that cannot practically exit a vendor relationship cannot effectively discipline that vendor through market mechanisms. And a regulator that has encouraged adoption without establishing prior accountability requirements has, in effect, subsidized that dependency.⏎⏎Siemens Healthineers | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 5⏎⏎WHY THE REIMBURSEMENT STRUCTURE DOES NOT SOLVE THE PROBLEM⏎⏎The RFI devotes considerable attention to reimbursement as a mechanism for accelerating adoption, and we understand the logic. If CMS and state Medicaid programs create billing pathways that recognize AI-assisted clinical services, practices will have financial incentive to adopt. Our vendor evaluation did encounter reimbursement uncertainty as a barrier — that is accurate. But reimbursement reform and accountability reform are not substitutes for each other, and treating the former as a proxy for the latter is a category error with real consequences. A practice that adopts an AI documentation tool because CMS has created a reimbursement pathway is not thereby protected from liability when that tool produces a clinically consequential error. The reimbursement incentive and the liability exposure exist on parallel tracks, and widening the former without narrowing the ambiguity of the latter simply means that more practices adopt tools under conditions where the accountability question remains unresolved.⏎⏎Hoffman and Podgurski (2013) made a point in their American Journal of Law and Medicine analysis of biomedical data use that seems applicable here: the scale of deployment does not, by itself, validate a deployment model. Widespread adoption of a tool whose accountability infrastructure is inadequate does not produce accountability; it produces widespread exposure to the consequences of inadequate accountability. The National Academy for State Health Policy's 2023 policy brief on state approaches to AI in health care documented that state legislatures and regulatory bodies are beginning to develop their own AI governance frameworks precisely because federal action has been insufficiently specific about accountability assignment (NASHP, 2023). The result is a patchwork in which practices operating across state lines — or simply seeking to understand their obligations — face inconsistent and sometimes conflicting requirements. HHS accelerating adoption at the federal level without establishing a binding accountability framework does not resolve that patchwork. It adds federal momentum to a situation that is already producing compliance confusion at the state level.⏎⏎Our concern is not that AI-assisted tools have no legitimate role in reducing administrative burden. The 34% of clinician time consumed by administrative tasks in our practice is a real problem with real consequences for patient access. We are not indifferent to that. The question is whether HHS's proposed approach — accelerating adoption through reimbursement reform and R&D investment while deferring accountability architecture to future rulemaking or voluntary frameworks — is an appropriate sequencing of those policy actions. Our view, grounded in two years of direct operational experience with the liability ambiguities that currently impede responsible adoption, is that it is not. A binding accountability framework that specifies, with legal force, how responsibility is assigned across developers, deployers, and clinicians when AI-assisted errors occur is a prerequisite for responsible scaling, not a follow-on action item. HHS should condition any deployment pathway created by this rulemaking process on the prior establishment of such a framework, and should be explicit that voluntary guidance and post-hoc study processes are not adequate substitutes for binding pre-deployment accountability requirements. The specific sequencing matters enormously, and the current draft of the RFI does not treat it as a⏎⏎Siemens Healthineers | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 6⏎⏎constraint on the acceleration it is proposing. We would ask the Department to reconsider that framing before the comment period closes, and to treat the accountability question not as one consideration among many but as the threshold condition that determines whether accelerated adoption serves patients or merely exposes them to a new category of institutional risk for which no one has been made answerable in advance.⏎⏎emily.allen@siemens-healthineers.com⏎⏎REFERENCES⏎⏎American Medical Association. (2023). Augmented intelligence in medicine: AMA policy principles and accountability framework. AMA Policy Report H-480.940.⏎⏎Cohen, I.G., Amarasingham, R., Shah, A., Xie, B., & Lo, B. (2014). The legal and ethical concerns that arise from using complex predictive analytics in health care. Health Affairs, 33(7), 1139–1147.⏎⏎Delvaux, M. (2017). Report with recommendations to the Commission on Civil Law Rules on Robotics. European Parliament Committee on Legal Affairs, 2015/2103(INL).⏎⏎Gerke, S., Minssen, T., & Cohen, G. (2020). Ethical and legal challenges of artificial intelligence-driven healthcare. In Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare (pp. 295–336). Academic Press.⏎⏎Hoffman, S., & Podgurski, A. (2013). The use and misuse of biomedical data: Is bigger really better? American Journal of Law and Medicine, 39(4), 497–538.⏎⏎Kesselheim, A.S., Cresswell, K., Phansalkar, S., Bates, D.W., & Sheikh, A. (2011). Clinical decision support systems could be modified to reduce 'alert fatigue' while still minimizing the risk of litigation. Health Affairs, 30(12), 2310–2317.⏎⏎Mello, M.M., Frakes, M.D., Blumenkranz, E., & Studdert, D.M. (2020). Malpractice liability and health care quality: A review. JAMA, 323(4), 352–366.⏎⏎National Academy for State Health Policy. (2023). State approaches to artificial intelligence in health care: Emerging legislative and regulatory frameworks. NASHP Policy Brief.⏎⏎Obermeyer, Z., & Emanuel, E.J. (2016). Predicting the future — big data, machine learning, and clinical medicine. New England Journal of Medicine, 375(13), 1216–1219.⏎⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology. (2021). Strategy on reducing regulatory and administrative burden relating to the use of health IT and EHRs. ONC Report to Congress.⏎⏎Price, W.N., II, Gerke, S., & Cohen, I.G. (2019). Potential liability for physicians using artificial intelligence. JAMA, 322(18), 1765–1766.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0095♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Clark♔♔New York♔♔United States♔Aidoc♔♔♔♔♔♔Van Pelt & Company⏎1411 Broadway, Suite 2800⏎New York, NY 10018⏎⏎Michael Clark, MHA⏎Senior Vice President, Health Systems Operations⏎Aidoc⏎⏎March 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company submits this comment in response to HHS's solicitation of public input on regulatory, reimbursement, and research-and-development actions the Department might take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings. We do so not as skeptics of AI technology, but as a management consultancy with direct operational visibility into 47 healthcare system clients across 19 states, and with a professional obligation to report what we have actually observed in those systems rather than what vendors and trade associations prefer to say is happening. The record we have accumulated is not reassuring, and we think HHS should hear it clearly before committing the federal government's reimbursement and regulatory architecture to an accelerationist posture that our data suggests will produce foreseeable and documentable harm to the clinical workforce.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 1⏎⏎Our Q1 2024 operational review (Van Pelt & Company, 2024, AI Integration in Health Systems: Operational Barriers, Cost Structures, and Workforce Adoption Patterns, internal policy report on file with authors) found that 68% of our client health systems had piloted at least one AI-assisted clinical decision support tool within the prior 24 months. Only 19% had achieved sustained integration beyond the pilot phase. When we asked system leadership why pilots failed to scale, inconsistent reimbursement pathways and unclear liability frameworks were cited most frequently — and we understand why HHS reads that as a reason to act. But the same roundtable process surfaced a finding that does not appear anywhere in the Department's framing of this RFI: the AI tools that did achieve sustained deployment were disproportionately tools that generated administrative productivity metrics useful to management, not tools that frontline clinicians reported as reducing their cognitive load or improving their experience of care delivery. That distinction matters enormously for how HHS should think about what it is actually accelerating.⏎⏎⏎WHAT AI TOOLS ARE BEING USED FOR IN HEALTH SYSTEMS⏎⏎We want to be direct about something that the vendor community and many health system trade associations have been careful not to say plainly: a significant share of AI deployment in clinical settings is functioning as workforce surveillance infrastructure. This is not a polemical characterization. It is a description of the actual operational footprint of tools that track clinician throughput, flag documentation delays, monitor deviation from algorithmic care pathways, and generate productivity reports that flow upward to administrative leadership rather than back to the clinician as clinical support. Kellogg, Valentine, and Christin (2020, Algorithms at Work: The New Contested Terrain of Control, Academy of Management Annals, 14(1), 366-410) documented precisely this dynamic in knowledge-work environments, and their framework maps with uncomfortable accuracy onto what our client-facing teams have observed in healthcare settings over the past two years. The tools are marketed as burnout-reduction and efficiency solutions. The operational reality, as we have documented it across dozens of system engagements, is that they are being deployed to justify reduced staffing ratios and increased caseloads, with the productivity gains extracted from the workforce rather than returned to it in the form of reduced burden.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎The burnout framing deserves particular scrutiny because HHS has cited it in the context of this RFI as a rationale for accelerating AI adoption. The primary drivers of clinician burnout are alert fatigue, documentation burden, and loss of professional autonomy (Shanafelt et al., 2022, Changes in Burnout and Satisfaction With Work-Life Integration in Physicians and the General US Working Population Between 2011 and 2020, Mayo Clinic Proceedings, 97(3), 491-506). AI deployment in its current form is more likely to intensify all three than to resolve them. Alert fatigue is not reduced by adding AI-generated alerts to existing EHR alert loads; Bates et al. (2021, The Potential of Artificial Intelligence to Reduce Alert Fatigue and Improve Clinical Decision Support, npj Digital Medicine, 4(1), 1-6) found that AI-assisted clinical decision support tools, when deployed without disciplined governance frameworks, produced net increases in alert burden in several study environments. Documentation burden is similarly not addressed by AI scribing tools that require clinicians to review, correct, and attest to AI-generated notes — a process that shifts rather than eliminates the cognitive work, as Patel et al. (2023, Electronic Health Record Burden and Its Association With Physician Burnout: A Systematic Review, Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association, 30(4), 712-724) have demonstrated. And the loss of professional autonomy — which Shanafelt's longitudinal data identifies as among the most powerful predictors of burnout severity — is directly worsened by surveillance architectures that monitor clinician behavior against algorithmic benchmarks and flag deviations for administrative review.⏎⏎o The AI tools most aggressively marketed to our client health systems between 2022 and 2024 have been ambient documentation assistants, predictive deterioration alerts, and "workflow optimization" platforms.⏎⏎o In 11 of our 47 client systems, deployment of workflow optimization platforms was accompanied within 18 months by administrative proposals to reduce nurse-to-patient ratios or expand provider caseloads, citing AI-enabled efficiency gains as justification.⏎⏎o In those same 11 systems, frontline nursing and physician staff were not involved in the procurement decision for the tools whose productivity data was subsequently used to justify the staffing proposals.⏎⏎o The American Hospital Association's 2023 workforce report to Congress documented acute and ongoing staffing shortfalls across the health sector; our client data suggests that AI adoption, as currently structured, is being used to manage around those shortfalls rather than address them.⏎⏎The staffing question cannot be separated from patient safety. Aiken et al. (2014, Nurse Staffing and Education and Hospital Mortality in Nine European Countries: A Retrospective Observational Study, The Lancet, 383(9931), 1824-1830) established with considerable methodological rigor that each additional patient added to a nurse's workload was associated with a 7% increase in the likelihood of a patient dying within 30 days of admission. If AI adoption is functioning as a mechanism for increasing nurse caseloads — and our data from 11 client systems suggests it is, at least in some cases — then the Department's reimbursement incentives for AI deployment are, in a meaningful sense, reimbursement incentives for the conditions Aiken's research associates with elevated patient mortality. We are not certain this is the outcome HHS intends. We are certain it is a foreseeable one.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 3⏎⏎⏎THE STRUCTURAL EXCLUSION OF FRONTLINE WORKERS FROM AI PROCUREMENT⏎⏎The governance problem at the center of this issue is that the people who use these tools every day have essentially no structural role in the decisions about whether to acquire them, how to configure them, or what performance standards they must meet before deployment. Procurement authority in health systems sits with C-suite and administrative leadership. Vendor relationships are managed by operations and IT. The ONC's 2024 Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability final rule (89 Fed. Reg. 1192) made meaningful progress on algorithm transparency requirements, and we supported that rulemaking, but transparency requirements directed at health system administrators do not solve the problem of frontline workers being excluded from governance. A nurse who has no seat at the procurement table does not benefit from an administrator's access to an algorithm's training data documentation. The National Nurses United 2023 policy report on AI in healthcare (Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare: Workforce Impacts and the Case for Nurse-Led Governance, NNU Policy Report, Washington, D.C.) made this point with force and specificity, and the Department should engage with it seriously rather than treating labor organizations as stakeholders to be consulted after decisions are made.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 4⏎⏎Our Q1 2024 roundtables found that clinical staff adoption rates improved by an estimated 31% when AI tools were accompanied by structured reimbursement certainty — a finding we reported in our internal summary and that we believe HHS has cited as evidence that payment policy drives meaningful integration. We want to be careful about how that finding is read. Higher adoption rates are not the same as better outcomes for patients or the workforce. If a tool is imposed on a clinical staff through administrative mandate backed by productivity monitoring, adoption rates will be high and the workforce will be harmed. The 31% figure reflects a real relationship between reimbursement certainty and tool deployment, but it tells us nothing about whether that deployment is good for anyone other than the vendor and the administrative leadership that purchased the tool. Gartner's 2023 Hype Cycle for Healthcare Providers (AI and Automation in Clinical Workflows, Gartner Research Note G00789341) observed that many AI tools in clinical settings are in a period of inflated expectations relative to demonstrated performance, and that real-world deployment has routinely produced outcomes that diverge significantly from vendor-cited benchmark data. Obermeyer and Emanuel (2016, Predicting the Future — Big Data, Machine Learning, and Clinical Medicine, New England Journal of Medicine, 375(13), 1216-1219) made the same point about the gap between model performance metrics and clinical utility eight years ago, and the problem has not been resolved in the intervening period.⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS MUST REQUIRE BEFORE INCENTIVIZING DEPLOYMENT⏎⏎We are addressing this comment primarily to the workforce-protection dimensions of the RFI rather than to the full range of questions the Department has posed, because we think those dimensions are the ones most at risk of being omitted from the public record in a comment process dominated by health system trade associations and AI vendors. Our specific request is this: HHS should impose precautionary workforce-protection standards as a condition of any reimbursement incentive or regulatory acceleration associated with AI clinical deployment. The failure to do so is not a neutral policy choice. It is a decision to subsidize, with federal reimbursement dollars, the deployment of surveillance infrastructure onto a workforce that is already experiencing burnout at rates that Shanafelt et al. documented as historically elevated even before the COVID-19 period. That subsidy will produce foreseeable harm, and the Department will have been warned.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 5⏎⏎Those workforce-protection standards should include, at minimum, the following:⏎⏎1. A requirement that any AI clinical deployment seeking reimbursement recognition demonstrate, through prospective data collection rather than vendor self-report, measurable benefit to frontline workforce experience as a condition of sustained incentive eligibility. "Benefit" should be defined to include documented reductions in alert burden, documentation time, or self-reported cognitive load — not administrative efficiency metrics that flow to management rather than to the clinician.⏎⏎2. A requirement that health systems receiving reimbursement incentives for AI deployment document the governance process through which frontline clinical staff were involved in procurement decisions, including evidence that staff concerns were recorded and addressed. A system that purchases a surveillance tool without nursing or physician input and then deploys it under administrative mandate should not qualify for federal reimbursement support for that tool.⏎⏎3. A prohibition on the use of AI-generated productivity data as the primary or sole basis for staffing ratio reductions within a defined period following tool deployment, with HHS monitoring of whether AI adoption in incentivized settings is correlated with adverse workforce outcomes including staff turnover, reported burnout, or complaints filed with state labor agencies.⏎⏎4. Mandatory disclosure to clinical staff, prior to deployment, of the categories of data collected by any AI tool operating in their work environment, the parties who have access to that data, and the administrative uses to which it may be put. Surveillance that is invisible is surveillance that workers cannot contest or negotiate.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 6⏎⏎We recognize that some of these requirements will be characterized by the vendor community and by health system administrators as burdensome or as barriers to adoption. We think that framing inverts the relevant question. The question is not whether precautionary workforce-protection requirements will slow AI adoption. The question is whether AI adoption without those requirements will harm a workforce that the American Hospital Association itself has documented as already operating under acute and sustained stress (AHA, 2023). Our client data, our roundtable findings, and the peer-reviewed literature we have cited above converge on an answer that HHS should take seriously: absent workforce-protection conditions, federal incentives for AI deployment will function as a policy subsidy for the systematic extraction of productivity from a clinical workforce that has limited structural power to resist it, with downstream consequences for patient safety that are not speculative. The per-facility compliance and validation costs we documented in our 2024 review — averaging $340,000 annually across our client base — are being absorbed by health systems while the workforce bears costs of a different and more serious kind that do not appear on any balance sheet.⏎⏎We urge the Department to require demonstrated evidence of workforce benefit, with independent verification, before any reimbursement or regulatory pathway is opened for AI clinical deployment tools. The alternative is a policy record that will be difficult to defend when the outcomes become visible.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company⏎New York, NY⏎mclark@vanpeltco.com⏎www.vanpeltco.com/policy♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0096♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔16♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Mitchell♔♔California♔♔United States♔Partnership for AI in Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Partnership for AI in Health⏎1400 K Street NW, Suite 700⏎Washington, DC 20005⏎government.affairs@partnershipforaihealth.org⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Re: Comments of the Partnership for AI in Health on the HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information, Docket No. HHS-OD-2024-AI-RFI⏎⏎⏎Partnership for AI in Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎The Partnership for AI in Health (the Partnership) submits these comments in response to the Department of Health and Human Services' Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. The Partnership is a national advocacy organization representing radiology practices, hospital-based imaging programs, and health system administrators across 47 states, with a particular focus on the operational and policy conditions that govern AI deployment in clinical workflows. These comments address specifically the equity and safety dimensions of the Department's proposed acceleration agenda, and the near-total absence from the RFI of any mandatory framework for auditing algorithmic bias before deployment or monitoring for disparate harm after deployment. The Partnership does not oppose the goal of accelerating beneficial AI adoption. We are, however, compelled by the empirical record to register serious institutional concern that the posture reflected in this RFI will, if adopted without modification, produce systematic and foreseeable harm concentrated precisely in the communities and institutions that are already the most underserved.⏎⏎The basis for that concern is not theoretical. In a 2024 survey of 412 radiology practice administrators conducted in partnership with the Radiology Business Management Association, the Partnership found that 67 percent of responding practices had deployed at least one FDA-cleared AI algorithm in clinical workflows. Of those practices, 58 percent reported that existing reimbursement structures failed to account for the additional physician oversight and workflow integration costs associated with AI-assisted interpretation. More relevant to the equity question this comment addresses: among practices in rural and safety-net markets, AI adoption rates lagged urban counterparts by nearly 24 percentage points, and inadequate coding pathways were cited as the primary barrier in 71 percent of those rural responses. That adoption gap is not, in itself, the problem the Partnership is raising here. The problem is that the practices most likely to adopt AI first — urban academic medical centers with the technical infrastructure, revenue, and research capacity to evaluate and integrate these tools — are also the practices least representative of the patient populations where algorithmic bias tends to cause the most severe harm. When HHS accelerates adoption without equity conditions, it is in effect accelerating the diffusion of systems validated in one population context into care settings serving entirely different patient populations, without any mandatory mechanism for detecting when those systems fail.⏎⏎⏎Partnership for AI in Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎SECTION I: BIAS IS ENCODED IN TRAINING DATA AND REPRODUCED AT SCALE⏎⏎The foundational problem is structural, not incidental. AI systems trained on historical healthcare data do not merely reflect the past — they operationalize it. The documented literature makes clear that decades of discriminatory treatment patterns are embedded in the electronic health record data on which most clinical AI systems are trained. Obermeyer and colleagues, writing in Science in 2019, demonstrated that a widely used commercial algorithm for managing population health systematically underestimated the illness severity of Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent clinical need, because the algorithm used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need — and Black patients, due to longstanding barriers to access, had historically generated lower costs even when equally sick (Obermeyer Z, Powers B, Vogeli C, Mullainathan S., "Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations," Science, 2019;366(6464):447-453). The algorithm did not intend to discriminate. It learned to discriminate from data generated by a healthcare system that already discriminated. That distinction matters enormously for regulatory design, because it means bias auditing cannot be satisfied by reviewing the code or the intent of the developers. It requires examining model outputs stratified by race, ethnicity, sex, primary language, socioeconomic status, and geography — before deployment, not after harm has accumulated.⏎⏎Gianfrancesco and colleagues have further documented how electronic health record data used to train machine learning models carries systematic missingness and measurement error that is non-randomly distributed across patient populations, such that models trained on these data will encode the biases of documentation practices, diagnostic access, and care-seeking behavior in ways that are invisible to developers who do not explicitly test for them (Gianfrancesco MA, Tamang S, Yazdany J, Schmajuk G., "Potential biases in machine learning algorithms using electronic health record data," JAMA Intern Med., 2018;178(11):1544-1547). Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones have separately documented how the incorporation of race as a variable in clinical algorithms — including in widely used tools like the estimated glomerular filtration rate equation, prior versions of which assigned higher kidney function scores to Black patients and thereby delayed nephrology referral — produces systematic undertreatment of minority populations while appearing, on average performance metrics, to function normally (Vyas DA, Eisenstein LG, Jones DS., "Hidden in plain sight — reconsidering the use of race correction in clinical algorithms," N Engl J Med., 2020;383(9):874-882). The point the Partnership draws from this body of work is not that AI is uniquely dangerous. It is that AI systems trained on compromised data will reproduce and scale the harms embedded in that data, and that FDA clearance based on average diagnostic accuracy metrics provides no assurance whatsoever that a system performs equitably across patient subgroups.⏎⏎⏎Partnership for AI in Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 3⏎⏎⏎SECTION II: AVERAGE PERFORMANCE METRICS CONCEAL CATASTROPHIC FAILURES IN MARGINALIZED POPULATIONS⏎⏎This is the point that the RFI's framing of "accelerating adoption" most conspicuously fails to reckon with. A system can achieve 92 percent sensitivity on a held-out test set while performing at 74 percent sensitivity for a specific demographic subgroup — and both numbers can be technically accurate. The system clears its regulatory threshold. It gets deployed. It gets reimbursed. And in the emergency department of a safety-net hospital in Fresno or rural Mississippi, it is quietly missing diagnoses at a rate that would be considered unacceptable if anyone were measuring it.⏎⏎The sepsis prediction literature is particularly instructive on this point. Wong and colleagues published an external validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model — the Epic Sepsis Model — across more than 27,000 hospitalizations at the University of Michigan Health System and found that the model had a positive predictive value of only 12.8 percent, that it missed 67 percent of sepsis cases identified by the Sepsis-3 definition, and that its performance was materially worse in patient subpopulations underrepresented in its training data (Wong A, Otles E, Donnelly JP, et al., "External validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model in hospitalized patients," JAMA Intern Med., 2021;181(8):1065-1070). This model had been deployed in hundreds of hospitals before that external validation was published. The question the Partnership puts to HHS is: what regulatory mechanism would have detected that failure before hundreds of thousands of patient-encounters had been processed through a model that did not work as advertised? The RFI does not answer that question because the RFI does not ask it.⏎⏎Seyyed-Kalantari and colleagues examined chest radiograph AI algorithms applied to datasets from three large academic medical centers and found that underserved patient populations — defined by insurance status, self-reported race and ethnicity, and sex — were systematically underdiagnosed by the AI models relative to their actual pathology burden, and that this underdiagnosis bias persisted even when controlling for image quality and clinical presentation (Seyyed-Kalantari L, Zhang H, McDermott MBA, Chen IY, Ghassemi M., "Underdiagnosis bias of artificial intelligence algorithms applied to chest radiographs in under-served patient populations," Nat Med., 2021;27(12):2176-2182). Radiology is the Partnership's home domain, and this finding is one our member practices cannot set aside. When an AI-assisted chest radiograph interpretation system misses findings at higher rates in Medicaid patients than in commercially insured patients, that is not a technical artifact to be corrected in the next model version. That is a patient safety failure occurring in real time, in the exact settings — community hospitals, federally qualified health centers, rural critical access hospitals — where the Partnership's survey data shows AI adoption is already lagging and where radiologist coverage is thinnest.⏎⏎⏎Partnership for AI in Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 4⏎⏎⏎SECTION III: AUTOMATION BIAS SUPPRESSES THE ERROR-CORRECTION MECHANISMS THAT MIGHT OTHERWISE CATCH THESE FAILURES⏎⏎There is a second-order problem that compounds the first. Even when AI systems produce errors, clinicians who work alongside AI outputs are systematically less likely to catch and correct those errors than they would be if they had made the same assessment unaided. This is automation bias — the well-documented tendency of human operators to over-rely on algorithmic outputs, particularly when those outputs are presented with apparent confidence and quantitative precision. Char, Shah, and Magnus, writing in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2018, identified automation bias as one of the central ethical challenges of clinical AI implementation, noting that clinicians trained to treat algorithmic outputs as authoritative decision support may effectively abdicate the independent clinical judgment that serves as the primary error-correction mechanism in human medicine (Char DS, Shah NH, Magnus D., "Implementing machine learning in health care — addressing ethical challenges," N Engl J Med., 2018;378(11):981-983). The authoritative appearance of AI outputs — the confidence score, the visual overlay, the EHR alert — is precisely what makes them useful to clinicians under time pressure. It is also precisely what makes the errors they produce harder to catch.⏎⏎The automation bias problem has a specific interaction with algorithmic bias that the Partnership finds particularly troubling. If an AI system systematically underestimates illness severity in Black patients, and if the clinicians using that system have been conditioned to treat its outputs as reliable, then the system does not merely reproduce a disparity — it creates a clinical environment in which the disparity is less likely to be noticed and challenged than equivalent human error would be. A physician who makes a clinical judgment that later proves wrong is subject to peer review, quality assurance processes, and the patient's own advocacy. An AI system that systematically underperforms for a demographic subgroup produces a pattern of harm that is distributed across thousands of encounters, attributed to no individual clinician, and invisible in aggregate quality metrics unless someone is specifically looking for it. The Partnership's concern is that HHS's acceleration agenda will substantially increase the number of clinical encounters processed through AI systems, while doing nothing to increase the probability that systematic disparate harm will be detected.⏎⏎⏎Partnership for AI in Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 5⏎⏎⏎SECTION IV: VALIDATION STUDIES CONDUCTED IN ACADEMIC CENTERS DO NOT GENERALIZE TO THE COMMUNITY AND RURAL SETTINGS WHERE DISPARITIES ARE ALREADY WORST⏎⏎The Partnership recognizes that this concern is not novel. The generalizability problem in clinical AI has been documented extensively. Nagendran and colleagues conducted a systematic review of 81 deep learning studies in medical imaging published through 2019 and found that the overwhelming majority were conducted on retrospective datasets from major academic medical centers, that external validation in independent populations was rare, and that claims of performance equivalence or superiority to clinicians were frequently unsupported by the study designs used to generate them (Nagendran M, Chen Y, Lovejoy CA, et al., "Artificial intelligence versus clinicians: systematic review of design, reporting standards, and claims of deep learning studies in medical imaging," BMJ, 2020;368:m689). The datasets used in these studies skew heavily toward patients who are insured, English-speaking, and receiving care at institutions with high documentation quality — which is to say, they systematically underrepresent the patients in whom algorithmic bias is most likely to cause harm.⏎⏎Wiens and colleagues, writing in Nature Medicine in 2019, articulated a responsible machine learning roadmap for healthcare that specifically flagged the gap between academic validation and real-world deployment performance as a structural risk requiring prospective monitoring and outcome tracking after deployment, not merely pre-deployment validation studies (Wiens J, Saria S, Sendak M, et al., "Do no harm: a roadmap for responsible machine learning for health care," Nat Med., 2019;25(9):1337-1340). That roadmap was published five years ago. The Partnership is not aware that HHS has operationalized any of its core recommendations in the form of regulatory requirements applicable to clinical AI systems receiving Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement. The RFI does not indicate that HHS intends to do so now.⏎⏎The generalizability problem is particularly acute in radiology, and the Partnership's survey data speaks directly to it. Radiology AI algorithms are typically validated on imaging datasets from large academic programs — UCSF, Mass General, Mayo — that use standardized imaging protocols, high-field MRI equipment, and patient populations that skew toward higher socioeconomic status relative to the national Medicaid population. When those algorithms are deployed in a 25-bed critical access hospital in rural Montana using a ten-year-old CT scanner and serving a predominantly Native American patient population, the performance characteristics documented in the validation study may bear no relationship to actual clinical performance. The Partnership's member practices in these settings have no mechanism for knowing whether the AI tools they are deploying are performing as validated. There is no post-deployment surveillance requirement. There is no standardized reporting pathway. There is no feedback loop from clinical outcome to algorithm performance. The RFI proposes to accelerate adoption into exactly these settings without addressing any of these gaps.⏎⏎⏎Partnership for AI in Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 6⏎⏎⏎SECTION V: DOCUMENTED EXAMPLES FROM ADJACENT CLINICAL DOMAINS CONFIRM THAT THIS IS AN OBSERVED PATTERN, NOT A THEORETICAL RISK⏎⏎The Partnership wants to be precise about what the empirical record shows, because the Department's RFI appears to treat algorithmic bias as a concern to be noted rather than an observed failure mode requiring mandatory safeguards. The pulse oximetry literature is probably the clearest example of what unmonitored algorithmic bias looks like in clinical practice. Sjoding and colleagues, writing in the New England Journal of Medicine in December 2020, documented that pulse oximeters — devices that have been in clinical use for decades — systematically overestimated blood oxygen saturation in Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent arterial oxygen levels, and that this measurement error was associated with occult hypoxemia that went undetected and untreated at materially higher rates in Black patients (Sjoding MW, Dickson RP, Iwashyna TJ, Gay SE, Valley TS., "Racial bias in pulse oximetry measurement," N Engl J Med., 2020;383(25):2477-2478). This was not a novel algorithmic system. This was a mature, FDA-cleared medical device that had been in widespread clinical use for thirty-plus years. The disparity it produced had almost certainly been occurring throughout that period. It was detected when researchers looked for it. The regulatory and reimbursement systems that governed pulse oximetry deployment had never required anyone to look for it.⏎⏎The dermatology AI literature tells a similar story. Adamson and Smith, writing in JAMA Dermatology in 2018, documented that machine learning algorithms for skin lesion classification were trained predominantly on images of lighter-skinned patients, and that their diagnostic accuracy for melanoma and other skin cancers in patients with darker skin tones was substantially lower — a finding with direct life-or-death implications given that delayed melanoma diagnosis in patients of color is already a documented disparity (Adamson AS, Smith A., "Machine learning and health care disparities in dermatology," JAMA Dermatol., 2018;154(11):1247-1248). And Pierson and colleagues have documented that AI-based pain assessment tools trained on historical prescribing data systematically underestimate pain severity in Black and Hispanic patients, reproducing at scale the well-documented clinical pattern of undertreating pain in minority populations (Pierson E, Cutler DM, Leskovec J, Mullainathan S, Obermeyer Z., "An algorithmic approach to reducing unexplained pain disparities in underserved populations," Nat Med., 2021;27(1):136-140).⏎⏎The pattern across these domains is consistent: AI systems trained on historically biased data reproduce those biases in deployment; average performance metrics conceal the disparity; the disparity persists undetected until researchers specifically investigate it; and the regulatory framework governing the device's deployment contains no mechanism that would have required detection before harm had accumulated across thousands or millions of patient encounters. The Partnership submits that HHS's acceleration agenda, as currently framed in the RFI, will replicate this pattern at scale across the full breadth of clinical AI applications in Medicare and Medicaid.⏎⏎⏎Partnership for AI in Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 7⏎⏎⏎SECTION VI: THE RFI CONTAINS NO MANDATORY EQUITY AUDITING OR SURVEILLANCE FRAMEWORK⏎⏎The Partnership has reviewed the RFI in detail and finds no proposed requirement for pre-deployment equity auditing of AI systems as a condition of HHS endorsement or reimbursement. The RFI solicits input on how to accelerate adoption, how to address reimbursement barriers, and how to support research and development — but it does not propose, and does not ask whether to propose, a mandatory requirement that AI systems demonstrate equitable performance across demographic subgroups before receiving CMS reimbursement codes or HHS program endorsement. The Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology's own 2023 Issue Brief on Algorithmic Bias and Health Equity identified pre-deployment bias testing and post-deployment monitoring as the two foundational requirements for equitable AI deployment in healthcare settings (ONC, "Algorithmic Bias and Health Equity: Considerations for AI Tool Developers and Deployers," ONC Issue Brief, 2023). The fact that ONC has identified these requirements in a guidance document while the companion RFI from the same agency contains no proposal to make them mandatory is a policy inconsistency that the Partnership finds difficult to reconcile.⏎⏎The absence of mandatory equity requirements in the RFI is particularly consequential given the reimbursement incentive structure the Department is apparently contemplating. If HHS creates new CPT codes or add-on payment mechanisms to support AI-assisted clinical decision-making — as several RFI respondents and CMS advisory bodies have recommended — those payment mechanisms will create powerful financial incentives for rapid AI deployment that will operate independently of any voluntary equity guidance. Practices that deploy AI quickly will be reimbursed. Practices that pause to conduct equity validation will not receive any additional compensation for that caution. The reimbursement structure will, in effect, penalize equity due diligence and reward speed. The Partnership is not arguing against new reimbursement mechanisms for AI-assisted care. The Partnership is arguing that those mechanisms must be conditioned on demonstrated equitable performance, not merely on FDA clearance, which — as the evidence reviewed above makes clear — provides no such assurance.⏎⏎⏎Partnership for AI in Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 8⏎⏎⏎SECTION VII: SPECIFIC POLICY RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎The Partnership's comments focus on two specific RFI question areas: the questions pertaining to regulatory and reimbursement conditions for AI adoption, and the questions pertaining to research and development priorities. The Partnership does not address all RFI questions, and notes that the scope of the RFI substantially exceeds what a single organization can address responsibly in a comment period of this length.⏎⏎On the reimbursement and regulatory conditions questions, the Partnership recommends the following:⏎⏎1. Pre-Deployment Equity Auditing as a Condition of Reimbursement. HHS and CMS should establish, through notice-and-comment rulemaking, a requirement that any AI system seeking Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement — whether through a new CPT code, an add-on payment, or inclusion in an HHS-endorsed technology program — must demonstrate, prior to reimbursement eligibility, that its performance has been validated in stratified subgroup analyses covering race, ethnicity, sex, primary language, socioeconomic status, and geographic setting (rural/urban/frontier). The stratified validation must be conducted on an external dataset independent of the training and primary validation datasets. FDA clearance based on aggregate performance metrics should not be sufficient for reimbursement eligibility absent this stratified equity validation.⏎⏎2. Mandatory Post-Deployment Bias Surveillance. HHS should require, as a condition of continued reimbursement eligibility, that AI system deployers implement and report on post-deployment performance monitoring stratified by the same demographic dimensions listed above. Reporting should occur on at minimum an annual basis, should be submitted to a centralized HHS registry, and should include any evidence of performance degradation or disparate harm identified during the reporting period. The 2023 ONC Issue Brief on Algorithmic Bias and Health Equity should be operationalized as a mandatory reporting standard, not voluntary guidance.⏎⏎3. Equity Weighting in Reimbursement Rate-Setting. To the extent CMS establishes add-on payment mechanisms for AI-assisted clinical services, the Partnership recommends that payment rates be structured to provide higher reimbursement for AI deployments that have completed stratified equity validation and that serve patient populations with a Medicaid share of 40 percent or greater. This would create a positive financial incentive for equity due diligence rather than the current structure, which provides no such incentive.⏎⏎o In the radiology context specifically, the Partnership notes that the physician work component of AI-assisted interpretation should reflect the additional oversight burden associated with monitoring AI performance for disparate error patterns — a cost that existing RVU structures do not capture and that our survey data indicates is borne disproportionately by practices serving safety-net populations.⏎⏎4. Dedicated Funding for Equity-Focused External Validation Studies. The RFI's research and development questions ask about HHS investment priorities for AI. The Partnership recommends that HHS direct NIH and AHRQ to fund a sustained program of external validation studies specifically targeting AI algorithm performance in rural, safety-net, and minority-serving institutions. The academic center datasets that currently dominate the validation literature are systematically unrepresentative of the patient populations where algorithmic bias causes the most harm. Correcting that representational gap requires dedicated public investment, because the commercial incentive structure does not reward it.⏎⏎5. Harmonization of FDA and CMS Equity Standards. The current regulatory structure creates a gap between FDA device clearance standards, which are based on aggregate performance metrics, and the equity performance standards that CMS would need to apply as conditions of reimbursement. HHS should convene an interagency working group including FDA's Digital Health Center of Excellence, CMS, and ONC to develop harmonized equity performance standards applicable across the device approval and reimbursement pathways. The Partnership notes that this harmonization work was contemplated but not completed under the FDA's Predetermined Change Control Plan framework, and that the gap between the two regulatory pathways remains a structural impediment to equity-conditioned reimbursement.⏎⏎⏎Partnership for AI in Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 9⏎⏎⏎On the research and development priority questions, the Partnership adds one further recommendation. The differential between academic-center validation performance and real-world community-setting performance for clinical AI systems is not well-characterized in the current literature, and the gap in characterization itself constitutes a patient safety risk. HHS should fund a multi-site prospective registry study — analogous in structure to the National Cardiovascular Data Registry — that tracks AI algorithm performance outcomes in real-world clinical settings stratified by institution type, patient population, and demographic subgroup. Such a registry would generate the population-representative performance data that the current literature lacks, would provide an early-warning mechanism for detecting disparate harm before it accumulates to the scale seen in the pulse oximetry case, and would create a public data infrastructure that supports equity-conditioned regulatory decision-making. The Partnership estimates that a properly designed registry of this kind, enrolling 200 to 300 clinical sites across the rural-urban spectrum, would require initial HHS investment in the range of $45 to $60 million over five years — a figure that is modest relative to the scale of potential harm from unmonitored disparate AI performance across the Medicare and Medicaid populations.⏎⏎⏎Partnership for AI in Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 10⏎⏎⏎SECTION VIII: ADDITIONAL CONTEXT ON RADIOLOGY-SPECIFIC DEPLOYMENT PATTERNS⏎⏎The Partnership wants to add some additional context on the radiology setting specifically, because radiology is both the clinical domain where AI deployment is most advanced and the domain where the equity surveillance gap is most immediately consequential. As of the date of these comments, FDA has cleared more than 700 AI/ML-enabled medical devices, the substantial majority of which are imaging-based applications in radiology, cardiology, and pathology. The radiology AI market is growing at a compound annual rate estimated between 35 and 40 percent. CMS has issued a small number of reimbursement codes applicable to AI-assisted imaging interpretation, and the pressure from commercial vendors and some professional societies to expand those codes is substantial.⏎⏎Against that backdrop, the Partnership's survey finding that 58 percent of practices with deployed AI report that reimbursement structures fail to account for the oversight and integration costs of AI-assisted interpretation takes on additional significance. Those oversight costs are real, and the Partnership supports appropriate reimbursement for physician work associated with AI integration. But the Partnership is concerned that the current policy conversation about radiology AI reimbursement is focused almost entirely on the cost and workflow dimensions of AI adoption, and not at all on the equity performance dimensions. There is no current mechanism by which a radiology practice deploying an AI-assisted chest radiograph interpretation algorithm would know whether that algorithm is performing equitably across its patient population. There is no standardized reporting format for disparate performance. There is no reimbursement adjustment, upward or downward, based on equity performance. The algorithm gets deployed, the reads get billed, and if the system is systematically missing findings in Medicaid patients at a higher rate than in commercially insured patients, that pattern will not appear in any quality metric the practice is currently required to track.⏎⏎The Partnership's member practices are not indifferent to this problem. Several of our larger member systems in California and Illinois have begun conducting internal equity audits of their deployed AI tools, and the preliminary results from those voluntary efforts are not uniformly reassuring. But voluntary internal auditing by the practices with the most resources is not a substitute for mandatory pre-deployment equity validation and post-deployment surveillance applicable to all deploying institutions. The practices with the least capacity for voluntary equity auditing are, by definition, the practices serving the most vulnerable patient populations.⏎⏎The Partnership closes by reiterating that these comments are not opposition to AI adoption in clinical care. The evidence that well-validated AI tools can improve diagnostic accuracy, reduce radiologist burnout, and expand access to specialist-level interpretation in underserved settings is real and the Partnership takes it seriously. The concern is specifically with the sequencing and conditionality of adoption — with the fact that HHS is proposing to accelerate deployment without first establishing the equity safeguards that would make that deployment safe for the populations most at risk from algorithmic bias. The ONC's own 2023 Issue Brief acknowledged this risk. The literature from Obermeyer, Sjoding, Wong, Seyyed-Kalantari, Adamson, Pierson, Vyas, and others has documented it across multiple clinical domains and multiple demographic dimensions. The responsible regulatory response is to condition acceleration on equity, not to treat equity as a downstream consideration to be addressed after the deployment infrastructure is already in place.⏎⏎The Partnership respectfully requests that HHS treat the pre-deployment equity auditing and post-deployment bias surveillance recommendations in Section VII of these comments as priority action items in any forthcoming AI policy framework, and that any new reimbursement mechanisms for AI-assisted clinical services be structured from the outset to condition payment eligibility on demonstrated equitable performance across demographic subgroups. The Partnership would welcome the opportunity to brief HHS staff on our survey methodology and findings, and to participate in any interagency working group convened to address the equity conditionality questions raised in these comments.⏎⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎⏎William Mitchell⏎Director of Government Affairs⏎Partnership for AI in Health⏎1400 K Street NW, Suite 700⏎Washington, DC 20005⏎government.affairs@partnershipforaihealth.org⏎(202) 555-0183⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎⏎Partnership for AI in Health | HHS AI RFI Comments | October 2024 | Page 11⏎⏎⏎APPENDIX NOTE: The survey data referenced throughout these comments was collected between January and March 2024 in partnership with the Radiology Business Management Association. Survey instrument and full tabular results are available upon request. The Partnership notes that the survey was conducted under IRB exemption determination dated December 2023 from an independent IRB, and that individual practice-level data is protected under respondent confidentiality agreements. Aggregate findings are available for review by HHS staff. A more complete methodological description, including sampling frame, response rate calculations, and the weighting adjustments applied to account for practice-size variation, can be provided separately.⏎⏎The Partnership also notes that the citations to peer-reviewed literature throughout these comments are drawn from publicly available published sources and do not represent endorsement by the cited authors of the Partnership's specific policy recommendations. Several of the cited authors have, however, published separately on the policy implications of their findings in ways that are broadly consistent with the equity conditionality framework the Partnership is recommending here.⏎⏎One final note on the RFI process itself: the comment period for this RFI closed on a timeline that the Partnership found compressed relative to the complexity and scope of the questions being asked. The equity surveillance framework questions in particular would have benefited from a longer comment period and, ideally, from a set of more targeted sub-questions that would have allowed respondents to provide more structured input on specific regulatory mechanisms. The Partnership encourages HHS to consider a supplemental RFI or an advisory committee process specifically focused on equity conditions for AI reimbursement, and notes that the American College of Radiology's AI-LAB program and the Coalition for Health AI's CHAI framework both represent existing multi-stakeholder infrastructure that could support♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0097♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔10♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Wright♔♔Kansas♔♔United States♔Apple Health Technologies♔♔♔♔♔♔Ferz.AI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎SUBMISSION TO THE U.S. DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary, in Collaboration with ASTP/ONC⏎⏎Re: Request for Information on Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Submitted by: Thomas Wright, Chief Compliance Officer⏎Organization: Apple Health Technologies / Ferz.AI⏎Date: March 2025⏎⏎⏎INTRODUCTORY CONTEXT AND ORGANIZATIONAL VANTAGE POINT⏎⏎Ferz.AI submits these comments on the basis of operational experience that is, we believe, directly relevant to the questions HHS has posed. Through 47 integrated health system partnerships spanning 14 states, our deployment analytics have documented measurable improvements in specific clinical workflow metrics: an average 23% reduction in diagnostic cycle times and a 31% decrease in clinician documentation burden over the 18-month implementation window ending in Q4 2024. We are a company that benefits commercially from AI adoption in healthcare. We want to be plain about that. And it is precisely because we operate at the intersection of AI deployment and healthcare accountability that we feel obligated to register concerns about the framing of this RFI that we do not think serve the Department's long-term policy goals, or frankly the market conditions under which companies like ours can sustain credibility.⏎⏎The RFI's framing of administrative efficiency as an unqualified good is the concern we want to address most directly. HHS asks, in several of its solicited question categories, how the Department can reduce administrative burden through AI-assisted tools covering care coordination, prior authorization review, patient navigation, and benefits counseling. We understand the impulse. Himmelstein et al. (2014) documented that U.S. hospital administrative costs consume roughly 25% of total hospital expenditures, far exceeding comparable nations, and Sinsky et al. (2017) found that for every hour physicians spent in direct patient care, they spent nearly two hours on administrative tasks in the EHR and desk work. The burden is real and the cost is real. We are not disputing any of that. What we are disputing is the assumption that the administrative functions listed in this RFI are primarily a matter of processing throughput rather than skilled, judgment-intensive labor, and that AI systems trained on historical data can substitute for the contextual expertise those functions require without generating systematic, difficult-to-detect harms.⏎⏎⏎THE CATEGORY ERROR IN FRAMING ADMINISTRATIVE WORK AS CLERICAL PROCESSING⏎⏎Ferz.AI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎Care coordinators, patient navigators, and benefits counselors do not primarily process structured information. They operate in what Michael Polanyi described as the domain of tacit knowledge: knowledge that "can be transmitted only by example, not by precept" and that "we can know more than we can tell" (Polanyi, 1966, p. 4). A care coordinator working in a rural Kansas health system knows, for instance, that the county's only Medicaid-enrolled transportation vendor stopped accepting new patients in September 2024 because of a contract dispute that has not been resolved and is not reflected in any database the AI system would query. She knows that a particular patient's daughter, listed as an emergency contact, is estranged from the family and should not be contacted for care coordination purposes despite appearing in the record. She knows that a local food bank recently shifted its distribution schedule in a way that affects whether a diabetic patient's meal timing will align with insulin protocols. None of this is in a structured data field. None of it can be recovered from historical training corpora. It is knowledge that exists in the embodied, locally-situated practice of doing the job, and when it is displaced by an AI system that presents its outputs with apparent confidence, the errors it produces will be invisible precisely because no one will know to look for them.⏎⏎This is not a theoretical concern. Ash, Berg, and Coiera (2004) documented the phenomenon of "juxtaposition errors" in clinical information systems, in which the automation of information presentation creates conditions where the most consequential errors are the ones least likely to be caught, because the system's apparent completeness discourages the verification behavior that would catch them. Their framework applies with equal force to administrative AI systems: a patient navigator using an AI-generated referral recommendation will not typically call three community organizations to verify current capacity, because the system has already told her which one to use. If that recommendation is wrong because the training data is six months old, or because it was trained on utilization patterns from a demographically different population, the error propagates forward invisibly. Bates et al. (2023) found that nearly 25% of hospital admissions involve at least one adverse event, and while that study focused on clinical rather than administrative errors, the methodological lesson is the same: safety events in complex systems are systematically underdetected, and automation that reduces verification behavior makes the detection problem worse.⏎⏎⏎EQUITY CONCERNS THAT HHS'S FRAMING DOES NOT ADEQUATELY ADDRESS⏎⏎The populations most dependent on administrative support in healthcare are also the populations most likely to be harmed by AI systems that perform well on aggregate metrics while failing on the margins. Obermeyer et al. (2019) demonstrated that a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to manage population health systematically underestimated the care needs of Black patients, because it used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need without accounting for the structural inequities that cause Black patients to have lower healthcare utilization at equivalent levels of illness severity. The algorithm appeared to perform well by conventional validation metrics. The bias was invisible until researchers explicitly looked for it using race-stratified analysis. The Office of Inspector General's 2022 report on Medicare Advantage prior authorization denials found that some Medicare Advantage organizations denied prior authorization requests that met Medicare coverage criteria at rates that raised substantial concerns about beneficiary access to medically necessary care (OIG, 2022, OEI-09-18-00260). That report examined human decision-making in prior authorization. The question HHS should be asking is what happens when those decisions are made or pre-filtered by AI systems that optimize for denial-rate reduction or processing speed without adequate transparency into the criteria being applied.⏎⏎Virginia Eubanks's work in Automating Inequality (2018) is not a healthcare policy text, but her analysis of how automated eligibility systems in public benefits contexts systematically disadvantage low-income populations is directly applicable here. The patients who most need a human benefits counselor to navigate a Medicaid eligibility determination, a prior authorization appeal, or a care coordination referral are precisely the patients who are least well-represented in the training data of AI systems built on historical utilization patterns from commercially insured populations. When those systems produce errors, those patients typically lack the resources, health literacy, or institutional access to identify the error, appeal it, or find an alternative pathway. The harm is real and the accountability is diffuse enough that it rarely surfaces in the administrative record.⏎⏎Ferz.AI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 3⏎⏎⏎THE "FREES UP HUMAN TIME" ARGUMENT REQUIRES CLOSER EXAMINATION⏎⏎Ferz.AI wants to address directly the argument that AI automation of administrative functions will free up human workers for higher-value tasks. We hear this argument constantly, including from our own sales materials, and we think it deserves more scrutiny than it typically receives in this policy context. The argument assumes two things that our deployment analytics do not consistently support. First, it assumes that the administrative workforce has surplus capacity that is currently occupied by low-value processing tasks. In practice, the healthcare administrative workforce is already severely understaffed. A 2022 GAO report on AI in health care noted that workforce constraints are among the primary barriers to AI implementation, and the same workforce constraints that make AI adoption attractive also mean that when AI systems generate errors, the corrective labor falls on workers who have no margin to absorb it (GAO, 2022, GAO-22-104629). A care coordinator who has to identify, investigate, and correct an erroneous AI-generated referral has not had her burden reduced; she has had a different kind of burden added, one that is arguably more cognitively demanding than the original task because it requires reconstructing what the system did and why.⏎⏎Second, the argument assumes that the tasks being automated are genuinely lower-value than the tasks the freed-up time would be used for. This is not obviously true for the administrative functions named in this RFI. Prior authorization review, when done by a skilled human reviewer with access to patient context, is a checkpoint where clinical appropriateness errors are sometimes caught before they reach the patient. Care coordination is not a clerical task; it is a clinical support function that requires understanding of patient circumstances, family dynamics, community resource availability, and provider relationships. The National Academy for State Health Policy's 2023 issue brief on Medicaid prior authorization and AI-assisted review documented that states implementing AI-assisted prior authorization review have encountered accountability gaps that existing regulatory frameworks were not designed to address, precisely because those frameworks assumed human decision-makers whose reasoning could be reviewed (NASHP, 2023). When AI systems make prior authorization decisions or recommendations, the question of who is accountable for an erroneous denial is genuinely unresolved in current regulatory architecture.⏎⏎⏎EVIDENTIARY STANDARDS AND THE PRE-DEPLOYMENT VALIDATION PROBLEM⏎⏎Our cross-partner compliance audit identified regulatory ambiguity in liability attribution, algorithmic transparency requirements, and reimbursement coding alignment as the three primary friction points slowing broader adoption across 68% of participating facilities. We want to be specific about what this means in practice. When a health system asks us whether they are liable if our AI-assisted care coordination tool produces a referral recommendation that results in a patient not receiving timely care, we cannot give them a definitive answer. Current regulatory frameworks do not clearly address whether the AI developer, the health system deploying the tool, or the clinician who relied on the output bears responsibility for adverse outcomes. This ambiguity is not primarily a problem for us as a vendor; it is a problem for the patients who may be harmed and who need a clear accountability pathway to seek redress.⏎⏎Ferz.AI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 4⏎⏎⏎The analogy to clinical trial evidentiary standards is instructive here. Bhatt and Mehta (2016) described adaptive clinical trial designs as a mechanism for generating rigorous evidence while allowing trial parameters to evolve in response to accumulating data. The key principle is that evidentiary standards must be commensurate with the risk profile of the intervention. A drug that is administered to patients with life-threatening conditions under conditions of close medical supervision requires a different evidentiary threshold than a dietary supplement. The same logic should apply to AI systems used in healthcare administrative functions. An AI tool that assists in scheduling optimization has a different risk profile than one that pre-filters prior authorization requests or generates care coordination recommendations for patients with complex chronic conditions. HHS's current framework does not make this distinction clearly, and the result is that health systems are making deployment decisions without adequate guidance on what validation evidence is sufficient for which risk tier.⏎⏎Ferz.AI urges HHS to require pre-deployment validation studies for any AI system used in administrative healthcare functions that affect patient access to care, with specific requirements that include the following:⏎⏎1. Prospective performance validation in the demographic and clinical population in which the system will be deployed, not merely in the population on which it was trained. The Obermeyer et al. (2019) finding is a direct consequence of deploying a system validated on one population in a different one.⏎⏎2. Mandatory human override capacity with documentation requirements. Every AI-assisted administrative decision affecting patient care access should be subject to human review upon request, and the rate at which human reviewers override AI recommendations should be tracked, disclosed, and reviewed by an institutional governance body on a regular schedule.⏎⏎3. Prospective error-rate disclosure. Health systems deploying AI-assisted administrative tools should be required to disclose error rates to CMS and to state Medicaid agencies on a reporting schedule commensurate with the risk tier of the tool. This is not a novel requirement; CMS already requires quality reporting for a wide range of clinical functions. Extending that framework to high-risk administrative AI applications is a logical extension of existing accountability architecture.⏎⏎4. Race- and income-stratified performance reporting. Given the documented tendency of AI systems to perform well on aggregate metrics while producing disparate outcomes for marginalized populations, disaggregated performance reporting should be a baseline requirement rather than an optional best practice.⏎⏎Ferz.AI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 5⏎⏎⏎We want to be direct about why we are recommending requirements that will increase the compliance burden on vendors including ourselves. Our Q3 2024 policy working group found that reimbursement uncertainty caused 41% of our health system partners to defer or cancel planned AI expansion projects, representing an estimated $220 million in foregone capital deployment. In our assessment, that deferral reflects not just reimbursement uncertainty but a deeper anxiety about deploying systems whose accountability framework is unresolved. Health systems are not primarily worried about whether they will be reimbursed for AI-assisted care coordination; they are worried about whether they will be held liable when something goes wrong, and whether they will be able to demonstrate to regulators, patients, and plaintiff's attorneys that they deployed the system responsibly. Clear pre-deployment validation requirements and mandatory human override capacity would actually reduce that anxiety and accelerate adoption more reliably than reimbursement incentives alone. This is not a paradox. It is what the clinical device regulatory framework learned over decades: rigorous pre-market standards reduce post-market liability uncertainty and thereby create more favorable conditions for sustained investment.⏎⏎⏎THE ADMINISTRATIVE FRICTION ARGUMENT AND ITS LIMITS⏎⏎There is one more point Ferz.AI wants to make, and it is one we have some internal disagreement about, which is why we are flagging it rather than presenting it as a settled organizational position. Some administrative friction in healthcare is not waste. It is a protective checkpoint. A prior authorization reviewer who calls a physician's office to clarify the clinical rationale for a procedure is doing something more than processing a transaction; she is creating an opportunity for a clinical appropriateness error to be identified and corrected before it reaches the patient. The question of how much of that friction is genuinely protective versus genuinely wasteful is not answerable at the aggregate level. It depends on the specific administrative function, the specific patient population, and the specific institutional context. HHS's RFI does not ask this question. It assumes that friction reduction is the goal and asks only how AI can achieve it. We think that framing is incomplete, and that a more defensible policy goal is distinguishing between administrative friction that is protective and administrative friction that is purely transactional, and ensuring that AI automation is targeted at the latter rather than the former.⏎⏎Ferz.AI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 6⏎⏎⏎The difficulty, of course, is that this distinction is itself context-dependent and requires the kind of locally-situated judgment that Polanyi described. A prior authorization requirement that is purely bureaucratic overhead in a large urban academic medical center with robust care management infrastructure may function as a meaningful access checkpoint in a rural critical access hospital where the care coordinator is also the billing manager and the prior authorization reviewer and the patient navigator, and where the physician ordering the procedure has not reviewed the patient's full medication history because the patient's primary care provider is in a different system. We are not arguing that AI cannot play a role in administrative healthcare functions. We are arguing that the role it plays should be defined by demonstrated performance in the specific context of deployment, not by theoretical efficiency gains projected from aggregate data. That is a higher standard than HHS's current framing implies, and we think it is the right one.⏎⏎The NASHP (2023) brief on Medicaid prior authorization noted that at least seven states had implemented or were piloting AI-assisted prior authorization review as of early 2023 without having established clear accountability frameworks for erroneous denials. That is a regulatory gap that exists right now, affecting Medicaid beneficiaries right now. Whatever reimbursement incentives HHS creates to accelerate AI adoption in administrative functions, those incentives will operate in that gap unless HHS takes explicit action to close it. Ferz.AI respectfully urges the Department to prioritize the accountability architecture before the reimbursement incentives, not because we oppose reimbursement reform, but because incentivizing deployment into an unresolved accountability environment is likely to produce exactly the kind of high-profile failures that will set back adoption more broadly for years.⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎To summarize Ferz.AI's core recommendations on the questions most directly addressed in this submission:⏎⏎Regarding regulatory actions: HHS should develop a risk-tiered regulatory framework for AI systems used in administrative healthcare functions that affect patient access to care. Systems that pre-filter or recommend outcomes in prior authorization, care coordination, patient navigation, or benefits determination should be subject to mandatory pre-deployment validation in the target deployment population, with race- and income-stratified performance disclosure requirements and mandatory human override capacity. Liability attribution frameworks should be developed in consultation with CMS, OCR, and OIG to clarify the respective responsibilities of AI developers, deploying health systems, and individual clinicians.⏎⏎Regarding research and development actions: HHS should fund prospective research on the performance of AI administrative tools in safety-net and rural health system contexts, where the populations served are least well-represented in commercial training data and the administrative workforce is most constrained. The GAO (2022) report identified the absence of this kind of context-specific evidence as a gap; two years later, that gap has not been meaningfully closed.⏎⏎Ferz.AI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 7⏎⏎⏎We have deliberately not addressed the RFI's questions on clinical AI, reimbursement coding alignment, or interoperability in these comments, not because those questions are unimportant but because our most specific and evidence-grounded concerns are in the administrative domain and we did not want to dilute them with comments in areas where we have less to add to what other commenters will say. We are available to discuss any of these recommendations with Department staff and can provide additional data from our deployment analytics on request.⏎⏎Contact: Thomas Wright, Chief Compliance Officer⏎Apple Health Technologies / Ferz.AI⏎twrigh⏎⏎Ferz.AI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 8⏎⏎t@applehealthtech.com⏎913-[redacted per submission protocol]⏎⏎The specific concern about the Q4 2024 compliance audit findings on liability attribution is something we are prepared to discuss in more technical detail if HHS convenes a follow-up technical working group, as several of the edge cases we documented in rural Kansas and southeastern Missouri health system deployments do not fit cleanly into any of the existing regulatory categories and would benefit from direct dialogue with ONC and CMS staff rather than resolution through the comment process alone.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0098♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Wright♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔American Heart Association♔♔♔♔♔♔American Heart Association⏎7272 Greenville Avenue⏎Dallas, TX 75231⏎⏎James Wright, MPA⏎Policy Director, Federal Health Policy⏎james.wright@heart.org⏎⏎September 12, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care (Docket No. HHS-OS-2024-0007)⏎⏎---⏎⏎AHA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket HHS-OS-2024-0007 | Page 1⏎⏎---⏎⏎Dear Deputy Secretary and ONC/ASTP Staff:⏎⏎The American Heart Association (AHA) submits these comments in response to the Department's Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. The AHA represents more than 35 million volunteers, supporters, and patients whose cardiovascular health outcomes are directly implicated by how federal health policy shapes the deployment of AI-assisted clinical tools. The AHA's concern here is narrowly but urgently focused on two dimensions of the RFI that the Department has, in our assessment, addressed with insufficient specificity: the failure to establish mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing requirements, and the absence of any framework for ongoing post-deployment surveillance that would detect algorithmic bias before it causes systematic, population-level harm. The framing of the RFI, while broad and genuinely responsive to the complexity of AI adoption, treats equity as one consideration among many rather than as a foundational constraint on deployment. That framing is, we believe, inadequate.⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE EQUITY PROBLEM IS EMPIRICALLY DOCUMENTED, NOT THEORETICAL⏎⏎The Department's RFI solicits comment on how to "accelerate" adoption. That framing presupposes that speed is the primary challenge. For the populations the AHA serves — patients with cardiovascular disease, disproportionately concentrated among Black, Hispanic, and low-income communities — the more pressing challenge is not the pace of adoption but the conditions under which adoption occurs. There is now a substantial and convergent body of peer-reviewed literature establishing that AI systems trained on historically biased clinical data reproduce and entrench those biases at scale, often in ways that are invisible to the clinicians relying on them.⏎⏎The Obermeyer et al. study published in Science in 2019 (Obermeyer Z, Powers B, Vogeli C, Mullainathan S. Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations. Science. 2019;366(6464):447-453) documented that a widely deployed commercial risk-scoring algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent disease burden, because the algorithm used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need — a substitution that encoded decades of racially differential access to care directly into the model's predictions. This was not a fringe or experimental system. It was deployed across hundreds of health systems before the bias was characterized. The AHA notes that similar structural logic applies to cardiovascular risk stratification tools, many of which rely on utilization-based proxies that carry the same embedded assumptions.⏎⏎The problem is not confined to risk scoring. Sjoding et al., writing in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 (Sjoding MW, Dickson RP, Iwashyna TJ, Gay SE, Valley TS. Racial bias in pulse oximetry measurement. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(25):2477-2478), documented that pulse oximetry — the ubiquitous bedside measurement of blood oxygen saturation — systematically overestimates oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin pigmentation, producing occult hypoxemia that standard AI-assisted monitoring systems would not flag. During the COVID-19 pandemic, this bias translated directly into delayed recognition of respiratory deterioration among Black patients. The AHA finds it notable that this bias was detectable through basic stratified analysis of existing clinical data, yet it persisted for decades in a device that has been FDA-cleared and broadly deployed. The lesson is not that the technology failed in an unexpected way. The lesson is that the validation framework was never designed to detect this class of failure.⏎⏎---⏎⏎AHA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket HHS-OS-2024-0007 | Page 2⏎⏎---⏎⏎Sepsis prediction offers a parallel illustration at the algorithmic level. Wong et al., publishing in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2021 (Wong A, Otles E, Donnelly JP, et al. External validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model in hospitalized patients. JAMA Intern Med. 2021;181(8):1065-1070), conducted external validation of a proprietary sepsis alert system that had been deployed across hundreds of hospitals and found that the system's performance degraded substantially when applied to patient populations that differed from the original training cohort. Sensitivity and specificity figures that appeared acceptable in the development setting did not hold when the model encountered the demographic and comorbidity mix of community hospitals, safety-net institutions, or rural facilities. This is precisely the validation-to-deployment degradation problem that the AHA regards as among the most serious structural failures in current AI governance frameworks. A system that performs adequately in an academic medical center's patient population and then gets licensed and deployed into a federally qualified health center without revalidation is not a system that has been responsibly deployed. The Department's RFI does not, in the AHA's reading, contain a framework that would prevent this.⏎⏎The dermatology AI literature provides a further data point that is directly relevant to the cardiovascular context, because the underlying mechanism — training data that underrepresents darker-skinned patients — applies with equal force to imaging-based cardiovascular diagnostics. Adamson and Smith, in JAMA Dermatology in 2018 (Adamson AS, Smith A. Machine learning and health care disparities in dermatology. JAMA Dermatol. 2018;154(11):1247-1248), documented that machine learning systems for skin lesion classification were trained predominantly on images of lighter-skinned patients, producing diagnostic accuracy that was substantially lower for patients of color. The AHA's concern is that echocardiography AI, cardiac MRI interpretation systems, and retinal imaging tools used for cardiovascular risk assessment are being developed under training-data conditions that may replicate this same underrepresentation without any regulatory mechanism requiring developers to characterize the demographic composition of their training datasets prior to deployment.⏎⏎---⏎⏎AUTOMATION BIAS AS A MECHANISM FOR CONCEALMENT⏎⏎The literature on automation bias, surveyed extensively in Cabitza, Rasoini, and Gensini's 2017 JAMA commentary (Cabitza F, Rasoini R, Gensini GF. Unintended consequences of machine learning in medicine. JAMA. 2017;318(6):517-518), establishes that clinicians systematically over-rely on algorithmic outputs relative to their own clinical judgment, particularly under conditions of cognitive load, time pressure, or uncertainty — conditions that are endemic to the clinical environments where AI is being most aggressively promoted. This is not a critique of clinicians. It is a structural observation about how human cognition interacts with decision-support systems. The consequence, in the context of biased AI, is that algorithmic errors are harder to surface than equivalent errors made by human clinicians, because the algorithm's output carries an implicit authority that tends to suppress the clinician's independent assessment. Char, Shah, and Magnus made a related point in their 2018 NEJM analysis (Char DS, Shah NH, Magnus D. Implementing machine learning in health care — addressing ethical challenges. N Engl J Med. 2018;378(11):981-983) — that the opacity of many AI systems makes it structurally difficult for clinicians to know when to distrust the output, which means that bias embedded in the model is far less likely to be detected through ordinary clinical practice than bias in a physician's own reasoning, which is at least visible and subject to peer review.⏎⏎---⏎⏎AHA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket HHS-OS-2024-0007 | Page 3⏎⏎---⏎⏎The AHA is particularly concerned about this dynamic in cardiovascular care contexts where AI-assisted clinical decision support systems (CDSS) are being used to inform triage, risk stratification, and treatment prioritization. A physician who independently underestimates the severity of a Black patient's cardiac presentation is engaged in a form of clinical error that can, in principle, be identified through mortality review, quality improvement processes, or peer evaluation. A CDSS that systematically underscores Black patients' risk — because it was trained on data from a population with different baseline risk distributions, or because it uses utilization-based proxies — produces the same downstream harm through a mechanism that is invisible to standard quality review processes. Gianfrancesco et al., in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2018 (Gianfrancesco MA, Tamang S, Yazdany J, Schmajuk G. Potential biases in machine learning algorithms using electronic health record data. JAMA Intern Med. 2018;178(11):1544-1547), documented the specific pathways through which EHR-derived training data introduces and amplifies bias, including differential documentation practices, differential access to care that produces differential data density, and the use of administrative codes that encode clinical decisions already distorted by structural inequity. These are not hypothetical mechanisms. They are empirically characterized pathways between historical inequity and algorithmic perpetuation of that inequity.⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE DEPLOYMENT-SETTING PROBLEM⏎⏎The AHA's membership survey data, while not the primary subject of this comment, is relevant context. In the AHA's engagement with practicing cardiologists and cardiac care teams across the country, a consistent pattern has emerged: AI tools are being adopted most rapidly in well-resourced academic and large-system environments, while the populations experiencing the greatest cardiovascular disease burden — rural communities, safety-net hospital patients, low-income urban populations — are served by institutions that lack the informatics infrastructure, data science capacity, or regulatory expertise to conduct meaningful independent validation of the tools they are being sold. Seyyed-Kalantari et al., publishing in Nature Medicine in 2021 (Seyyed-Kalantari L, Zhang H, McDermott MBA, Chen IY, Ghassemi M. Underdiagnosis bias of artificial intelligence algorithms applied to chest radiographs in under-served patient populations. Nat Med. 2021;27(12):2176-2182), documented precisely this dynamic in chest radiograph interpretation: AI systems showed systematically lower diagnostic performance for patients in underserved settings, with the performance gap concentrated among patients who were female, Black, or publicly insured. The systems were not developed in those settings, were not validated in those settings, and were not monitored for performance degradation in those settings after deployment.⏎⏎This is the crux of the AHA's concern. The Department's RFI asks how to accelerate AI adoption. The AHA's answer is that accelerating adoption without mandatory equity safeguards is not a neutral act. It is a policy choice to deploy systems that are likely to perform worst for the populations experiencing the worst health outcomes. Wiens et al., in their 2019 Nature Medicine roadmap for responsible machine learning in health care (Wiens J, Saria S, Sendak M, et al. Do no harm: a roadmap for responsible machine learning for health care. Nat Med. 2019;25(9):1337-1340), were explicit that deployment in clinical settings should require prospective monitoring, ongoing revalidation, and clear criteria for discontinuation when performance falls below acceptable thresholds. The U.S. Government Accountability Office reached a similar conclusion in its 2022 report on AI in health care (U.S. Government Accountability Office. Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Benefits and Challenges of Technologies to Augment Patient Care. GAO-22-104629. Washington, DC: GAO; 2022), noting that existing oversight frameworks were not designed to address the continuous learning, model drift, and post-market performance degradation characteristics of deployed AI systems. Neither of these analyses has been translated into binding regulatory requirements. The AHA believes the Department's response to this RFI is an opportunity to begin that translation.⏎⏎---⏎⏎AHA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket HHS-OS-2024-0007 | Page 4⏎⏎---⏎⏎A further dimension of this problem involves the use of race as a variable in clinical algorithms more broadly. Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones, writing in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 (Vyas DA, Eisenstein LG, Jones DS. Hidden in plain sight — reconsidering the use of race correction in clinical algorithms. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(9):874-882), documented that numerous widely used clinical algorithms incorporate race as a correction factor in ways that systematically direct resources and interventions away from Black and other minority patients, often without clinical justification grounded in biology. The AHA regards this literature as directly applicable to the AI context: if underlying clinical algorithms used to generate training labels or to define outcome variables incorporate race-based corrections, then AI systems trained on those labels or outcomes will inherit and potentially amplify those corrections. The Department's RFI does not appear to contemplate this pathway, and the AHA believes it should.⏎⏎---⏎⏎SPECIFIC POLICY RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎The AHA's recommendations in response to this RFI are organized around the two dimensions identified at the outset: pre-deployment requirements and post-deployment surveillance. These are not the only dimensions of responsible AI governance, but they are the dimensions most directly relevant to the equity concerns documented above, and they are the dimensions where the current regulatory framework is most clearly inadequate.⏎⏎Regarding pre-deployment requirements, the AHA recommends:⏎⏎1. HHS should establish, through binding guidance or rulemaking as appropriate, a mandatory pre-deployment equity audit requirement for all AI-assisted clinical decision support tools used in Medicare and Medicaid reimbursed settings. The audit should require developers to characterize the demographic composition of training datasets, including race, ethnicity, sex, age, insurance status, and geographic setting, and to provide stratified performance metrics across those subgroups. Aggregate accuracy figures are insufficient. A system that achieves 90% sensitivity overall while achieving 72% sensitivity for Black patients is not a system that meets an acceptable performance standard, and the current regulatory framework does not require that the 72% figure be disclosed.⏎⏎2. HHS should require that pre-deployment validation be conducted in settings representative of the intended deployment population. A sepsis prediction model validated exclusively in an academic medical center cannot be assumed to perform adequately in a critical access hospital. The Department should adopt the external validation framework described by Wiens et al. (2019) as a minimum standard for high-stakes clinical applications.⏎⏎3. HHS should issue explicit guidance prohibiting the use of AI systems that incorporate race as a biological variable in clinical scoring, except where there is documented biological justification reviewed and approved through a defined regulatory process. This recommendation builds directly on the framework articulated by Vyas et al. (2020) and is consistent with existing HHS civil rights obligations under Section 1557 of the Affordable Care Act.⏎⏎Regarding post-deployment surveillance, the AHA recommends:⏎⏎o Health systems deploying AI-assisted CDSS in cardiovascular care, sepsis prediction, and other high-stakes clinical domains should be required to submit annual performance reports stratified by race, ethnicity, sex, and payer type to CMS or a designated HHS entity. These reports should be publicly accessible in machine-readable format.⏎⏎o HHS should establish a centralized adverse event reporting mechanism specifically for AI-related clinical errors, analogous to the FDA's MedWatch system, that allows clinicians and patients to report suspected cases of algorithmically mediated diagnostic or treatment error. The absence of such a mechanism means that the population-level harm caused by biased AI systems is currently invisible in federal data systems.⏎⏎o HHS should establish performance floor thresholds below which AI systems may not continue to be used in clinical decision-making without revalidation. The specific thresholds should be developed through a notice-and-comment process with input from clinical societies, patient advocates, and health equity researchers, but the principle — that there exists a performance level below which deployment is not permissible — should be established now.⏎⏎---⏎⏎AHA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket HHS-OS-2024-0007 | Page 5⏎⏎---⏎⏎The AHA also recommends that HHS consider a targeted moratorium on the use of AI systems in coverage determination and prior authorization decisions until the pre-deployment equity auditing and post-deployment surveillance frameworks described above are in place. The use of AI in coverage decisions is a domain where the consequences of algorithmic bias are particularly severe — a patient who is incorrectly assessed as low-risk by a biased algorithm and denied coverage for a necessary intervention has no practical mechanism to challenge the algorithmic basis of that denial under current frameworks. The AHA notes that the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology has addressed related concerns about information blocking and data access in its 2023 report (Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology. Report on Health Information Blocking and the ONC Health IT Certification Program. Washington, DC: HHS; 2023), but the coverage determination context raises distinct equity concerns that go beyond data access and require specific attention.⏎⏎The AHA is aware that these recommendations involve regulatory requirements that some stakeholders will characterize as barriers to adoption. The AHA's position is that this characterization misframes the relevant tradeoff. The choice is not between AI adoption with equity safeguards and AI adoption without them. The choice is between AI adoption that is more likely to reduce cardiovascular health disparities and AI adoption that is more likely to perpetuate them. Given the documented evidence base summarized above, the AHA does not believe that the Department has the option of treating equity auditing and post-deployment surveillance as optional enhancements. They are baseline requirements for responsible deployment.⏎⏎---⏎⏎One additional point that the AHA wants to flag, which is perhaps slightly outside the core equity-and-bias argument but which came up repeatedly in the AHA's internal policy consultations and we believe deserves at least brief mention: the reimbursement ambiguity problem identified in recent physician surveys, including the TMA membership survey finding that 74% of responding physicians cited uncertainty over coverage denials as a barrier to AI adoption, interacts with the equity problem in a specific and underappreciated way. Reimbursement ambiguity falls disproportionately on smaller, independent, and safety-net practices that lack the administrative infrastructure to navigate payer uncertainty. The result is that AI adoption in those settings is suppressed not because the tools are unavailable but because the economic risk of adoption is too high. This creates a perverse dynamic: the settings most likely to serve high-disparity populations are the settings least likely to adopt AI tools, while the settings already serving well-resourced populations adopt first, benefit first, and generate the performance data that informs future regulatory and reimbursement decisions. The equity gap gets built into the evidence base before HHS has had a chance to require that the evidence base be equitable.⏎⏎The AHA appreciates the Department's engagement through this RFI process and looks forward to participating in subsequent regulatory proceedings. Questions regarding these comments may be directed to the AHA's Federal Health Policy team.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎James Wright, MPA⏎Policy Director, Federal Health Policy⏎American Heart Association⏎7272 Greenville Avenue⏎Dallas, TX 75231⏎james.wright@heart.org⏎(214) 706-1388⏎⏎---⏎⏎AHA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket HHS-OS-2024-0007 | Page 6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0099♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Allen♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔UnitedHealth Group♔♔♔♔♔♔UnitedHealth Group Comments on HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL-CARE-RFI⏎Submitted by: William Allen, Director of Government Relations, UnitedHealth Group⏎Date: October 2024⏎⏎UnitedHealth Group | HHS AI RFI | October 2024 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎THE FRAMING PROBLEM⏎⏎This RFI arrives pre-loaded with premises that deserve direct challenge before any substantive response is possible. HHS has structured its inquiry around the question of how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, a framing that treats deployment velocity as the operative policy goal and treats regulatory ambiguity, reimbursement gaps, and R&D coordination failures as the primary obstacles to that goal. Our analysis, drawing on structured feedback from over 200 clinical informatics directors and deployment benchmarking data across a network of 47 health system partners, confirms that 83% of clinical AI deployments stall at the integration phase, and that institutions investing in AI-assisted diagnostics face an average 14-month delay between FDA clearance and meaningful clinical adoption due to unresolved billing classification issues. We cite these figures not to validate the RFI's framing but to complicate it. The question HHS has not asked is: who benefits from resolving these barriers, and who bears the cost when the resolution is designed primarily by the parties with the largest lobbying footprint in this proceeding?⏎⏎That is the question we are submitting to address. The barriers identified in this RFI, including CMS reimbursement ambiguity and the absence of standardized FDA-CMS coordination pathways, are real. But they are symptoms of a deeper structural condition, which is that the clinical AI market is already exhibiting the consolidation dynamics that have historically preceded regulatory capture in health care. Dafny and Lee (2016) documented the mechanisms by which market concentration in health care creates conditions where dominant incumbents shape regulatory frameworks in their commercial favor, and Gee and Gurwitz (2018) extended that analysis to show how provider-side consolidation translates into structural market power over procurement and technology adoption decisions. The Federal Trade Commission's 2023 policy brief on generative AI and the healthcare sector named vendor concentration and competitive foreclosure as primary risks in the clinical AI market specifically. HHS appears to have accepted the acceleration agenda without first asking whether the market structure into which it proposes to accelerate adoption is capable of producing competitive outcomes or patient benefit. Our analysis suggests it is not.⏎⏎⏎UnitedHealth Group | HHS AI RFI | October 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE REIMBURSEMENT MODERNIZATION QUESTION AND WHO IT ACTUALLY BENEFITS⏎⏎HHS has asked for input on reimbursement modernization as a mechanism for accelerating clinical AI adoption. Our deployment benchmarking data confirms that facilities operating under value-based care contracts are 2.3 times more likely to pilot clinical AI tools than those in traditional fee-for-service arrangements, which the Department appears to read as evidence that reimbursement architecture shapes adoption velocity and that modernizing that architecture will therefore accelerate adoption broadly. This reading is incomplete in a way that matters for policy. What the data also shows, and what HHS's framing elides, is that the primary beneficiaries of reimbursement modernization for clinical AI will be the EHR and AI platform vendors whose products are already embedded in health system infrastructure and whose proprietary integration architectures make switching prohibitively expensive.⏎⏎Schulte and Fry (2019) documented in exhaustive detail how the meaningful use program, analyzed by Blumenthal and Tavenner (2010) at the time of its design as a mechanism for broad health IT adoption, functioned in practice to entrench a small number of EHR incumbents whose market position became structurally self-reinforcing once federal subsidy was attached to their products. Holmgren, Apathy, and Adler-Milstein (2022) showed that vendor control of data infrastructure continued to produce interoperability failures more than a decade after meaningful use was enacted, failures that vendors had direct commercial incentives to perpetuate. Gans and Stern (2023) extended the platform economics literature to clinical AI specifically, showing that switching costs and proprietary lock-in dynamics in AI-integrated EHR environments are structurally analogous to the conditions that produced the EHR consolidation story, except that the AI layer adds a second rent-extraction mechanism on top of existing licensing and maintenance contracts. HHS is proposing to attach federal reimbursement to clinical AI products before conducting any independent analysis of whether the market structure those products inhabit is capable of supporting competitive outcomes.⏎⏎The CRADA mechanism HHS has referenced as a vehicle for public-private R&D partnership compounds this concern rather than addressing it. Rai (2023) analyzed the IP capture dynamics in federally funded AI development partnerships and found that CRADA structures routinely transfer public-sector data assets and research infrastructure into proprietary vendor control through licensing arrangements that give incumbents durable competitive advantages over potential market entrants. The GAO's 2022 report on AI in health care identified the absence of accountability mechanisms for vendor product performance as a significant regulatory gap, a finding that connects directly to Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan's (2019) Science paper documenting how a commercially deployed population health algorithm produced racially discriminatory outputs that the vendor had no regulatory obligation to correct. Maddox, Rumsfeld, and Payne (2019) raised the deployment accountability question in JAMA the same year and received no satisfactory regulatory answer. Four years later, HHS is proposing to accelerate the deployment of the same category of vendor products into a regulatory environment that still lacks the accountability infrastructure those authors identified as absent.⏎⏎UnitedHealth Group | HHS AI RFI | October 2024 | Page 3⏎⏎⏎We want to be direct about what we are observing in this proceeding. The comment infrastructure around this RFI, the breadth of participation from large AI vendors, EHR platform companies, and their trade associations, is not evidence of a well-functioning public input process. It is evidence that entities with the resources to field dedicated regulatory affairs teams are using this proceeding to shape the reimbursement and partnership frameworks that will govern a multi-billion dollar market in their favor. That is what regulatory capture looks like in its early stages. HHS has an obligation to account for the structural forces that will determine who actually benefits from the policies it develops in response to this RFI, and the Department has not done so. The acceleration agenda as currently framed will, if implemented along the lines the RFI suggests, produce a reimbursement and partnership architecture that entrenches incumbent vendors, transfers value from health systems to technology companies through licensing and maintenance contracts, and forecloses the competitive dynamics that would otherwise be necessary conditions for patient benefit.⏎⏎Our demand is specific: HHS should impose a moratorium on further regulatory acceleration actions in this space until an independent market structure analysis, conducted by parties without commercial relationships to the vendors whose products are under consideration, is completed and its findings are codified as anti-capture safeguards in any resulting rulemaking. That analysis should address vendor concentration ratios in the clinical AI and EHR markets, switching cost burdens on health system procurers, the IP allocation terms in any proposed CRADAs, and the reimbursement classification mechanisms being proposed, with specific attention to whether those mechanisms will function as⏎⏎UnitedHealth Group | HHS AI RFI | October 2024 | Page 4⏎⏎competitive-neutral incentives or as de facto subsidies to incumbents. The Department's contact for follow-up questions regarding this submission is:⏎⏎william.allen@uhg-govrelations.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0100♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔10♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔White♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔Vanderbilt University Medical Center Biomedical Informatics♔♔♔♔♔♔VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY MEDICAL CENTER⏎DEPARTMENT OF BIOMEDICAL INFORMATICS⏎FORMAL COMMENT SUBMISSION⏎⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-RFI-001⏎Submitted by: Michelle White, Professor of Health Policy⏎Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Department of Biomedical Informatics⏎Nashville, Tennessee 37232⏎mwhite@vumc.org⏎⏎---⏎⏎VUMC Biomedical Informatics Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 1⏎⏎I. THE ACCOUNTABILITY QUESTION HHS HAS NOT ANSWERED⏎⏎The Department's request for information arrives at a moment when the gap between what AI systems can do in clinical settings and what existing legal and institutional frameworks can handle has become genuinely difficult to ignore. This comment does not oppose the deployment of AI in clinical care. The research conducted within this institution has contributed to the development of predictive models now in active use at Vanderbilt University Medical Center, and the potential of these technologies to improve diagnostic accuracy, reduce preventable harm, and extend access to specialist-level clinical reasoning in underserved settings is real and well-documented (ONC, 2021). What this comment argues, with considerable urgency, is that HHS has structured its RFI in a way that treats accountability as a downstream implementation question rather than a precondition for responsible scaling — and that this sequencing error is not a minor procedural oversight but a structural commitment with lasting consequences.⏎⏎The concern is specific. When an AI system assists in a clinical decision that results in patient harm, the question of who bears legal and ethical responsibility is not resolved by existing doctrine. The hospital may argue that it deployed a vendor-certified tool according to manufacturer specifications. The vendor may argue that its system performed within validated parameters and that clinicians retained final authority. The clinician may argue that the AI recommendation appeared in the EHR workflow in a manner that made deviation practically difficult, and that institutional pressure to follow algorithmic outputs was substantial. This is not a hypothetical failure mode. It is what Hoffman and Podgurski (2023) describe as the "responsibility gap" in AI-assisted medical decision-making — a structural condition in which each party to a clinical AI deployment possesses plausible deniability, and in which the diffusion of causal contribution across vendor, institution, and clinician makes traditional tort doctrines, including respondeat superior and proximate causation standards, extraordinarily difficult to apply. Price, Gerke, and Cohen (2019) documented this problem in JAMA five years ago. The problem has not been resolved. HHS is now proposing to scale the deployment model that generates it.⏎⏎VUMC Biomedical Informatics Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎II. INSTITUTIONAL EVIDENCE OF THE TRANSLATION GAP⏎⏎Before addressing the structural accountability argument directly, this comment offers institutional context that bears on the Department's framing of the problem. A 2024 survey conducted by the Computing Research Association across 412 academic computing departments found that 68% of faculty conducting AI and machine learning research identified healthcare and clinical applications as a primary or significant area of investigation, yet fewer than 22% reported active collaboration with clinical partners or reimbursement-eligible deployment pathways (CRA, 2024). This is a striking finding. It means that the majority of federally supported academic AI research oriented toward clinical care is being conducted without the institutional infrastructure — contractual relationships with clinical sites, reimbursement pathway analysis, liability allocation agreements — that would be necessary to translate validated models into actual clinical practice.⏎⏎Our analysis of federally funded AI research portfolios from fiscal years 2020 through 2023 found a consistent pattern in which algorithmic development milestones were met on schedule while the regulatory scaffolding needed to translate those models into clinical workflows was absent or incomplete. In 74% of the grant final reports reviewed, principal investigators cited unclear HHS guidance as a top barrier to translational progress. This is worth sitting with for a moment, because the Department may read that finding as an argument for regulatory clarification that accelerates adoption — which is essentially what the RFI proposes. This comment reads it differently. The barrier is not merely a lack of guidance on how to deploy AI systems. It is a lack of guidance on who is responsible when those systems cause harm. Researchers know how to build models. They do not know, and neither does the Department, how to assign legal accountability when those models fail in a clinical workflow. Clarifying the deployment pathway without resolving the accountability question does not remove the barrier; it moves it downstream, where it will be encountered by patients.⏎⏎III. ACCOUNTABILITY DIFFUSION AS A DESIGN FLAW, NOT A GAP⏎⏎This is the structural argument this comment most wants the Department to absorb. Accountability diffusion in clinical AI deployments is not a regulatory gap that can be patched after the fact. It is a design feature of the current deployment model, in the sense that the contractual and institutional arrangements under which AI systems enter clinical workflows are structured, often deliberately, to distribute responsibility in ways that make clear legal accountability impossible to establish. Kearns and Roth (2023) reviewed vendor contracts for clinical AI systems deployed at academic medical centers and found that indemnification clauses, limitation-of-liability provisions, and "clinician final authority" language were nearly universal — and that these provisions, taken together, function to ensure that no single party can be held accountable for an adverse outcome attributable to AI-assisted decision-making. This is not an accident of drafting. It is a foreseeable consequence of deploying AI in a legal environment that has not established clear, non-delegable accountability obligations.⏎⏎VUMC Biomedical Informatics Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 3⏎⏎Cohen, Amarasingham, Shah, Xie, and Lo (2022) made a related point in Health Affairs: the use of complex predictive analytics in clinical care raises accountability concerns that existing malpractice doctrine is not designed to address, and that the failure to resolve these concerns before widespread deployment will create a body of adverse outcomes for which patients have no meaningful legal recourse. Gerke, Minssen, and Cohen (2020) reached a similar conclusion in their analysis of ethical and legal challenges in AI-driven healthcare, noting that the allocation of legal responsibility among developers, deployers, and clinicians is "deeply unsettled" and that this unsettled state creates perverse incentives: vendors have incentives to over-claim the autonomy of their systems when marketing and under-claim it when defending against liability, while institutions have incentives to frame AI adoption as a quality improvement initiative rather than a clinical decision-support tool that implicates the duty of care. The European Parliament recognized this problem as early as 2017, when the Delvaux resolution on civil law rules on robotics explicitly called for the establishment of strict liability frameworks for autonomous systems before their widespread deployment — a call that the United States regulatory apparatus has not answered in the seven years since (Delvaux, 2017).⏎⏎Pasquale (2022) has argued, in the context of algorithmic systems generally, that attribution and responsibility must be treated as non-negotiable design requirements, not optional features to be added after a system achieves market penetration. This argument has particular force in clinical contexts, where the asymmetry between institutional knowledge and patient capacity to identify and contest AI-related harms is extreme. Patients do not typically know whether an AI system was involved in their care. They cannot audit the model. They cannot review the vendor contract. When an AI-assisted diagnosis is wrong, or when an AI-generated risk score leads to a treatment decision that causes harm, the patient's ability to seek accountability depends entirely on whether the legal and regulatory framework has assigned that accountability to someone in advance. Right now, it has not. HHS's RFI proposes to accelerate the spread of a deployment model that systematically forecloses that accountability, and this comment regards that as a serious policy error.⏎⏎IV. THE REIMBURSEMENT AND REGULATORY PATHWAY QUESTIONS⏎⏎The RFI asks several questions about reimbursement mechanisms and regulatory pathways for clinical AI. This comment addresses those questions only insofar as they interact with the accountability problem, because the accountability problem is prior to both.⏎⏎On reimbursement: The Department is considering whether and how to create billing codes or alternative payment mechanisms for AI-assisted clinical services. This comment does not oppose the development of such mechanisms in principle. However, the creation of reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted care before accountability frameworks are in place would be a significant error, for the following reason. Reimbursement creates institutional incentives to deploy. Once those incentives are embedded in payment structures, the institutional and political pressure to maintain deployment will be substantial, even if subsequent evidence reveals systematic harms or accountability failures. Mello, Frakes, Blumenkranz, and Studdert (2020) have documented the relationship between malpractice liability structures and healthcare quality, finding that clear liability exposure is one of the mechanisms through which quality standards are enforced in clinical practice. If reimbursement for AI-assisted care is established before liability for AI-assisted harm is clearly assigned, the accountability mechanism that malpractice law provides will be absent at precisely the moment when deployment is expanding most rapidly.⏎⏎VUMC Biomedical Informatics Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 4⏎⏎On regulatory pathways: Kesselheim, Cresswell, and Sheikh (2023) conducted a comparative analysis of regulatory frameworks for clinical AI across multiple jurisdictions and found that the United States regulatory approach — which relies primarily on FDA pre-market clearance for software as a medical device and post-market surveillance — is poorly suited to AI systems that update over time, exhibit performance variation across patient populations, and interact with clinical workflows in ways that are difficult to characterize at the time of clearance. The concern about algorithmic bias is directly relevant here. Obermeyer, Nissan, Stern, Eaneff, Bernstein, and Mullainathan (2021) documented the mechanisms by which AI systems trained on historical clinical data can encode and perpetuate racial and socioeconomic disparities in care, and the accountability gap makes this problem worse: if no party is clearly responsible for monitoring and correcting discriminatory outputs in a deployed system, the incentive to do so is weak. This is not a hypothetical concern. It is a documented pattern with identifiable mechanisms.⏎⏎The specific questions this comment would like the Department to address in any subsequent rulemaking are the following:⏎⏎1. What legal entity — vendor, deploying institution, or individual clinician — bears non-delegable accountability for an adverse outcome attributable to an AI-assisted clinical decision, and how does the Department propose to make that assignment enforceable against contractual provisions designed to diffuse it?⏎⏎2. Will reimbursement for AI-assisted clinical services be conditioned on the existence of a binding accountability agreement that identifies a responsible party for each deployed system, and will that agreement be required to be disclosed to patients?⏎⏎3. What post-market surveillance obligations will attach to clinical AI systems deployed under any new reimbursement pathway, and who will bear the cost of that surveillance — the vendor, the deploying institution, or CMS?⏎⏎4. How does the Department propose to address the performance variation problem identified by Kesselheim et al. (2023), in which AI systems validated on one patient population exhibit materially different performance characteristics when deployed in a different clinical setting, and who is accountable when that variation produces harm?⏎⏎5. Will the Department require that patients be informed when AI systems have contributed to clinical decisions affecting their care, and if so, what form will that disclosure take?⏎⏎VUMC Biomedical Informatics Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 5⏎⏎V. THE INSTITUTIONAL DEPENDENCY PROBLEM⏎⏎There is a second structural concern that this comment wants to name explicitly, because it is present in the RFI's framing but not acknowledged. Once AI systems are embedded in clinical workflows, and once reimbursement structures create institutional incentives to maintain that embedding, the practical ability to revisit accountability questions diminishes. This is not speculation. It is what happens with any technology that becomes load-bearing in a clinical workflow. The EHR experience is instructive: interoperability and accountability problems that were identified during early EHR adoption in the mid-2000s have proven extraordinarily difficult to resolve precisely because institutional dependency on specific systems created both technical and contractual lock-in that made remediation costly and politically difficult. The same dynamic will operate with clinical AI, and it will operate faster, because AI systems are being deployed at a pace and scale that EHR adoption did not approach in its early years.⏎⏎The argument that accountability frameworks can be built after adoption has begun therefore carries a hidden assumption: that the political and institutional conditions for building those frameworks will be as favorable after adoption as before. This comment does not accept that assumption. The vendors, institutions, and clinical professional organizations that will have invested in specific deployment models will have strong incentives to resist accountability frameworks that impose retrospective liability or require costly system modifications. The patients who will have been harmed by accountability gaps in the interim will not be organized. HHS is in a stronger position to require accountability infrastructure now, before deployment scales, than it will be after institutional dependency deepens. Acceleration without that infrastructure is not neutral with respect to accountability. It is a policy choice to externalize accountability risk onto patients.⏎⏎VUMC Biomedical Informatics Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 6⏎⏎VI. WHAT HHS SHOULD DO BEFORE ACCELERATING ADOPTION⏎⏎This comment concludes with a concrete policy request, because the preceding analysis is intended to support a specific ask, not merely to register concern.⏎⏎HHS should condition any regulatory, reimbursement, or research-and-development action designed to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care on the prior establishment of a mandatory accountability framework that satisfies the following minimum conditions:⏎⏎o The framework must assign non-delegable legal responsibility for each AI-assisted clinical decision to an identifiable human or institutional party. Contractual provisions that purport to distribute or disclaim that responsibility must be unenforceable against patients as a matter of federal regulatory policy.⏎⏎o The framework must require disclosure to patients when AI systems have materially contributed to clinical decisions affecting their care, in language that is accessible and that identifies the responsible party.⏎⏎o The framework must establish post-market surveillance obligations with defined triggers — including performance degradation thresholds and adverse outcome reporting requirements — and must assign those obligations to a party with both the technical access and the legal incentive to act on them.⏎⏎o The framework must include an algorithmic bias monitoring requirement, with defined performance standards across demographic subgroups, and must condition continued reimbursement on demonstrated compliance with those standards.⏎⏎o The framework must be established through notice-and-comment rulemaking, not sub-regulatory guidance, so that its provisions are binding and judicially reviewable.⏎⏎This comment acknowledges that building this framework will take time and that the Department faces legitimate pressure to move quickly. That pressure is understandable. But the sequencing question — whether to accelerate adoption before or after accountability infrastructure exists — is not a technical question about implementation speed. It is a policy question about who bears the risk of getting this wrong.⏎⏎VUMC Biomedical Informatics Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 7⏎⏎The academic community conducting AI research relevant to clinical care has a direct stake in this question. Researchers at institutions like Vanderbilt have invested substantial effort in developing and validating models that could improve patient outcomes. Those models will not achieve their potential if the deployment environment systematically produces harms that erode patient and clinician trust in AI-assisted care. The accountability diffusion problem is not just a legal or ethical concern. It is a practical threat to the long-term viability of clinical AI as a field. A single high-profile adverse outcome attributable to an unaccountable AI system — and the CRA survey data suggests the conditions for such an outcome are present in a significant fraction of current deployments — could set back adoption by years and consume the institutional goodwill that researchers have spent a decade building.⏎⏎This comment also wants to note, without developing the point at length, that the international comparison is not favorable to the current U.S. approach. The comparative regulatory analysis in Kesselheim, Cresswell, and Sheikh (2023) found that several peer jurisdictions have moved toward mandatory pre-deployment accountability requirements for clinical AI, while the U.S. continues to rely on a combination of voluntary standards, post-market surveillance, and existing malpractice doctrine — none of which, individually or collectively, resolves the accountability gap that this comment has described. The Department should be aware that the regulatory vacuum it is proposing to fill with adoption incentives is a vacuum that other jurisdictions have concluded requires accountability requirements, not just deployment support.⏎⏎VUMC Biomedical Informatics Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 8⏎⏎One practical note on implementation: the framework this comment describes does not require the Department to resolve every open question about AI liability doctrine before any deployment occurs. There is a meaningful difference between deploying AI in a legal environment where accountability is unclear because no one has addressed the question, and deploying AI in a legal environment where accountability is clearly assigned even if the assignment is imperfect and subject to future refinement. The latter environment allows for learning, correction, and iterative improvement. The former produces institutional dependency on an accountability-free deployment model that becomes progressively harder to change. The Department should aim for the former, and the mandatory framework this comment proposes is a means to that end, not an obstacle to adoption.⏎⏎The specific provision this comment regards as most urgent is the non-delegability requirement. The vendor contract analysis in Kearns and Roth (2023) found that limitation-of-liability provisions in clinical AI vendor agreements were present in effectively all contracts reviewed, and that these provisions were structured to survive even negligence findings in some cases. Federal regulatory policy that renders such provisions unenforceable against patients — not against other commercial parties, but specifically against patients seeking accountability for AI-related harm — would be a targeted and enforceable intervention that does not require the resolution of broader questions about AI liability doctrine. It is, in the view of this comment, the single most important step the Department could take to address the structural accountability defect before scaling deployment further.⏎⏎The Department's timeline for responding to this RFI and any subsequent rulemaking is not known to this comment's author at the time of submission, but given that the CRA survey was fielded in the first quarter of 2024 and reflects conditions that have likely worsened since, the⏎⏎VUMC Biomedical Informatics Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 9⏎⏎urgency of the accountability question is not diminishing. The volume of clinical AI deployments documented in the ONC's own reporting has increased materially in each of the past three years, and the absence of a mandatory accountability framework has not prevented that growth — which is precisely the problem. Growth in the absence of accountability is not evidence that accountability is unnecessary. It is evidence that the harm is accumulating, untracked and unassigned, in a population of patients who have no reliable mechanism to identify it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
